window.PLAZA_ORGS = [{"id":"guadalajara","name":"Guadalajara Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Guadalajara","Félix Gallardo organization","The Federation (original)","Padrino's cartel"],"founded":1980,"dissolved":1989,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"JAL","city":"Guadalajara","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496},"summary":"The Guadalajara Cartel was Mexico's first nationwide trafficking federation, built around 1980 by Sinaloan smugglers Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo, Rafael Caro Quintero and Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo after Operation Condor pushed them out of the Sierra Madre. It industrialized marijuana cultivation, opened Mexico to Colombian cocaine through Honduran broker Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros, and bought protection from the DFS. The 1985 torture-murder of DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena destroyed it, and its 1987-89 partition of plazas created the Tijuana, Juárez, Sonora and Sinaloa cartels.","history":"The cartel grew out of the Sinaloan smuggling world of the 1970s. When the Mexican army launched Operation Condor in January 1977, spraying and burning poppy and marijuana fields in the Golden Triangle of Sinaloa, Durango and Chihuahua, the leading traffickers moved their families and money to Guadalajara. Their patriarch, Pedro Avilés Pérez, the first Mexican smuggler to use aircraft, was killed by federal police near Culiacán on 15 September 1978. His lieutenants divided the inheritance: Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo ('Don Neto', born 1930 in Badiraguato), Rafael Caro Quintero (born 1952 in La Noria, Badiraguato) and Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo (born 1946 in Culiacán), a former Sinaloa state police officer and bodyguard to governor Leopoldo Sánchez Celis. By 1980 the three ran a single organization from Guadalajara, with the ex-DFS agent Juan José Esparragoza Moreno ('El Azul') as their political fixer.\n\nThe model was industrial. Caro Quintero planted sinsemilla on ranches across Chihuahua, Sonora and Zacatecas and employed thousands of pickers; Fonseca handled finances and opium; Félix Gallardo ran corruption and logistics. From about 1982 the Honduran Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros connected them to the Medellín and Cali cartels, and Mexico became the main transshipment corridor for Colombian cocaine after the US Caribbean interdiction campaign. A US appeals court later found that Félix Gallardo and Matta were earning more than 5 million dollars a week; journalists put the federation's revenue in the billions. Protection came from the Dirección Federal de Seguridad under Miguel Nazar Haro, whose credentials cartel gunmen carried, and from the Federal Judicial Police. In November 1984, acting on DEA intelligence, 450 Mexican soldiers raided Rancho Búfalo near Allende, Chihuahua, a 1,000-hectare (2,500-acre) complex whose marijuana the DEA valued at up to 8 billion dollars, with seizure figures reported as high as 10,000 tons.\n\nRetaliation followed. On 7 February 1985 DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena was seized outside the US consulate in Guadalajara and taken to a house at Lope de Vega 881, where he and pilot Alfredo Zavala Avelar were tortured over more than 30 hours and killed. Their bodies were found on 5 March 1985 near La Angostura, Michoacán. Washington all but closed the border under Operation Camarena and opened Operation Leyenda, the largest homicide investigation in DEA history. Caro Quintero, who had fled with DFS help, was arrested in Alajuela, Costa Rica, on 4 April 1985; Fonseca was arrested in Puerto Vallarta on 7 April 1985. Both received 40-year sentences in Mexico. Matta was seized in Tegucigalpa on 5 April 1988 and received life terms in the United States; Rubén Zuno Arce, brother-in-law of ex-president Echeverría, was convicted in Los Angeles and died in prison in 2012. Matta's Camarena-kidnapping convictions were vacated in May 2017 because of discredited FBI hair evidence and the charges were dropped in December 2018, but his drug-trafficking life sentences stood: a compassionate release granted in May 2025 was reversed by the Ninth Circuit on 8 October 2025, and he died in the federal medical prison at Springfield, Missouri, on 30 October 2025, aged 80.\n\nFélix Gallardo, still protected, kept operating for four more years. At a 1987 meeting in Acapulco he divided the country into plazas: Tijuana to his nephews the Arellano Félix brothers, Ciudad Juárez to the Carrillo Fuentes family, Sonora to Miguel Caro Quintero, the Pacific and Sinaloa to Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, Héctor 'El Güero' Palma and Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, while Juan García Ábrego kept Matamoros. He was arrested in Guadalajara on 8 April 1989 by federal commander Guillermo González Calderoni, held in Reclusorio Sur and moved in 1991 to the Almoloya maximum-security prison. The cartel formally ceased to exist; its heirs went to war, and the Tijuana-Sinaloa feud produced the killing of Cardinal Posadas Ocampo at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993.\n\nThe founders' legal sagas outlived the organization by decades. Félix Gallardo was moved to the Puente Grande complex in Jalisco after a December 2014 transfer approval on health grounds, was sentenced on 23 August 2017 to a further 37 years for the Camarena and Zavala murders after a first-instance trial of more than three decades (confirmed on appeal in February 2018, with 20.8 million pesos in damages), was denied house arrest on 20 February 2019, and gave his only interview to Telemundo that year, denying responsibility. In March 2022 the Supreme Court refused to review his original 40-year sentence. On 12 September 2022 a judge granted him house arrest, but the Attorney General's Office appealed and the transfer was suspended within days; he has since been reported to remain in the Jalisco prison. On 28 May 2025 the First Collegiate Criminal Tribunal of Jalisco asked the Supreme Court to take up his direct amparo (16/2024) against the 37-year Camarena conviction, a request admitted by Chief Justice Piña and assigned to Justice Margarita Ríos Farjat. Fonseca was moved to house arrest in Atizapán de Zaragoza on 27-28 July 2016, after Mexico refused a US extradition request in 2015 on the ground that he had already been tried for the murders, and completed his 40-year sentence on 5 April 2025 at 94; in November 2025 he sued for the return of money and accounts seized in 1985, a claim a Mexico City district judge dismissed and he appealed.\n\nCaro Quintero served 28 years before a Jalisco court freed him on a jurisdictional technicality on 9 August 2013. A new warrant followed within a day; the FBI put him on its Ten Most Wanted list in April 2018 with a 20-million-dollar reward, and he rebuilt a trafficking base in Caborca, Sonora. Marines captured him on 15 July 2022 in San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa; a Navy Black Hawk supporting the operation crashed in Los Mochis, killing 14 marines. On 27 February 2025 Mexico sent him and 28 other prisoners to the United States, and on 28 February he pleaded not guilty in Brooklyn federal court to running a continuing criminal enterprise and to the Camarena murder. On 5 August 2025 the Justice Department said it would not seek the death penalty. At a 19 March 2026 hearing Judge Frederic Block set trial for 8 March 2027; at a ten-minute status conference on 17 June 2026 prosecutors asked for an anonymous, partly sequestered jury transported by US Marshals and for classified-information procedures, defense lawyer Mark DeMarco said he would oppose the jury motion, and prosecutors said plea discussions were possible though no offer had been made. Judge Block indicated he would rule at the next conference, set for 1 October 2026.\n\nAs of September 2026 the Guadalajara Cartel is a historical organization whose three founders are all still alive: Félix Gallardo, 80, imprisoned in Jalisco; Fonseca, 96, free in the State of Mexico; and Caro Quintero, 73, awaiting a March 2027 trial in New York.","leaders":[{"name":"Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo","alias":"El Padrino / El Jefe de Jefes","role":"Founder and supreme boss","from":1980,"to":1989,"fate":"Arrested Guadalajara 8 Apr 1989; 40-year sentence, plus 37 years imposed 23 Aug 2017 for the Camarena and Zavala murders (confirmed Feb 2018); house arrest granted 12 Sept 2022 but suspended after an FGR appeal; reported still held in the Puente Grande complex, Jalisco, aged 80, nearly blind and deaf; on 28 May 2025 a Jalisco tribunal asked the SCJN to review his Camarena conviction (amparo directo 16/2024), pending as of Sept 2026"},{"name":"Rafael Caro Quintero","alias":"El Narco de Narcos / El Príncipe","role":"Co-founder; marijuana production chief","from":1980,"to":1985,"fate":"Arrested Costa Rica 4 Apr 1985; 40-year sentence; freed on technicality 9 Aug 2013; recaptured Choix, Sinaloa 15 Jul 2022; sent to US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded not guilty EDNY 28 Feb 2025; death penalty ruled out 5 Aug 2025; trial set 8 Mar 2027 before Judge Frederic Block; anonymous-jury motion pending, next conference 1 Oct 2026"},{"name":"Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo","alias":"Don Neto","role":"Co-founder; finances and opium","from":1980,"to":1985,"fate":"Arrested Puerto Vallarta 7 Apr 1985; 40-year sentence; US extradition refused by Mexico in 2015; house arrest in Atizapán de Zaragoza from 27-28 Jul 2016; completed sentence 5 Apr 2025 aged 94; free; sued in Nov 2025 for return of assets seized in 1985"},{"name":"Juan José Esparragoza Moreno","alias":"El Azul","role":"Ex-DFS agent; political broker","from":1980,"to":1989,"fate":"Passed to Juárez and Sinaloa cartels; reported dead of a heart attack in June 2014, never officially confirmed"},{"name":"Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros","alias":"El Negro","role":"Honduran cocaine broker to Medellín and Cali","from":1980,"to":1988,"fate":"Seized in Tegucigalpa 5 Apr 1988; life sentences in US for trafficking; Camarena kidnapping convictions vacated May 2017 and charges dropped Dec 2018; compassionate release granted May 2025 reversed by Ninth Circuit 8 Oct 2025; died in federal custody at MCFP Springfield, Missouri, 30 Oct 2025, aged 80"},{"name":"Manuel Salcido Uzeta","alias":"El Cochiloco","role":"Chief enforcer, Mazatlán plaza","from":1980,"to":1989,"fate":"Shot dead in Guadalajara on 9 Oct 1991"},{"name":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Señor de los Cielos","role":"Lieutenant; Ojinaga and air logistics","from":1985,"to":1989,"fate":"Became head of the Juárez Cartel; died 4 Jul 1997 in Mexico City after plastic surgery"}],"factions":[{"id":"tijuana_plaza","name":"Tijuana plaza (Arellano Félix)","from":1987,"to":1989,"area":"Baja California","leader":"Benjamín and Ramón Arellano Félix","note":"Assigned at the 1987 Acapulco meeting; became the Tijuana Cartel"},{"id":"juarez_plaza","name":"Ciudad Juárez plaza (Carrillo Fuentes)","from":1987,"to":1989,"area":"Chihuahua","leader":"Rafael Aguilar Guajardo / Amado Carrillo Fuentes","note":"Became the Juárez Cartel after Aguilar Guajardo's 1993 murder"},{"id":"sonora_plaza","name":"Sonora corridor (Caro Quintero)","from":1987,"to":1989,"area":"Sonora, Arizona border","leader":"Miguel Caro Quintero","note":"Became the Sonora Cartel"},{"id":"pacific_plaza","name":"Pacific / Sinaloa plaza","from":1987,"to":1989,"area":"Sinaloa, Nayarit, Jalisco, Durango","leader":"Joaquín Guzmán Loera, Héctor Palma Salazar, Ismael Zambada García","note":"Nucleus of the Sinaloa Cartel"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"government","from":1980,"to":1985,"note":"Protection from the DFS under Miguel Nazar Haro, the Federal Judicial Police and Jalisco officials; DFS credentials carried by cartel gunmen"},{"cartel":"other","from":1982,"to":1988,"note":"Medellín and Cali cartels: Colombian cocaine transshipped through Mexico via Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":1987,"to":1989,"note":"Juan García Ábrego's Matamoros organization recognized as a separate plaza at the 1987 Acapulco meeting"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"us_government","from":1984,"to":1989,"note":"Rancho Búfalo raid Nov 1984, Operation Camarena border slowdown Feb 1985, Operation Leyenda manhunt"},{"cartel":"government","from":1985,"to":1989,"note":"Post-Camarena crackdown: arrests of Caro Quintero and Fonseca (Apr 1985), dissolution of the DFS (1985), arrest of Félix Gallardo (Apr 1989)"}],"activities":["Industrial sinsemilla marijuana cultivation (Chihuahua, Sonora, Zacatecas, Sinaloa)","Opium gum and brown heroin","Transshipment of Colombian cocaine for Medellín and Cali","Bribery of DFS, federal police and state officials","Money laundering through hotels, ranches and real estate in Guadalajara","Kidnapping and murder (Camarena and Zavala, 1985)"],"peak":{"from":1982,"to":1985,"states":["JAL","SIN","CHH","SON","BCN","DUR","NAY","MIC"],"note":"Controlled most marijuana exports and the new cocaine corridor from Guadalajara; Rancho Búfalo alone employed thousands of workers before its November 1984 destruction"},"facts":["Rancho Búfalo, raided in November 1984 by 450 soldiers, covered about 1,000 hectares (2,500 acres) and its marijuana was valued by the DEA at up to 8 billion dollars","A US appeals court found that Félix Gallardo and Matta-Ballesteros were earning more than 5 million dollars a week","Camarena was tortured for more than 30 hours at Lope de Vega 881 after his 7 February 1985 abduction; his body was found on 5 March 1985 in Michoacán","Caro Quintero was arrested in Costa Rica on 4 April 1985, 56 days after the kidnapping, and Fonseca three days later in Puerto Vallarta","Félix Gallardo's first-instance trial for the murders lasted more than three decades and ended on 23 August 2017 with a 37-year sentence on top of his original 40, plus 20.8 million pesos in damages","Caro Quintero served 28 years, was freed on 9 August 2013, and was recaptured on 15 July 2022; a Navy Black Hawk crash that day killed 14 marines","The FBI placed Caro Quintero on its Ten Most Wanted list in April 2018 with a reward of up to 20 million dollars","Mexico transferred Caro Quintero and 28 other prisoners to the US on 27 February 2025; his trial is set for 8 March 2027 before 92-year-old Judge Frederic Block","Fonseca completed his 40-year sentence on 5 April 2025 at age 94 and in November 2025 sued for the return of assets seized at his 1985 arrest","Matta-Ballesteros, captured on 5 April 1988, died in US federal custody on 30 October 2025 at 80, three weeks after the Ninth Circuit reversed his compassionate release"],"status_2026":"Defunct since 1989, but its founders remain in the news. Rafael Caro Quintero is held in New York awaiting a trial set for 8 March 2027 in the Eastern District of New York, with the death penalty off the table since August 2025; prosecutors have asked for an anonymous jury, which the defense opposes, and Judge Block is due to rule at a status conference on 1 October 2026, while plea talks have been described as possible but no offer has been made. Félix Gallardo, 80, is reported to remain in the Puente Grande complex in Jalisco after prosecutors blocked his September 2022 house-arrest order, and the Supreme Court was asked in May 2025 to review his Camarena conviction. Fonseca Carrillo has been free since completing his sentence on 5 April 2025, and cocaine broker Matta-Ballesteros died in US custody on 30 October 2025.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiki_Camarena","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_%C3%81ngel_F%C3%A9lix_Gallardo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Caro_Quintero","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernesto_Fonseca_Carrillo","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/2016/07/28/1107610","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Matta-Ballesteros","https://www.laprensa.hn/honduras/muere-juan-ramon-matta-ballesteros-carcel-estados-unidos-BE27985958","https://irp.fas.org/congress/1998_hr/ct980226.htm","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2025/4/9/don-neto-ya-esta-libre-cumplio-su-condena-de-40-anos-por-el-caso-kiki-camarena-349085.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/11/10/don-neto-exlider-del-cartel-de-guadalajara-exige-al-gobierno-la-devolucion-de-su-dinero/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/nacion/piden-a-la-corte-resolver-si-felix-gallardo-es-culpable-del-asesinato-de-kiki-camarena-capo-busca-echar-abajo-sentencia/","https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/us-wont-seek-death-penalty-mexican-drug-lords-zambada-quintero-rcna223278","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/mundo/juicio-contra-rafael-caro-quintero-en-ny-comenzara-el-8-de-marzo-de-2027/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/mundo/rafael-caro-quintero-comparece-en-audiencia-en-nueva-york-fiscalia-busca-blindar-al-jurado-para-evitar-acoso-o-intimidacion/"],"verified":true},{"id":"sinaloa","name":"Sinaloa Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Sinaloa","Cártel del Pacífico","The Federation (La Federación, c. 2001-2008)","Guzmán-Loera Organization","Blood Alliance (La Alianza de Sangre)"],"founded":1989,"dissolved":0,"parent":"guadalajara","origin":{"state":"SIN","city":"Culiacán","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394},"summary":"The Sinaloa Cartel is the Pacific-coast trafficking confederation that emerged from the 1989 break-up of the Guadalajara Cartel and, under Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán and Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, became the most powerful drug-trafficking organization in the Western Hemisphere. Built as a network of allied families and cells rather than a strict hierarchy, it moved cocaine, marijuana, heroin, methamphetamine and later fentanyl into the United States for more than three decades. Since September 2024 it has been consumed by a civil war between Guzmán's sons (Los Chapitos) and Zambada's heirs (La Mayiza) that has killed thousands in Sinaloa and fragmented the organization.","history":"The cartel was born from the collapse of Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara Cartel after the February 1985 murder of DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena and Félix Gallardo's arrest on April 8, 1989. The old federation's plazas were divided, and the Sinaloa wing based in Culiacán fell to Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán Loera, Héctor 'El Güero' Palma Salazar, Juan José 'El Azul' Esparragoza Moreno and Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada García, later joined by the Beltrán Leyva brothers and Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel. Their first war was with the Arellano Félix brothers' Tijuana Cartel: a November 1992 shootout at the Christine discotheque in Puerto Vallarta and, on May 24, 1993, the Guadalajara airport gun battle that killed Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo and six others. Guzmán was arrested in Guatemala on June 9, 1993 and Palma on June 23, 1995 after his jet crash-landed in Nayarit, leaving Zambada and Esparragoza to run the business in a loose alliance with Amado Carrillo Fuentes' Juárez Cartel until Carrillo's death on July 4, 1997.\n\nGuzmán's escape from Puente Grande prison on January 19, 2001 opened the cartel's golden age. With Zambada, Esparragoza, Coronel and Arturo, Alfredo and Héctor Beltrán Leyva he assembled 'The Federation,' which absorbed the Milenio, Sonora and Colima networks and pushed into Nuevo Laredo against the Gulf Cartel and Los Zetas from 2003. The alliance with Juárez broke when Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes was killed in Culiacán on September 11, 2004; Guzmán's brother Arturo was murdered inside Altiplano prison on December 31, 2004 in retaliation. The Federation itself shattered after Alfredo 'El Mochomo' Beltrán Leyva was arrested on January 21, 2008, a betrayal the brothers blamed on Guzmán; Guzmán's son Édgar was shot dead in a Culiacán parking lot on May 8, 2008, and the Sinaloa-Beltrán Leyva war spread across Sinaloa, Sonora, Durango and Guerrero until Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed by marines in Cuernavaca on December 16, 2009. Simultaneously the cartel fought the Juárez Cartel for Ciudad Juárez (2008-2012, more than 10,000 dead in the city) and, allied with the Gulf Cartel, fought Los Zetas across the northeast (2010-2013). Coronel was killed by the army in Zapopan on July 29, 2010; Forbes listed Guzmán among the world's billionaires from 2009 to 2012.\n\nGuzmán was captured in Mazatlán on February 22, 2014, escaped Altiplano through a 1.5 km tunnel on July 11, 2015, was recaptured in Los Mochis on January 8, 2016 and extradited to the United States on January 19, 2017. Esparragoza was reported dead of a heart attack in June 2014, a death never confirmed. The vacuum produced the 2016-2017 war between Dámaso López Núñez, Guzmán's former operator, and Guzmán's sons, settled when López was arrested in Mexico City on May 2, 2017 and later testified against his old boss. A Brooklyn jury convicted Guzmán on February 12, 2019; on July 17, 2019 Judge Brian Cogan sentenced him to life plus 30 years with a $12.6 billion forfeiture order. From 2016 the cartel's main external enemy was the Jalisco New Generation Cartel (CJNG), which contested Baja California, Zacatecas, Sonora, Chihuahua and Chiapas.\n\nUnder Zambada's umbrella, Guzmán's sons Iván Archivaldo, Jesús Alfredo, Ovidio and Joaquín turned Los Chapitos into the cartel's fentanyl engine. The state's failed attempt to arrest Ovidio on October 17, 2019 (the 'Culiacanazo') ended with his release after gunmen seized Culiacán. He was captured on January 5, 2023 in an operation that killed 10 soldiers and 19 gunmen, and was extradited on September 15, 2023. On April 14, 2023 US prosecutors unsealed indictments against 28 Sinaloa figures including all four brothers, and Chapitos security chief Néstor Isidro 'El Nini' Pérez Salas was captured on November 22, 2023 and extradited on May 25, 2024.\n\nOn July 25, 2024 Joaquín Guzmán López flew Zambada to the Santa Teresa airfield outside El Paso, where both were arrested; Zambada said in an August 10, 2024 letter that he had been ambushed at a meeting arranged with Governor Rubén Rocha Moya and rector Héctor Melesio Cuén, who was murdered that same day. Fighting between La Mayiza and Los Chapitos began in Culiacán on September 9, 2024. In the first year roughly 1,850 to 2,000 people were killed and hundreds to nearly 2,000 disappeared in Sinaloa. Washington designated the cartel a Foreign Terrorist Organization on February 20, 2025; the DEA warned in May 2025 that Los Chapitos had allied with CJNG; the Treasury designated Los Chapitos on June 9, 2025 and 'Los Mayos / La Mayiza' on September 18, 2025; Ovidio pleaded guilty in Chicago on July 11, 2025; Zambada pleaded guilty in Brooklyn on August 25, 2025; Mexico transferred 26 cartel figures, including Zambada's son-in-law 'El Chavo Félix', to the United States on August 12, 2025; and Joaquín pleaded guilty on December 1, 2025 to drug conspiracy and continuing-criminal-enterprise counts, stipulating that he had organized Zambada's kidnapping.\n\nIn 2026 the war outlived its protagonists. CJNG chief Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera was killed in Tapalpa, Jalisco on February 22, 2026; on February 26 the US unsealed narcoterrorism charges against Tijuana plaza boss René 'La Rana' Arzate and offered up to $5 million each for him and his brother Alfonso 'El Aquiles'; a Southern District of New York indictment unsealed April 29, 2026 accused Governor Rocha Moya and nine other current and former officials of protecting Los Chapitos, and he took leave on May 2 (interim governor Yeraldine Bonilla), returned briefly on August 21-22 and was granted indefinite leave on August 23, 2026. Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch said on June 16, 2026 that the Chapitos-CJNG alliance had ended with El Mencho's death. Judge Cogan sentenced Zambada to life without parole with a $15 billion forfeiture on July 20, 2026. The army killed Chapitos plaza chief Cristhian Guadalupe Pérez 'El Texas' or 'El 01' in Culiacán on July 8, 2026 and arrested southern-Sinaloa regional chief Adrián 'El 20' in Villa Unión on July 28; analysts in mid-2026 described the cartel as a set of rival blocs and dozens of cells fighting from Culiacán to Mazatlán and the sierra, and La Mayiza and its Los Cabrera allies were also battling CJNG in Zacatecas by July-August. As of September 2026 local tallies count more than 4,000 people dead or missing in Sinaloa since September 2024, Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo Guzmán and Ismael 'Mayito Flaco' Zambada Sicairos remain fugitives with $10 million rewards on the Guzmán brothers, and the cartel survives as a fractured confederation rather than a single command.","leaders":[{"name":"Joaquín Guzmán Loera","alias":"El Chapo","role":"Co-founder and principal leader","from":1989,"to":2016,"fate":"Arrested Guatemala June 9 1993; escaped Puente Grande Jan 19 2001; captured Mazatlán Feb 22 2014; escaped Altiplano July 11 2015; recaptured Los Mochis Jan 8 2016; extradited Jan 19 2017; life + 30 years July 17 2019, ADX Florence"},{"name":"Ismael Zambada García","alias":"El Mayo","role":"Co-founder; senior leader and de facto chief after 2016","from":1989,"to":2024,"fate":"Detained July 25 2024 at Santa Teresa airfield near El Paso after Joaquín Guzmán López flew him in; pleaded guilty Aug 25 2025 (EDNY) to racketeering conspiracy and CCE; sentenced to life without parole July 20 2026 with $15 billion forfeiture"},{"name":"Héctor Luis Palma Salazar","alias":"El Güero Palma","role":"Co-founder","from":1989,"to":1995,"fate":"Arrested June 23 1995 after jet crash in Nayarit; extradited to US 2007; returned to Mexico June 2016; held at Altiplano on Mexican charges"},{"name":"Juan José Esparragoza Moreno","alias":"El Azul","role":"Co-founder, mediator and logistics chief","from":1989,"to":2014,"fate":"Reported dead of a heart attack June 2014; death never confirmed"},{"name":"Ignacio Coronel Villarreal","alias":"Nacho Coronel","role":"Federation partner; Jalisco/Colima plaza and methamphetamine chief","from":2001,"to":2010,"fate":"Killed by the army in Zapopan, Jalisco, July 29 2010"},{"name":"Arturo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Barbas","role":"Federation partner; security and Guerrero/Morelos operations","from":2001,"to":2008,"fate":"Split from the cartel Jan 2008; killed by marines in Cuernavaca Dec 16 2009"},{"name":"Dámaso López Núñez","alias":"El Licenciado","role":"Guzmán's operator; rebel faction leader 2016-17","from":2001,"to":2017,"fate":"Arrested Mexico City May 2 2017; extradited July 2018; pleaded guilty Sept 2018 in Virginia; life sentence later reduced to 14 years for cooperation, projected release 2032"},{"name":"Iván Archivaldo Guzmán Salazar","alias":"El Chapito","role":"Leader of Los Chapitos faction","from":2016,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; up to $10 million US reward; indicted SDCA July 2014 and NDIL April 2023"},{"name":"Ismael Zambada Sicairos","alias":"El Mayito Flaco","role":"Leader of La Mayiza faction","from":2024,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; US arrest warrant since 2020"},{"name":"Aureliano Guzmán Loera","alias":"El Guano","role":"Leader of Gente del Guano faction in the Badiraguato sierra","from":2016,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; $5 million US reward since Nov 2021; survived repeated capture operations 2023-26"}],"factions":[{"id":"chapitos","name":"Los Chapitos","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Culiacán, Sonora, Chihuahua, Baja California","leader":"Iván Archivaldo Guzmán Salazar","note":"Guzmán's sons; fentanyl engine of the cartel; at war with La Mayiza since Sept 9 2024"},{"id":"mayiza","name":"La Mayiza","from":2024,"to":0,"area":"Culiacán valley, southern Sinaloa, Durango, Zacatecas, Tijuana","leader":"Ismael Zambada Sicairos","note":"Zambada loyalists organized around Los Rusos, Los Cabrera and the Arzate brothers; designated by OFAC as 'Los Mayos / La Mayiza' Sept 18 2025"},{"id":"gente_del_guano","name":"Gente del Guano","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Badiraguato and Tamazula (Golden Triangle)","leader":"Aureliano Guzmán Loera","note":"Chapo's brother; has fought his nephews for poppy and route control in the sierra since 2023; alignment in the 2024-26 war reported inconsistently"},{"id":"los_salazar","name":"Los Salazar","from":2000,"to":0,"area":"Sonora sierra and Sonora-Arizona corridor","leader":"Salazar family (Adán Salazar Zamorano lineage)","note":"Sonora enforcement arm; fought Caro Quintero's Caborca Cartel 2020-23; aligned with Los Chapitos"},{"id":"gente_nueva","name":"Gente Nueva","from":2007,"to":0,"area":"Chihuahua (Ciudad Juárez, sierra)","leader":"Various cell chiefs","note":"Armed wing created for the Juárez war; later split into Chapitos- and Mayo-aligned cells"},{"id":"los_antrax","name":"Los Ántrax","from":2008,"to":2020,"area":"Culiacán","leader":"José Rodrigo Aréchiga Gamboa 'El Chino Ántrax'","note":"Zambada's elite hit squad; Aréchiga arrested Amsterdam Dec 2013, murdered in Culiacán May 2020"},{"id":"los_cabrera","name":"Los Cabrera","from":2005,"to":0,"area":"Durango and southern Zacatecas","leader":"Cabrera Sarabia family","note":"Durango network loyal to Zambada; fighting CJNG in Zacatecas alongside La Mayiza in 2026"},{"id":"los_mazatlecos","name":"Los Mazatlecos / Cártel de Guasave","from":2008,"to":0,"area":"Northern Sinaloa (Guasave), Mazatlán","leader":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores 'El Chapo Isidro'","note":"Beltrán Leyva remnant; historic enemy that sided with La Mayiza against Los Chapitos from May 2025"},{"id":"los_damasos","name":"Los Dámasos","from":2016,"to":2018,"area":"Eldorado, Culiacán, La Paz","leader":"Dámaso López Núñez","note":"Rebel faction defeated after López's May 2017 arrest; son 'Mini Lic' surrendered in US July 2017"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"juarez","from":1989,"to":2004,"note":"Shared Pacific-to-border logistics with Amado Carrillo Fuentes until the 2004 rupture"},{"cartel":"milenio","from":2001,"to":2009,"note":"Valencia network was a Federation branch until its 2009-10 split produced CJNG"},{"cartel":"sonora","from":1990,"to":2005,"note":"Caro Quintero brothers' Sonora corridor worked with the Culiacán leadership"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2010,"to":2013,"note":"Joint front against Los Zetas in Tamaulipas and Nuevo León"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2006,"to":2010,"note":"Anti-Zetas alliance in Michoacán and Guanajuato"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Jalisco group first fought as the 'Matazetas' under Sinaloa patronage"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2025,"to":2026,"note":"CJNG backed Los Chapitos against La Mayiza (DEA warning May 2025); ended with El Mencho's death Feb 2026 per Harfuch (June 16 2026)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"tijuana","from":1989,"to":2010,"note":"Arellano Félix war; 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting"},{"cartel":"juarez","from":2004,"to":2012,"note":"Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes killed Sept 2004; battle for Ciudad Juárez 2008-12"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Split after El Mochomo's Jan 2008 arrest; Édgar Guzmán killed May 2008"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2010,"to":2013,"note":"Fought across the northeast, Durango and Zacatecas"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"War for Baja California, Zacatecas, Sonora, Chiapas; CJNG vs La Mayiza in Zacatecas 2026"},{"cartel":"caborca","from":2020,"to":2023,"note":"Caro Quintero's Caborca Cartel fought Los Chapitos and Los Salazar in northern Sonora"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2011,"to":2014,"note":"Clashes over Michoacán and Jalisco routes"}],"activities":["Cocaine wholesale from Colombia, Peru, Ecuador and Bolivia","Fentanyl synthesis from Chinese and Indian precursors","Methamphetamine production in Sinaloa, Durango and Jalisco","Heroin and opium poppy (Golden Triangle)","Marijuana cultivation and trafficking","Money laundering via Chinese brokers, shell companies and real estate","Fuel theft (huachicol) and mining extortion","Arms smuggling from the United States","Bribery of police, military and politicians"],"peak":{"from":2008,"to":2016,"states":["SIN","DUR","CHH","SON","BCN","BCS","NAY","JAL","ZAC","COA","NLE","GRO","MEX","CMX","ROO","CHP"],"note":"Controlled the Pacific and Arizona corridors, won Ciudad Juárez, and supplied an estimated majority of US cocaine while Guzmán featured on the Forbes billionaires list (2009-2012)"},"facts":["The May 24, 1993 Guadalajara airport shootout between Sinaloa and Tijuana gunmen killed Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo and six others.","El Chapo escaped Altiplano prison on July 11, 2015 through a 1.5 km tunnel fitted with rails and a motorcycle.","El Chapo's July 17, 2019 sentence was life plus 30 years with a $12.6 billion forfeiture order.","The January 5, 2023 capture of Ovidio Guzmán cost the lives of 10 soldiers and 19 gunmen in a single day.","El Mayo Zambada was ordered to forfeit $15 billion when sentenced to life without parole on July 20, 2026; prosecutors said they had not yet located assets to satisfy it.","The Chapitos-Mayiza war killed roughly 1,850-2,000 people in Sinaloa in its first year (Sept 2024-Sept 2025); by September 2026 local tallies counted more than 4,000 people dead or missing.","The US designated the Sinaloa Cartel a Foreign Terrorist Organization on February 20, 2025.","On August 12, 2025 Mexico transferred 26 cartel figures to the United States in a single day, including El Mayo's son-in-law Juan Carlos 'El Chavo Félix' Félix Gastélum.","A Southern District of New York indictment unsealed April 29, 2026 named sitting Governor Rubén Rocha Moya and nine other current and former officials as cartel collaborators; he took leave on May 2 and indefinite leave on August 23, 2026.","At least ten social-media content creators were murdered in Sinaloa during the Mayiza-Chapitos war by August 2026, including César Gastélum, shot dead while livestreaming in Culiacán on August 4, 2026."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Sinaloa Cartel no longer has a single head: El Mayo is serving life in the US, El Chapo is in ADX Florence, Ovidio and Joaquín Guzmán López have pleaded guilty in Chicago and await sentencing with no dates set, and the organization is split into rival Chapitos and Mayiza blocs plus semi-independent sierra factions and dozens of local cells. La Mayiza is widely reported to hold the momentum in Culiacán and is expanding into Zacatecas against CJNG, while Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo Guzmán, Mayito Flaco and El Guano all remain fugitives. The war has left more than 4,000 people dead or missing since September 9, 2024, drove Governor Rocha Moya to take leave after his US indictment (May 2 and again, indefinitely, August 23, 2026), and continues to drive violence in Culiacán, Mazatlán, Durango and Baja California.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/sinaloa-cartel-profile/","https://insightcrime.org/news/four-decades-of-el-mayos-criminal-career-end-in-a-life-sentence/","https://insightcrime.org/news/the-new-era-of-the-sinaloa-cartel/","https://insightcrime.org/news/violence-zacatecas-mexico-surges-cjng-mayiza-clash/","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/governor-sinaloa-and-nine-other-current-and-former-mexican-officials-charged-drug","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/joaquin-guzman-lopez-son-el-chapo-and-leader-sinaloa-cartel-pleads-guilty-federal-drug","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0252","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/cartel-leader-ismael-el-mayo-zambada-sentenced-life-us-prison/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/06/16/con-la-muerte-de-el-mencho-termino-la-alianza-con-los-chapitos-harfuch/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Garc%C3%ADa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ovidio_Guzm%C3%A1n_L%C3%B3pez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rub%C3%A9n_Rocha_Moya"],"verified":true},{"id":"chapitos","name":"Los Chapitos","aka":["La Chapiza","Los Menores","Guzmán Salazar / Guzmán López brothers","Cártel de los Chapitos"],"founded":2016,"dissolved":0,"parent":"sinaloa","origin":{"state":"SIN","city":"Culiacán","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394},"summary":"Los Chapitos is the Sinaloa Cartel faction built by Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán's sons Iván Archivaldo, Jesús Alfredo, Ovidio and Joaquín Guzmán López after their father's 2014-2016 arrests. It became the cartel's dominant fentanyl producer and exporter and its most violent wing through the Los Ninis hit squads. Since September 2024 it has fought a war against La Mayiza that has cost it Ovidio and Joaquín to US custody and much of its grip on Culiacán.","history":"The Guzmán brothers grew up inside the business. Iván Archivaldo (born 1983 according to the US State Department) and Jesús Alfredo (born 1986) are sons of Guzmán's first wife, Alejandrina Salazar; Joaquín (born 1986) and Ovidio (born March 29, 1990) are sons of Griselda López. Their brother Édgar was killed in a Culiacán parking lot on May 8, 2008 at the outbreak of the Beltrán Leyva war, an event that pushed the surviving siblings into leadership roles. Iván was designated a Kingpin Act 'lieutenant' of his father in June 2012 and indicted in the Southern District of California in July 2014. On August 15, 2016, months after their father's final arrest, Iván and Jesús Alfredo were kidnapped from the La Leche restaurant in Puerto Vallarta by CJNG gunmen and released within days after El Mayo Zambada's mediation.\n\nThe faction took its independent shape in the 2016-2017 war against Dámaso 'El Licenciado' López Núñez, the family's longtime operator, who tried to inherit Guzmán's networks after the January 2017 extradition. The brothers, backed by Zambada, defeated the Dámasos; López was arrested in Mexico City on May 2, 2017 and his son surrendered in the United States that July. Los Chapitos then rebuilt their father's Culiacán, Sonora and Chihuahua routes and, after 2017, moved decisively into fentanyl, importing precursors from China and running labs across Sinaloa, protected by a security apparatus known as Los Ninis under Néstor Isidro 'El Nini' Pérez Salas.\n\nTheir power was demonstrated on October 17, 2019, when the National Guard detained Ovidio in Culiacán and hundreds of Chapitos gunmen paralyzed the city until President López Obrador ordered his release. On December 15, 2021 the State Department offered $5 million for each brother. On January 5, 2023 the army recaptured Ovidio near Jesús María in an operation that killed 10 soldiers and 19 gunmen; on April 14, 2023 the Justice Department unsealed indictments against the four brothers and 24 associates, calling them the world's largest fentanyl operation; Ovidio was extradited on September 15, 2023; and El Nini was captured on November 22, 2023 and extradited on May 25, 2024.\n\nOn July 25, 2024 Joaquín Guzmán López flew El Mayo Zambada to a Santa Teresa airfield outside El Paso, where both were arrested. Zambada's camp treated the delivery as a kidnapping, and on September 9, 2024 open war began in Culiacán between La Mayiza and Los Chapitos. The faction lost key operators quickly: José Ángel 'El Güerito' Canobbio was arrested in Culiacán on February 19, 2025 and Kevin Alonso 'El 200' Gil Acosta the same month, and Chicago prosecutors kept pressing the brothers in custody. The DEA warned in May 2025 that Los Chapitos had allied with CJNG, which supplied money and fighters; videos in mid-May 2025 and reports of a CJNG operative nicknamed 'El Jardinero' serving as Chapitos security chief supported the claim. The Treasury designated Los Chapitos as a criminal organization on June 9, 2025, adding secondary-sanctions risk to Iván and Jesús Alfredo; Ovidio pleaded guilty in Chicago on July 11, 2025 to two drug-distribution counts and two continuing-criminal-enterprise counts and began cooperating, after 17 relatives were escorted into the United States in May 2025; Joaquín pleaded guilty on December 1, 2025 to one drug-conspiracy count and one continuing-criminal-enterprise count and stipulated that he had organized Zambada's kidnapping, for which prosecutors said he would receive no cooperation credit.\n\nIn 2026 the pressure grew. The death of CJNG leader Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera on February 22, 2026 weakened the faction's outside patron; the April 29, 2026 indictment of Governor Rubén Rocha Moya and nine officials, which stemmed from the Chapitos case, alleged the faction had helped him win the 2021 election in exchange for protection. On June 16, 2026 Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch said the CJNG alliance, which he described as El Mencho financing and supplying gunmen to a Chapitos faction in southern Sinaloa, had ended with El Mencho's death. In Culiacán the army killed plaza chief Cristhian Guadalupe Pérez, 'El Texas' or 'El 01', on July 8, 2026, and on July 28 federal forces arrested Adrián 'El 20', the cartel's regional chief for Mazatlán, Villa Unión, Rosario, Concordia and Escuinapa, linked to the 2019 and 2023 Culiacanazos; other operators fell in Mazatlán and Escuinapa in June and July. ICE circulated a 'two down and two to go' wanted poster on July 6, 2026 reiterating the up-to-$10 million rewards for Iván and Jesús Alfredo, whom InSight Crime described as the last Chapitos standing.\n\nAs of September 2026 Los Chapitos are led from hiding by Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo, retain cells in Culiacán's north, Sonora, Chihuahua and parts of Baja California, and still ship fentanyl and methamphetamine, but they are on the defensive against a Mayiza that has taken ground in Culiacán, counts on El Chapo Isidro's Mazatlecos in the north, and enjoys the momentum that Ovidio's and Joaquín's guilty pleas and cooperation with US prosecutors have stripped from the brothers.","leaders":[{"name":"Iván Archivaldo Guzmán Salazar","alias":"El Chapito","role":"Principal leader","from":2016,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; up to $10 million US reward (reiterated July 2026); indicted SDCA July 2014 and NDIL April 2023"},{"name":"Jesús Alfredo Guzmán Salazar","alias":"El Alfredillo","role":"Co-leader","from":2016,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; up to $10 million US reward; kidnapped by CJNG Aug 15 2016 and released"},{"name":"Ovidio Guzmán López","alias":"El Ratón","role":"Co-leader; fentanyl and methamphetamine operations","from":2016,"to":2023,"fate":"Released after Oct 17 2019 Culiacanazo; captured Jan 5 2023; extradited Sept 15 2023; pleaded guilty Chicago July 11 2025; cooperating, no sentencing date set"},{"name":"Joaquín Guzmán López","alias":"El Güero Moreno","role":"Co-leader; logistics and finances","from":2016,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested July 25 2024 after flying El Mayo to Santa Teresa, NM; pleaded guilty Chicago Dec 1 2025 (drug conspiracy and CCE); sentencing pending, no date set"},{"name":"Néstor Isidro Pérez Salas","alias":"El Nini","role":"Chief of security (Los Ninis)","from":2017,"to":2023,"fate":"Captured Culiacán Nov 22 2023; extradited May 25 2024; held at MDC Brooklyn awaiting trial in SDNY"},{"name":"José Ángel Canobbio Inzunza","alias":"El Güerito","role":"Security and financial operator in Culiacán","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested Culiacán Feb 19 2025"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_ninis","name":"Los Ninis","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Culiacán","leader":"Néstor Isidro Pérez Salas 'El Nini' (to 2023)","note":"Elite security and hit squad; decimated by arrests and the Mayiza war"},{"id":"los_salazar","name":"Los Salazar","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Sonora sierra and border","leader":"Salazar family","note":"Sonora allies; fought Caborca Cartel 2020-23"},{"id":"gente_nueva_chapitos","name":"Gente Nueva (Chapitos-aligned cells)","from":2018,"to":0,"area":"Chihuahua","leader":"Various","note":"Retain routes through the Chihuahua sierra to Ciudad Juárez"},{"id":"la_chapiza","name":"La Chapiza (Culiacán cells)","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Northern Culiacán, Navolato, Mazatlán","leader":"Cristhian Guadalupe Pérez 'El Texas' (killed July 8 2026)","note":"Urban plaza cells that bore the brunt of the 2024-26 fighting"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2016,"to":2024,"note":"Operated under El Mayo's umbrella until the July 25 2024 betrayal"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2025,"to":2026,"note":"CJNG money and fighters against La Mayiza; ended with El Mencho's death Feb 22 2026 (Harfuch, June 16 2026)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Civil war since Sept 9 2024"},{"cartel":"caborca","from":2020,"to":2023,"note":"War with Caro Quintero's network in northern Sonora"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2016,"to":2024,"note":"CJNG kidnapped Iván and Alfredo in Puerto Vallarta Aug 2016; fought over Baja California and Sonora"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Édgar Guzmán killed May 2008"}],"activities":["Fentanyl production and export","Methamphetamine","Cocaine","Heroin","Precursor chemical imports from China","Money laundering through Chinese brokers and cryptocurrency","Extortion and kidnapping in Culiacán","Fuel theft (huachicol) in Sinaloa and neighboring states"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2024,"states":["SIN","SON","CHH","BCN","DUR","NAY"],"note":"After the 2019 Culiacanazo the brothers controlled Culiacán outright and, per the 2023 US indictment, ran the largest fentanyl pipeline into the United States"},"facts":["On October 17, 2019 gunmen forced the release of Ovidio Guzmán within hours; López Obrador later said he acted to prevent up to 200 deaths.","The January 5, 2023 operation to capture Ovidio killed 10 soldiers and 19 gunmen.","US indictments unsealed April 14, 2023 charged 28 Sinaloa members including all four brothers.","Rewards for Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo rose from $5 million each (December 2021) to up to $10 million each.","Seventeen of Ovidio's relatives were escorted into the United States in May 2025 under his cooperation deal.","Ovidio pleaded guilty on July 11, 2025 and Joaquín on December 1, 2025, both in Chicago; Joaquín stipulated to organizing El Mayo's kidnapping.","El Nini was extradited on May 25, 2024 and faces charges in New York including the murder of a DEA informant.","The DEA first warned publicly of a Chapitos-CJNG alliance in its National Drug Threat Assessment released May 15, 2025; Harfuch said on June 16, 2026 the alliance ended with El Mencho's death.","The Treasury designated Los Chapitos as a criminal organization on June 9, 2025, alongside three individuals and 12 Mazatlán companies."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 Los Chapitos are reduced to two fugitive leaders, Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo Guzmán, each carrying a US reward of up to $10 million reiterated on an ICE poster in July 2026, while Ovidio and Joaquín have pleaded guilty in Chicago and await sentencing. The faction has lost successive security chiefs and plaza bosses (El Texas killed July 8, El 20 arrested July 28, 2026), has been losing ground to La Mayiza in Culiacán, and lost its CJNG backing after El Mencho's death on February 22, 2026, though it still runs fentanyl and methamphetamine cells in northern Sinaloa, Sonora, Chihuahua and Baja California. The April 2026 US indictment of Governor Rocha Moya, built on the Chapitos case, has also stripped away the political protection the brothers enjoyed in Sinaloa.","sources":["https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/joaquin-guzman-lopez-son-el-chapo-and-leader-sinaloa-cartel-pleads-guilty-federal-drug","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/governor-sinaloa-and-nine-other-current-and-former-mexican-officials-charged-drug","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20250609","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/defensa-abate-culiacan-texas-presunto-jefe-plaza-chapitos","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/29/detienen-a-el-20-del-cartel-de-sinaloa-lo-ligan-a-culiacanazos-y-a-la-muerte-de-cinco-militares/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/06/16/con-la-muerte-de-el-mencho-termino-la-alianza-con-los-chapitos-harfuch/","https://www.foxnews.com/politics/ice-renews-hunt-el-chapos-last-two-fugitive-sons-massive-reward","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iv%C3%A1n_Archivaldo_Guzm%C3%A1n_Salazar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ovidio_Guzm%C3%A1n_L%C3%B3pez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joaqu%C3%ADn_Guzm%C3%A1n_L%C3%B3pez","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Chapitos","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/sinaloa-cartel-profile/","https://insightcrime.org/news/the-new-era-of-the-sinaloa-cartel/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rub%C3%A9n_Rocha_Moya"],"verified":true},{"id":"mayiza","name":"La Mayiza","aka":["Los Mayos","Zambada faction","Gente del Mayo","MZ"],"founded":2024,"dissolved":0,"parent":"sinaloa","origin":{"state":"SIN","city":"Culiacán","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394},"summary":"La Mayiza is the Sinaloa Cartel faction loyal to Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, now led by his son Ismael 'El Mayito Flaco' Zambada Sicairos, with Los Rusos as its armed wing and networks in the Culiacán valley, southern Sinaloa, Durango, Zacatecas and Tijuana. It became a distinct fighting bloc after Joaquín Guzmán López delivered El Mayo to US agents on July 25, 2024, and has waged war on Los Chapitos since September 9, 2024. By 2026 it was reported to be gaining ground in Sinaloa and expanding into Zacatecas, where it clashes with CJNG.","history":"The Zambada wing of the Sinaloa Cartel predates its name. From the 1990s El Mayo Zambada ran the cartel's most disciplined logistics network from El Álamo and El Salado south of Culiacán, with the Ántrax hit squad of José Rodrigo 'El Chino Ántrax' Aréchiga as its enforcement arm and a family bench that included sons Vicente 'El Vicentillo' (arrested March 18, 2009, sentenced to 15 years in Chicago in 2019), Serafín (arrested at the Nogales border crossing in November 2013) and Ismael 'Mayito Gordo' Zambada Imperial (arrested November 12, 2014, sentenced in San Diego in June 2022), and brother Jesús 'El Rey' Zambada (arrested October 2008, later a US cooperating witness). Zambada mediated the cartel's internal disputes, sheltered Los Chapitos after 2016 and stood with them against Dámaso López's 2017 rebellion, whose gunmen ambushed the Guzmán brothers in February 2017. His youngest active son, Ismael Zambada Sicairos (born circa 1982), alias 'El Mayito Flaco', 'El MF' or 'El Caballero', was placed under a US arrest warrant in 2020 for drug trafficking and money laundering while quietly running his father's southern Sinaloa operations.\n\nEverything changed on July 25, 2024, when Joaquín Guzmán López flew the 76-year-old Zambada to the Santa Teresa airfield near El Paso, where both were arrested. On August 10, 2024 Zambada released a letter saying he had been ambushed at a meeting arranged to mediate between Governor Rubén Rocha Moya and university rector Héctor Melesio Cuén, who was murdered the same day. Mayito Flaco assumed command, and after weeks of tension the 'Mayiza' opened war on Los Chapitos in Culiacán on September 9, 2024. Its main striking force was Los Rusos, a Mexicali-born cell that had already fought the Chapitos-aligned Salazar network on the Sonora-Baja California border, whose leader 'El Ruso' was long reported as Jesús Alexander Sánchez Félix; the US Treasury identified him in September 2025 as Juan José Ponce Félix, listing 'Jesús Alexandro Sánchez Félix' as his alias. Los Cabrera of Durango, the Arzate brothers' Tijuana cells and, from May 2025, the old Beltrán Leyva enemy Fausto Isidro 'El Chapo Isidro' Meza Flores of Los Mazatlecos lined up with the Zambadas, while Aureliano 'El Guano' Guzmán, at odds with his nephews in the Badiraguato sierra since 2023, fought his own war.\n\nThe first year cost Sinaloa roughly 1,850 to 2,000 dead and hundreds to nearly 2,000 disappeared. La Mayiza suffered blows of its own: Zambada's son-in-law and second-in-command Juan Carlos 'El Chavo Félix' Félix Gastélum was arrested in Quilá, Culiacán on January 19, 2025, was transferred to the United States with 25 other cartel figures on August 12, 2025, and pleaded guilty in San Diego on June 26, 2026 to three drug-trafficking conspiracy counts and one money-laundering count, with sentencing set for March 2027. In May 2025 the DEA warned that Los Chapitos had turned to CJNG for fighters and money. Washington then targeted the Zambada side directly: on September 18, 2025 the Treasury designated 'Los Mayos / La Mayiza' as a criminal organization and sanctioned Ponce Félix and six other individuals plus 16 companies, most tied to a Playas de Rosarito network in Baja California, and the State Department offered up to $5 million for El Ruso. El Mayo pleaded guilty in Brooklyn on August 25, 2025 to racketeering conspiracy and running a continuing criminal enterprise, acknowledging decades of bribes to Mexican police and military commanders.\n\nBy 2026 the balance tilted toward the Zambadas. The killing of CJNG chief El Mencho on February 22, 2026 weakened the Chapitos' patron; on February 26 the US unsealed narcoterrorism charges in San Diego against Tijuana plaza boss René 'La Rana' Arzate and offered up to $5 million each for him and his brother Alfonso 'El Aquiles', describing the pair as having run the Tijuana plaza for the Sinaloa Cartel for 15 years. On March 19, 2026 a navy-led operation at El Álamo aimed at the Mayos operative Omar Torres 'El Patas' reportedly killed 11 gunmen and briefly held Zambada's daughter Mónica Zambada Niebla before releasing her hours later without charge. The April 29, 2026 indictment of Governor Rocha Moya as a Chapitos ally, and his leave from office from May 2 (made indefinite on August 23), removed a political adversary; by mid-2026 analysts and local reporting described La Mayiza gaining ground against Los Chapitos. Judge Brian Cogan sentenced El Mayo to life without parole with a $15 billion forfeiture on July 20, 2026, and InSight Crime described Mayito Flaco as the figure most likely to inherit what remains of his father's network.\n\nLa Mayiza has also expanded outward. In southeastern Zacatecas its fighters and Los Cabrera killed ten people in Pánuco, Morelos and Sain Alto between July 16 and 18, 2026 after a local truce collapsed, hanging five bodies from bridges on the Zacatecas-Saltillo highway and warning Governor David Monreal to honor 'agreements'; on August 24, 2026 CJNG videos declared war on Mayito Flaco and the Cabreras, and on August 30 a car bomb with some 200 kg of explosives injured 11 outside police headquarters in Ojocaliente, an attack prosecutors attributed to CJNG. Meanwhile the Sinaloa war fragmented, spreading to Mazatlán and the sierra, and claimed at least ten content creators, including César Gastélum, shot dead on a livestream in Culiacán on August 4, 2026.\n\nAs of September 2026 Mayito Flaco remains a fugitive directing a faction that controls much of the Culiacán valley, southern Sinaloa, Durango and Tijuana, is contesting Zacatecas with CJNG, and is widely described as the strongest of the blocs that emerged from El Mayo's fall, even as local tallies put the war's toll above 4,000 dead or missing since September 2024.","leaders":[{"name":"Ismael Zambada García","alias":"El Mayo","role":"Founder and patriarch of the faction","from":1989,"to":2024,"fate":"Detained July 25 2024 near El Paso; pleaded guilty Aug 25 2025 (EDNY); life without parole July 20 2026 with $15 billion forfeiture"},{"name":"Ismael Zambada Sicairos","alias":"El Mayito Flaco","role":"Leader since July 2024","from":2024,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; US arrest warrant since 2020 for drug trafficking and money laundering"},{"name":"Juan José Ponce Félix","alias":"El Ruso","role":"Founder and commander of Los Rusos, the faction's armed wing","from":2024,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned Sept 18 2025; up to $5 million US reward; Treasury lists 'Jesús Alexandro Sánchez Félix' as his alias, the name used in earlier reporting"},{"name":"Juan Carlos Félix Gastélum","alias":"El Chavo Félix","role":"Second-in-command; El Mayo's son-in-law","from":2024,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested Quilá, Culiacán Jan 19 2025; transferred to US Aug 12 2025; pleaded guilty San Diego June 26 2026 to drug trafficking and money laundering; sentencing March 2027"},{"name":"Alfonso Arzate García","alias":"El Aquiles","role":"Tijuana plaza chief","from":2008,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; up to $5 million US reward announced Feb 26 2026"},{"name":"René Arzate García","alias":"La Rana","role":"Tijuana plaza boss and enforcer","from":2008,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; charged with narcoterrorism and material support in SDCA superseding indictment unsealed Feb 26 2026; up to $5 million reward"},{"name":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores","alias":"El Chapo Isidro","role":"Allied leader of Los Mazatlecos / Cártel de Guasave","from":2025,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; FBI Ten Most Wanted since Feb 4 2025 with $5 million reward; more than a ton of fentanyl linked to him seized in Ahome and Guasave Dec 2024"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_rusos","name":"Los Rusos","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Mexicali, San Luis Río Colorado, Rosarito, Culiacán","leader":"Juan José Ponce Félix 'El Ruso'","note":"Armed wing of La Mayiza in the Culiacán war; leader sanctioned by OFAC Sept 18 2025"},{"id":"los_cabrera","name":"Los Cabrera","from":2005,"to":0,"area":"Durango and southern Zacatecas","leader":"Cabrera Sarabia family","note":"Durango loyalists fighting CJNG in Zacatecas with La Mayiza in 2026"},{"id":"los_aquiles","name":"Los Aquiles / Arzate cells","from":2008,"to":0,"area":"Tijuana and Baja California","leader":"Alfonso and René Arzate García","note":"Zambada's Tijuana franchise; narcoterrorism charges against René Feb 2026"},{"id":"los_antrax","name":"Los Ántrax","from":2008,"to":2020,"area":"Culiacán","leader":"José Rodrigo Aréchiga Gamboa 'El Chino Ántrax'","note":"Earlier Zambada hit squad; leader murdered May 2020"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2016,"to":2024,"note":"Sheltered Guzmán's sons until the July 25 2024 betrayal"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"Chapo Isidro's Los Mazatlecos, a Beltrán Leyva remnant, sided with La Mayiza against Los Chapitos from May 2025"},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Arellano Félix remnants coexist with the Arzate cells in Tijuana against CJNG"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Civil war since Sept 9 2024 over Culiacán and the cartel's succession"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"CJNG backed Los Chapitos in 2025 and declared war on Mayiza leaders in Zacatecas Aug 24 2026; Ojocaliente car bomb Aug 30 2026"},{"cartel":"caborca","from":2020,"to":2022,"note":"Los Rusos fought Caborca-aligned groups in northern Sonora before Caro Quintero's 2022 capture"}],"activities":["Cocaine wholesale and Pacific maritime routes","Fentanyl and methamphetamine trafficking through Arizona, Texas and California","Heroin from Golden Triangle poppy","Money laundering through companies in Culiacán and Baja California (Rosarito hospitality and transport firms sanctioned 2025)","Extortion of businesses and local officials in Sinaloa and Zacatecas","Migrant smuggling in Baja California"],"peak":{"from":2025,"to":2026,"states":["SIN","BCN","DUR","ZAC","NAY","SON"],"note":"Reported gaining ground in Culiacán by mid-2026 and projecting force into Zacatecas against CJNG, making it the strongest of the post-Mayo factions"},"facts":["El Mayo was 76 when flown to the United States on July 25, 2024; he was sentenced to life without parole on July 20, 2026 with a $15 billion forfeiture.","The Mayiza-Chapitos war began September 9, 2024 and killed roughly 1,850-2,000 people in its first year in Sinaloa.","The Treasury's September 18, 2025 action designated 'Los Mayos / La Mayiza', El Ruso and six other individuals and 16 companies, and came with a $5 million State Department reward for El Ruso.","The US offered up to $5 million each ($10 million combined) for the Arzate brothers of Tijuana on February 26, 2026.","A March 19, 2026 navy-led raid at El Álamo reportedly killed 11 gunmen and briefly detained Mónica Zambada Niebla, released within hours without charges.","Between July 16 and 18, 2026 Mayiza and Los Cabrera killed ten people in three Zacatecas municipalities, hanging five bodies from bridges.","A car bomb with roughly 200 kg of explosives struck Ojocaliente, Zacatecas on August 30, 2026, injuring 11 and damaging dozens of homes (reports range from about 30 to more than 60).","Explosive attacks in Zacatecas rose from one in 2024 to six in 2025 and ten through early September 2026."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 La Mayiza, led by the fugitive Ismael 'Mayito Flaco' Zambada Sicairos with Los Rusos as its armed wing, is reported to hold the upper hand in the Culiacán war, retains Tijuana through the Arzate brothers, and is pushing into Zacatecas alongside Los Cabrera, where CJNG declared war on it on August 24, 2026 and a car bomb attributed to CJNG hit Ojocaliente on August 30. Its patriarch El Mayo is serving life in the United States after his July 20, 2026 sentencing, and the faction has lost El Chavo Félix to US custody, but it faces no unified command against it since El Mencho's death and Rocha Moya's departure on leave. Analysts widely count it as the strongest of the blocs now splitting the Sinaloa Cartel.","sources":["https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0252","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20250918","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/sinaloa-cartel-leader-charged-narcoterrorism-material-support-terrorism-and-drug-trafficking","https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/02/up-to-10-million-in-reward-offers-for-info-leading-to-arrests-convictions-of-mexican-sinaloa-cartel-bosses","https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2026/jun/26/son-in-law-of-sinaloa-cartel-boss-el-mayo-pleads-g/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/03/19/retienen-a-monica-zambada-niebla-hija-de-el-mayo-durante-operativo/","https://insightcrime.org/news/violence-zacatecas-mexico-surges-cjng-mayiza-clash/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/un-coche-bomba-11-heridos-y-una-investigacion-que-apunta-al-cjng-lo-que-se-sabe-del-ataque-en-ojocaliente-zacatecas/","https://insightcrime.org/news/four-decades-of-el-mayos-criminal-career-end-in-a-life-sentence/","https://insightcrime.org/news/the-new-era-of-the-sinaloa-cartel/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/sinaloa-cartel-profile/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Sicairos","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Garc%C3%ADa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rub%C3%A9n_Rocha_Moya"],"verified":true},{"id":"tijuana","name":"Tijuana Cartel","aka":["Arellano Félix Organization (AFO)","Cártel de los Arellano Félix (CAF)","Cártel Tijuana Nueva Generación (CTNG)"],"founded":1989,"dissolved":0,"parent":"guadalajara","origin":{"state":"BCN","city":"Tijuana","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382},"summary":"Family-run trafficking organization of the Arellano Félix siblings that inherited the Tijuana–San Diego corridor when the Guadalajara Cartel broke up in 1989 and, through the 1990s, was Mexico's most feared and violent cartel. Decapitated by killings and arrests between 2002 and 2014 and eclipsed by the Sinaloa Cartel, it survived as a toll-collecting franchise, then as a CJNG-branded 'Cártel Tijuana Nueva Generación', and since 2023 has been quietly rebuilt by Enedina Arellano Félix and her nephew 'El Ingeniero'.","history":"The Arellano Félix siblings grew up in Culiacán, Sinaloa, and moved to Tijuana in the early 1980s inside Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara Cartel. When Félix Gallardo was arrested on 8 April 1989 and his plazas were parcelled out, the Tijuana–Mexicali corridor fell to the family: Benjamín as strategist, Ramón as chief enforcer, Francisco Rafael as the public face, Eduardo and Francisco Javier as operators, and their sister Enedina, a trained accountant, in charge of laundering. Controlling the San Ysidro and Otay crossings, the busiest land border in the world, the Arellano Félix Organization (AFO) moved Colombian cocaine from the Cali and later Norte del Valle cartels alongside Mexican marijuana, heroin and methamphetamine, charged every independent smuggler a 'piso', recruited 'narcojuniors' from Tijuana's wealthy families and Logan Heights gang members from San Diego as gunmen, and bought police at every level.\n\nA dispute over Tijuana with Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán and Héctor 'El Güero' Palma turned into open war in 1992. After Guzmán's gunmen attacked the brothers at the Christine discotheque in Puerto Vallarta on 8 November 1992, the AFO tried to kill him at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993 and instead shot dead Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo and six others, a scandal that made it Mexico's most wanted organization. Francisco Rafael was arrested in Tijuana on 4 December 1993, but through the 1990s the AFO murdered police chiefs, prosecutors and journalists (the 27 November 1997 ambush of Zeta editor Jesús Blancornelas, in which gunman David Barrón Corona died, among them) while the DEA made it a top priority. The arrests of financier Jesús 'El Chuy' Labra (March 2000) and operations chief Ismael 'El Mayel' Higuera (May 2000) began to erode the structure.\n\nThe founding generation fell in 2002. Ramón was killed by Sinaloa state police in Mazatlán on 10 February 2002 while hunting Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada; Benjamín was captured by the army in Puebla on 9 March 2002, extradited on 29 April 2011 and sentenced in San Diego to 25 years on 2 April 2012. Francisco Javier 'El Tigrillo' led until the US Coast Guard seized him on the sport-fishing boat Dock Holiday off Baja California Sur on 14 August 2006; sentenced to life on 5 November 2007, his term was cut to 23.5 years in June 2015 for cooperation. Eduardo 'El Doctor' ran the cartel with Enedina until federal police caught him after a shoot-out in Tijuana on 25 October 2008, leaving command to Enedina and her nephew Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano, 'El Ingeniero'.\n\nThat succession split the organization. Teodoro 'El Teo' García Simental, the lieutenant who ran kidnappings and executions, broke away after a 26 April 2008 gun battle in Tijuana that left 14 gunmen dead and allied with the Sinaloa Cartel's local cell under the Arzate brothers. The war produced more than 800 homicides in Tijuana in 2008 and the confession of Santiago 'El Pozolero' Meza, who said he had dissolved some 300 bodies in caustic soda for El Teo. Federal police arrested García Simental in La Paz on 12 January 2010; Sinaloa became the dominant wholesaler in Baja California while the AFO lived on as a toll-collecting franchise. El Ingeniero was arrested at a Carl's Jr. in Tijuana's Otay district on 23 June 2014 carrying US$119,200; Francisco Rafael, freed in the US in 2008, had been shot dead by a gunman dressed as a clown at a party in Los Cabos on 18 October 2013.\n\nFrom about 2016 the Jalisco New Generation Cartel moved into Baja California, absorbing AFO remnants under the banner 'Cártel Tijuana Nueva Generación' (CTNG) to fight the Sinaloa cell. The battle for retail drug corners drove Tijuana to a record of roughly 2,500 homicide victims in 2018 (the state prosecutor counted 2,518; the federal SESNSP tally was lower), the highest raw total of any city in the world, and roughly 2,000 a year through 2022 (about 2,058 that year). CTNG chiefs fell in succession: 'El Babay' (June 2017), 'El Piolín' (September 2017), 'El Kaibil' (February 2018) and CJNG plaza boss Édgar Herrera Pardo, 'El Caimán', head of the enforcer group Los Cabos, arrested in 2019, extradited in early August 2023 and sentenced in San Diego to 28 years on 13 June 2025. Eduardo Arellano Félix was released from US prison on 17 August 2021 after serving most of his 15-year sentence, deported across the Matamoros bridge on 23 August 2021 and immediately re-arrested by Mexico on organized-crime charges and sent to the Altiplano prison.\n\nThe family regrouped after El Ingeniero was acquitted of money laundering on 8 February 2023 and released on bail from the Altiplano on 25 December 2023 in a bribery (cohecho) case; he failed to appear in January 2024, drew a reapprehension order that police tried to serve at his home on 1 March 2024, and has fought it through an amparo filed on 7 March 2024 while at large. Narco-banners signed 'El Ingeniero' followed the 17 June 2025 arrest in Tijuana of operator Pablo Edwin Huerta Nuño, 'El Flaquito', who was transferred (not formally extradited) to the United States on 12 August 2025 and arraigned in San Diego. In February 2026 Infobae, citing Zeta's Adela Navarro and Mexican and US reports, said Enedina, 'La Narcomami', and her nephew had resumed operational control, using used-auto-parts shops as fronts and ceding zones to the CJNG for money and weapons; in April–June 2026 El Financiero and Infobae described a pact with Los Chapitos, backed by CJNG money and arms, against the Mayo-aligned Arzate cell, for whom Washington posted US$5 million rewards each on 26 February 2026. Federal forces arrested Baja California operator Héctor Manuel Gil García, 'El Kado', tipped as El Flaquito's successor, in Valladolid, Yucatán, on 23 July 2026, and former sicario chief Emilio Valdez Mainero, 'El CP', was last seen in Tijuana's Zona Río on 28 August 2026, his disappearance reported on 31 August and confirmed by the state prosecutor on 3 September 2026. As of September 2026 the AFO is a small but active organization, absent from the US terrorist list, in a city whose homicides fell to about 1,800 in 2024 (state prosecutor: 1,809) and 1,219 in 2025.","leaders":[{"name":"Benjamín Arellano Félix","alias":"El Min","role":"Top leader and strategist","from":1989,"to":2002,"fate":"Arrested by the army in Puebla 9 Mar 2002; extradited to the US 29 Apr 2011; pleaded guilty to racketeering and laundering 4 Jan 2012, sentenced to 25 years 2 Apr 2012; held at USP Coleman I with a projected release of 28 Apr 2032"},{"name":"Ramón Arellano Félix","alias":"El Mon","role":"Chief enforcer, head of armed wing","from":1989,"to":2002,"fate":"Shot dead by Sinaloa state police in Mazatlán 10 Feb 2002 while hunting El Mayo Zambada"},{"name":"Francisco Rafael Arellano Félix","alias":"","role":"Eldest brother, early figurehead","from":1989,"to":1993,"fate":"Arrested in Tijuana 4 Dec 1993; extradited 16 Sep 2006 via Matamoros–Brownsville; freed in the US 4 Mar 2008 with credit for time served and deported; shot dead by a gunman in a clown costume at his birthday party in Los Cabos 18 Oct 2013"},{"name":"Francisco Javier Arellano Félix","alias":"El Tigrillo","role":"Top leader after 2002","from":2002,"to":2006,"fate":"Captured by the US Coast Guard aboard the Dock Holiday off Baja California Sur 14 Aug 2006; life sentence 5 Nov 2007, reduced to 23.5 years in Jun 2015 for cooperation; BOP location undisclosed"},{"name":"Eduardo Arellano Félix","alias":"El Doctor","role":"Top leader with Enedina","from":2006,"to":2008,"fate":"Arrested after a shoot-out in Tijuana 25 Oct 2008; extradited 31 Aug 2012; 15-year US sentence Aug 2013; released 17 Aug 2021, deported at Matamoros 23 Aug 2021 and immediately re-arrested by Mexico, held at the Altiplano"},{"name":"Enedina Arellano Félix","alias":"La Narcomami / La Jefa","role":"Financial chief (1990s–2008), top leader since 2008","from":2008,"to":0,"fate":"Never arrested; designated by OFAC under the Kingpin Act 31 Jan 2002; reported in 2026 to be leading a reorganization allied with Los Chapitos"},{"name":"Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano","alias":"El Ingeniero","role":"Operational leader (nephew of the brothers)","from":2008,"to":0,"fate":"Arrested in Tijuana 23 Jun 2014; acquitted of laundering 8 Feb 2023; released on bail from the Altiplano 25 Dec 2023 in a bribery case, missed his Jan 2024 hearing; reapprehension order early 2024 contested by an amparo filed 7 Mar 2024; at large and reportedly back in command 2025–26"},{"name":"Teodoro García Simental","alias":"El Teo / El Tres Letras","role":"Enforcer and kidnapping chief; led breakaway faction 2008–10","from":1995,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by federal police in La Paz, BCS, 12 Jan 2010; sentenced in Mexico to 20 years (reported 2 Jun 2025); held at the Altiplano; amparo alleging torture dismissed 1 Jul 2026"},{"name":"Ismael Higuera Guerrero","alias":"El Mayel","role":"Operations chief, Tijuana–Ensenada","from":1993,"to":2000,"fate":"Arrested in Ensenada 3 May 2000; extradited to the US in Jan 2007; sentenced in San Diego"},{"name":"Jesús Labra Avilés","alias":"El Chuy","role":"Financier and mentor of the brothers","from":1989,"to":2000,"fate":"Arrested at a Tijuana youth football game 11 Mar 2000; later extradited to the US and sentenced in San Diego (40 years, 2010, per press reports)"},{"name":"Pablo Edwin Huerta Nuño","alias":"El Flaquito","role":"Principal operator in Tijuana","from":2014,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested in Tijuana's Fundadores district 17 Jun 2025 by Defensa/FGR; transferred to the US 12 Aug 2025 under Mexico's national-security transfer mechanism; pleaded not guilty in San Diego 13 Aug 2025"},{"name":"Héctor Manuel Gil García","alias":"El Kado / Ka2ador","role":"Baja California cell operator; tipped successor to El Flaquito","from":2020,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested by FGR/FEMDO in Valladolid, Yucatán, 23 Jul 2026; bound over 3 Aug 2026 on a 2016 military-weapons case and evasion of justice, held in Mérida; also under a federal homicide investigation"}],"factions":[{"id":"logan_heights_crew","name":"Logan Heights / narcojunior hit squads","from":1989,"to":2002,"area":"Tijuana, San Diego","leader":"Ramón Arellano Félix; David Barrón Corona","note":"US gang members and Tijuana rich kids used as gunmen; Barrón died in the 27 Nov 1997 Blancornelas ambush; narcojunior chief Emilio Valdez Mainero jailed in the US 1996–2015"},{"id":"el_teo_faction","name":"Facción de El Teo","from":2008,"to":2010,"area":"East Tijuana, Rosarito, La Paz","leader":"Teodoro García Simental","note":"Broke away Apr 2008, allied with Sinaloa's Arzate cell; kidnapping and 'pozole' disposal of bodies; collapsed after El Teo's Jan 2010 arrest"},{"id":"ingeniero_loyalists","name":"Facción de El Ingeniero","from":2008,"to":2014,"area":"Tijuana, Tecate, Mexicali","leader":"Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano","note":"Legacy AFO structure focused on trafficking and piso; reduced to a franchise under Sinaloa dominance"},{"id":"ctng","name":"Cártel Tijuana Nueva Generación (CTNG)","from":2016,"to":2025,"area":"Tijuana, Rosarito, Tecate","leader":"CJNG plaza bosses incl. Édgar Herrera Pardo 'El Caimán' (Los Cabos)","note":"CJNG-branded coalition absorbing AFO remnants against Sinaloa; string of leader arrests 2017–19; El Caimán extradited Aug 2023, 28-year sentence 13 Jun 2025"},{"id":"caf_reorganized","name":"CAF reorganized under Enedina and El Ingeniero","from":2023,"to":0,"area":"Tijuana","leader":"Enedina Arellano Félix; Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano","note":"Rebuilt with business fronts (used-auto-parts shops) after El Ingeniero's Dec 2023 release; CJNG money and arms in exchange for territory; pact with Los Chapitos reported Apr–Jun 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"guadalajara","from":1980,"to":1989,"note":"Family rose inside Félix Gallardo's federation and inherited the Tijuana plaza"},{"cartel":"sonora","from":2000,"to":2005,"note":"Cooperation with the Caro Quintero clan on Sonora routes in the early 2000s (InSight Crime)"},{"cartel":"milenio","from":2000,"to":2009,"note":"Valencia network supplied cocaine and shared anti-Sinaloa interests in the early 2000s (InSight Crime)"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"El Ingeniero's faction reported to have sought Zetas backing against Sinaloa and El Teo"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"CTNG alliance against Sinaloa; frayed as CJNG sought dominance; 2025–26 AFO ceded zones for money and arms"},{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"Pact reported by El Financiero/Infobae Apr–Jun 2026, citing Zeta and Baja California security officials, aimed at the Mayo-aligned Arzate cell"},{"cartel":"other","from":1989,"to":2002,"note":"Colombian suppliers: Cali cartel, later Norte del Valle"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":1989,"to":0,"note":"War with El Chapo and El Güero Palma from 1992 (Posadas 1993); Ramón killed hunting El Mayo 2002; Sinaloa's Arzate cell took Tijuana after 2010"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Arzate brothers 'La Rana' and 'Aquiles' (La Mayiza) control much of Tijuana; US$5m rewards each announced 26 Feb 2026"},{"cartel":"juarez","from":1993,"to":2002,"note":"Sporadic hostility with Amado Carrillo Fuentes' organization over Sonora/Sinaloa routes"},{"cartel":"government","from":2006,"to":2012,"note":"Calderón-era offensive removed Javier, Eduardo, El Teo and (in 2014) El Ingeniero"}],"activities":["Cocaine wholesale (Colombian supply) via Tijuana–San Diego","Marijuana and heroin trafficking","Methamphetamine trafficking","Fentanyl (2020s retail and cross-border)","Cross-border tunnels","'Piso' taxation of independent smugglers","Kidnapping for ransom (El Teo era)","Extortion","Retail drug sales in Tijuana","Money laundering through businesses","Migrant smuggling"],"peak":{"from":1992,"to":2002,"states":["BCN","BCS","SON","SIN","JAL","COL","MIC","CMX"],"note":"Dominated the busiest land border in the world; DEA rated it the most violent Mexican trafficking organization of the 1990s"},"facts":["On 24 May 1993 an AFO hit squad trying to kill El Chapo at Guadalajara airport killed Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo and six other people.","Ramón was killed on 10 February 2002 and Benjamín arrested on 9 March 2002, 27 days apart, ending the founders' rule.","Francisco Javier was captured by the US Coast Guard about 25 km off Baja California Sur on 14 August 2006; his 2007 life sentence was cut to 23.5 years in June 2015.","El Pozolero, El Teo's body-disposal man, confessed in January 2009 to dissolving about 300 corpses in caustic soda.","Benjamín was extradited on 29 April 2011 and sentenced to 25 years on 2 April 2012; his projected release date is 28 April 2032.","El Ingeniero was arrested on 23 June 2014 carrying US$119,200 in cash; a court acquitted him of laundering on 8 February 2023 and he walked free on bail on 25 December 2023.","Francisco Rafael was shot dead by a gunman dressed as a clown at his birthday party in Los Cabos on 18 October 2013.","Tijuana logged a record of roughly 2,500 homicide victims in 2018 (state count 2,518) and about 2,058 in 2022, then fell to about 1,800 in 2024 (state prosecutor: 1,809) and 1,219 in 2025.","CJNG/CTNG enforcer chief 'El Caimán' was extradited in August 2023 and sentenced in San Diego to 28 years on 13 June 2025.","'El Flaquito' was arrested on 17 June 2025 and transferred to the US on 12 August 2025; Baja California operator 'El Kado' was arrested in Valladolid, Yucatán, on 23 July 2026."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Arellano Félix organization is a diminished but active player in Tijuana: Enedina Arellano Félix and her fugitive nephew 'El Ingeniero' (free since 25 Dec 2023) reorganized it in 2025–26, reportedly allying with Los Chapitos and taking CJNG money and arms in exchange for territory, while the Mayo-aligned Arzate cell remains the dominant Sinaloa force. Setbacks include the arrests of 'El Flaquito' (17 Jun 2025, transferred to the US 12 Aug 2025) and 'El Kado' (23 Jul 2026) and the disappearance of ex-sicario chief Emilio Valdez Mainero (last seen 28 Aug 2026). It is not on the US Foreign Terrorist Organization list, and Tijuana homicides fell to 1,219 in 2025 (down about 32% from 2024); Baja California as a whole had recorded 892 homicides in 2026 by 8 August.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/tijuana-cartel-profile/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjam%C3%ADn_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2023/02/23/el-ingeniero-lider-de-los-arellano-felix-le-gana-a-la-fiscalia-es-exonerado-por-lavado-de-dinero/","https://zetatijuana.com/2024/07/profugo-otra-vez-el-ingeniero-del-caf/","https://zetatijuana.com/2025/12/libre-el-ingeniero-se-ampara-contra-recaptura/","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdca/pr/three-fugitives-transferred-united-states-mexico-arraigned-san-diego","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases/2025/06/13/cartel-enforcer-sentenced-28-years-for-drug-trafficking-and-violence","https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/02/up-to-10-million-in-reward-offers-for-info-leading-to-arrests-convictions-of-mexican-sinaloa-cartel-bosses","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/02/04/la-narcomami-y-el-ingeniero-estarian-de-nuevo-al-frente-del-cartel-de-tijuana-asi-se-reorganizan/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/04/21/la-heredera-de-un-cartel-hace-alianza-con-los-chapitos-el-pacto-de-los-arellano-felix-y-los-guzman/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/06/02/de-enemigos-a-aliados-el-pacto-que-une-a-los-arellano-felix-con-los-chapitos/","https://zetatijuana.com/2026/07/femdo-detiene-en-yucatan-a-el-kado/","https://puntonorte.info/2026/08/31/reportan-como-desaparecido-a-emilio-valdez-mainero-narcojunior-del-caf-en-los-90s-fue-jefe-del-brazo-armado-de-ramon-arellano-felix/","https://el-mexicano.com.mx/Noticia/Tijuana/88227/Reducen-homicidios-en-el-2025","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/former-tijuana-cartel-boss-released-from-us-prison-deported-to-mexico/","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20020131"],"verified":true},{"id":"juarez","name":"Juárez Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Juárez","Vicente Carrillo Fuentes Organization (VCFO)","Carrillo Fuentes Organization","La Línea","Nuevo Cártel de Juárez (NCDJ)"],"founded":1989,"dissolved":0,"parent":"guadalajara","origin":{"state":"CHH","city":"Ciudad Juárez","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245},"summary":"Chihuahua-based trafficking organization that became autonomous after the 1989 Guadalajara split and, under Amado Carrillo Fuentes, 'El Señor de los Cielos', was the most powerful cocaine enterprise of the 1990s. After Amado's death in 1997 and a catastrophic 2008–2012 war with the Sinaloa Cartel it survived through its armed wing La Línea, which now runs Ciudad Juárez and Chihuahua's sierra as a regional franchise and was designated a US Foreign Terrorist Organization in July 2026 (signed 2 July, effective 16 July).","history":"The Chihuahua corridor was worked in the 1970s and 1980s by Pablo Acosta Villarreal, the 'Zorro de Ojinaga', until Mexican federal police killed him at Santa Elena on 24 April 1987. Rafael Aguilar Guajardo, a former commander of the Federal Security Directorate (DFS), then ran Ciudad Juárez for the Guadalajara Cartel with Amado Carrillo Fuentes, a nephew of Ernesto 'Don Neto' Fonseca, as his lieutenant. After Félix Gallardo's arrest in April 1989 the Juárez plaza became an organization in its own right. Aguilar Guajardo was shot dead while on holiday in Cancún on 12 April 1993, reportedly on Carrillo Fuentes' orders, and Amado took control.\n\nCarrillo Fuentes turned the Juárez Cartel into the most powerful trafficking enterprise of the 1990s. Using Boeing 727s and other jets to fly Colombian cocaine from the Cali cartel to airstrips across northern Mexico, which earned him the nickname 'El Señor de los Cielos', he supplied a large share of the cocaine entering the United States, operated in some 20 states, partnered with Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada and Juan José 'El Azul' Esparragoza, and bought protection as high as Mexico's anti-drug czar, General Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo, arrested in February 1997. Amado died in a Mexico City clinic on 4 July 1997 after hours of plastic surgery and liposuction; two of his surgeons were found tortured and encased in concrete in oil drums in November 1997. After a bloody succession his brother Vicente, 'El Viceroy', took over with brother Rodolfo and Amado's son Vicente Carrillo Leyva, and in 2002 joined Zambada, Esparragoza, the Beltrán Leyvas and Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán in the 'Federation'.\n\nThe Federation broke when Guzmán's gunmen killed Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes and his wife outside a Culiacán cinema on 11 September 2004; Chapo's brother Arturo Guzmán Loera was murdered in the Altiplano prison on 31 December 2004 in apparent retaliation. From 2007 Sinaloa's 'Gente Nueva' moved on Juárez, and the Carrillo Fuentes organization answered with La Línea, an armed wing built from corrupt municipal and state police under Juan Pablo 'El JL' Ledezma and José Antonio 'El Diego' Acosta Hernández, plus the El Paso prison gang Barrio Azteca and the army-deserter unit Los Linces. Despite Operativo Conjunto Chihuahua (from March 2008), Ciudad Juárez recorded roughly 1,600 homicides in 2008, 2,700 in 2009 and more than 3,000 in 2010, the highest murder rate of any city in the world; atrocities included the Villas de Salvárcar student massacre (31 January 2010, 15 dead), the US consulate killings (13 March 2010), Mexico's first car bomb (15 July 2010) and the Horizontes del Sur party massacre (23 October 2010, 14 dead). El Diego, arrested on 29 July 2011, admitted ordering some 1,500 killings. By 2012 Sinaloa controlled the wholesale corridor and homicides fell to about 750, then 430 in 2014.\n\nVicente Carrillo Fuentes was arrested without a shot in Torreón on 9 October 2014 and sentenced in Mexico to 28 years on 14 September 2021. What remained was La Línea: a Chihuahua network living off extortion, retail drug sales, migrant smuggling, illegal logging and taxes on other traffickers, allied at times with the Beltrán Leyvas and Zetas. From 2016 it fought Sinaloa's Los Salazar and 'Gente Nueva del Jaguar' across the Sierra Tarahumara and the Sonora line; sierra chief Carlos Arturo 'El 80' Quintana was captured in Namiquipa on 17 May 2018, and César Daniel Manjarrez Alonso, 'El H2', emerged that year heading a family-based faction, 'Los H', that styles itself the Nuevo Cártel de Juárez. Juárez homicides climbed back to 1,247 in 2018 and 1,499 in 2019. On 4 November 2019 La Línea gunmen killed nine women and children of the LeBarón and Langford families near Bavispe, Sonora, and a 4 April 2020 battle with Gente Nueva in Madera left 19 dead.\n\nThe 2020s brought new alignments. La Línea reached an understanding with the Jalisco New Generation Cartel (reported from 2021; the US Treasury dates CJNG supplies of cocaine, methamphetamine and fentanyl to September 2023). Sinaloa-allied gangs remained deadly rivals: on 1 January 2023 Los Mexicles stormed Ciudad Juárez's Cereso 3 prison, killing 17 and freeing 30 inmates including leader Ernesto 'El Neto' Piñón de la Cruz, who was shot dead on 5 January. On 31 October 2024 OFAC sanctioned five La Línea figures and two companies, among them Chihuahua plaza boss Jesús 'El Chuyín' Salas Aguayo. The city recorded about 1,170 homicides in 2023 and 1,108 in 2024.\n\nOn 27 February 2025 Mexico flew Vicente Carrillo Fuentes and 28 other prisoners to the United States; he pleaded not guilty in Brooklyn federal court the next day to a seven-count superseding indictment (a 2009 case updated in 2019) charging a continuing criminal enterprise including murder conspiracy, cocaine conspiracies, firearms and money laundering. Prosecutors announced on 5 August 2025 that they would not seek the death penalty, hearings were repeatedly postponed for plea talks (8 January and 28 April 2026), and on 28 July 2026 Judge Joan Azrack granted 90 more days and set the next hearing for 20 October 2026. In Chihuahua the war shifted to the Ojinaga–Aldama corridor against Los Cabrera Sarabia's 'Fariseos', a Sinaloa-aligned cell whose fight with La Línea intensified from September 2024 (its chief Roberto 'N', 'El 02', was captured with ten others in El Pueblito, Aldama, on 2 December 2025), and to Guadalupe y Calvo against a Salgueiro–Mayito Flaco alliance. Secretary of State Marco Rubio signed the designation of the Juárez Cartel, listing La Línea and Barrio Azteca as aliases, as a Foreign Terrorist Organization alongside Los Viagras on 2 July 2026; it took effect on Federal Register publication on 16 July 2026, bringing the number of Mexican groups on the list to eight. State police arrested cell chief Adrián Alexis 'El Fantasma' in Aldama on 8 August 2026, and El H2's nephew Roy Iván Manjarrez Daniel, 'El Roy' or 'El 19', head of 'Los HS' in San Juanito, was killed in a shoot-out with state agents on the night of 22 August 2026 (confirmed 23 August), with another nephew reported as his successor. As of September 2026 La Línea/NCDJ, with El JL still a fugitive, remains the dominant force in Ciudad Juárez, where homicides fell to 926 in 2025, below 1,000 for the first time since 2017, but it is a regional franchise rather than the transnational empire Amado built.","leaders":[{"name":"Pablo Acosta Villarreal","alias":"El Zorro de Ojinaga","role":"Ojinaga plaza boss, predecessor organization","from":1980,"to":1987,"fate":"Killed by Mexican federal police in Santa Elena, Chihuahua, 24 Apr 1987"},{"name":"Rafael Aguilar Guajardo","alias":"","role":"Founder and top leader; ex-DFS commander","from":1987,"to":1993,"fate":"Shot dead in Cancún 12 Apr 1993, reportedly on Amado Carrillo's orders; extensive properties and assets later seized"},{"name":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Señor de los Cielos","role":"Top leader","from":1993,"to":1997,"fate":"Died in Mexico City 4 Jul 1997 after plastic surgery; two surgeons found dead in concrete-filled drums Nov 1997"},{"name":"Vicente Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Viceroy","role":"Top leader","from":1997,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested in Torreón 9 Oct 2014; 28-year Mexican sentence 14 Sep 2021; transferred to the US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded not guilty in EDNY 28 Feb 2025 to a seven-count superseding indictment; death penalty ruled out 5 Aug 2025; plea talks ongoing, next hearing 20 Oct 2026 before Judge Joan Azrack"},{"name":"Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Niño de Oro","role":"Brother and senior operator","from":1997,"to":2004,"fate":"Shot dead with his wife outside a Culiacán cinema 11 Sep 2004 by El Chapo's gunmen"},{"name":"Vicente Carrillo Leyva","alias":"El Ingeniero","role":"Amado's son; senior figure","from":1997,"to":2009,"fate":"Arrested in Mexico City 1 Apr 2009 (announced 2 Apr); acquitted of laundering, fined for weapons; freed 17 Dec 2010 and dropped from view (reportedly under a new identity)"},{"name":"Juan Pablo Ledezma","alias":"El JL","role":"Founder and leader of La Línea; de facto cartel head","from":2004,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; US reward reported at up to US$2.5 million (some outlets cite US$5 million) and 15 million pesos offered in Mexico"},{"name":"José Antonio Acosta Hernández","alias":"El Diego","role":"La Línea chief in Ciudad Juárez","from":2008,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested in Chihuahua 29 Jul 2011; admitted ordering about 1,500 killings; extradited 2012; life sentences in El Paso Apr 2014 for the consulate murders"},{"name":"Luis Carlos Vázquez Barragán","alias":"El 20","role":"La Línea leader","from":2009,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested in Chihuahua 2010"},{"name":"Carlos Arturo Quintana","alias":"El 80","role":"La Línea sierra chief (Namiquipa, Madera)","from":2011,"to":2018,"fate":"Captured in Namiquipa 17 May 2018; imprisoned in Mexico"},{"name":"César Daniel Manjarrez Alonso","alias":"El H2","role":"La Línea/Nuevo Cártel de Juárez sierra chief (Los H)","from":2018,"to":0,"fate":"At large; nephew Roy Iván Manjarrez Daniel 'El 19' killed in San Juanito 22 Aug 2026; another nephew, Jesús Fidel Manjarrez Barrios, reported as successor there"},{"name":"Eduardo Ravelo","alias":"El Tablas","role":"Barrio Azteca leader","from":2003,"to":2018,"fate":"Arrested in Chihuahua 26 Jun 2018 after nearly nine years on the FBI Ten Most Wanted list (added 20 Oct 2009)"},{"name":"Jesús Salas Aguayo","alias":"El Chuyín","role":"Chihuahua plaza boss (Valle de Juárez)","from":2015,"to":0,"fate":"OFAC-sanctioned 31 Oct 2024; indicted in the District of New Mexico; at large"}],"factions":[{"id":"carrillo_fuentes_family","name":"Carrillo Fuentes family leadership","from":1993,"to":2014,"area":"Ciudad Juárez, Culiacán, Mexico City","leader":"Amado, Vicente and Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes","note":"Transnational wholesale structure; effectively ended with Vicente's Oct 2014 arrest"},{"id":"la_linea","name":"La Línea","from":2004,"to":0,"area":"Ciudad Juárez, Valle de Juárez, Chihuahua sierra","leader":"Juan Pablo Ledezma 'El JL'; El Diego (2008–11)","note":"Armed wing formed from corrupt police; now the organization's core; listed as a Juárez Cartel alias in the FTO designation effective 16 Jul 2026"},{"id":"barrio_azteca","name":"Barrio Azteca / Los Aztecas","from":2008,"to":0,"area":"El Paso, Ciudad Juárez, New Mexico","leader":"Arturo Gallegos Castrellón 'El Farmero'; Eduardo Ravelo","note":"Prison gang founded 1986 in Texas; carried out the 13 Mar 2010 US consulate killings; listed as a Juárez Cartel alias in the 2026 designation"},{"id":"los_linces","name":"Los Linces","from":2008,"to":2012,"area":"Ciudad Juárez","leader":"Unknown ex-special forces","note":"Roughly 80 army deserters used as bodyguards and hit men during the Juárez war"},{"id":"nuevo_cartel_de_juarez","name":"Nuevo Cártel de Juárez (Los H / Los HS)","from":2018,"to":0,"area":"Bocoyna–San Juanito, Guerrero, Madera, Cuauhtémoc","leader":"César Daniel Manjarrez Alonso 'El H2'","note":"La Línea's self-styled sierra branch run by the Manjarrez Alonso family; CJNG-aligned; illegal logging; 'El 19' killed 22 Aug 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"guadalajara","from":1980,"to":1989,"note":"Aguilar Guajardo and Amado ran the Juárez plaza inside Félix Gallardo's federation"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":1997,"to":2004,"note":"Partnership with El Mayo and El Azul; 2002 'Federation' with El Chapo, broken by Rodolfo's Sept 2004 killing"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2014,"note":"Renewed alliance against Sinaloa after the BLO split"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Anti-Sinaloa pact announced June 2011"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"Reported from 2021; Treasury says CJNG supplied cocaine, meth and fentanyl from Sept 2023"},{"cartel":"other","from":1990,"to":1997,"note":"Colombian suppliers: Cali cartel; earlier links to Pablo Escobar's Medellín network"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2004,"to":0,"note":"Juárez war 2008–12; Gente Nueva/Los Salazar in the sierra 2016–26"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Salgueiro–Mayito Flaco alliance in Guadalupe y Calvo; Cabrera Sarabia 'Fariseos' in Ojinaga–Aldama from Sept 2024"},{"cartel":"other","from":2008,"to":0,"note":"Sinaloa-allied street gangs Los Mexicles and Artistas Asesinos in Ciudad Juárez"},{"cartel":"government","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Operativo Conjunto Chihuahua deployed thousands of troops and federal police"},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":1993,"to":2002,"note":"Sporadic hostility with the Arellano Félix over Sonora and Sinaloa routes"}],"activities":["Cocaine wholesale by air and land (Colombian supply)","Marijuana, heroin and methamphetamine trafficking","Fentanyl trafficking (CJNG-supplied since 2023)","'Plaza' taxation of other traffickers","Extortion of businesses","Kidnapping","Migrant smuggling","Retail drug sales in Ciudad Juárez","Illegal logging in the Sierra Tarahumara","Vehicle theft","Money laundering"],"peak":{"from":1993,"to":1997,"states":["CHH","SIN","DUR","SON","COA","JAL","NAY","COL","MIC","GRO","OAX","VER","CMX","ROO","YUC","CAM","CHP","PUE","MOR","ZAC"],"note":"Amado's jet fleet moved Colombian cocaine at a scale unmatched in Mexico; fortune estimated around US$25 billion and operations in some 20 states"},"facts":["Amado Carrillo Fuentes died on 4 July 1997 after plastic surgery; in November 1997 two of his surgeons were found tortured and encased in concrete in steel drums.","Rafael Aguilar Guajardo, the ex-DFS commander who founded the organization, was shot dead in Cancún on 12 April 1993, reportedly on Amado Carrillo's orders.","Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes' killing on 11 September 2004 triggered the Sinaloa–Juárez war; Ciudad Juárez went from roughly 1,600 homicides in 2008 to more than 3,000 in 2010.","La Línea chief 'El Diego', arrested 29 July 2011, confessed to ordering about 1,500 murders, including the 31 January 2010 Villas de Salvárcar massacre of 15 students.","Barrio Azteca gunmen killed three people tied to the US consulate in Ciudad Juárez on 13 March 2010.","On 4 November 2019 La Línea gunmen killed nine dual-national women and children of the LeBarón and Langford families in Sonora.","The 1 January 2023 Mexicles assault on Cereso 3 killed 17 people and freed 30 inmates; 'El Neto' was shot dead four days later.","OFAC sanctioned five La Línea members and two companies on 31 October 2024 and dated CJNG fentanyl supplies to September 2023.","Vicente Carrillo Fuentes was transferred to the US on 27 February 2025 with 28 other prisoners and faces a seven-count Brooklyn indictment after a 28-year Mexican sentence; prosecutors dropped the death penalty on 5 August 2025.","The US designated the Juárez Cartel (aliases La Línea, Barrio Azteca) a Foreign Terrorist Organization, signed 2 July and effective 16 July 2026; Juárez homicides fell from 1,108 in 2024 to 926 in 2025."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Juárez Cartel exists as La Línea and its sierra branch styled Nuevo Cártel de Juárez under César Daniel Manjarrez Alonso, 'El H2', with founder 'El JL' Ledezma still a fugitive; the US designated it a Foreign Terrorist Organization in July 2026 (signed 2 July, effective 16 July) and it depends on CJNG supply while fighting Sinaloa factions in Ojinaga–Aldama, Guadalupe y Calvo and Bocoyna, where El H2's nephew 'El 19' was killed on 22 August 2026. Vicente Carrillo Fuentes is in plea negotiations in Brooklyn federal court with a hearing set for 20 October 2026. Ciudad Juárez recorded 926 homicides in 2025, fewer than 1,000 for the first time since 2017, and Chihuahua still ranks among Mexico's most violent states.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/juarez-cartel-profile/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_L%C3%ADnea_(gang)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny/pr/alleged-mexican-drug-kingpin-charged-leading-continuing-criminal-enterprise-and-other","https://laopinion.com/2026/07/28/el-viceroy-exlider-del-cartel-de-juarez-no-llego-a-un-acuerdo-con-la-fiscalia-de-ee-uu-y-fijan-nueva-audiencia/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy2704","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-07-16/pdf/2026-14259.pdf","https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/07/terrorist-designations-of-the-juarez-cartel-and-los-viagras/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/nacion/detienen-a-el-02-integrante-de-los-fariseos-objetivo-prioritario-en-chihuahua-caen-10-mas-con-armas-y-explosivos/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/08/cae-el-fantasma-en-aldama-autoridades-lo-vinculan-al-mando-de-una-celula-delictiva-en-chihuahua/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/08/23/estados/fiscalia-de-chihuahua-confirma-muerte-de-el-roy-integrante-del-grupo-delictivo-los-hs-del-ncdj","https://diario.mx/estado/2026/aug/26/otro-sobrino-de-el-h2-sucesor-en-san-juanito-1135161.html","https://eldiariodechihuahua.mx/local/2026/jan/03/fue-2025-el-de-menos-asesinatos-en-10-anos-761331.html"],"verified":true},{"id":"gulf","name":"Gulf Cartel","aka":["Cártel del Golfo","CDG","Osiel Cárdenas-Guillén Organization","García Ábrego Organization","Matamoros Cartel"],"founded":1984,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"TAM","city":"Matamoros","lat":25.869,"lng":-97.5027},"summary":"Mexico's oldest surviving trafficking organization, rooted in Juan Nepomuceno Guerra's 1930s contraband network in Matamoros and converted into a cocaine cartel by his nephew Juan García Ábrego in 1984. Under Osiel Cárdenas Guillén it created Los Zetas, whose 2010 rebellion and a decade of arrests shattered the cartel into rival Tamaulipas factions (Metros, Ciclones, Escorpiones, Rojos, Panteras, plus Grupo Sombra in Veracruz). Designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization effective February 20, 2025, it survives as a fragmented but still dominant border franchise built on migrant smuggling, extortion and fuel theft.","history":"The Gulf Cartel grew out of a contraband network that Juan Nepomuceno Guerra (born 1915) built in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, in the 1930s, running alcohol across the Rio Grande into Texas in the final years of U.S. Prohibition and under Texas' dry laws, then diversifying into gambling, car theft and political patronage. His nephew Juan García Ábrego turned the family business into a cocaine pipeline and took control in 1984, negotiating directly with Colombia's Cali Cartel to be paid in product, up to half of each load, rather than a transport fee. Protected by senior federal police and politicians, the organization was earning billions a year with distribution cells from Houston to Los Angeles by the mid-1990s. García Ábrego became the first drug trafficker placed on the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list on March 9, 1995; he was arrested on a ranch outside Monterrey on January 14, 1996, flown to Houston, convicted in October 1996 on 22 counts and sentenced on January 31, 1997 to 11 consecutive life terms, with a jury-ordered $350 million forfeiture. A succession fight followed among Humberto García Ábrego, Óscar Malherbe de León (arrested in Mexico City on February 26, 1997) and Salvador Gómez Herrera.\n\nOsiel Cárdenas Guillén, a former mechanic and police informant, settled the fight by having Gómez Herrera killed in July 1999, earning the nickname 'El Mata Amigos'. Between 1997 and 1999 he recruited more than 30 deserters from the army's GAFE special forces, led by Lieutenant Arturo Guzmán Decena ('Z-1'), as a praetorian guard that became Los Zetas. In November 1999 he surrounded FBI and DEA agents in Matamoros at gunpoint, an act that later formed part of his U.S. indictment. The Gulf-Zetas machine fought the Sinaloa Federation for Nuevo Laredo from 2003 to 2007 and pushed Zetas cells into Veracruz, Tabasco, the Yucatán Peninsula, Michoacán and Guerrero. Cárdenas was captured in Matamoros after a shootout on March 14, 2003, kept running the cartel from La Palma prison, and was extradited on January 19, 2007; in February 2010 a Houston court sentenced him to 25 years and a $50 million forfeiture. His brother Antonio Ezequiel 'Tony Tormenta' Cárdenas Guillén and Jorge Eduardo 'El Coss' Costilla Sánchez took over, while the Zetas under Heriberto Lazcano grew increasingly autonomous.\n\nThe rupture came on January 18, 2010, when Gulf gunmen in Reynosa killed Zetas finance chief Víctor Peña Mendoza ('Concord 3') and the cartel refused to hand over the killers. Open war broke out across Tamaulipas in February 2010. The Gulf Cartel allied with the Sinaloa Cartel and La Familia Michoacana in an anti-Zetas coalition, but the Zetas emptied towns such as Ciudad Mier and massacred migrants in San Fernando. On November 5, 2010, Tony Tormenta died in an eight-hour battle with marines in Matamoros, and the cartel fractured: Costilla's Reynosa loyalists became Los Metros, Cárdenas-family loyalists Los Rojos, and Matamoros gunmen Los Ciclones and Los Escorpiones. Metros boss Samuel 'Metro 3' Flores Borrego was murdered on September 2, 2011; Rafael Cárdenas Vela was arrested in Port Isabel, Texas, on October 20, 2011; Mario Cárdenas Guillén fell in Altamira on September 3, 2012 and El Coss in Tampico nine days later. Mario 'X-20' Ramírez Treviño then fought a Metros civil war that killed Miguel 'El Gringo' Villarreal in Reynosa in March 2013 before his own arrest on August 17, 2013.\n\nFrom 2013 no single boss ruled again. The Ciclones and Escorpiones held Matamoros under the Cárdenas clan (Homero Cárdenas Guillén reportedly died in March 2014; José Alfredo 'El Contador' Cárdenas Martínez was arrested in 2018, 2019 and again on February 27, 2022; Evaristo 'El Vaquero' Cruz Sánchez was captured on April 6, 2021). The Metros held Reynosa through repeated purges, losing 'Comandante Toro' to marines on April 22, 2017 and, in 2023, the defection of César 'El Primito' Morfín Morfín to the CJNG. Los Rojos drifted to the Tampico-Altamira corridor and Grupo Sombra, formed in 2017, enforced in northern Veracruz. Meanwhile the Cártel del Noreste (the Zetas' heirs) entrenched in Nuevo Laredo and the CJNG probed Reynosa, San Fernando and Victoria. Revenue shifted from wholesale cocaine to the kidnapping and taxing of migrants, extortion, retail drugs and stolen fuel. Extraditions continued: El Coss (2015, sentenced to life on September 22, 2022), X-20 (December 18, 2017) and Mario Cárdenas Guillén (May 17, 2022).\n\nThe Escorpiones' kidnapping of four Americans in Matamoros on March 3, 2023, two of whom were killed along with a Mexican bystander, brought unprecedented U.S. pressure; the faction dumped five of its own men with an apology letter on March 9. 'El Pingüa', a Ciclones chief and nephew of 'La Kena', fell in Matamoros on March 4, 2024, and Escorpiones/Ciclones chief José Alberto 'La Kena' García Vilano was arrested in Monterrey on January 19, 2024. Osiel Cárdenas walked out of FCI Terre Haute on August 30, 2024, was handed to Mexico at San Ysidro on December 16, 2024 and jailed the same day at Altiplano. On February 20, 2025 the FTO and SDGT designation of the Cártel del Golfo took effect; a week later, on February 27, La Kena and El Vaquero were among 29 prisoners Mexico flew to U.S. courts. X-20 died in U.S. custody on March 13, 2025. OFAC sanctioned the Morfín Morfín brothers on May 1, 2025 as CJNG fuel smugglers in Tamaulipas; Reynosa's Metros were blamed for the murder of the five musicians of Grupo Fugitivo (May 25, 2025) and for the August 4, 2025 assassination of FGR delegate Ernesto Vásquez Reyna in Reynosa, while inmates rioted against Grupo Sombra's control of the Tuxpan prison on August 2, 2025, leaving seven dead. Veteran boss Zeferino 'Don Zefe' Peña Cuéllar was killed by state agents in Santiago, Nuevo León, on December 23, 2025.\n\nIn 2026 the state offensive focused on fuel theft. Ex-Gulf trafficker Ricardo Cortez-Mateos was among 37 prisoners transferred to the U.S. on January 20; Los Rojos gunman 'El Sapo' fell in Aldama on February 4; El Vaquero pleaded guilty in Brownsville on March 31; an Escorpiones huachicol storage site was dismantled in Matamoros on April 25; Metros leader Alexander 'Metro 9' Benavides Flores was arrested in Reynosa on April 27, triggering blockades, shootings and arson; Grupo Sombra's operational chief 'Delta 1' was captured in Coatzacoalcos on July 2 over the abduction and murder of journalist Roxana Guzmán; and on August 26-27 the Navy raided five Rojos properties in Tampico and Altamira, seizing 135 exotic animals, 68,000 liters of diesel and assets worth 33.9 million pesos. On September 3, 2026 the FGR's Guanajuato delegate said the Escorpiones now run pipeline theft in the Laja-Bajío in alliance with the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima (Los Marros). As of September 2026 the Gulf Cartel has no unified command, faces CJNG and Cártel del Noreste encroachment and steady defections, yet its factions still control the Matamoros and Reynosa crossings and the extortion economy of Tamaulipas.","leaders":[{"name":"Juan Nepomuceno Guerra","alias":"Don Juan N.","role":"Founder and patriarch of the Matamoros smuggling network that preceded the cartel","from":1930,"to":1984,"fate":"Never held more than hours in jail; died of respiratory illness in Matamoros on July 12, 2001, aged 85"},{"name":"Juan García Ábrego","alias":"El Muñeco","role":"Top leader; built the Cali cocaine partnership","from":1984,"to":1996,"fate":"FBI Ten Most Wanted March 9, 1995; arrested near Monterrey Jan 14, 1996 and flown to Houston; convicted Oct 1996 on 22 counts; 11 consecutive life terms and a $350M jury forfeiture Jan 31, 1997; held at USP Hazelton"},{"name":"Óscar Malherbe de León","alias":"El Malherbe","role":"Interim leader after García Ábrego","from":1996,"to":1997,"fate":"Arrested in Mixcoac, Mexico City, Feb 26, 1997; tried and imprisoned in Mexico; still in Mexican custody as of 2026"},{"name":"Salvador Gómez Herrera","alias":"El Chava","role":"Co-leader with Osiel Cárdenas","from":1997,"to":1999,"fate":"Murdered in July 1999 on Cárdenas's orders"},{"name":"Osiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"El Mata Amigos","role":"Top leader; founder of Los Zetas","from":1999,"to":2003,"fate":"Captured in Matamoros Mar 14, 2003; extradited Jan 19, 2007; 25 years and $50M forfeiture Feb 2010; released from FCI Terre Haute Aug 30, 2024 after 14 years; handed to Mexico at San Ysidro Dec 16, 2024 and jailed the same day at Altiplano on organized-crime, drug and money-laundering charges, with three pending warrants and seven reactivated cases"},{"name":"Antonio Ezequiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"Tony Tormenta","role":"Co-leader (Matamoros)","from":2003,"to":2010,"fate":"Killed by marines in an eight-hour battle in Matamoros on Nov 5, 2010"},{"name":"Jorge Eduardo Costilla Sánchez","alias":"El Coss","role":"Co-leader, then top leader; patron of Los Metros","from":2003,"to":2012,"fate":"Arrested in Tampico Sept 12, 2012; extradited Sept 30, 2015; pleaded guilty Sept 26, 2017; life sentence and $5M forfeiture Sept 22, 2022"},{"name":"Mario Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"El Gordo / M-1","role":"Cárdenas-family faction chief (Rojos/Matamoros)","from":2010,"to":2012,"fate":"Arrested in Altamira Sept 3, 2012; 20-year Mexican sentence Apr 2021; extradited to the US May 17, 2022; sentenced in E.D. Texas in late 2023 to 14 years 5 months and released from FCI Beaumont Oct 11, 2024"},{"name":"Samuel Flores Borrego","alias":"Metro 3","role":"Reynosa plaza boss; founder of Los Metros","from":2009,"to":2011,"fate":"Shot dead on the Reynosa-Monterrey highway Sept 2, 2011, allegedly by Los Rojos"},{"name":"Rafael Cárdenas Vela","alias":"El Junior / Comandante 900","role":"San Fernando, Río Bravo and Matamoros boss; Ciclones founder","from":2011,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested at a traffic stop in Port Isabel, Texas, Oct 20, 2011; convicted in Brownsville (S.D. Tex.) and sentenced Nov 17, 2014 to 240 months and a $1M fine; cooperating witness"},{"name":"Mario Ramírez Treviño","alias":"X-20 / El Pelón","role":"Metros chief and last recognized overall leader","from":2011,"to":2013,"fate":"Arrested Aug 17, 2013 in Reynosa; extradited Dec 18, 2017; became a cooperating witness; died in US custody Mar 13, 2025, aged 63, before his case concluded"},{"name":"Homero Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"El Majadero","role":"Ciclones/Matamoros de facto leader after X-20's arrest","from":2013,"to":2014,"fate":"Reportedly died Mar 28, 2014 of complications after liposuction surgery; the death was never officially confirmed"},{"name":"Julián Manuel Loisa Salinas","alias":"Comandante Toro (also reported as Juan Manuel Loisa Salinas)","role":"Los Metros leader, Reynosa","from":2015,"to":2017,"fate":"Killed by marines in Reynosa Apr 22, 2017; his death set off citywide blockades"},{"name":"José Alfredo Cárdenas Martínez","alias":"El Contador","role":"Ciclones leader, Matamoros; nephew of Osiel Cárdenas","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Arrested Feb 19, 2018 (released), Oct 29, 2019 (released) and recaptured in Mexico City Feb 27, 2022; a judge ruled his US extradition admissible in Aug 2024 and the SRE approved it in Dec 2024, but he was freed from Reclusorio Oriente on Nov 20, 2024 and remains at large; ODNI still listed him as Los Ciclones leader in April 2026"},{"name":"Evaristo Cruz Sánchez","alias":"El Vaquero","role":"Ciclones regional chief, Matamoros; reported second-in-command to El Contador","from":2018,"to":2021,"fate":"Arrested Apr 6, 2021 in Salinas Victoria, Nuevo León; transferred to the US Feb 27, 2025; pleaded guilty to a federal drug charge in Brownsville Mar 31, 2026, facing up to 10 years, with sentencing set for Jul 7, 2026"},{"name":"José Alberto García Vilano","alias":"La Kena / Ciclón 19","role":"Escorpiones/Ciclones leader, Matamoros","from":2021,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested in Monterrey Jan 19, 2024; sent to US custody Feb 27, 2025 and held at FDC Houston on drug and money-laundering charges tied to the 2023 Matamoros kidnapping"},{"name":"Juan Enrique N.","alias":"El Pingüa","role":"Ciclones chief, Matamoros; nephew of La Kena","from":2024,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested with five bodyguards in Matamoros on Mar 4, 2024 for drug trafficking, migrant smuggling, kidnapping and money laundering"},{"name":"Armando López Garcés","alias":"Escorpión 7 / El Pajarito / Bravo 1","role":"Reported Escorpiones leader and Matamoros plaza chief after La Kena","from":2024,"to":0,"fate":"At large as of September 2026"},{"name":"Alexander Benavides Flores","alias":"Metro 9 / R9","role":"Los Metros leader, Reynosa","from":2024,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested by federal ministerial police in Reynosa Apr 27, 2026, triggering blockades and arson across Tamaulipas; bound over for trial on military-grade weapons charges May 8, 2026"}],"factions":[{"id":"metros","name":"Los Metros","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Reynosa, Río Bravo, Miguel Alemán and the Ribereña, TAM","leader":"Samuel Flores Borrego 'Metro 3' (k. 2011); Mario Ramírez Treviño 'X-20' (arr. 2013); 'Comandante Toro' (k. 2017); César Morfín Morfín 'El Primito' (defected to CJNG 2023); Alexander Benavides Flores 'Metro 9' (arr. Apr 27, 2026)","note":"Costilla loyalists formed after Tony Tormenta's death; bloodied by internal purges (Reynosa 2013, 2017); armed cell Los Panteras; since 2023 allied with the CJNG, which ODNI says protects it from the Sinaloa Cartel, Cártel del Noreste and the Escorpiones; blamed for the May 2025 murder of the five musicians of Grupo Fugitivo and the Aug 4, 2025 assassination of the FGR delegate in Reynosa"},{"id":"ciclones","name":"Los Ciclones","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Matamoros, Valle Hermoso and the Brownsville crossing, TAM; cells in NLE and SLP","leader":"Rafael Cárdenas Vela (arr. 2011); Homero Cárdenas Guillén (d. 2014); José Alfredo 'El Contador' Cárdenas Martínez (freed Nov 2024, at large); 'El Vaquero' (arr. 2021); 'La Kena' (arr. 2024); Juan Enrique N. 'El Pingüa' (arr. Mar 4, 2024)","note":"Cárdenas-family franchise that kept the Matamoros plaza; overlaps with the Escorpiones, which serve as its armed wing; fights Los Metros and the Cártel del Noreste"},{"id":"escorpiones","name":"Los Escorpiones (Grupo Escorpión)","from":2009,"to":0,"area":"Matamoros, TAM; fuel-theft cells reported in Guanajuato (2026)","leader":"Armando López Garcés 'Escorpión 7' (reported, 2024-)","note":"Originally Tony Tormenta's bodyguard unit, now the Gulf Cartel's armed wing; responsible for the March 3, 2023 kidnapping of four Americans; a huachicol storage site was dismantled in Matamoros Apr 25, 2026 (8.3 million pesos in damage, two arrests); the FGR reported an alliance with the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima (Los Marros) in Guanajuato on Sept 3, 2026"},{"id":"rojos_cdg","name":"Los Rojos","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Originally Reynosa and Matamoros; later Tampico, Altamira, Ciudad Madero and Aldama, TAM","leader":"Mario Cárdenas Guillén (arr. 2012); Juan Mejía González 'R-1'; Serapio N. 'El Sapo / R-105' (arr. Aldama, Feb 4, 2026)","note":"Cárdenas loyalists who fought the Metros from 2011; on Aug 26-27, 2026 the Navy, FGR and SSPC raided five Rojos properties in Tampico and Altamira, seizing 135 exotic animals, 68,000 liters of diesel and 33.9 million pesos in assets"},{"id":"grupo_sombra","name":"Grupo Sombra (Fuerzas Especiales Grupo Sombra / Mafia Veracruzana)","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Northern and southern Veracruz: Tuxpan, Poza Rica, Pánuco, El Higo, Tempoal, Álamo Temapache, Nanchital, Coatzacoalcos","leader":"Francisco Mendiola Cisneros 'El Comandante Miranda / El Patrón' (founder); Javier Iván N. 'Delta 1' (operational chief, arr. Coatzacoalcos, Jul 2, 2026)","note":"Gulf Cartel offshoot founded in 2017 in Tuxpan and Poza Rica; reported in 2026 to influence more than 52 Veracruz municipalities; extortion, huachicol and rivalry with the CJNG and Zetas Vieja Escuela; inmates rioted against its control of the Tuxpan prison on Aug 2, 2025 (7 dead, 11 injured), and its operatives were charged with the June 2026 abduction and murder of journalist Roxana Berenice Guzmán Ramírez"},{"id":"panteras","name":"Los Panteras","from":2015,"to":0,"area":"Reynosa, TAM","leader":"Unknown","note":"Armed enforcement cell of the Metros, repeatedly named in Reynosa shootouts and internal purges"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":1999,"to":2010,"note":"Created by Osiel Cárdenas as the cartel's armed wing; grew autonomous and broke away in early 2010"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Anti-Zetas coalition (styled 'Cárteles Unidos' in 2010 narco-banners) after the split"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Joined the 2010 alliance against Los Zetas"},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":2005,"to":2006,"note":"Brief pact between Cárdenas and Benjamín Arellano Félix against Sinaloa; collapsed"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2023,"to":2026,"note":"Metros faction only: after César 'El Primito' Morfín Morfín took the Reynosa cell over to the CJNG in 2023, ODNI (April 2026) described Los Metros as allied with the CJNG for protection, and El País and FGR investigators tied the two to a cross-border fuel-smuggling network; other Gulf factions still fight CJNG incursions"},{"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima","from":2026,"to":0,"note":"The FGR's Guanajuato delegate reported in May 2026 and again on Sept 3, 2026 that the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima (Los Marros) is allied with the Escorpiones for pipeline theft in the Laja-Bajío region"},{"cartel":"other","from":1985,"to":1996,"note":"Cali Cartel (Colombia) supplied cocaine, paying the Gulf Cartel up to 50% of each load instead of a transport fee"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":2010,"to":2015,"note":"War across Tamaulipas, Nuevo León and Veracruz from February 2010; the bloodiest conflict of the drug war"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2003,"to":2007,"note":"Sinaloa Federation's push into Nuevo Laredo fought off by Gulf-Zetas forces"},{"cartel":"cdn","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Zetas successor controls Nuevo Laredo and contests northern Tamaulipas against the Escorpiones, Ciclones and Metros"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"CJNG incursions in Reynosa, Miguel Alemán, Camargo, San Fernando and Victoria; absorbed the defecting Metros cell of 'El Primito' in 2023 while still fighting the Escorpiones and Ciclones"},{"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Fights Grupo Sombra in northern Veracruz and the Huasteca"}],"activities":["Cocaine trafficking (Cali partnership, Matamoros and Reynosa crossings into Texas)","Marijuana, methamphetamine, heroin and, more recently, fentanyl smuggling","Migrant smuggling and mass kidnapping-for-ransom of migrants at the border","Extortion of businesses, transport and taxi drivers (cobro de piso)","Fuel theft (huachicol), clandestine gas stations and crude/diesel smuggling to Texas","Weapons trafficking and bulk-cash smuggling southbound","Cargo theft, vehicle theft and retail drug sales","Money laundering through logistics and transport companies","Assassination of civilians and Mexican government officials, and bribery of police and customs personnel"],"peak":{"from":1999,"to":2010,"states":["TAM","NLE","VER","TAB","CAM","ROO","YUC","COA","SLP","ZAC","HID","MIC","GRO","OAX","CHP"],"note":"With Los Zetas as its army the Gulf Cartel controlled the Gulf coast corridor from the Guatemalan border to the Texas crossings and fought Sinaloa to a standstill for Nuevo Laredo; by 2026 ODNI placed its core operations only in Tamaulipas, Nuevo León and Coahuila, with limited presence in Jalisco and Tabasco"},"facts":["Juan García Ábrego was the first drug trafficker ever placed on the FBI Ten Most Wanted list (March 9, 1995); after his January 14, 1996 arrest he was convicted on 22 counts and given 11 consecutive life terms with a $350 million jury-ordered forfeiture.","The cartel was paid by the Cali Cartel in cocaine, up to 50% of each load, instead of a per-kilo transport fee, turning a smuggling service into a wholesale trafficker.","Osiel Cárdenas recruited more than 30 army special-forces (GAFE) deserters between 1997 and 1999, creating Los Zetas.","Cárdenas was sentenced in February 2010 to 25 years and a $50 million forfeiture, released from FCI Terre Haute on August 30, 2024 after serving 14 years, handed over at San Ysidro on December 16, 2024 and jailed at Altiplano the same day, where Mexican prosecutors say pending cases could total up to 730 years.","Antonio 'Tony Tormenta' Cárdenas Guillén died on November 5, 2010 in an eight-hour firefight with marines in Matamoros.","Jorge Eduardo 'El Coss' Costilla Sánchez received a U.S. life sentence and a $5 million forfeiture on September 22, 2022.","On March 3, 2023 Escorpiones gunmen kidnapped four Americans in Matamoros, killing two of them and a Mexican bystander; the faction handed over five of its own men with an apology letter on March 9.","The Cártel del Golfo was designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization and SDGT alongside seven other groups; Secretary of State Marco Rubio signed the determination on February 6, 2025 and it took effect on publication on February 20, 2025.","Two faction bosses, 'La Kena' and 'El Vaquero', were among the 29 prisoners Mexico transferred to U.S. courts on February 27, 2025; a further 26 followed in August 2025 and 37 more on January 20, 2026, the last group including ex-Gulf trafficker Ricardo Cortez-Mateos, 'Billeton'.","On August 26-27, 2026 the Navy seized 135 exotic animals (35 of them endangered or protected, including tigers, hyenas and a camel), 68,000 liters of diesel and 33.9 million pesos in assets from five Los Rojos properties in Tampico and Altamira.","ODNI's April 2026 counterterrorism guide put the Gulf Cartel's core operations in Tamaulipas, Nuevo León and Coahuila, with distribution reaching at least 16 U.S. states."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Gulf Cartel is a fractured federation with no overall leader: the Escorpiones under the reported command of 'Escorpión 7' hold Matamoros as the armed wing of the Ciclones, whose nominal boss 'El Contador' has been at large since his November 2024 release despite an approved U.S. extradition; the Metros in Reynosa are reeling from the April 27, 2026 arrest of 'Metro 9' and their absorption into the CJNG's orbit; Los Rojos were hit by the August 26-27 Navy raids in Tampico-Altamira; and Grupo Sombra lost operational chief 'Delta 1' in July. A U.S.-designated FTO since February 20, 2025, its factions have pivoted to fuel theft and crude smuggling (Escorpiones storage site dismantled in Matamoros on April 25, 2026; FGR-reported alliance with the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima in Guanajuato on September 3, 2026) while still controlling migrant kidnapping and extortion at the Matamoros and Reynosa crossings under pressure from the CJNG and Cártel del Noreste.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/gulf-cartel-profile/","https://insightcrime.org/news/gulf-cartel-og-who-created-mexicos-zetas-released-from-us-prison/","https://www.odni.gov/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/CT-Guide-Gulf-Cartel_508.pdf","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/02/20/2025-02873/foreign-terrorist-organization-designations-of-tren-de-aragua-mara-salvatrucha-cartel-de-sinaloa","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/attorney-general-pamela-bondi-announces-29-wanted-defendants-mexico-taken-us-custody","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/37-mexican-nationals-wanted-serious-crimes-transferred-united-states-mexico-including","https://www.ice.gov/news/releases/gulf-cartel-plaza-boss-receives-20-year-prison-sentence","https://dhs.gov/archive/hsi/news/2024/12/17/former-leader-gulf-cartel-removed-after-being-released-prison","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20250501","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/27/megaoperativo-contra-huachicol-destapa-rancho-con-135-animales-exoticos-en-tamaulipas-les-arrebatan-hienas-tigres-y-un-camello/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/04/27/arde-reynosa-por-captura-del-metro-9-lider-del-cartel-del-golfo-hay-bloqueos-balaceras-e-incendios/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/03/31/el-vaquero-segundo-al-mando-del-cartel-del-golfo-se-declara-culpable-en-eeuu/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/04/grupo-sombra-el-cartel-que-domina-veracruz-tras-el-asesinato-de-roxana-guzman/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/03/05/cayo-en-tamaulipas-el-pingua-presunto-lider-de-los-ciclones-del-cartel-del-golfo/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/11/07/mario-cardenas-guillen-exlider-del-cartel-del-golfo-ya-no-esta-bajo-custodia-del-buro-de-prisiones-de-eeuu/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2025/08/05/politica/asesinan-en-calles-de-reynosa-a-jefe-de-la-fgr-en-tamaulipas","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/12/23/abaten-en-nuevo-leon-a-zeferino-pena-alias-don-zefe-lider-de-fundador-de-los-zetas/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Matamoros_kidnappings"],"verified":true},{"id":"zetas","name":"Los Zetas","aka":["La Compañía","Compañía Z","La Última Letra","Cártel de Los Zetas"],"founded":1999,"dissolved":2015,"parent":"gulf","origin":{"state":"TAM","city":"Matamoros","lat":25.869,"lng":-97.5027},"summary":"Los Zetas were founded in 1999 by Mexican army special-forces (GAFE) deserters recruited by Gulf Cartel boss Osiel Cárdenas Guillén as his bodyguard and enforcement unit. After breaking with the Gulf Cartel in early 2010 they became, by 2011-12, the cartel with the largest territorial reach in Mexico and the perpetrators of its worst massacres, before leadership losses in 2012-2015 shattered them into the Cártel del Noreste, the Zetas Vieja Escuela and Los Talibanes.","history":"Los Zetas began in 1997-1999, when Lieutenant Arturo Guzmán Decena, a Grupo Aeromóvil de Fuerzas Especiales (GAFE) officer posted to Tamaulipas, deserted and recruited roughly 31 fellow special-forces soldiers to serve as the bodyguard and enforcement unit of Gulf Cartel boss Osiel Cárdenas Guillén. Based in Matamoros and named after their radio call-sign \"Z\", the unit brought military discipline, intelligence work and heavy weapons into cartel violence. Guzmán Decena (Z-1) was shot dead by the Army in a Matamoros restaurant on 21 November 2002; Rogelio González Pizaña (Z-2) led until his capture on 29 October 2004, after which Heriberto Lazcano Lazcano (Z-3), a Hidalgo-born GAFE deserter, took command.\n\nOsiel Cárdenas's arrest on 14 March 2003 and extradition in January 2007 loosened the Gulf Cartel's grip and made the Zetas its de facto operational core. They fought the Sinaloa Federation for Nuevo Laredo in 2004-2006, and from October 2004 Lazcano recruited Guatemalan Kaibiles to train recruits. The group pushed into Veracruz, Tabasco, Coahuila, San Luis Potosí, Zacatecas, Michoacán (whose Familia Michoacana emerged in 2006 to expel them) and Guatemala's Petén and Alta Verapaz, where commander Daniel Pérez Rojas \"Z-200\" was captured in April 2008. Unlike older cartels, the Zetas taxed every economy they touched: extortion, kidnapping, migrant smuggling, fuel theft, piracy and coal. Losses mounted (Efraín Teodoro Torres, Z-14, was killed in a shootout at a horse-race meeting at Villarín, Veracruz, on 3 March 2007, and his gunmen stole his body from the cemetery that same night; Jaime González Durán \"El Hummer\" was captured in Reynosa on 7 November 2008), but the structure absorbed them.\n\nThe Gulf Cartel's killing of Zeta lieutenant Víctor Peña Mendoza \"Concord 3\" in Reynosa on 18 January 2010 triggered the split; by February-March 2010 the Zetas were an independent cartel at war with their former bosses across Tamaulipas and Nuevo León. The years 2010-2012 were their apogee and their most murderous: 72 migrants were executed at El Huizachal, San Fernando, in August 2010; 193 bodies were exhumed from 47 San Fernando graves in April 2011; Miguel Ángel (Z-40) and Omar (Z-42) Treviño Morales ordered the razing of Allende, Coahuila, on 18-20 March 2011; 27 farmworkers were beheaded at Los Cocos, Petén, on 15 May 2011; arson at Monterrey's Casino Royale killed 52 on 25 August 2011; and 49 dismembered bodies were dumped at Cadereyta on 13 May 2012. Coahuila prosecutors later estimated that about 150 people were killed and their bodies dissolved inside the Piedras Negras state prison, which the Zetas ran as a base. With bases from Nuevo Laredo to Cobán, the Zetas held territory in at least 11 and by some counts 17 Mexican states and were judged the country's largest cartel by reach.\n\nInternal fracture followed. Iván Velázquez Caballero \"El Talibán\" (Z-50) broke with Z-40 in mid-2012, allied with the Gulf Cartel and was captured in San Luis Potosí on 26 September 2012. Lazcano was killed by marines in Progreso, Coahuila, on 7 October 2012; gunmen then stole his body from a funeral home in Sabinas. Z-40, already the organization's real power, was captured without a shot near Anáhuac, Nuevo León, on 15 July 2013 with US$2 million in cash and eight weapons; in Austin, Texas, his brother José was convicted in May 2013 of laundering Zeta money through quarter horses. Omar Treviño ran the remnant until his arrest in San Pedro Garza García on 4 March 2015.\n\nAfter 2015 the Zetas brand dissolved into rival heirs. The Treviño nephews Juan Francisco \"El Kiko\" and Juan Gerardo \"El Huevo\" Treviño Chávez rebranded the Nuevo Laredo core as the Cártel del Noreste (CDN), while old-guard commanders under José María Guízar Valencia (Z-43) and Luis Reyes Enríquez \"El Rex\" (Z-12) formed the Zetas Vieja Escuela in Ciudad Victoria and northern Veracruz in 2015-2016. From March 2016 the Vieja Escuela, Grupo Bravo and Los Talibanes allied with the Gulf Cartel against the CDN. Z-43's arrest in Mexico City on 9 February 2018 and El Rex's murder inside the Nuevo Laredo prison on 17 November 2018 left the CDN as the only heir of national weight.\n\nThe final chapter is judicial, and it has run on two tracks. The old founding generation was tried first: Z-43, a dual US-Mexican citizen born in Tulare, California, waived extradition, pleaded guilty in the Eastern District of Texas on 20 December 2021 to cocaine and money-laundering conspiracies and was sentenced to 480 months (40 years) on 15 November 2022 by Judge Richard A. Schell; \"El Hummer\", extradited on 20 October 2022, pleaded guilty on 28 February 2025 and was sentenced in Washington, DC, on 20 June 2025 to 35 years with a forfeiture order reported at some US$792 million. The Treviño brothers followed. On 27 February 2025 Mexico transferred Z-40, Z-42 and founding member Raúl Lucio Hernández Lechuga \"El Lucky\" to the United States among 29 prisoners, the Treviños to Washington and El Lucky to McKinney, Texas. The brothers pleaded not guilty in Washington, DC, before Judge Trevor McFadden to racketeering, drug, weapons and money-laundering charges; the death penalty was withdrawn, but both are held under Special Administrative Measures. Z-42 was moved from the Alexandria, Virginia, detention centre to FCI Lewisburg, Pennsylvania, on 18 November 2025 after threatening a guard, a case that became public in March 2026; Z-40 remains at the Northern Neck Regional Jail in Virginia, where in a 45-page motion filed on 19 January 2026 his lawyers said he had spent more than 275 days in solitary confinement. On 10 July 2026 prosecutors ruled out any plea deal, citing nearly 5 million evidence files and 1.5 million recordings, and the court kept 2028 as the tentative trial date; a status hearing on 10 September 2026 is to fix it. As of September 2026 Los Zetas survive only through the CDN, the Vieja Escuela remnants and Los Talibanes.","leaders":[{"name":"Arturo Guzmán Decena","alias":"Z-1","role":"Founder and first commander","from":1999,"to":2002,"fate":"Shot dead by the Army in a Matamoros restaurant, 21 Nov 2002"},{"name":"Rogelio González Pizaña","alias":"Z-2 / El Kelín","role":"Co-founder; commander after Z-1","from":2002,"to":2004,"fate":"Captured 29 Oct 2004 in Matamoros; released from Altiplano 30 Aug 2014 after his sentence was cut; killed with his family in Matamoros in early Dec 2015 (reported 5-6 Dec), blamed on the Gulf Cartel; body never found"},{"name":"Heriberto Lazcano Lazcano","alias":"Z-3 / El Lazca / El Verdugo","role":"Supreme leader; led the 2010 break from the Gulf Cartel","from":2004,"to":2012,"fate":"Killed by Navy marines in Progreso, Coahuila, 7 Oct 2012; body stolen from a Sabinas funeral home; US$5M US reward"},{"name":"Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-40 / L-40","role":"Second-in-command 2008-12; leader 2012-13","from":2012,"to":2013,"fate":"Captured 15 Jul 2013 near Anáhuac, NL; transferred to US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded not guilty in Washington DC; held under SAMs at Northern Neck Regional Jail, Virginia; trial tentatively 2028, status hearing 10 Sept 2026"},{"name":"Omar Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-42","role":"Leader after his brother's capture","from":2013,"to":2015,"fate":"Captured 4 Mar 2015 in San Pedro Garza García, NL; transferred to US 27 Feb 2025; moved 18 Nov 2025 from Alexandria, VA, to FCI Lewisburg, Pennsylvania, under SAMs after threatening a guard"},{"name":"Efraín Teodoro Torres","alias":"Z-14 / El Efra","role":"Founding member; Veracruz regional commander","from":1999,"to":2007,"fate":"Killed 3 Mar 2007 in a shootout at a horse-race meeting at Villarín, Veracruz; his gunmen stole the body from the cemetery the same night"},{"name":"Jaime González Durán","alias":"El Hummer","role":"Founding member; third-in-command; Reynosa and Miguel Alemán plaza boss","from":1999,"to":2008,"fate":"Captured 7 Nov 2008 in Reynosa; extradited to the US 20 Oct 2022; pleaded guilty 28 Feb 2025; sentenced in Washington DC on 20 Jun 2025 to 35 years, with a forfeiture reported at about US$792M"},{"name":"Jesús Enrique Rejón Aguilar","alias":"Z-7 / El Mamito","role":"Founding member; trainer and Coahuila commander","from":1999,"to":2011,"fate":"Captured 4 Jul 2011 in Atizapán de Zaragoza; extradited Sept 2012; pleaded guilty in Washington DC on 1 Feb 2013 before Judge Barbara J. Rothstein and testified for the government at the Austin quarter-horse trial; sentence not publicly reported"},{"name":"Raúl Lucio Hernández Lechuga","alias":"Z-16 / Z-100 / El Lucky","role":"Founding member; Veracruz-Puebla-Oaxaca regional boss","from":1999,"to":2011,"fate":"Captured 12 Dec 2011 at a ranch in Córdoba, Veracruz (133 rifles seized); transferred to US 27 Feb 2025 and sent to McKinney, Texas"},{"name":"Iván Velázquez Caballero","alias":"Z-50 / El Talibán","role":"Regional boss for SLP, Zacatecas and Monterrey; broke away 2012","from":2005,"to":2012,"fate":"Captured by Navy 26 Sep 2012 in San Luis Potosí; extradited Nov 2013; pleaded guilty 7 Apr 2014; 30-year US sentence 21 Jul 2017"},{"name":"José María Guízar Valencia","alias":"Z-43","role":"Last leader claiming the Zetas name (Vieja Escuela wing)","from":2015,"to":2018,"fate":"Arrested 9 Feb 2018 in Mexico City's Roma district (US$5M reward); as a dual US citizen waived extradition; pleaded guilty in the Eastern District of Texas 20 Dec 2021; sentenced to 40 years (480 months) on 15 Nov 2022"}],"factions":[{"id":"trevino_wing","name":"Treviño Morales wing (Nuevo Laredo core)","from":2012,"to":2015,"area":"Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas; Coahuila; Nuevo León","leader":"Miguel Ángel and Omar Treviño Morales","note":"Ran the organization after Lazcano's death; the Treviño nephews rebranded it as the Cártel del Noreste in 2014-15"},{"id":"vieja_escuela_wing","name":"Vieja Escuela / old guard","from":2014,"to":2016,"area":"Ciudad Victoria, Tamaulipas; northern Veracruz","leader":"José María Guízar Valencia 'Z-43'","note":"Veteran commanders opposed to the Treviño family; became the Zetas Vieja Escuela"},{"id":"talibanes_cell","name":"Cell of Iván Velázquez 'El Talibán' (Z-50)","from":2007,"to":2012,"area":"San Luis Potosí, Zacatecas, Aguascalientes, Guanajuato, Monterrey","leader":"Iván Velázquez Caballero","note":"About 400 men; broke away in mid-2012 and allied with the Gulf Cartel; survives as Los Talibanes"},{"id":"guatemala_cell","name":"Zetas Guatemala","from":2007,"to":2013,"area":"Petén and Alta Verapaz (Cobán), Guatemala","leader":"Daniel Pérez Rojas 'Z-200' (captured April 2008)","note":"Kaibil-trained enforcers; beheaded 27 farmworkers at Los Cocos, La Libertad, Petén, 15 May 2011, prompting a state of siege"},{"id":"veracruz_command","name":"Veracruz command","from":2004,"to":2012,"area":"Veracruz, Tabasco, Puebla","leader":"Efraín Teodoro Torres 'Z-14' (d. 2007), then Raúl Lucio Hernández Lechuga 'El Lucky'","note":"Controlled Gulf-coast routes; target of the CJNG 'Matazetas' campaign of 2011"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":1999,"to":2010,"note":"Created as the Gulf Cartel's armed wing under Osiel Cárdenas; split Feb-Mar 2010"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Anti-Sinaloa pact after the Beltrán Leyva brothers broke with El Chapo"},{"cartel":"juarez","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Cooperation with the Carrillo Fuentes organization and La Línea against the Sinaloa Federation"},{"cartel":"other","from":2004,"to":2012,"note":"Guatemalan Kaibiles recruited as trainers/enforcers and local Guatemalan trafficking networks in Petén and Alta Verapaz"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2004,"to":2015,"note":"Nuevo Laredo war 2004-06; nationwide war 2008-12"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2010,"to":2015,"note":"Tamaulipas-Nuevo León war after the killing of 'Concord 3' in Jan 2010"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2006,"to":2011,"note":"La Familia announced itself in 2006 as a movement to expel the Zetas from Michoacán"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2011,"to":2014,"note":"Successor of La Familia; fought the Zetas in Michoacán and Guanajuato"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2011,"to":2015,"note":"CJNG 'Matazetas' dumped 35 bodies in Boca del Río, Veracruz, 20 Sep 2011"},{"cartel":"talibanes","from":2012,"to":2015,"note":"El Talibán's breakaway faction allied with the Gulf Cartel against Z-40"},{"cartel":"government","from":2007,"to":2015,"note":"Army and Navy campaigns killed Lazcano and captured Z-40 and Z-42"},{"cartel":"us_government","from":2009,"to":2026,"note":"Kingpin Act 2009, TCO designation 2011, US$5M rewards, DC prosecution of Z-40 and Z-42"}],"activities":["Cocaine trafficking (Colombia-Guatemala-Gulf coast corridor)","Marijuana and methamphetamine trafficking","Extortion of businesses (derecho de piso)","Kidnapping for ransom, including mass kidnapping of migrants","Migrant smuggling","Fuel theft (huachicol) from Pemex pipelines","Pirated goods and retail drug sales","Human trafficking and prostitution rackets","Coal mining and mineral rackets in Coahuila","Money laundering (racehorses, businesses)"],"peak":{"from":2010,"to":2012,"states":["TAM","NLE","COA","VER","SLP","ZAC","HID","TAB","CAM","YUC","ROO","CHP","OAX","GUA","AGU","PUE","MEX"],"note":"After the 2010 split the Zetas held the largest territorial footprint of any Mexican cartel, with bases from Nuevo Laredo and Fresnillo to Veracruz and Cobán, Guatemala, and cells in some 37 US cities"},"facts":["Founded by roughly 31 GAFE special-forces deserters recruited by Arturo Guzmán Decena for Osiel Cárdenas around 1997-99 (CTC West Point).","72 migrants (58 men, 14 women) were executed at El Huizachal, San Fernando, in August 2010; a lone Ecuadorian survivor walked to an Army checkpoint.","193 bodies were recovered from 47 clandestine graves around San Fernando between April and June 2011.","The Casino Royale arson in Monterrey on 25 August 2011 killed 52 people over an unpaid extortion fee of about US$10,000-11,000 a week.","The Allende massacre of 18-20 March 2011, ordered by Z-40 and Z-42 after a DEA intelligence leak, left 42 officially confirmed disappeared and local estimates of around 300 victims.","49 dismembered bodies (43 men, 6 women) were dumped on Highway 40 at Cadereyta, Nuevo León, on 13 May 2012.","On 15 May 2011 Zetas beheaded 27 farmworkers at the Los Cocos farm in La Libertad, Petén, Guatemala.","The US offered US$5 million each for Lazcano, Z-40, Z-42 and Z-43; Z-40 was captured with US$2 million in cash and eight weapons.","Founder Z-43 was sentenced to 40 years in the Eastern District of Texas on 15 November 2022, and founder 'El Hummer' to 35 years in Washington, DC, on 20 June 2025.","The DC case against Z-40 and Z-42 involves nearly 5 million evidence files and 1.5 million audio recordings; prosecutors refused a plea deal on 10 July 2026 and 2028 remains the tentative trial year.","José Treviño Morales, Z-40's brother, was convicted in Austin in May 2013 of laundering Zeta money through quarter-horse racing."],"status_2026":"Los Zetas no longer exist as a unified organization; their heirs are the Cártel del Noreste (a US-designated FTO since 20 Feb 2025), the residual Zetas Vieja Escuela and Los Talibanes. The last supreme leaders, Miguel Ángel (Z-40) and Omar (Z-42) Treviño Morales, were transferred to the United States on 27 Feb 2025 and await a racketeering trial in Washington, DC: on 10 July 2026 prosecutors ruled out a plea deal, 2028 remains the tentative trial year and a status hearing is set for 10 Sept 2026. Z-40 is held at the Northern Neck Regional Jail in Virginia and Z-42, since 18 Nov 2025, at FCI Lewisburg, both under Special Administrative Measures. Of the founding generation, Z-43 is serving 40 years in the United States, 'El Hummer' 35 years, and 'El Lucky' is in US custody in the Eastern District of Texas.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/zetas-profile/","https://ctc.westpoint.edu/a-profile-of-los-zetas-mexicos-second-most-powerful-drug-cartel/","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edtx/pr/two-high-ranking-los-zetas-cartel-members-latest-sentenced-federal-prison-drug","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/06/20/el-hummer-exjefe-de-plaza-de-los-zetas-se-declara-culpable-y-lo-sentencian-a-35-anos-de-prision-en-eeuu/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/10/fiscalia-de-eeuu-descarta-acuerdo-con-el-z-40-y-el-z-42-los-llevara-a-juicio/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/03/05/te-gustan-los-doberman-el-z-42-exlider-de-los-zetas-amenazo-a-guardia-en-prision-de-eeuu/","https://www.elimparcial.com/mexico/2026/01/19/miguel-angel-trevino-morales-el-z-40-pide-a-juez-federal-salir-del-aislamiento-en-carcel-de-estados-unidos-denuncia-mas-de-275-dias-en-confinamiento-solitario-y-restricciones-para-comunicarse-con-su-familia/","https://www.nmas.com.mx/nacional/seguridad/quienes-son-29-narcos-mexicanos-extraditados-estados-unidos-hoy-lista-completa/","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases/2013/02/01/high-ranking-member-mexican-los-zetas-cartel-pleads-guilty-drug","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Zetas"],"verified":true},{"id":"cdn","name":"Cártel del Noreste","aka":["CDN","Northeast Cartel","Los Zetas (Treviño faction)","Tropa del Infierno (armed wing)"],"founded":2015,"dissolved":0,"parent":"zetas","origin":{"state":"TAM","city":"Nuevo Laredo","lat":27.4764,"lng":-99.5164},"summary":"The Cártel del Noreste is the Treviño family's successor to Los Zetas, built around total control of Nuevo Laredo and the Laredo, Texas, port of entry. Known for its paramilitary Tropa del Infierno and repeated attacks on the Mexican Army, it was designated a US Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025 and, as of 2026, is led by Juan Cisneros Treviño 'El Juanito'.","history":"The Cártel del Noreste (CDN) is the Treviño family's continuation of Los Zetas in Nuevo Laredo, the busiest commercial crossing on the US-Mexico border. After Miguel Ángel \"Z-40\" (July 2013) and Omar \"Z-42\" Treviño Morales (March 2015) were captured, their nephews Juan Francisco \"El Kiko\" and Juan Gerardo \"El Huevo\" Treviño Chávez kept control of the border plaza and, in 2014-2015, began signing banners as \"Cártel del Noreste\" to distinguish themselves from old-guard Zetas who accused the family of betraying the founders; InSight Crime dates El Kiko's leadership of the splinter as early as 2012. Kiko, a US citizen, was arrested on 28 September 2016 in Baytown, near Houston; a federal jury in Waco convicted him on all seven counts on 24 July 2018, and on 8 November 2018 Judge Alia Moses sentenced him to two consecutive life terms plus 20 years and US$4 million in fines and money judgments, on evidence covering more than 250,000 kilos of cocaine and 800 firearms. El Huevo, born in 1982, took command.\n\nEl Huevo militarized the group. He built the Tropa del Infierno (\"Hell Troop\"), a uniformed assault force with armored \"monster\" trucks that fought the Army and Navy in the streets: on 4 January 2017 five Tamaulipas prosecutors' officials were executed; on 24-25 March 2018 the Tropa attacked Navy bases in Nuevo Laredo, killing a captain; and on 30 November 2019 dozens of gunmen in armored trucks stormed Villa Unión, Coahuila, attacking the town hall in a battle that, with the pursuit over the following days, left about 23 to 25 people dead. The CDN fought Gulf Cartel factions for Tamaulipas and the Zetas Vieja Escuela for Ciudad Victoria and Veracruz, and pushed into Coahuila (Piedras Negras), Nuevo León (where it repeatedly ambushed the state Fuerza Civil), Zacatecas and San Luis Potosí, although InSight Crime judges its expansion beyond Nuevo Laredo to have largely failed.\n\nAttrition and retaliation defined 2020-2022. Eight buses were burned in Nuevo Laredo on 17 March 2020 over protection payments; Tropa leader Hugo Alejandro Salcido \"El Porras\" was killed by state police on 29 March 2020; dozens of gunmen died in Army and Navy clashes through 2020; and Tropa chiefs fell in succession, \"El Werko\" in August 2021, Martín Rodríguez Barbosa \"El Cadete\" on 8 September 2021 and Heriberto Rodríguez Hernández \"El Negrolo\" on 28 November 2022. On 13 March 2022 the Army captured El Huevo in Nuevo Laredo; the CDN answered with 38 attacks on military installations and gunfire and grenades at the US consulate, which closed for nearly a month. El Huevo was expelled to Texas on 14 March and his cousin Juan Cisneros Treviño \"El Juanito\" or \"La Sombra\", also a nephew of Z-40, took over. On 26 June 2022 six Fuerza Civil officers were murdered on the Anáhuac-Colombia highway.\n\nFrom 2023 the United States became the CDN's main adversary. Nuevo Laredo plaza boss César Alejandro Silva Delgado \"El Tartas\", who had taken the plaza after El Negrolo's arrest, was captured on 29 November 2023 with two firearms and 2,000 fentanyl pills, and killed himself in a Nuevo Laredo prison on 18 July 2024 while awaiting extradition; an attack on 17 August 2024 killed two soldiers and wounded five; El Huevo pleaded guilty to 15 counts in San Antonio on 19 November 2024; and second-in-command Ricardo González Sauceda \"El Ricky\" was captured on 3 February 2025 in an operation that set off gun battles across Nuevo Laredo and closed its airport. On 20 February 2025 the State Department designated the CDN a Foreign Terrorist Organization, and a week later Z-40 and Z-42 were flown to Washington. Treasury sanctioned arms buyer Miguel Ángel de Anda Ledezma \"El Miguelón\" and El Ricky on 21 May 2025, then second-in-command Abdón Federico Rodríguez García \"Cucho\", tactical chief Francisco Daniel Esqueda Nieto \"Franky Esqueda\" (who OFAC says took over operations after El Ricky's arrest and commanded an attack on a SEDENA helicopter during it), high-ranking member Antonio Romero Sánchez and the narco-rapper Ricardo Hernández Medrano \"El Makabelico\" on 6 August 2025. Supplied with fentanyl and methamphetamine by La Mayiza, the CDN also taxed migrant smuggling and moved US guns south along Interstate 35.\n\nIn 2026 the pressure shifted to money. On 14 April 2026 OFAC blacklisted six targets: Casino Centenario in Nuevo Laredo, used as a drug stash house and torture site, the Diamante Casino in Tampico and its gambling website, their operator CAMSA, human-smuggling chief Eduardo Javier Islas Valdez \"Crosty\", defence lawyer Juan Pablo Penilla Rodríguez, who relayed messages for the imprisoned Miguel Treviño, and Jesús Reymundo Ramos Vázquez, accused of running a CDN disinformation campaign while posing as a human-rights activist. On 19 July 2026 financial operator Justo Aurelio Rivera Parrazales was arrested in Mérida; searches in Mérida and Komchén yielded 15 luxury vehicles, weapons, jewellery and cash worth 145.2 million pesos (about US$7.7 million), and in August he was bound over for trial for money laundering and drug dealing. Milenio reported in August 2026 that El Juanito still leads the structure, laundering through casinos, nightclubs and the music business. As of September 2026 the CDN retains total control of Nuevo Laredo, contests eastern and northern Zacatecas and the San Luis Potosí border, remains at war with Gulf factions, the Vieja Escuela and the CJNG, and awaits the Treviño brothers' Washington trial, tentatively set for 2028.","leaders":[{"name":"Juan Francisco Treviño Chávez","alias":"El Kiko","role":"Founder and first leader; nephew of Z-40","from":2015,"to":2016,"fate":"Arrested 28 Sept 2016 in Baytown, Texas; convicted by a Waco jury 24 Jul 2018; sentenced 8 Nov 2018 by Judge Alia Moses to two consecutive life terms plus 20 years and US$4M in fines/judgments"},{"name":"Juan Gerardo Treviño Chávez","alias":"El Huevo","role":"Leader; founder of the Tropa del Infierno","from":2016,"to":2022,"fate":"Arrested 13 Mar 2022 in Nuevo Laredo; expelled to the US 14 Mar 2022; pleaded guilty to 15 counts in San Antonio 19 Nov 2024; faces up to life, sentencing not publicly confirmed"},{"name":"Juan Cisneros Treviño","alias":"El Juanito / La Sombra","role":"Leader; commands the Tropa del Infierno","from":2022,"to":0,"fate":"At large; reported still in command in Aug 2026"},{"name":"Ricardo González Sauceda","alias":"El Ricky / Mando R","role":"Second-in-command; chief of the armed wing (Los Chukys)","from":2021,"to":2025,"fate":"Captured 3 Feb 2025 in Nuevo Laredo; OFAC-sanctioned 21 May 2025"},{"name":"Abdón Federico Rodríguez García","alias":"Cucho","role":"Second-in-command; drugs, laundering, fuel theft, extortion","from":2025,"to":0,"fate":"At large; OFAC-sanctioned 6 Aug 2025"},{"name":"Francisco Daniel Esqueda Nieto","alias":"Franky Esqueda","role":"Head of tactical operations in Nuevo Laredo","from":2025,"to":0,"fate":"At large; OFAC-sanctioned 6 Aug 2025 for attacking soldiers and a SEDENA helicopter"},{"name":"Martín Rodríguez Barbosa","alias":"El Cadete","role":"Tropa del Infierno commander; El Huevo's security chief","from":2019,"to":2021,"fate":"Arrested 8 Sep 2021"},{"name":"Miguel Ángel de Anda Ledezma","alias":"El Miguelón","role":"Firearms procurement chief (US straw purchases)","from":2022,"to":0,"fate":"At large; OFAC-sanctioned 21 May 2025"},{"name":"César Alejandro Silva Delgado","alias":"El Tartas","role":"Nuevo Laredo plaza boss","from":2022,"to":2023,"fate":"Captured 29 Nov 2023 by Army and National Guard with 2,000 fentanyl pills; killed himself in a Nuevo Laredo prison 18 Jul 2024 while awaiting extradition"},{"name":"Hugo Alejandro Salcido","alias":"El Porras / Pin Pon","role":"Tropa del Infierno cell leader","from":2019,"to":2020,"fate":"Killed in a shootout with Tamaulipas state police in Nuevo Laredo, 29 Mar 2020"}],"factions":[{"id":"tropa_del_infierno","name":"Tropa del Infierno","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Nuevo Laredo and rural Tamaulipas-Nuevo León-Coahuila border","leader":"Martín Rodríguez Barbosa 'El Cadete' (arr. Sept 2021); also 'El Werko' (arr. Aug 2021) and 'El Negrolo' (arr. Nov 2022)","note":"Uniformed assault wing created by El Huevo, with armored 'monster' trucks; notorious for clashes with the Army, Navy and CJNG from 2019"},{"id":"fuerzas_especiales_chukys","name":"Fuerzas Especiales 35 / Los Chukys","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Coahuila and Nuevo Laredo","leader":"Ricardo González Sauceda 'El Ricky' (captured Feb 2025)","note":"High-impact strike unit blamed for attacks on soldiers and Nuevo León Fuerza Civil officers"},{"id":"operativo_nuevo_leon","name":"Operativo Nuevo León","from":2018,"to":0,"area":"Anáhuac, Lampazos, Colombia crossing, Galeana (Nuevo León)","leader":"Not publicly identified","note":"Armed cell behind ambushes on Fuerza Civil, including six officers killed 26 Jun 2022; includes the 'Los Fantasmas' cell in Galeana"},{"id":"piedras_negras_plaza","name":"Piedras Negras-Acuña plaza","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Northern Coahuila border (Piedras Negras, Ciudad Acuña, Villa Unión)","leader":"Not publicly identified; Antonio Romero Sánchez sanctioned by OFAC Aug 2025 as a senior CDN figure","note":"Staging area for the 30 Nov 2019 Villa Unión assault; disputes with CJNG and Gulf cells"},{"id":"operativa_texas","name":"Operativa Texas","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Laredo and south Texas","leader":"Not publicly identified","note":"Cross-border cell handling drug distribution, gun buying and kidnappings on the US side"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2022,"to":0,"note":"Supplies fentanyl, methamphetamine and cocaine to the CDN for the Laredo corridor (InSight Crime)"},{"cartel":"other","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Cártel de Guasave, a Sinaloa-linked network, listed as an ally"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"War with the Metros, Ciclones and Escorpiones for Tamaulipas; Gulf factions agreed a truce in July 2021 to confront the CDN"},{"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Old-guard Zetas who rejected the Treviño family; fought over Ciudad Victoria and Veracruz"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Clashes in Zacatecas, Coahuila and Veracruz; CJNG expansion toward the northeast border"},{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2023,"to":0,"note":"CDN sided with La Mayiza in the Sinaloa civil war"},{"cartel":"talibanes","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Competition for the Zacatecas-San Luis Potosí corridor (Pinos and the SLP border in 2026)"},{"cartel":"government","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Repeated attacks on Army, Navy, National Guard and state police; 38 attacks on military bases after El Huevo's arrest"},{"cartel":"us_government","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"FTO/SDGT designation 20 Feb 2025; OFAC actions May 2025, Aug 2025, Apr 2026; prosecutions in Texas and DC"}],"activities":["Fentanyl, methamphetamine, cocaine, heroin and marijuana trafficking through Nuevo Laredo-Laredo","Firearms trafficking from Texas via straw purchasers (Interstate 35)","Human smuggling and taxing of migrants","Extortion of businesses and transport (derecho de piso)","Kidnapping","Fuel theft and fuel smuggling","Vehicle theft","Money laundering through casinos, nightclubs and the music industry","Retail drug sales and prostitution rackets"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2026,"states":["TAM","NLE","COA","SLP","ZAC","VER"],"note":"Undisputed control of Nuevo Laredo, a violent presence along the Coahuila and Nuevo León border strips and contested corridors into Zacatecas, San Luis Potosí and Veracruz; Mexican press has put membership near 9,000 in 2022, a figure this project could not verify against a primary source"},"facts":["Founding leader 'El Kiko' Treviño Chávez was sentenced on 8 November 2018 to two consecutive life terms plus 20 years; trial evidence covered more than 250,000 kilos of cocaine and 800 firearms.","On 30 November 2019 dozens of CDN gunmen in armored trucks attacked Villa Unión, Coahuila; about 23 to 25 people died in the assault and the pursuit that followed.","After El Huevo's arrest on 13 March 2022 the CDN launched 38 attacks on military installations and fired on the US consulate in Nuevo Laredo, which closed for nearly a month.","El Huevo pleaded guilty on 19 November 2024 to 15 federal counts (11 in the Western District of Texas, 4 in the Eastern District).","A CDN attack on 17 August 2024 killed two soldiers and wounded five; El Ricky was later charged over it.","El Ricky was captured on 3 February 2025 with a rifle, a handgun, 434 g of methamphetamine and 1,509 fentanyl pills.","The CDN was one of eight groups designated Foreign Terrorist Organizations by the US State Department on 20 February 2025.","On 14 April 2026 OFAC sanctioned six CDN-linked targets, including Casino Centenario in Nuevo Laredo, described as a fentanyl and cocaine stash house and torture site, and the cartel's defence lawyer and its disinformation operator.","On 19 July 2026 financial operator Justo Aurelio Rivera Parrazales was arrested in Mérida and assets worth 145.2 million pesos (about US$7.7 million), including 15 luxury vehicles, were seized.","Narco-rapper Ricardo Hernández Medrano 'El Makabelico' was sanctioned on 6 August 2025; Treasury said half his streaming royalties went to the CDN."],"status_2026":"Active and dominant in Nuevo Laredo under Juan Cisneros Treviño 'El Juanito', whom Milenio reported in Aug 2026 remains in command, with 'Cucho' as second-in-command and 'Franky Esqueda' running tactical operations after El Ricky's Feb 2025 capture. A US-designated FTO since 20 Feb 2025, the CDN was hit by OFAC casino and operative sanctions on 14 Apr 2026 and the July 2026 Mérida arrest of a financial operator, yet still supplies fentanyl via La Mayiza, contests eastern Zacatecas and the San Luis Potosí border and fights Gulf factions, the Vieja Escuela and the CJNG.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/northeast-cartel/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0146","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0440","https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdtx/pr/leader-cartel-del-noreste-and-nephew-los-zetas-leaders-z-40-and-z-42-receives-two","https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdtx/pr/us-attorneys-western-district-texas-and-eastern-district-texas-announce-former-cartel","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2025/02/04/estados/029n1est","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/07/21/reportan-supuesta-muerte-de-el-tartas-jefe-del-cartel-del-noreste-en-prision-de-nuevo-laredo/","https://www.elimparcial.com/mexico/2026/07/19/detienen-en-merida-a-justo-aurelio-rivera-presunto-operador-financiero-del-cartel-del-noreste-aseguran-ferrari-lamborghini-porsche-y-bienes-por-mas-de-145-millones-de-pesos/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/quien-es-juanito-trevino-lider-cartel-del-noreste-mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Villa_Uni%C3%B3n_shootout"],"verified":true},{"id":"beltran_leyva","name":"Beltrán Leyva Organization","aka":["Cártel de los Beltrán Leyva","BLO","Cártel de los Hermanos Beltrán Leyva","Los Beltranes","Meza Flores Organization / Cártel de Guasave (post-2014 core remnant)"],"founded":2008,"dissolved":0,"parent":"sinaloa","origin":{"state":"SIN","city":"Badiraguato","lat":25.3637,"lng":-107.5508},"summary":"Family-run trafficking clan from Badiraguato, Sinaloa, that ran security, corruption and Pacific cocaine logistics for the Sinaloa Federation until it broke with Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán in 2008 and fought a war that reshaped Mexico's criminal map. Decapitated by the killing of Arturo (2009) and the arrests of Alfredo (2008), Carlos (2009) and Héctor (2014), it splintered into Guerreros Unidos, Los Rojos, CIDA, the Pacífico Sur and H2 cells, and the Guasave-based network of Fausto Isidro Meza Flores, which US authorities still treat as the BLO in 2026. Washington now prosecutes that network as a faction of the terrorist-designated Sinaloa Cartel, and its second-in-command, Pedro Inzunza Noriega, was captured in December 2025 and sent to the United States a month later.","history":"The Beltrán Leyva brothers, Arturo, Alfredo, Héctor, Carlos and Mario Alberto, came from the ranch of La Palma in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, and began as opium-poppy growers before working as transporters and gunmen for Amado Carrillo Fuentes' Juárez Cartel in the 1990s. Related by kinship and marriage to Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, they joined the Sinaloa Federation that Guzmán, Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada and Juan José Esparragoza consolidated around 2002. Arturo ran the Federation's security apparatus and its bribery networks, reaching into the Federal Police, the Army and the attorney general's office, while Alfredo handled logistics in Sinaloa. Their enforcer Édgar Valdez Villarreal, 'La Barbie', led the paramilitary Los Negros against Los Zetas in Nuevo Laredo from 2003 to 2006, and the clan controlled Acapulco, Morelos, the State of Mexico corridor into Mexico City and crossings in Sonora.\n\nThe rupture came when Army special forces, acting with the attorney general's office, arrested Alfredo, 'El Mochomo', in the Burócrata district of Culiacán on 21 January 2008, seizing some US$900,000 in cash, weapons and a collection of watches. The brothers blamed Guzmán for handing him over. On the evening of 8 May 2008 gunmen killed Édgar Guzmán López, El Chapo's son, in the City Club parking lot in Culiacán in a barrage of roughly 500 rounds and a grenade; hours earlier that same day Federal Police coordinator Édgar Millán Gómez had been shot at his Mexico City home and died soon after. The BLO allied with Los Zetas and the Juárez Cartel in an anti-Sinaloa axis, and Culiacán, Sonora, Durango, Guerrero and Morelos filled with corpses. At its 2008-2009 peak the organization operated in at least eleven states plus Mexico City and moved multi-ton cocaine loads by sea from Colombia.\n\nOn 16 December 2009 some 200 Navy marines stormed the Altitude condominium complex in Cuernavaca and killed Arturo, 'El Barbas', along with six bodyguards. The only marine killed in the raid, Melquisedec Angulo Córdova, was buried on 21 December; that night gunmen murdered his mother, two siblings and an aunt at the family home in Paraíso, Tabasco. Federal Police captured Carlos in Culiacán on 30 December 2009. Héctor, 'El H', claimed the succession, but La Barbie refused to submit, and through 2010 the two factions fought in Cuernavaca, Acapulco and the State of Mexico, leaving more than 150 dead within months. Héctor rebranded his loyalists the Cártel del Pacífico Sur in April 2010 and kept Sergio Villarreal 'El Grande' as his top enforcer. Gerardo Álvarez 'El Indio' was arrested in Huixquilucan on 21 April 2010, La Barbie by federal police at a house in Lerma on 30 August 2010 and El Grande in Puebla on 12 September 2010. In Acapulco, La Barbie's orphaned lieutenants formed the Cártel Independiente de Acapulco and La Barredora.\n\nBetween 2011 and 2014 the BLO ceased to be a unified cartel. In Morelos and Guerrero its cells became Los Rojos and Guerreros Unidos, the latter responsible for the disappearance of 43 Ayotzinapa students on 26 September 2014. In northern Sinaloa, Fausto Isidro Meza Flores, 'Chapo Isidro', kept the Guasave-Los Mochis corridor for the brothers and fought the Sinaloa Cartel through the decade; in Nayarit, Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez, 'H2', ran the Pacific coast until Navy marines killed him and seven other gunmen in Tepic on 9 February 2017. Héctor lived quietly in Querétaro and San Miguel de Allende posing as an art and real-estate dealer until soldiers arrested him, alongside his financier Germán Goyenechea, at a seafood restaurant there on 1 October 2014; he died of a heart attack in Toluca on 18 November 2018. Alfredo was extradited on 15 November 2014, pleaded guilty on 23 February 2016 and was sentenced to life on 5 April 2017 in Washington, with a US$529.2 million forfeiture; La Barbie was extradited in September 2015 and received 49 years and one month in June 2018.\n\nThe brand survived in the north. The US Treasury, which had sanctioned Meza Flores in 2013 and 2021, on 6 December 2023 sanctioned fifteen people and two Mexican companies tied to the BLO and named Óscar Manuel Gastélum Iribe and Pedro Inzunza Noriega as its leaders, describing a network moving cocaine, heroin, methamphetamine and fentanyl. Testimony from El Grande and La Barbie's cooperation underpinned the February 2023 conviction of former security minister Genaro García Luna, whom the BLO had bribed for years.\n\nThe capture of El Mayo Zambada on 25 July 2024 and the Sinaloa civil war that erupted on 9 September 2024 gave the remnant new relevance. On 3 December 2024 Mexican forces seized more than a ton of fentanyl in Ahome and Guasave, the largest such seizure in the country's history, from BLO-linked cells. The FBI added Meza Flores to its Ten Most Wanted list on 4 February 2025 with a US$5 million reward, and OFAC sanctioned Alfredo's son Jesús Alfredo Beltrán Guzmán, 'El Mochomito', on 9 April 2025, calling Meza Flores the BLO's current leader; Mexican reporting, however, places Mochomito's own cell alongside Los Chapitos rather than with Chapo Isidro. In May 2025 Milenio and Infobae reported that the Guasave faction had abandoned neutrality and joined La Mayiza against Los Chapitos and their CJNG partners, with clashes in Mocorito, Guamúchil, Guasave and Navolato, though by August 2025 the same outlets reported the pact fraying and InSight Crime still found no confirmed Guasave intervention. Washington escalated instead: in May 2025 it charged Inzunza Noriega and his son Pedro Inzunza Coronel with narcoterrorism as leaders of a Sinaloa Cartel faction, the first such use of terrorism statutes against cartel figures. The son was killed by soldiers on 30 November 2025, the father captured in Culiacán on 31 December 2025 and transferred to the United States with 36 other fugitives on 21 January 2026. Operator Iván 'N', 'El 24', fell in Sinaloa on 15 June 2026. As of September 2026 the BLO exists as a federation of regional heirs rather than a cartel, with Meza Flores still at large at its centre.","leaders":[{"name":"Arturo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Barbas / El Jefe de Jefes","role":"Top leader; ran Federation security and corruption networks, then BLO chief after 2008 split","from":2008,"to":2009,"fate":"Killed by Mexican Navy marines with six bodyguards at the Altitude condominium complex, Cuernavaca, Morelos, 16 Dec 2009"},{"name":"Alfredo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Mochomo","role":"Co-founder; Sinaloa logistics and security chief","from":2008,"to":2008,"fate":"Arrested by Army special forces with the PGR in Culiacán 21 Jan 2008; extradited 15 Nov 2014; pleaded guilty 23 Feb 2016; life sentence and US$529.2 million forfeiture, D.D.C., 5 Apr 2017; held at ADX Florence since June 2022; compassionate-release plea (2021-22) unsuccessful"},{"name":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El H / El Ingeniero","role":"Leader after Arturo's death; founder of Cártel del Pacífico Sur","from":2009,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested by the Army with his financier Germán Goyenechea at a seafood restaurant in San Miguel de Allende, Guanajuato, 1 Oct 2014; held at Altiplano; died of a heart attack in Toluca 18 Nov 2018, aged 56"},{"name":"Carlos Beltrán Leyva","alias":"Carlos Gámez Orpineda (false identity used at arrest)","role":"Co-founder; lowest-profile brother, finances and Sinaloa operations","from":2008,"to":2009,"fate":"Arrested by the Federal Police in Culiacán 30 Dec 2009 carrying a forged driver's licence, a .45 pistol and a .308 rifle; prosecuted in Mexico on drug-trafficking, weapons and money-laundering charges; later status not publicly documented"},{"name":"Mario Alberto Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El General","role":"Brother and senior member","from":2008,"to":0,"fate":"Never captured; whereabouts unknown as of 2026 (the alias 'El General' is also attributed to Héctor in some sources)"},{"name":"Édgar Valdez Villarreal","alias":"La Barbie","role":"Chief of sicarios (Los Negros); led rival faction against Héctor in 2010","from":2008,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by federal police at a house in Lerma, Estado de México, 30 Aug 2010; extradited 30 Sep 2015; pleaded guilty Jan 2016 in Atlanta; sentenced 11 Jun 2018 to 49 years 1 month and US$192 million forfeiture; dropped from BOP records Nov 2022 as a prospective García Luna witness and returned to BOP rolls in early 2023; BOP lists a release date of 27 Jul 2056"},{"name":"Sergio Villarreal Barragán","alias":"El Grande","role":"Lieutenant and enforcer for Arturo, then Héctor; finances and cocaine","from":2008,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by marines in Puebla 12 Sep 2010; extradited to the US in 2012; pleaded guilty and cooperated; released as a protected witness c. Dec 2019; testified against Genaro García Luna Jan 2023"},{"name":"Gerardo Álvarez Vázquez","alias":"El Indio","role":"Plaza chief, Estado de México / Morelos / Guerrero","from":2008,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by Federal Police in Huixquilucan, Estado de México, 21 Apr 2010"},{"name":"Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez","alias":"H2","role":"BLO chief in Nayarit and coastal Jalisco","from":2010,"to":2017,"fate":"Killed with seven other gunmen by Navy marines, with helicopter support, in Tepic, Nayarit, 9 Feb 2017"},{"name":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores","alias":"El Chapo Isidro","role":"Leader of the Guasave-based BLO remnant (Los Mazatlecos / Cártel de Guasave); named by US Treasury as the BLO's current leader","from":2010,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive as of Sept 2026; OFAC-designated 17 Jan 2013 (again 2021 and 2023); indicted in the US; added to the FBI Ten Most Wanted list 4 Feb 2025 with a US$5 million State Department reward"},{"name":"Pedro Inzunza Noriega","alias":"Sagitario / El Señor de la Silla","role":"Second-in-command under Meza Flores per US and Mexican authorities; fentanyl production and maritime cocaine trafficking","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"OFAC-sanctioned 6 Dec 2023; charged in the Southern District of California in May 2025 with narcoterrorism and material support of terrorism, the first such charges against cartel figures; captured by the military in Culiacán 31 Dec 2025; transferred to the United States 21 Jan 2026"},{"name":"Pedro Inzunza Coronel","alias":"El Pichón","role":"Son of Inzunza Noriega; co-leader of the faction's fentanyl network","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"Indicted with his father in May 2025 on narcoterrorism charges; killed in a clash with the Mexican military 30 Nov 2025"},{"name":"Óscar Manuel Gastélum Iribe","alias":"El Músico","role":"Co-leader per US Treasury; oversees South America-to-US drug transport","from":2023,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned 6 Dec 2023 along with a Guadalajara equestrian publisher he controlled"},{"name":"Juan Pablo Bastidas Erenas","alias":"Payo","role":"High-ranking lieutenant of the Beltrán Leyva faction","from":2020,"to":2025,"fate":"Captured 28 May 2025; transferred to the United States 21 Jan 2026 on cocaine, fentanyl and methamphetamine trafficking, money laundering, weapons and kidnapping charges"},{"name":"Jesús Alfredo Beltrán Guzmán","alias":"El Mochomito / El Tito","role":"Son of Alfredo; heads a cell in the Golden Triangle sanctioned by OFAC as BLO, though Mexican reporting places him fighting alongside Los Chapitos","from":2021,"to":0,"fate":"Arrested in Zapopan, Jalisco, in 2016 and released in 2021; a reported detention at Mojolo, Culiacán, on 29 Nov 2024 was denied by Sinaloa authorities; OFAC-sanctioned 9 Apr 2025; at large"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_negros","name":"Los Negros","from":2003,"to":2010,"area":"Nuevo Laredo, Acapulco, Morelos, Estado de México","leader":"Édgar Valdez Villarreal 'La Barbie'","note":"Paramilitary enforcement wing built to fight Los Zetas; became La Barbie's faction in the 2010 succession war and seed of CIDA"},{"id":"pacifico_sur","name":"Cártel del Pacífico Sur","from":2010,"to":2014,"area":"Morelos, Guerrero, Estado de México","leader":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva","note":"Héctor's rebranded loyalist wing; see separate profile"},{"id":"los_mazatlecos","name":"Los Mazatlecos / Cártel de Guasave","from":2008,"to":0,"area":"Guasave, Ahome, Los Mochis, Mazatlán (Sinaloa); southern Sonora; Nayarit; Baja California Sur","leader":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores 'El Chapo Isidro'","note":"Armed wing that stayed loyal to the brothers and the BLO's main heir; Mexican press reported it joining La Mayiza in May 2025, though InSight Crime found no confirmed intervention as of June 2026"},{"id":"inzunza_network","name":"Inzunza fentanyl network","from":2020,"to":2026,"area":"Guasave, Ahome, Culiacán (Sinaloa)","leader":"Pedro Inzunza Noriega 'Sagitario' and Pedro Inzunza Coronel 'El Pichón'","note":"Production arm implicated in the record December 2024 fentanyl seizure; the son killed 30 Nov 2025, the father captured 31 Dec 2025 and transferred to the US 21 Jan 2026"},{"id":"h2_nayarit","name":"H2 faction","from":2010,"to":2017,"area":"Tepic, Bahía de Banderas, Nayarit and coastal Jalisco","leader":"Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez 'H2'","note":"Collapsed after H2 was killed in February 2017; territory taken by CJNG and the Sinaloa Cartel"},{"id":"cida","name":"Cártel Independiente de Acapulco","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Acapulco","leader":"Moisés Montero Álvarez 'El Koreano' (founder)","note":"Formed by La Barbie's Acapulco lieutenants; see separate profile"},{"id":"la_barredora_acapulco","name":"La Barredora (Acapulco)","from":2010,"to":2012,"area":"Acapulco","leader":"Eder Jair Sosa Carvajal 'El Cremas'; Christian Hernández Tarín 'El Chris'","note":"Split from CIDA within weeks in 2010 and fought it, reportedly with Sinaloa backing; distinct from the Tabasco group of the same name"},{"id":"guerreros_unidos","name":"Guerreros Unidos","from":2011,"to":0,"area":"Iguala and northern Guerrero, Morelos, Estado de México; Chicago distribution","leader":"Mario Casarrubias Salgado 'El Sapo Guapo' (arrested May 2014)","note":"Heroin-trafficking splinter responsible for the Ayotzinapa disappearances of 26 Sep 2014"},{"id":"los_rojos","name":"Los Rojos","from":2010,"to":2022,"area":"Morelos, central Guerrero, Puebla","leader":"Santiago Mazari Hernández 'El Carrete' (arrested 1 Aug 2019)","note":"Named for Jesús Nava Romero 'El Rojo'; fought Guerreros Unidos and Los Ardillos; dismantled by 2022, and Mazari sentenced to 105 years in July 2026"},{"id":"la_oficina_bcs","name":"La Oficina","from":2014,"to":0,"area":"Baja California Sur","leader":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores (attributed)","note":"Multi-cartel cell in BCS linked by InSight Crime to Chapo Isidro"},{"id":"mochomito_cell","name":"Beltrán Guzmán cell","from":2021,"to":0,"area":"Golden Triangle (Sinaloa, Chihuahua, Durango)","leader":"Jesús Alfredo Beltrán Guzmán 'El Mochomito'","note":"Sanctioned by OFAC 9 Apr 2025 as BLO, but reported by Mexican media to fight for Los Chapitos against La Mayiza, placing it opposite the Guasave faction"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2002,"to":2008,"note":"Core member of the Sinaloa Federation; provided security and corruption networks until the January 2008 rupture"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2008,"to":2012,"note":"Anti-Sinaloa alliance after the split; Zetas supplied gunmen in Sinaloa, Sonora, Guerrero and Morelos"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2008,"to":2010,"note":"Alliance via the Gulf-Zetas bloc until the Gulf-Zetas split of early 2010"},{"cartel":"juarez","from":2008,"to":2014,"note":"Anti-Sinaloa axis with Carrillo Fuentes; renewed tactical alignment in 2014 per InSight Crime"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2014,"to":2015,"note":"Temporary alignment of BLO remnants with CJNG against Sinaloa, per InSight Crime"},{"cartel":"caborca","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Reported Caro Quintero-family logistics ties and a Badiraguato meeting with Chapo Isidro and Mayito Flaco in Sept 2024; sourcing is Mexican press rather than official documents"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"Milenio and Infobae reported the Guasave faction joining La Mayiza in May 2025 against Los Chapitos; Infobae reported the pact fraying by August 2025 and InSight Crime found no confirmed intervention as of June 2026"},{"cartel":"cdn","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Reported cooperation between Los Mazatlecos and Cártel del Noreste (Wikipedia; weakly sourced)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2008,"to":2024,"note":"The 2008 split war; Chapo Isidro's cells fought Sinaloa in Guasave, Los Mochis and southern Sonora through the 2010s"},{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Principal enemy of the Guasave faction in the Sinaloa civil war; the separate Mochomito cell is reported to fight on the Chapitos side"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"CJNG displaced BLO heirs in Nayarit after 2017 and backs Los Chapitos since 2024"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2008,"to":2011,"note":"Fought La Familia for Guerrero and the State of Mexico"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Enemy after siding with Los Zetas in the 2010 Gulf-Zetas war"},{"cartel":"government","from":2008,"to":0,"note":"Navy killed Arturo 2009; Army arrested Alfredo 2008 and Héctor 2014; marines killed H2 2017; record fentanyl seizure Dec 2024; Inzunza Coronel killed Nov 2025 and Inzunza Noriega captured Dec 2025"},{"cartel":"us_government","from":2008,"to":0,"note":"OFAC designations 2013-2025, extraditions of Alfredo and La Barbie, FBI Ten Most Wanted listing 2025 and the first cartel narcoterrorism charges in May 2025"}],"activities":["Cocaine trafficking from Colombia by sea and through Central America","Heroin (Guerrero and Sinaloa poppy) to the US East Coast and Midwest","Methamphetamine production and trafficking","Fentanyl production and trafficking (record 1-ton seizure Dec 2024 tied to BLO cells)","Marijuana","Arms and ammunition procurement","Bribery of federal police, military and prosecutors (García Luna case)","Kidnapping and extortion","Money laundering through front companies (a Culiacán pharmacy and a Guadalajara equestrian publisher sanctioned in 2023)"],"peak":{"from":2008,"to":2009,"states":["SIN","SON","DUR","NAY","JAL","GRO","MOR","MEX","CMX","QUE","PUE","NLE","TAM","ROO","CHP"],"note":"InSight Crime counts eleven states plus Mexico City at the peak; the wider list here adds secondary presence documented in later trafficking and sanctions records, including Nayarit and Durango as core poppy and transit zones. The organization commanded thousands of gunmen and a bribery network reaching the top of the Federal Police."},"facts":["Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed on 16 Dec 2009 by roughly 200 Navy marines at the Altitude complex in Cuernavaca; the only marine killed, Melquisedec Angulo Córdova, was buried on 21 December and his mother, two siblings and an aunt were murdered at their home in Paraíso, Tabasco, that same night.","On 8 May 2008 gunmen killed El Chapo's son Édgar Guzmán López in a Culiacán parking lot in a barrage of some 500 rounds; the killing is generally attributed to the Beltrán Leyva brothers, though Vicente Zambada's later account described it as a mistaken hit. Federal Police coordinator Édgar Millán was shot dead in Mexico City hours earlier the same day.","Alfredo 'El Mochomo' was sentenced to life without parole in Washington on 5 Apr 2017 with a US$529.2 million forfeiture order, an amount the court called an extremely conservative estimate of his cocaine proceeds.","La Barbie received 49 years and one month and a US$192 million forfeiture on 11 Jun 2018; in Nov 2022 he vanished from Bureau of Prisons rolls as a prospective witness against Genaro García Luna, returning to BOP records in early 2023.","On 6 Dec 2023 OFAC sanctioned 15 individuals and 2 Mexican companies tied to the BLO and named Óscar Manuel Gastélum Iribe and Pedro Inzunza Noriega as its leaders.","On 3 Dec 2024 Mexican forces seized more than 1,000 kg of fentanyl across two sites in Ahome and Guasave, about 20 million doses and an estimated US$400 million loss to the traffickers, the largest fentanyl seizure in Mexico's history.","On 4 Feb 2025 Meza Flores became the 533rd fugitive added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list, with a US$5 million State Department reward.","In May 2025 Pedro Inzunza Noriega and his son became the first cartel figures charged under US terrorism statutes; the son was killed on 30 Nov 2025 and the father was captured on 31 Dec 2025 and sent to the United States on 21 Jan 2026 among 37 transferred fugitives.","The BLO's splinter Guerreros Unidos disappeared 43 Ayotzinapa students on 26 Sep 2014.","The BLO was not itself among the six Mexican groups designated Foreign Terrorist Organizations on 20 Feb 2025, but the Justice Department prosecutes it as a faction of the designated Sinaloa Cartel, which is what made the 2025 narcoterrorism charges possible."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the BLO no longer exists as a unified cartel, but the US Treasury and the FBI treat the Guasave-based network of Fausto Isidro Meza Flores, still a fugitive, as its continuation, and the Justice Department prosecutes it as a faction of the terrorist-designated Sinaloa Cartel. Mexican outlets reported the faction joining La Mayiza against Los Chapitos and CJNG from May 2025 and described Chapo Isidro as the conflict's quiet winner in late 2025 and January 2026, but the alliance was reported fraying by August 2025 and InSight Crime's June 2026 assessment still found no confirmed Guasave intervention in the war. The network lost its production arm in a year of raids: Pedro Inzunza Coronel was killed on 30 November 2025, his father Pedro Inzunza Noriega captured on 31 December 2025 and transferred to the United States on 21 January 2026, and operator 'El 24' arrested on 15 June 2026. Other heirs persist as independent groups: Guerreros Unidos in Guerrero and Morelos, El Mochomito's Golden Triangle cell (sanctioned April 2025 but fighting on the Chapitos side) and CIDA in Acapulco, while Los Rojos (dismantled 2022, their leader sentenced to 105 years in July 2026) and the Nayarit H2 faction (destroyed 2017) are gone. InSight Crime's BLO profile, updated 5 June 2026, calls the organization defunct and its splinters regional rather than top-tier threats.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/beltran-leyva-organization-profile/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/fausto-isidro-meza-flores-chapo-isidro/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1952","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0076","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/37-mexican-nationals-wanted-serious-crimes-transferred-united-states-mexico-including","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/sinaloa-cartel-leaders-charged-narco-terrorism-material-support-terrorism-and-drug","https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/alfredo-beltran-leyva-sentenced-life-prison-leading-international-drug-trafficking-conspiracy","https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndga/pr/mexican-cartel-leader-edgar-valdez-villareal-aka-la-barbie-sentenced-federal-prison","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/pedro-inzunza-arrested/","https://www.state.gov/fausto-isidro-meza-flores","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/12/5/mexico-announces-largest-fentanyl-seizure-in-its-history-amid-us-pressure","https://www.milenio.com/policia/la-mayiza-y-el-cartel-de-guasave-se-unen-contra-los-chapitos","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/11/04/quien-es-el-chapo-isidro-el-lider-del-cartel-de-guasave-que-se-perfila-a-ganar-la-guerra-en-sinaloa-segun-anabel-hernandez/","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna34531363","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2010/01/03/politica/004n1pol"],"verified":true},{"id":"milenio","name":"Milenio Cartel","aka":["Cártel del Milenio","Cártel de los Valencia","Los Valencia","Los Reyes del Aguacate"],"founded":1999,"dissolved":2010,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"MIC","city":"Uruapan","lat":19.4208,"lng":-102.0628},"summary":"The Milenio Cartel (Cártel de los Valencia) was the Valencia family's Michoacán-born trafficking organization, which grew from avocado-country marijuana and poppy growers into a major cocaine and methamphetamine wholesaler operating as a branch of the Sinaloa Federation. Broken by the Zetas' invasion of Michoacán, the 15 August 2003 arrest of Armando Valencia Cornelio and the 2009-2010 loss of its Guadalajara leadership and its patron Ignacio Coronel, it fractured in 2010 into La Resistencia and the faction, Los Torcidos, that became the CJNG.","history":"The Valencia family were avocado growers from Uruapan, in the highlands of Michoacán, who by the 1970s were also planting marijuana and opium poppy in the sierra straddling Michoacán and Jalisco. Under Armando Valencia Cornelio, alias \"El Maradona\" or \"El Juanito\", who took charge in the early 1990s, the clan moved into cocaine, buying directly from Colombian suppliers and landing multi-ton loads at Manzanillo and Lázaro Cárdenas, and into methamphetamine cooked in Michoacán and Jalisco. Their avocado packing and export businesses gave them cover and a laundering channel, earning the nickname \"Los Reyes del Aguacate\". Federal prosecutors began calling the network the Cártel del Milenio around 1999-2000, as it consolidated control of Michoacán, Jalisco, Colima and Nayarit and fed distribution cells in California, Texas and Illinois.\n\nThe Valencias' rise provoked their downfall. Around 2001 a former associate, Carlos Rosales Mendoza \"El Tísico\", aligned with the Gulf Cartel and brought its paramilitary arm, Los Zetas, into Michoacán to wrest away the Tierra Caliente and Uruapan. The ensuing war was ferocious, and the Valencias sought shelter inside the Sinaloa Federation, answering to Ignacio \"Nacho\" Coronel in Guadalajara. On 15 August 2003 Army special forces (GAFE), working with the PGR, captured Armando Valencia at Tlajomulco de Zúñiga in the Guadalajara metropolitan area; a Mexican court gave him 47 years and six months at the Altiplano, but he was later extradited to the United States, sentenced there to 19 years and seven months, and released on 21 April 2020 from the federal medical prison in Lexington, Kentucky, with terminal lymphatic cancer. Leadership passed to Luis Valencia Valencia and to Armando's nephew Óscar Orlando Nava Valencia, \"El Lobo\", and the organization's center of gravity shifted to Zapopan and Tlajomulco. Michoacán was largely lost, first to the Zetas and then to La Familia Michoacana, which announced itself in late 2006 after its gunmen rolled five severed heads onto an Uruapan dance floor that September.\n\nIn its Guadalajara phase the Milenio was less an independent cartel than the Valencia branch of Coronel's Pacific operation. El Lobo and his brother Juan Carlos, \"El Tigre\", coordinated cocaine shipments from South and Central America through Manzanillo; El Lobo later testified in Brooklyn that he watched Arturo Beltrán Leyva hand Genaro García Luna $2.5 million in 2006 and personally paid the security secretary 3 million pesos at a Guadalajara car wash in 2008, putting his total payments to García Luna at some $10 million. Money passed through Los Cuinis, the eighteen González Valencia siblings from Aguililla led by Abigael \"El Cuini\", whose eldest sister Rosalinda had been married to Armando Valencia Cornelio and, after their separation, married a rising Aguililla-born gunman and former Jalisco municipal policeman, Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes, \"El Mencho\", in 1996. The 2008 rupture between Sinaloa and the Beltrán Leyvas exposed Coronel's flank, and the blows came fast: El Lobo was captured after a firefight in Tlajomulco on 28 October 2009; El Tigre was taken with a sister in Guadalajara's Providencia district on 6 May 2010; and on 29 July 2010 the Army killed Coronel in Zapopan.\n\nWithout a patron, the Milenio split. Ramiro Pozos González \"El Molca\", with Elpidio Mojarro and Víctor Torres \"El Papirrín\", founded La Resistencia in June 2010, and it allied with La Familia and, from September 2011, with Los Zetas. The rival faction that La Resistencia derided as \"Los Torcidos\" (the twisted ones) gathered around El Mencho, Erick Valencia Salazar \"El 85\" and Martín Arzola \"El 53\" and became the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación. The Guadalajara war of 2010-2012 went the CJNG's way: the Zeta-Resistencia side dumped 26 bodies in three trucks on a Guadalajara avenue by the Arcos del Milenio on 24 November 2011, the CJNG answered with blockades and executions, and El Molca was arrested by the Federal Police in Metepec on 11 September 2012 (sentenced in 2021 to 28 years). El Lobo, extradited on 29 January 2011, received 25 years in Houston on 7 January 2014, had the term cut to 200 months for cooperation, and walked free on 27 November 2023.\n\nThe Milenio Cartel ceased to exist as a unified organization in 2010, but its bloodlines still shape Mexican crime. Los Cuinis became the CJNG's financial spine: Abigael was arrested in 2015 and handed to US authorities on 12 August 2025, Gerardo drew a life sentence in July 2023, and Rosalinda, sentenced to five years for money laundering in December 2023, was released early on 27 February 2025 after more than three years inside. And on 22 February 2026, when the Army killed El Mencho at Tapalpa, command of the CJNG passed to Juan Carlos Valencia González, \"El 03\", the Santa Ana, California-born son of Armando Valencia Cornelio and Rosalinda González Valencia and stepson of El Mencho. By September 2026 he carried a $25 million US bounty, the largest ever placed on a Mexican trafficker; the Milenio name survives only as a lineage, but the Valencia dynasty once again sits atop Mexico's most expansive cartel.","leaders":[{"name":"Armando Valencia Cornelio","alias":"El Maradona / El Juanito","role":"Founder and boss","from":1990,"to":2003,"fate":"Arrested 15 Aug 2003 at Tlajomulco de Zúñiga (Guadalajara metro) by Army special forces (GAFE) with the PGR; sentenced in Mexico to 47 years 6 months at the Altiplano; later extradited to the United States and sentenced to 19 years 7 months; released 21 Apr 2020 from the Federal Medical Center, Lexington, Kentucky, with terminal lymphatic cancer. Father of CJNG leader Juan Carlos Valencia González."},{"name":"Luis Valencia Valencia","alias":"","role":"Co-leader after 2003","from":2003,"to":2009,"fate":"Never captured; dropped from view after the 2009-2010 collapse, whereabouts unknown."},{"name":"Óscar Orlando Nava Valencia","alias":"El Lobo","role":"Boss (nephew of Armando), cocaine logistics via Manzanillo","from":2003,"to":2009,"fate":"Captured after a firefight in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga 28 Oct 2009; extradited 29 Jan 2011; sentenced to 25 years in Houston (S.D. Texas) 7 Jan 2014, reduced to 200 months for cooperation; testified against Genaro García Luna Jan 2023; released 27 Nov 2023 after about 12 years and entered witness protection in the US."},{"name":"Juan Carlos Nava Valencia","alias":"El Tigre","role":"Boss after his brother's capture","from":2009,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested 6 May 2010 with his sister Jaqueline Patricia in Guadalajara's Providencia district by military forces; later held at the federal prison in Matamoros, Tamaulipas."},{"name":"Ignacio Coronel Villarreal","alias":"Nacho Coronel / El Rey del Cristal","role":"Sinaloa Federation overseer of the Valencia network","from":2003,"to":2010,"fate":"Killed by the Army in Zapopan, Jalisco, 29 Jul 2010; his death triggered the Milenio split."},{"name":"Abigael González Valencia","alias":"El Cuini","role":"Financier and cocaine broker; head of the González Valencia clan (Los Cuinis)","from":1995,"to":2010,"fate":"Carried Los Cuinis into the CJNG; arrested Puerto Vallarta 28 Feb 2015; transferred to US custody 12 Aug 2025; pleaded not guilty in Washington, DC, and by late 2025 was reported to be negotiating a plea agreement and cooperating."},{"name":"Nemesio Rubén Oseguera Cervantes","alias":"El Mencho","role":"Gunman and plaza chief on the Jalisco coast for the Valencias and Coronel","from":1998,"to":2010,"fate":"Married Rosalinda González Valencia in 1996; founded the CJNG in 2010; killed by Army special forces at Tapalpa, Jalisco, 22 Feb 2026."},{"name":"Erick Valencia Salazar","alias":"El 85","role":"Lieutenant, Guadalajara","from":2005,"to":2010,"fate":"Co-founded CJNG; arrested in Zapopan in March 2012 (reported 9 Mar); freed 29 Dec 2017; joined and later led the rival Nueva Plaza; rearrested Tapalpa 4 Sep 2022; sent to the US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded guilty in Washington, DC, 7 Apr 2026, with sentencing set before Chief Judge James Boasberg for 31 Jul 2026."},{"name":"Ramiro Pozos González","alias":"El Molca","role":"Transporter and gunman; later founder of La Resistencia","from":1995,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by the Federal Police in Metepec, Estado de México, 11 Sep 2012; sentenced in 2021 to 28 years (Altiplano)."}],"factions":[{"id":"la_resistencia","name":"La Resistencia","from":2010,"to":2013,"area":"Guadalajara metro, southern Jalisco, Michoacán border","leader":"Ramiro Pozos González 'El Molca'","note":"Anti-Sinaloa splinter formed in June 2010 after Coronel's death; allied with La Familia and from Sept 2011 with Los Zetas; lost the Guadalajara war to the CJNG and faded after El Molca's 11 Sept 2012 arrest, remnants drifting into Michoacán coalitions."},{"id":"los_torcidos","name":"Los Torcidos","from":2010,"to":2011,"area":"Guadalajara metro (Zapopan, Tlajomulco), Puerto Vallarta and the Jalisco coast","leader":"Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes 'El Mencho', with Erick Valencia 'El 85' and Martín Arzola 'El 53'","note":"Name given by La Resistencia to the pro-Sinaloa faction; re-emerged publicly in 2011 as Los Matazetas and the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación."},{"id":"los_cuinis","name":"Los Cuinis","from":2003,"to":2010,"area":"Aguililla (Michoacán), Guadalajara, international finance","leader":"Abigael González Valencia 'El Cuini'","note":"The 18-sibling González Valencia clan, children of Abigael González Mendoza and Estela Valencia Farías, that handled the Valencias' cocaine purchasing and money laundering; migrated intact into the CJNG as its financial arm."}],"allies":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":1999,"to":2010,"note":"Long-standing partner of the Sinaloa network; after the Zetas' 2001 incursion the Valencias formally operated as a branch of the Federation under Ignacio Coronel in Guadalajara."}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2001,"to":2007,"note":"Gulf boss Osiel Cárdenas backed ex-Valencia associate Carlos Rosales Mendoza and sent Los Zetas into Michoacán to seize the Valencias' Tierra Caliente and Uruapan turf."},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2001,"to":2010,"note":"Zetas' war against the Valencias in Michoacán (2001-2006) and later, allied to La Resistencia, in Guadalajara (2010-2012)."},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2006,"to":2010,"note":"La Familia, born from the anti-Zeta backlash in Michoacán, displaced the Valencias from their home state and later backed La Resistencia against Los Torcidos."},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2010,"note":"After the 2008 Sinaloa split the Beltrán Leyva organization targeted Ignacio Coronel's network, of which the Milenio was part."}],"activities":["Cocaine transshipment from Colombia via Manzanillo and Lázaro Cárdenas to the US","Methamphetamine production in Jalisco and Michoacán","Marijuana and opium poppy cultivation (early period)","Money laundering through avocado growing, packing and export businesses","Bribery of federal security officials"],"peak":{"from":1999,"to":2003,"states":["MIC","JAL","COL","NAY"],"note":"Around the turn of the millennium the Valencias controlled Uruapan and Michoacán's Tierra Caliente, the port of Manzanillo and Guadalajara-area meth labs, importing multi-ton cocaine loads directly from Colombia; the Zetas' incursion (2001) and Armando Valencia's arrest (Aug 2003) ended the apex."},"facts":["The Valencias were avocado growers from Uruapan, Michoacán, nicknamed 'Los Reyes del Aguacate' for laundering drug profits through avocado production and export.","In nine months (28 Oct 2009 to 29 Jul 2010) the cartel lost bosses Óscar and Juan Carlos Nava Valencia and its Sinaloa patron Ignacio Coronel, precipitating the 2010 split that produced the CJNG.","Founder Armando Valencia Cornelio was sentenced in Mexico to 47 years and 6 months, was later extradited to the United States and resentenced to 19 years and 7 months, and was released on 21 April 2020 from the Federal Medical Center in Lexington, Kentucky, with terminal lymphatic cancer.","Óscar Nava Valencia 'El Lobo' was sentenced to 25 years in Houston on 7 January 2014, had the term cut to 200 months for cooperation, and was released on 27 November 2023 after about 12 years in custody.","At Genaro García Luna's 2023 Brooklyn trial El Lobo testified he saw Arturo Beltrán Leyva hand the security chief $2.5 million in 2006 and personally paid him 3 million pesos at a Guadalajara car wash in 2008, part of some $10 million he said he paid in all.","La Resistencia founder Ramiro Pozos 'El Molca' was blamed for the 26 bodies left in three trucks by Guadalajara's Arcos del Milenio on 24 November 2011 and was sentenced in 2021 to 28 years.","The arrest of ex-Milenio lieutenant Erick Valencia 'El 85' in Zapopan in March 2012 triggered more than two dozen narcobloqueos across Guadalajara.","The González Valencia clan (Los Cuinis) comprised 18 siblings; a DEA official in 2015 called Los Cuinis 'the richest drug trafficking organization in the world'.","Founder Armando Valencia Cornelio's son, Juan Carlos Valencia González 'El 03', took command of the CJNG in February 2026 and on 5 August 2026 was given a $25 million US bounty, up from $5 million and the largest ever for a Mexican trafficker."],"status_2026":"Defunct as an organization since its 2010 fragmentation; no group operates under the Milenio name in 2026. Its lineages endure: Los Cuinis remain the CJNG's financial arm (Abigael González Valencia has awaited trial in Washington, DC, since his 12 August 2025 transfer), La Resistencia founder Ramiro Pozos is serving 28 years, Óscar Nava Valencia has been free in the United States since November 2023, Erick Valencia 'El 85' pleaded guilty in Washington on 7 April 2026, and Armando Valencia Cornelio's son Juan Carlos Valencia González has led the CJNG since El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milenio_Cartel","https://insightcrime.org/news/brief/mexico-captures-leader-founder-of-resistencia-gang/","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2023/12/oscar-nava-valencia-el-lobo-released.html","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2020/06/armando-valencia-cornelio-founder-of.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/04/09/cual-fue-la-enfermedad-terminal-que-devolvio-la-libertad-a-armando-valencia-cornelio-fundador-del-cartel-del-milenio/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/quien-es-armando-valencia-cornelio-fundador-cartel-milenio","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2021/12/08/ramiro-pozos-el-molca-el-lider-criminal-de-la-resistencia-fue-sentenciado-a-28-anos-de-prision/","https://www.informador.mx/Jalisco/Capturan-a-lider-del-cartel-de-Los-Valencia-20100510-0014.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Carlos_Valencia_Gonz%C3%A1lez","https://www.state.gov/juan-carlos-valencia-gonzalez","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/26-fugitives-wanted-violent-and-serious-crimes-returned-united-states-mexico-including-0"],"verified":true},{"id":"cjng","name":"Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación (CJNG)","aka":["Jalisco New Generation Cartel","Los Matazetas","Los Torcidos","Cártel de las Cuatro Letras"],"founded":2010,"dissolved":0,"parent":"milenio","origin":{"state":"JAL","city":"Guadalajara","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496},"summary":"The Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación emerged in Guadalajara in 2010 from the ruins of the Milenio Cartel and, under Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera Cervantes, became Mexico's most territorially expansive and militarized criminal organization, trafficking methamphetamine, fentanyl and cocaine while running extortion, fuel-theft and fraud rackets across most of the country. El Mencho was killed by the Army at Tapalpa, Jalisco, on 22 February 2026, triggering nationwide retaliation; his stepson Juan Carlos Valencia González, 'El 03', now leads a group designated a terrorist organization by the United States, Canada and Argentina.","history":"The CJNG rose from the collapse of the Milenio Cartel, the Valencia-family organization from Uruapan, Michoacán, that had relocated to Guadalajara as a branch of the Sinaloa Federation under Ignacio \"Nacho\" Coronel. The arrests of Óscar Nava Valencia \"El Lobo\" (28 October 2009) and his brother Juan Carlos (6 May 2010), and the Army's killing of Coronel in Zapopan on 29 July 2010, split the remnants. Ramiro Pozos \"El Molca\" formed La Resistencia; the faction he branded \"Los Torcidos\" gathered around Nemesio Rubén Oseguera Cervantes, \"El Mencho\" (born Aguililla, Michoacán, 17 July 1966), a onetime California heroin dealer jailed in the United States in the early 1990s and later a municipal policeman in Cabo Corrientes and Tomatlán, Jalisco. Husband since 1996 of Rosalinda González Valencia, he leaned on her brothers' financial network, Los Cuinis, and on operators Erick Valencia \"El 85\" and Martín Arzola \"El 53\".\n\nThe group debuted as \"Los Matazetas\", dumping 35 bodies on an underpass in Boca del Río, Veracruz, on 20 September 2011. It won the 2010-2012 Guadalajara war against La Resistencia and its Zeta allies, absorbed El 85's arrest in Zapopan in March 2012, and by 2013-2014 had broken with Sinaloa, pushing into Tijuana, Colima and Michoacán, where it bankrolled self-defense militias against the Knights Templar. In 2015 it killed 15 state police in an ambush at San Sebastián del Oeste (6 April), was designated under the Kingpin Act with Los Cuinis (8 April), and on 1 May shot down an Army helicopter near Villa Purificación, killing nine security personnel, amid blockades across Jalisco. Abigael \"El Cuini\" González Valencia had been arrested in Puerto Vallarta on 28 February 2015; El Mencho's son Rubén, \"El Menchito\", arrested that June, was extradited in February 2020.\n\nFrom 2016 the CJNG became Mexico's fastest-expanding cartel, with federal and analyst assessments by 2020-2022 placing it in a large majority of Mexico's 32 states. It fought the Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel in Guanajuato from 2017 (José Antonio Yépez \"El Marro\" fell on 2 August 2020, yet Guanajuato stayed Mexico's deadliest state), Cárteles Unidos in Michoacán's Tierra Caliente, and Sinaloa in Zacatecas, Baja California, Sonora and Chiapas. A Guadalajara faction under Carlos Enrique Sánchez Martínez \"El Cholo\" defected as Nueva Plaza in 2017 with Sinaloa backing. Washington raised El Mencho's bounty to $10 million on 16 October 2018 and to $15 million on 4 December 2024. On 26 June 2020 CJNG gunmen ambushed Mexico City security secretary Omar García Harfuch in Lomas de Chapultepec, wounding him and killing three; weeks later the cartel paraded its armored Grupo Élite on video.\n\nThe 2020s brought fentanyl and industrial methamphetamine, timeshare fraud in Puerto Vallarta, fuel theft, and a 2022 war in Colima against the breakaway Los Mezcales. Rosalinda González Valencia was jailed in November 2021 (five years; freed 27 February 2025); Antonio \"Tony Montana\" Oseguera was captured on 20 December 2022 at Tlajomulco de Zúñiga and, with El 85, flown to the United States on 27 February 2025 among 29 prisoners. Washington designated the CJNG a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025; on 5 March searchers exposed the Rancho Izaguirre recruitment camp at Teuchitlán; on 7 March El Menchito received life plus 30 years. In May 2025 the CJNG allied with Los Chapitos in Sinaloa's civil war; El Cuini was transferred to US custody on 12 August 2025; and on 1 November 2025 a CJNG-linked cell, Los Rs, assassinated Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo, triggering the federal Plan Michoacán.\n\nBefore dawn on 22 February 2026, Army and National Guard special forces guided by US intelligence raided a compound in the Tapalpa country club, about 100 km southwest of Guadalajara, after tracking an associate of one of El Mencho's partners. El Mencho, who fled with two bodyguards into wooded ground, was shot in the firefight and died, aged 59, aboard the helicopter flying him to Mexico City; four gunmen died at the scene, his two bodyguards died in transit with him, three soldiers were wounded and two men were arrested. Within hours the cartel unleashed the largest coordinated retaliation in Mexican memory: 252 roadblocks in 20 states, 25 National Guard members killed (six in Zapopan alone), a car bomb in San Juan de los Lagos that killed Army captain Leonel Cardoso Gómez, 23 inmates freed from the Puerto Vallarta prison, more than 200 Oxxo stores and 50 Banco del Bienestar branches attacked, and some 400,000 businesses shut at a cost of 1.5-2 billion pesos. DNA confirmed the body on 23 February; he was buried in Zapopan on 3 March.\n\nCommand passed to his stepson Juan Carlos Valencia González, \"El 03\" or \"El Pelón\", born in Santa Ana, California, on 12 September 1984 to Milenio founder Armando Valencia Cornelio and Rosalinda González Valencia, and founder of Grupo Élite in 2019. The Wall Street Journal reported his takeover on 18 March, and in July the US National Counterterrorism Center's antiterrorism guide named him leader. Rival heir Audias Flores Silva \"El Jardinero\" was captured without a shot fired by more than 500 Navy troops at El Mirador, Nayarit, north of Puerto Vallarta, on 27 April, found in a drainage ditch; Ricardo Ruiz Velasco \"Doble R\" remains free. Argentina listed the CJNG as terrorist on 26 March; the Chapitos pact broke down in Baja California in May; Treasury sanctioned over 50 targets on 23 July; and on 5 August Washington charged eight CJNG figures and lifted El 03's bounty from $5 million to $25 million within a $102 million package. As of September 2026 the cartel, estimated at 15,000-20,000 members, remains intact under El 03; the fragmentation many predicted has not yet come.","leaders":[{"name":"Nemesio Rubén Oseguera Cervantes","alias":"El Mencho","role":"Founder and supreme leader","from":2010,"to":2026,"fate":"Mortally wounded in an Army/National Guard special-forces raid at Tapalpa, Jalisco, 22 Feb 2026; died aged 59 aboard the helicopter to Mexico City; DNA-confirmed 23 Feb; buried Zapopan 3 Mar 2026."},{"name":"Juan Carlos Valencia González","alias":"El 03 / El Pelón / El R-3 / Tricky Tres","role":"Founder of Grupo Élite (2019); supreme leader since Feb 2026","from":2019,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; born Santa Ana, California, 12 Sep 1984 (dual US-Mexican citizen); indicted in Washington, DC, 8 Oct 2020; $5M reward from Dec 2021; named CJNG leader by the US NCTC in July 2026; reward raised to $25M on 5 Aug 2026."},{"name":"Abigael González Valencia","alias":"El Cuini","role":"Head of Los Cuinis, financial and cocaine-supply arm; El Mencho's brother-in-law","from":2010,"to":2015,"fate":"Arrested Puerto Vallarta 28 Feb 2015; Kingpin-designated 8 Apr 2015; transferred to US custody 12 Aug 2025 among 26 fugitives; pleaded not guilty in D.C. and was reported in late 2025 to be negotiating a plea and cooperating."},{"name":"Gerardo González Valencia","alias":"El Lalo","role":"Los Cuinis second-in-command, international money laundering","from":2010,"to":2016,"fate":"Arrested in Uruguay April 2016; extradited to the US May 2020; pleaded guilty Dec 2022; life sentence July 2023."},{"name":"Rosalinda González Valencia","alias":"La Jefa","role":"Chief financial operator; El Mencho's wife (married 1996)","from":2010,"to":2021,"fate":"Arrested 2018 (released for lack of evidence); rearrested Nov 2021; sentenced Dec 2023 to 5 years for money laundering; released early from CEFERESO 16 in Morelos on 27 Feb 2025 after more than three years; whereabouts unknown."},{"name":"Rubén Oseguera González","alias":"El Menchito","role":"Second-in-command (son of El Mencho)","from":2010,"to":2015,"fate":"Arrested Jan 2014 (released) and again 23 Jun 2015; extradited 20 Feb 2020; convicted Sept 2024; sentenced 7 Mar 2025 by Judge Beryl Howell to life plus 30 years with a $6 billion forfeiture."},{"name":"Erick Valencia Salazar","alias":"El 85","role":"Co-founder and top operator","from":2010,"to":2012,"fate":"Arrested Zapopan March 2012; freed 29 Dec 2017 on due-process grounds and joined the rival Nueva Plaza; rearrested Tapalpa 4 Sep 2022; sent to the US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded guilty to a cocaine-importation conspiracy count in Washington, DC, 7 Apr 2026, sentencing set for 31 Jul 2026."},{"name":"Martín Arzola Ortega","alias":"El 53 / El Negro","role":"Co-founder, operations chief and head of the Los Deltas hit squad","from":2010,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested 13 Jul 2011 in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga, Jalisco, by the Federal Police; sentenced to 24 years in Dec 2015 but freed after an amparo reduced the case to a weapons count; shot dead on 31 Jul 2019 in a restaurant at Plaza Galerías, Zapopan."},{"name":"Antonio Oseguera Cervantes","alias":"Tony Montana","role":"Arms procurement, logistics and money laundering (brother of El Mencho)","from":2010,"to":2022,"fate":"Arrested 20 Dec 2022 in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga (Guadalajara metro); transferred to the US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded guilty in 2026 to trafficking more than 450 kg of cocaine and over 45 kg of methamphetamine plus a firearms count, with sentencing set for 13 Nov 2026."},{"name":"Audias Flores Silva","alias":"El Jardinero","role":"Regional commander for the Pacific coast (Nayarit, Jalisco, Zacatecas, Guerrero, Michoacán); broker of the Chapitos alliance","from":2010,"to":2026,"fate":"Captured without a shot by Navy special forces at El Mirador, Nayarit, 27 Apr 2026, hiding in a drainage ditch; charged in the US, reward raised from $5M to $15M on 5 Aug 2026."},{"name":"Ricardo Ruiz Velasco","alias":"El Doble R / RR / Tripa","role":"Grupo Élite commander, Guadalajara and Jalisco plazas","from":2019,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; charged in the US 5 Aug 2026 with a $10M reward. A Mexicali man with the same nickname arrested in March 2026 was reported to be a different person."},{"name":"Julio Alberto Castillo Rodríguez","alias":"El Chorro","role":"Operations coordinator in the Colima region; lieutenant to El 03 and El Mencho's son-in-law","from":2026,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; charged in the US 5 Aug 2026 with a $15M reward."},{"name":"Hugo Gonzalo Mendoza Gaytán","alias":"El Sapo","role":"Overseer of weapons procurement and sicario training; in the succession line under El 03","from":2026,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; sanctioned by Treasury 23 Jul 2026; charged in the US 5 Aug 2026 with a $15M reward."},{"name":"Carlos Enrique Sánchez Martínez","alias":"El Cholo","role":"Guadalajara plaza chief and hit-squad boss","from":2010,"to":2017,"fate":"Broke away to found the rival Nueva Plaza in 2017; killed with a gunshot to the head, his body left on a bench in Jardín Hidalgo, Tlaquepaque, 18 Mar 2021."}],"factions":[{"id":"los_cuinis","name":"Los Cuinis","from":2010,"to":0,"area":"Jalisco, Michoacán (Aguililla); shell companies in Latin America, Europe and Asia","leader":"Abigael González Valencia 'El Cuini'","note":"González Valencia family finance and cocaine-supply network inherited from the Milenio; Kingpin-designated 8 Apr 2015; most senior brothers now in US custody or convicted, with the clan's ties running through El 03."},{"id":"matazetas","name":"Los Matazetas","from":2011,"to":2013,"area":"Veracruz (Boca del Río, Veracruz port), Guadalajara","leader":"El Mencho's armed wing","note":"Death-squad brand used in the 2011 Veracruz offensive against Los Zetas; 35 bodies dumped in Boca del Río 20 Sept 2011."},{"id":"grupo_elite","name":"Grupo Élite","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Jalisco, Michoacán, Zacatecas, Guanajuato","leader":"Juan Carlos Valencia González 'El 03' (founder); Ricardo Ruiz Velasco 'Doble R', Julio César Montero Pinzón 'Tarjetas' and Carlos Andrés Rivera Varela 'La Firma'","note":"Uniformed special-forces-style wing, also called Fuerzas Especiales Mencho (FEM), shown in a July 2020 armored-convoy video; the springboard for El 03's succession, and four of its commanders were charged by the US on 5 Aug 2026."},{"id":"ctng","name":"Cártel de Tijuana Nueva Generación","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Baja California (Tijuana, Tecate)","leader":"Arellano Félix remnants under CJNG sponsorship","note":"Alliance vehicle for the CJNG's push into Tijuana against Sinaloa factions; Baja California became the deadliest theater of the CJNG-Sinaloa war."},{"id":"nueva_plaza","name":"Cártel Nueva Plaza","from":2017,"to":2022,"area":"Guadalajara metro (Zapopan, Tlaquepaque, Tonalá, Tlajomulco)","leader":"Carlos Enrique Sánchez Martínez 'El Cholo', then Erick Valencia 'El 85'","note":"Sinaloa-backed breakaway that fought the CJNG in Guadalajara; El Cholo killed Mar 2021, El 85 arrested Sept 2022, after which the remnant reorganized under lesser figures."},{"id":"los_rs","name":"Los Rs","from":2025,"to":0,"area":"Uruapan, Apatzingán and Morelia, Michoacán, with presence in Jalisco","leader":"Ramón Ángel Álvarez Ayala 'R1', succeeded by his brother Rafael Álvarez Ayala 'R2'","note":"CJNG-linked cell blamed for the 1 Nov 2025 assassination of Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo; R1 was captured on 30 July 2026 as the alleged intellectual author, the 31st arrest in the case."}],"allies":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2010,"to":2013,"note":"Emerged as the Sinaloa Federation's Jalisco arm after Coronel's death; the partnership frayed in 2013-2014 as the CJNG asserted independence and moved on Tijuana."},{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2025,"to":2026,"note":"Alliance solidified May 2025 amid the Sinaloa civil war, with CJNG money, arms and gunmen backing Los Chapitos against La Mayiza; weakened after El Mencho's death and broke down in Baja California in May 2026."},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Arellano Félix remnants re-branded as Cártel de Tijuana Nueva Generación under CJNG sponsorship to contest Sinaloa in Tijuana."},{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2013,"to":2014,"note":"Reportedly financed and armed sections of Michoacán's self-defense movement against the Knights Templar; the relationship soured as ex-militia bosses formed Cárteles Unidos."},{"cartel":"barredora","from":2019,"to":2025,"note":"La Barredora operated as the CJNG's franchise in Tabasco; its alleged boss, ex-state security chief Hernán Bermúdez Requena, was arrested in Paraguay on 12 September 2025 and expelled to Mexico on 17 September."},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"InSight Crime lists some Gulf Cartel factions as tactical allies, chiefly against the Cártel del Noreste in Tamaulipas and Nuevo León."}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":2011,"to":2015,"note":"The Matazetas campaign in Veracruz and the Zetas-La Resistencia bloc in Guadalajara (26 bodies at the Arcos del Milenio, 24 Nov 2011)."},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"Mexico's defining cartel war: Baja California, Zacatecas, Sonora, Chiapas and Colima; Baja California authorities, cited by El Financiero in May 2026, put the decade's toll at about 30,000 homicides nationwide with 55% of them in Baja California."},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"CJNG fought alongside Los Chapitos against Ismael Zambada Sicairos's faction in central and coastal Sinaloa."},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2012,"to":2015,"note":"Contested Michoacán with the Knights Templar, partly through backing self-defense militias."},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Trench-and-drone warfare in the Tierra Caliente (Aguililla, Tepalcatepec, Buenavista) against Los Viagras and allied cells."},{"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"War over fuel theft and Guanajuato's industrial corridor; El Marro's capture 2 Aug 2020 did not end the killing."},{"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"Fighting in Guerrero's Tierra Caliente, Estado de México and eastern Michoacán."},{"cartel":"mezcales","from":2022,"to":0,"note":"Former CJNG cell in Colima whose 2022 defection, with Sinaloa backing, helped make Colima Mexico's least peaceful state; the Mexico Peace Index 2026 again ranked it last."},{"cartel":"talibanes","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Sinaloa-aligned Los Talibanes resisted the CJNG's push into Zacatecas and San Luis Potosí."},{"cartel":"cdn","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Clashes with the Cártel del Noreste in Nuevo León and Tamaulipas as the CJNG sought border crossings."},{"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Zeta remnants in Veracruz and Tamaulipas opposed CJNG expansion along the Gulf coast."},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"La Familia backed La Resistencia against Los Torcidos in the 2010-2011 Guadalajara fighting."},{"cartel":"other","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"La Resistencia (Ramiro Pozos 'El Molca'), the rival Milenio splinter, allied with La Familia and Los Zetas; defeated in Guadalajara by 2012."},{"cartel":"other","from":2017,"to":2022,"note":"Cártel Nueva Plaza, the Sinaloa-backed Guadalajara breakaway led by 'El Cholo' and later 'El 85'."}],"activities":["Methamphetamine production and export (industrial labs in Jalisco, Michoacán, Colima)","Fentanyl production and trafficking","Cocaine transshipment through Manzanillo and Lázaro Cárdenas","Heroin and marijuana","Extortion of avocado, lime, tortilla and construction sectors","Fuel theft (huachicol) in Veracruz, Guanajuato and Puebla","Timeshare fraud targeting US retirees (Puerto Vallarta)","Illegal mining and logging","Migrant smuggling and human trafficking","Arms trafficking and armed-drone/IED warfare","Money laundering via tequila, real estate, restaurants, security firms and front companies","Forced recruitment through fake job offers"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2025,"states":["JAL","NAY","COL","MIC","GUA","VER","ZAC","AGU","QUE","HID","PUE","ROO","BCN","BCS","CHP","GRO","MEX","MOR","SLP","TAB","TAM","TLA","CMX","OAX"],"note":"Between the Grupo Élite era and El Mencho's death, federal and analyst assessments placed the CJNG in a large majority of Mexico's 32 states, with dominant control of Jalisco, Nayarit, Colima, Veracruz, Guanajuato, Puebla, Querétaro and Hidalgo and reach into the Riviera Maya, plus supply networks in the US, Europe, Asia and Australia."},"facts":["Its 20 September 2011 debut as 'Los Matazetas' left 35 bodies on an underpass in Boca del Río, Veracruz.","On 1 May 2015 it became the first Mexican cartel to shoot down a military helicopter, killing nine (eight soldiers and one federal police officer) near Villa Purificación; on 6 April 2015 it had killed 15 state police in a single ambush at San Sebastián del Oeste.","The US bounty on El Mencho rose from $5 million to $10 million (16 Oct 2018) and $15 million (4 Dec 2024); on 5 August 2026 his successor El 03 was given $25 million, the largest ever for a Mexican trafficker, within a $102 million package on eight CJNG figures.","The 22-23 February 2026 retaliation for El Mencho's death produced 252 roadblocks in 20 states, 25 National Guard dead, 23 escaped inmates in Puerto Vallarta, attacks on 200+ Oxxo stores and 50 Banco del Bienestar branches, about 400,000 businesses closed and 1.5-2 billion pesos in losses.","El Menchito was sentenced on 7 March 2025 to life plus 30 years and ordered to forfeit more than $6 billion.","Membership estimates range from 18,800-33,100 (2020s tallies) to 15,000-20,000 (2026 reporting).","In the 26 June 2020 ambush on Omar García Harfuch, some two dozen gunmen with .50-caliber rifles hit him three times and killed three people in Lomas de Chapultepec.","Baja California state authorities, cited by El Financiero in May 2026, attribute about 30,000 homicides over a decade to the CJNG-Sinaloa war, 55% of them in Baja California.","Rancho Izaguirre at Teuchitlán, exposed 5 March 2025 by searching families, yielded hundreds of shoes and large quantities of discarded clothing; the former mayor was charged with organized crime and forced disappearance.","Treasury's 23 July 2026 action hit more than 50 individuals and entities, the largest ever against the CJNG; 29 prisoners including Tony Montana and El 85 were transferred to the US on 27 February 2025 and 26 more, including El Cuini, on 12 August 2025."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the CJNG remains active and cohesive under Juan Carlos Valencia González 'El 03', whom the US National Counterterrorism Center recognized as leader in July 2026 and for whom Washington now offers $25 million (raised 5 August 2026 within a $102 million package targeting eight CJNG figures); Treasury's 23 July 2026 sanctions on more than 50 people and companies were its largest ever against the cartel. Rival contender Audias Flores Silva 'El Jardinero' has been jailed since 27 April 2026, the alliance with Los Chapitos weakened after El Mencho's death and broke down in Baja California in May, and senior figures keep falling in US courts: El 85 pleaded guilty on 7 April 2026 and Tony Montana in 2026, with sentencings pending. The organization, estimated at 15,000-20,000 members with strongholds in Jalisco, Nayarit, Colima, Veracruz, Guanajuato, Puebla, Querétaro and Hidalgo, is designated a terrorist organization by the US (20 Feb 2025), Canada and Argentina (26 Mar 2026); the internal fragmentation analysts forecast after El Mencho's death has not materialized.","sources":["https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/mexican-army-kills-jalisco-new-generation-cartel-leader-el-mencho-during-operation-to-capture-him","https://www.nbcnews.com/world/mexico/jalisco-new-generation-cartel-leader-killed-rcna260184","https://insightcrime.org/news/whats-next-for-mexicos-cjng-after-the-killing-of-el-mencho/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/juan-carlos-valencia-gonzalez-alias-03/","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/new-charges-and-rewards-announced-capture-andor-conviction-senior-leaders-notorious-mexican","https://www.state.gov/juan-carlos-valencia-gonzalez","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20260723","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Mexico_cartel_unrest","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/28/mexicos-jalisco-drug-cartel-commander-el-jardinero-found-hiding-in-ditch","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/26/argentina-declares-jalisco-new-generation-cartel-a-terrorist-group","https://www.jurist.org/news/2026/04/california-man-pleads-guilty-to-drug-cartel-conspiracy-charge/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/05/28/rompen-cjng-y-cartel-de-sinaloa-en-baja-california-comienzan-nueva-guerra-por-el-control-del-estado/","https://theintercept.com/2025/03/07/ruben-oseguera-gonzalez-jalisco-new-generation-cartel-sentencing-trump/","https://www.dea.gov/stories/2024/2024-12/2024-12-04/statement-administrator-milgram-increased-reward-for-dea-priority","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/22/a-seis-meses-de-la-muerte-de-el-mencho-asi-se-ha-reacomodado-el-poder-dentro-del-cjng/"],"verified":true},{"id":"familia_michoacana","name":"La Familia Michoacana","aka":["La Familia","LFM","La Empresa (2000-2006 precursor)"],"founded":2006,"dissolved":2011,"parent":"zetas","origin":{"state":"MIC","city":"Apatzingán","lat":19.0886,"lng":-102.3508},"summary":"La Familia Michoacana was a Michoacán-based methamphetamine cartel that emerged in 2006 from a Zetas-trained structure known as La Empresa and wrapped its violence in a pseudo-evangelical code written by Nazario Moreno González, 'El Más Loco'. It dominated the Tierra Caliente and much of central Mexico from 2007 to 2010, fought the Zetas and the federal government, and fragmented in 2011 into the Knights Templar and the Guerrero-based rump that became La Nueva Familia Michoacana.","history":"La Familia Michoacana grew out of the criminal economy of Michoacán's Tierra Caliente, where the Valencia family's Milenio Cartel had dominated methamphetamine and marijuana production since the 1990s. Around 2000 Carlos Rosales Mendoza, 'El Tísico' (born February 12, 1963, in La Unión, Guerrero), a Valencia associate, broke with Armando Valencia Cornelio and allied himself with Gulf Cartel boss Osiel Cárdenas Guillén. With Zetas instructors he built an armed structure known as La Empresa to seize Michoacán from the Valencias. Rosales was captured by GAFE special forces in Morelia on October 24, 2004, and sent to the Puente Grande maximum-security prison four days later; command passed to Nazario Moreno González, 'El Chayo' or 'El Más Loco' (born March 8, 1970, in Apatzingán), and José de Jesús Méndez Vargas, 'El Chango', flanked by Servando Gómez Martínez, 'La Tuta', Enrique Plancarte Solís and Dionisio Loya Plancarte. In 2006 the Michoacán bosses broke with the Zetas, adopted the name La Familia Michoacana and turned their guns on their former mentors.\n\nThe group announced itself on September 6, 2006, when gunmen rolled five severed heads onto the dance floor of the Sol y Sombra nightclub in Uruapan with a note claiming to deliver 'divine justice'. On November 22, 2006, it bought newspaper advertisements presenting itself as a defender of Michoacán against kidnappers and meth dealers. President Felipe Calderón answered on December 11, 2006, with Operativo Conjunto Michoacán, the opening deployment of Mexico's drug war. Behind the moralizing facade, Nazario Moreno imposed a pseudo-evangelical code, distributed his own writings ('Pensamientos del Más Loco' and, in 2010, 'Me dicen: El Más Loco'), banned members from using drugs and recruited from rehabilitation centers, while the organization ran industrial-scale methamphetamine 'super-labs' in the Tierra Caliente, some reportedly able to produce about 45 kilograms in eight hours, and pushed product through distribution cells in Atlanta, Dallas and Chicago. President Obama identified La Familia Michoacana as a significant foreign narcotics trafficker under the Kingpin Act on April 15, 2009, and on February 25, 2010 the Treasury's Office of Foreign Assets Control made its first derivative designation against the group, naming seven leaders — Nazario Moreno, Méndez Vargas, Gómez Martínez, Enrique Plancarte Solís, Dionisio Loya Plancarte, José Arnoldo Rueda Medina and Nicandro Barrera Medrano — plus the trucking company Transportadora Purepecha S.A. de C.V.\n\nConfrontation with the state peaked in 2009. Regional chief Rafael Cedeño Hernández was seized in April, when some 400 Federal Police backed by two Black Hawk helicopters raided a family baptism at a Morelia social hall and detained 44 people without firing a shot; on May 26 federal agents detained 10 Michoacán mayors and roughly 20 other state and municipal officials in the 'Michoacanazo', most of whom were later released for lack of evidence. The July 11 arrest of operations chief Arnoldo Rueda Medina, 'La Minsa', triggered coordinated attacks on federal police in several cities, and on July 13-14 the bodies of 12 tortured federal agents were dumped beside a highway near La Huacana. Days later La Tuta phoned a Morelia television station to propose a pact with the government. On October 22, 2009, the US Project Coronado sweep arrested 303 people in 19 states in 48 hours.\n\nIn 2010 La Familia joined the Gulf and Sinaloa cartels against the Zetas and expanded into Guerrero, the State of México, Guanajuato, Querétaro and Morelos. On December 9, 2010, more than 2,000 federal police surrounded the village of El Alcalde, outside Apatzingán; after two days of fighting, in which gunmen blocked the town's entrances with burning vehicles, the government declared Nazario Moreno dead, though no body was recovered and his followers began venerating him as 'San Nazario'. On January 3, 2011, the cartel circulated a communiqué offering a truce, and in March 2011 La Tuta, Plancarte and Loya Plancarte broke away to found the Knights Templar. Chango Méndez kept the Familia name and reportedly sought Zetas help against his former partners, but he was captured at a road checkpoint in Aguascalientes on June 21, 2011. Project Delirium produced 221 more US arrests in July 2011, and by November the Mexican government described the original organization as virtually defunct.\n\nThe brand survived through its southern wing. In Guerrero and the State of México, loyalists led by José María Chávez Magaña, 'El Pony', kept operating under the Familia name until the Navy captured him on July 2, 2014, after which brothers Johnny and José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga built what US authorities call La Nueva Familia Michoacana. The founders' fates were settled one by one: Nazario Moreno was killed for real by the Army and Navy in Tumbiscatío on March 9, 2014, and identified by fingerprints and DNA; Plancarte died in a Navy raid in Colón, Querétaro, on March 31, 2014; La Tuta was captured in Morelia on February 27, 2015. Rosales Mendoza walked out of prison on May 22, 2014 after completing his ten-year term, was briefly re-arrested at a Morelia restaurant on August 5, 2014 and released without charge, and was tortured and shot dead on December 27, 2015, his body found the next day with three others in Gabriel Zamora. Chango Méndez was sentenced in December 2022 to 45 years and 10 months plus a 751,218-peso fine, a sentence upheld by an appeals court in Tepic, Nayarit on May 26, 2024, and was flown to the United States on February 27, 2025, among 29 prisoners transferred to US custody; La Tuta followed on August 12, 2025, among 26.\n\nAs of September 2026 the original La Familia Michoacana exists only as history and as a name: Chango Méndez awaits trial in the Southern District of New York, La Tuta pleaded not guilty there on March 17, 2026 before Judge John G. Koeltl and, after two postponements, has a hearing scheduled for September 16, 2026, and the Hurtado brothers' Nueva Familia Michoacana, designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization on February 20, 2025, remains the living heir to the network's Guerrero and State of México territories.","leaders":[{"name":"Carlos Rosales Mendoza","alias":"El Tísico","role":"Founder of La Empresa, the Gulf/Zetas-backed precursor","from":2000,"to":2004,"fate":"Born Feb 12, 1963 in La Unión, Guerrero; arrested by GAFE in Morelia Oct 24, 2004 and held at Puente Grande; released May 22, 2014 after a 10-year term; briefly re-arrested in Morelia Aug 5, 2014 and freed without charge; tortured and shot dead Dec 27, 2015, body found with three others in Gabriel Zamora, Michoacán, Dec 28, 2015"},{"name":"Nazario Moreno González","alias":"El Chayo / El Más Loco","role":"Supreme and spiritual leader; author of the group's moral code","from":2004,"to":2010,"fate":"Born Mar 8, 1970 in Apatzingán; declared killed by federal police at El Alcalde, Apatzingán, Dec 9, 2010 (no body); secretly led the Knights Templar; killed by Army and Navy in Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, Mar 9, 2014, identified by fingerprints and DNA"},{"name":"José de Jesús Méndez Vargas","alias":"El Chango","role":"Co-founder and operational chief; sole leader after Dec 2010","from":2006,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested at a road checkpoint in Aguascalientes June 21, 2011; sentenced in Mexico in Dec 2022 to 45 years 10 months plus a 751,218-peso fine, upheld on appeal in Tepic, Nayarit, May 26, 2024; held at CEFERESO 13 in Oaxaca; transferred to the US Feb 27, 2025 among 29 prisoners; charged in SDNY with conspiring to import cocaine and methamphetamine (indictment unsealed Feb 28, 2025)"},{"name":"Servando Gómez Martínez","alias":"La Tuta / El Profe","role":"Operations chief and public spokesman","from":2006,"to":2011,"fate":"Split to found the Knights Templar in March 2011; captured in Morelia Feb 27, 2015; sentenced to 55 years for kidnapping (June 17, 2019) and 47 years 6 months for organized crime and drug trafficking (Dec 9, 2022); transferred to the US Aug 12, 2025; pleaded not guilty in SDNY on Mar 17, 2026"},{"name":"Enrique Plancarte Solís","alias":"El Kike / La Chiva","role":"Methamphetamine production and trafficking chief","from":2006,"to":2011,"fate":"Split to the Knights Templar in 2011; killed in a Navy raid in Colón, Querétaro, Mar 31, 2014"},{"name":"Dionisio Loya Plancarte","alias":"El Tío","role":"Finance, political fixer and spokesman","from":2006,"to":2011,"fate":"Split to the Knights Templar in 2011; arrested by the Army in Morelia Jan 27, 2014 and moved to the Altiplano federal prison on Jan 29, 2014; no release or death confirmed as of Sept 2026 (a 2013 report of his death in a gunfight was false)"},{"name":"Rafael Cedeño Hernández","alias":"El Cede","role":"Regional chief for the Lázaro Cárdenas area and head of indoctrination and hitman training","from":2006,"to":2009,"fate":"Detained in April 2009 when about 400 Federal Police raided a family baptism at a Morelia social hall, one of 44 people arrested; reported still imprisoned"},{"name":"Arnoldo Rueda Medina","alias":"La Minsa / El Flaco","role":"Operations chief","from":2006,"to":2009,"fate":"Arrested in Morelia July 11, 2009; his capture triggered retaliatory attacks on federal police across Michoacán"}],"factions":[{"id":"la_empresa","name":"La Empresa","from":2000,"to":2006,"area":"Michoacán Tierra Caliente, Morelia","leader":"Carlos Rosales Mendoza","note":"Gulf Cartel/Zetas-backed precursor; broke with the Zetas and rebranded as La Familia in 2006"},{"id":"templarios_split","name":"La Tuta / Plancarte wing (Knights Templar)","from":2011,"to":2011,"area":"Apatzingán and Tierra Caliente, Michoacán","leader":"Servando Gómez Martínez","note":"Broke away in March 2011 with Nazario Moreno's blessing; became the independent Knights Templar (see templarios)"},{"id":"chango_loyalists","name":"El Chango rump faction","from":2011,"to":2011,"area":"Michoacán, Guanajuato, Estado de México","leader":"José de Jesús Méndez Vargas","note":"Kept the Familia name, reportedly sought Zetas support against the Templarios; collapsed with Méndez's June 21, 2011 arrest"},{"id":"guerrero_wing","name":"Guerrero / Estado de México wing","from":2011,"to":2014,"area":"Tierra Caliente of Guerrero, southern Estado de México","leader":"José María Chávez Magaña 'El Pony'","note":"Survived the 2011 collapse under 'El Pony' until his capture by the Navy on July 2, 2014, then passed to the Hurtado Olascoaga brothers (see nueva_familia_michoacana)"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2000,"to":2006,"note":"Osiel Cárdenas backed La Empresa against the Valencias; the alliance broke down in 2006"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2000,"to":2006,"note":"Zetas trained and armed La Empresa; La Familia turned on them in 2006"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Joined the Gulf and Sinaloa cartels against the Zetas in 2010"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Anti-Zetas alignment in 2010; Sinaloa supplied cocaine routes through Lázaro Cárdenas"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"milenio","from":2000,"to":2010,"note":"War against the Valencia family for Michoacán; Milenio was pushed back into Jalisco"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2006,"to":2011,"note":"Former mentors became mortal enemies; fighting spread into Guanajuato, Guerrero and Estado de México"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2008,"to":2010,"note":"Clashes in Guerrero and Morelos as both expanded southward"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2011,"to":2011,"note":"Succession war after Nazario's staged death; Chango's faction lost after his June 21, 2011 arrest"},{"cartel":"government","from":2006,"to":2011,"note":"Operativo Conjunto Michoacán (Dec 11, 2006), Michoacanazo (May 26, 2009), July 2009 attacks on federal police, El Alcalde/Apatzingán battle (Dec 9-11, 2010)"}],"activities":["Industrial methamphetamine production in Tierra Caliente super-labs","Methamphetamine and cocaine distribution networks in Atlanta, Dallas, Chicago and the US Midwest","Cocaine and precursor imports via the port of Lázaro Cárdenas","Marijuana and opium poppy cultivation","Extortion of businesses, agriculture and municipal governments","Kidnapping","Political corruption and infiltration of municipal governments","Money laundering","Pirated goods and local retail drug sales"],"peak":{"from":2008,"to":2010,"states":["MIC","GRO","MEX","GUA","QUE","MOR","JAL","CMX"],"note":"Controlled most of Michoacán and projected into Guerrero, Estado de México, Guanajuato, Querétaro and Morelos; principal supplier of methamphetamine to the US"},"facts":["Announced itself on Sept 6, 2006 by rolling five severed heads onto the dance floor of the Sol y Sombra nightclub in Uruapan","Bought newspaper advertisements on Nov 22, 2006 presenting itself as Michoacán's protector against kidnappers and meth dealers","The May 26, 2009 'Michoacanazo' detained 10 mayors and roughly 20 other Michoacán officials suspected of working for the cartel; most were later released for lack of evidence","After Arnoldo Rueda Medina's arrest on July 11, 2009, 12 federal police agents were tortured and killed and their bodies dumped near La Huacana on July 13-14","US Project Coronado (Oct 22, 2009) arrested 303 people across 19 states in 48 hours; Project Delirium (July 2011) added 221 US arrests","Super-labs were reported able to produce about 45 kg (100 lb) of methamphetamine in eight hours","Over 2,000 federal police fought the cartel at El Alcalde, outside Apatzingán, from Dec 9, 2010; Nazario Moreno was declared dead without a body and actually died on Mar 9, 2014","Designated under the US Kingpin Act on Apr 15, 2009; on Feb 25, 2010 OFAC designated seven leaders — including Nazario Moreno, Chango Méndez, La Tuta and Enrique Plancarte — plus the firm Transportadora Purepecha","Chango Méndez was sentenced in Mexico to 45 years 10 months (upheld May 26, 2024) and transferred to the US on Feb 27, 2025 among 29 prisoners"],"status_2026":"Defunct as a unified organization since 2011. Its founders are dead or in US custody: Chango Méndez was transferred to the Southern District of New York on Feb 27, 2025, and La Tuta, transferred Aug 12, 2025, pleaded not guilty before Judge John G. Koeltl on Mar 17, 2026; after postponements from June 24, his next hearing is set for Sept 16, 2026. The name lives on through La Nueva Familia Michoacana of the Hurtado Olascoaga brothers, a US-designated Foreign Terrorist Organization since Feb 20, 2025.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/familia-michoacana-mexico-profile/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Familia_Michoacana","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazario_Moreno_Gonz%C3%A1lez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_Jes%C3%BAs_M%C3%A9ndez_Vargas","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Rosales_Mendoza","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/mexican-cartel-leader-jesus-mendez-vargas-us-custody-drug-importation-charge","https://insightcrime.org/news/historic-mexico-extradites-top-narcos-united-states/","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20100225","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/archive-documents/final-chart--25-feb-2010.pdf","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2024/05/26/el-chango-mendez-fundador-de-la-familia-michoacana-pierde-apelacion-confirman-sentencia-de-45-anos/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2015/12/28/el-tisico-fundador-de-la-familia-michoacana-entre-los-ejecutados-en-autopista-156970.html","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2009/04/20/politica/010n1pol","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/06/25/la-tuta-exlider-de-la-familia-michoacana-consigue-aplazar-audiencia-en-nueva-york/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Familia_Michoacana"],"verified":true},{"id":"templarios","name":"Knights Templar Cartel","aka":["Los Caballeros Templarios","Caballeros Templarios de Michoacán","LCT"],"founded":2011,"dissolved":2017,"parent":"familia_michoacana","origin":{"state":"MIC","city":"Apatzingán","lat":19.0886,"lng":-102.3508},"summary":"The Knights Templar were the dominant successor to La Familia Michoacana, announced in March 2011 by Servando 'La Tuta' Gómez, Enrique Plancarte and Dionisio Loya Plancarte under the hidden leadership of Nazario Moreno. Between 2012 and 2013 they ran Michoacán as a parallel state, taxing avocados, limes, cattle and mining and exporting illegal iron ore to China through Lázaro Cárdenas, until a citizen militia uprising and federal intervention decapitated the group in 2014-2015.","history":"The Knights Templar were born from the December 2010 crisis of La Familia Michoacana. After the government declared Nazario Moreno González dead in the El Alcalde battle outside Apatzingán on December 9, 2010, a succession fight pitted José de Jesús Méndez Vargas, 'El Chango', against Servando Gómez Martínez, 'La Tuta', Enrique Plancarte Solís and Dionisio Loya Plancarte. In March 2011 banners hung across Michoacán announced that Los Caballeros Templarios would take over La Familia's 'altruistic' work. Nazario, alive and hidden in the Sierra, was the group's secret spiritual head; members swore oaths, carried a 22-page 'Código de los Caballeros Templarios de Michoacán' that ordered them to help the poor, respect women and children and abstain from drugs, were drug-tested and were told to venerate 'San Nazario'. A short war with Chango's loyalists ended with his arrest on June 21, 2011, leaving the Templarios in control of most of Michoacán's Tierra Caliente.\n\nBetween 2012 and 2013 the organization reached its peak. It ran methamphetamine labs and imported precursors and cocaine through the Pacific port of Lázaro Cárdenas, but its most distinctive business was extortion of the legal economy: avocado and lime growers, cattle ranchers, tortilla shops, mining companies and municipal governments paid quotas, and an estimated 85 percent of Michoacán's businesses paid protection. From roughly 2012 the cartel took over illegal iron ore mining on the Sierra-Costa and shipped ore to China through the port, a trade federal commissioner Alfredo Castillo later described as its main source of income; exports through Lázaro Cárdenas rose from a small fraction of Mexico's China-bound iron ore in 2008 to nearly half by 2012. The cartel courted public opinion: on March 17, 2012, it hung 14 banners on bridges across municipalities of Guanajuato welcoming Pope Benedict XVI and pledging no violence during his March 23-26 visit, La Tuta described the group as a 'brotherhood' in an August 2012 video, and at the end of 2012 it offered to disarm if President Enrique Peña Nieto kept his campaign promises. Meanwhile the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación pressed into western Michoacán.\n\nThe backlash began on February 24, 2013, when lime growers in La Ruana led by Hipólito Mora and residents of Tepalcatepec led by physician José Manuel Mireles took up arms as autodefensas. The movement spread through the Tierra Caliente during 2013; the Templarios answered by burning packing plants, and on October 27, 2013, they attacked electrical substations, blacking out large parts of the state. On November 4, 2013, the Navy took over security at Lázaro Cárdenas. In January 2014 the militias seized Nueva Italia and Parácuaro and marched on Apatzingán; on January 15 Peña Nieto named Alfredo Castillo federal security commissioner for Michoacán, on January 27 the government agreed to legalize the militias, and on May 10, 2014, they were folded into a Fuerza Rural.\n\nThe leadership was destroyed within 13 months. Dionisio Loya Plancarte was arrested in Morelia on January 27, 2014; Nazario Moreno was killed by the Army and Navy in Tumbiscatío on March 9, 2014, his death confirmed by fingerprints and DNA; Enrique Plancarte died in a Navy raid in Colón, Querétaro, on March 31, 2014. The state also went after the mining money: in early March 2014 authorities seized 119,000 tons of illegally extracted iron ore and 124 pieces of heavy machinery at Lázaro Cárdenas, and in the spring they intercepted a further 75,800 tons aboard the ship Jian Hua at Manzanillo, part of more than 220,000 tons of cartel-linked ore seized in Michoacán and Guerrero in the first half of 2014. La Tuta, now sole leader, fought back by leaking videos of himself with politicians, including the son of Governor Fausto Vallejo, and gave Britain's Channel 4 News a jungle interview published on February 25, 2014, before releasing a defiant 24-minute recording in October 2014 vowing to fight to the end. Federal Police captured him at 3 a.m. on February 27, 2015, in Morelia.\n\nWhat remained fragmented. Ignacio Rentería Andrade, 'El Cenizo', took over the main remnant and was arrested on June 21, 2017 in Parácuaro after a shootout with soldiers, later receiving a 20-year sentence reported in November 2025; Pablo Toscano Padilla, 'El 500', was reported killed in September 2017. Former Templarios and militiamen regrouped as Los Viagras under the Sierra Santana brothers and as the H3 in Nueva Italia; from 2019-2020 these joined Juan José Farías's Tepalcatepec group in Cárteles Unidos to resist the CJNG, while the December 16, 2014, shootout between Mora's and 'El Americano's' militias in La Ruana (11 dead, including Mora's son) foreshadowed the militias' own criminalization. Mireles died of COVID-19 on November 25, 2020, and Mora was ambushed and killed in La Ruana on June 29, 2023.\n\nLa Tuta was sentenced in Mexico to 55 years on June 17, 2019 for the 2011 kidnapping of a Uruapan businessman and to a further 47 years and six months on December 9, 2022 for organized crime and drug trafficking. On August 12, 2025, he was among 26 prisoners sent to the United States, where he faces cocaine and methamphetamine importation conspiracy charges in the Southern District of New York before Judge John G. Koeltl, carrying a 10-year mandatory minimum and a maximum of life; he pleaded not guilty on March 17, 2026 and, after his June 24 hearing was postponed at the joint request of prosecutors and defense lawyer Thomas Ambrosio, a hearing is scheduled for September 16, 2026. As of September 2026 the Knights Templar no longer exist as an organization; their former territory is contested between the CJNG and Cárteles Unidos, and their legacy is a Michoacán in which extortion of avocados, limes and mining remains the core criminal business.","leaders":[{"name":"Nazario Moreno González","alias":"El Chayo / El Más Loco / San Nazario","role":"Founder and hidden spiritual leader","from":2011,"to":2014,"fate":"Killed by Army and Navy in Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, Mar 9, 2014, after his staged death of Dec 9, 2010; identity confirmed by fingerprints and DNA"},{"name":"Servando Gómez Martínez","alias":"La Tuta / El Profe","role":"Co-founder, operations chief and spokesman; top leader from March 2014","from":2011,"to":2015,"fate":"Captured by Federal Police in Morelia Feb 27, 2015; sentenced to 55 years for kidnapping (June 17, 2019) and 47 years 6 months for organized crime and drug trafficking (Dec 9, 2022); transferred to the US Aug 12, 2025 among 26 prisoners; pleaded not guilty in SDNY Mar 17, 2026, hearing set for Sept 16, 2026"},{"name":"Enrique Plancarte Solís","alias":"El Kike / La Chiva","role":"Co-founder; methamphetamine production and trafficking chief","from":2011,"to":2014,"fate":"Killed in a shootout with the Navy in Colón, Querétaro, Mar 31, 2014"},{"name":"Dionisio Loya Plancarte","alias":"El Tío","role":"Co-founder; finances, political corruption and propaganda","from":2011,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested by the Army in Morelia Jan 27, 2014; transferred to the Altiplano federal prison Jan 29, 2014; still imprisoned, no sentence or death confirmed as of Sept 2026"},{"name":"Ignacio Rentería Andrade","alias":"El Cenizo","role":"Leader of the main remnant after La Tuta's capture","from":2015,"to":2017,"fate":"Arrested June 21, 2017 in Parácuaro, Michoacán, after a shootout with soldiers; sentenced to 20 years for organized crime and drug offenses, reported Nov 29, 2025"},{"name":"Pablo Toscano Padilla","alias":"El 500","role":"Remnant cell boss","from":2015,"to":2017,"fate":"Reported killed in September 2017, along with a lieutenant known as 'El Cheques'"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_viagras","name":"Los Viagras","from":2014,"to":0,"area":"Buenavista, Huetamo and the Tierra Caliente of Michoacán","leader":"Nicolás Sierra Santana 'El Gordo'","note":"Ex-Templarios and ex-Familia dissidents who joined the autodefensas and Fuerza Rural in 2013-2014; later core of Cárteles Unidos; reported to have reached an accommodation with the CJNG in 2024"},{"id":"h3","name":"H3 (La Tercera Hermandad)","from":2014,"to":2020,"area":"Nueva Italia (Múgica), Michoacán","leader":"","note":"Former Templarios who switched to the self-defense movement in 2014; absorbed into the Cárteles Unidos coalition"},{"id":"cenizo_remnant","name":"El Cenizo remnant","from":2015,"to":2017,"area":"Uruapan, Parácuaro and the Sierra of Michoacán","leader":"Ignacio Rentería Andrade","note":"Last structure to operate under the Templar name; dissolved after his June 21, 2017 arrest in Parácuaro"},{"id":"toscano_remnant","name":"El 500 cell","from":2015,"to":2017,"area":"Michoacán","leader":"Pablo Toscano Padilla","note":"Remnant cell; leadership reported killed in September 2017"}],"allies":[],"rivals":[{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2011,"to":2011,"note":"Succession war with Chango Méndez's loyalists, decided by his June 21, 2011 arrest"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2011,"to":2013,"note":"Templarios cast themselves as Michoacán's shield against the Zetas, whom Chango had courted"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2012,"to":2017,"note":"CJNG pushed into western Michoacán and was widely accused of arming autodefensas against the Templarios"},{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2013,"to":2015,"note":"La Ruana and Tepalcatepec uprising of Feb 24, 2013 spread across the Tierra Caliente and, with federal backing, expelled the cartel from its towns"},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2014,"to":2017,"note":"Los Viagras and H3 splinters turned on their former organization and later formed Cárteles Unidos"},{"cartel":"government","from":2011,"to":2017,"note":"Navy takeover of Lázaro Cárdenas (Nov 4, 2013), Castillo commission (Jan 15, 2014), killing of Nazario and Plancarte (Mar 2014), capture of La Tuta (Feb 27, 2015)"}],"activities":["Methamphetamine production and export","Cocaine and precursor imports through the port of Lázaro Cárdenas","Illegal iron ore mining and export to China","Extortion of avocado, lime, cattle, tortilla, mining and transport sectors","Kidnapping","Capture of municipal governments and public contracts","Money laundering","Arms trafficking"],"peak":{"from":2012,"to":2013,"states":["MIC","GUA","GRO","MEX","MOR"],"note":"Controlled Michoacán's Tierra Caliente, Apatzingán and the port of Lázaro Cárdenas, with cells in Guanajuato, Guerrero, Estado de México and Morelos"},"facts":["Announced in March 2011 with banners across Michoacán and a 22-page code of conduct booklet; recruits were drug-tested and swore oaths","Hung 14 banners on bridges in Guanajuato on Mar 17, 2012 welcoming Pope Benedict XVI and pledging no violence during his Mar 23-26 visit","An estimated 85% of legal businesses in Michoacán paid extortion at the cartel's peak, including avocado and lime growers","The Navy took control of the port of Lázaro Cárdenas on Nov 4, 2013 to cut the cartel's iron ore exports; about 119,000 tons of ore and 124 machines were seized there in March 2014 and 75,800 tons were intercepted aboard the ship Jian Hua at Manzanillo, part of over 220,000 tons seized in the first half of 2014","The autodefensa uprising began on Feb 24, 2013 in La Ruana and Tepalcatepec; the government named Alfredo Castillo commissioner on Jan 15, 2014 and created the Fuerza Rural on May 10, 2014","Four top leaders fell in 13 months: Loya Plancarte arrested Jan 27, 2014; Nazario killed Mar 9, 2014; Plancarte killed Mar 31, 2014; La Tuta captured Feb 27, 2015","La Tuta's Mexican sentences total more than 100 years: 55 years for kidnapping (June 17, 2019) plus 47 years 6 months for organized crime and drug trafficking (Dec 9, 2022)","La Tuta was one of 26 prisoners transferred to the US on Aug 12, 2025; he faces 10 years to life in SDNY and pleaded not guilty on Mar 17, 2026","Militia founders met violent or early ends: Hipólito Mora was assassinated in La Ruana on June 29, 2023; José Manuel Mireles died of COVID-19 on Nov 25, 2020"],"status_2026":"Extinct as an organization since about 2017; its former fighters and territories were absorbed by Los Viagras, Cárteles Unidos and the CJNG, which now contest Michoacán. La Tuta, transferred to US custody on Aug 12, 2025, pleaded not guilty to cocaine and methamphetamine importation conspiracy before Judge John G. Koeltl in the Southern District of New York on Mar 17, 2026; his June 24, 2026 hearing was postponed at the parties' joint request and a hearing is scheduled for Sept 16, 2026. Ignacio Rentería Andrade, the last leader to use the Templar name, received a 20-year sentence reported in November 2025.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/knights-templar-profile/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/servando-gomez-martinez-la-tuta/","https://insightcrime.org/news/brief/mexicos-76000-ton-iron-seizure-just-fraction-of-knights-templar-exports/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_Templar_Cartel","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_los_Caballeros_Templarios","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servando_G%C3%B3mez_Mart%C3%ADnez","https://www.channel4.com/news/mexico-knights-templar-drug-cartel-la-tuta-servando-gomez","https://www.mining.com/mexican-police-seizes-119000-tonnes-of-iron-ore-mined-by-drug-lords-59902/","https://zetatijuana.com/2025/08/quien-es-la-tuta-ex-lider-de-caballeros-templarios-y-familia-michoacana-trasladado-a-eu/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/11/29/sentencian-a-el-cenizo-a-20-anos-de-carcel-exlider-mas-buscado-de-los-caballeros-templarios-en-uruapan-michoacan/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2017/6/21/detienen-en-michoacan-el-cenizo-lider-de-los-caballeros-templarios-186482.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip%C3%B3lito_Mora"],"verified":true},{"id":"nueva_familia_michoacana","name":"La Nueva Familia Michoacana","aka":["La Familia Michoacana","LNFM","Familia Michoacana (Hurtado Olascoaga faction)","FAM"],"founded":2011,"dissolved":0,"parent":"familia_michoacana","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Arcelia","lat":18.315,"lng":-100.284},"summary":"La Nueva Familia Michoacana is the surviving southern wing of La Familia Michoacana, run since 2014 by brothers Johnny 'El Pez' and José Alfredo 'El Fresa' Hurtado Olascoaga from the Tierra Caliente where Guerrero, Michoacán and the State of México meet. It combines fentanyl, methamphetamine and heroin trafficking with pervasive extortion, illegal mining, drone and IED warfare, and was designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization by the United States on February 20, 2025.","history":"La Nueva Familia Michoacana is the Guerrero–State of México wing of La Familia Michoacana that outlived the parent organization. The Hurtado Olascoaga family comes from Arcelia, in Guerrero's Tierra Caliente, where the borders of Guerrero, Michoacán and the State of México meet; Johnny Hurtado Olascoaga, 'El Pez' (born March 1, 1973, in Arcelia, Guerrero, per his OFAC listing), and his younger brother José Alfredo, 'El Fresa', worked as Familia operators in the region from the mid-2000s. When La Tuta's faction left to found the Knights Templar in March 2011 and Chango Méndez was arrested that June, the southern cells kept the Familia name under José María Chávez Magaña, 'El Pony'. His capture by the Navy on July 2, 2014 left the Hurtado brothers in command of what US agencies label La Nueva Familia Michoacana (LNFM), a group locals still call simply La Familia Michoacana.\n\nFrom their base in Tuzantla, Michoacán, and the Arcelia–Teloloapan corridor, the brothers built a territorial enterprise rather than a trafficking pipeline. They taxed poppy and later fentanyl and methamphetamine production, extorted avocado, lime, cattle and timber producers, tortilla shops and transport operators, tapped the Tula–Toluca pipeline for fuel and took over illegal mining. By the late 2010s they controlled the southern State of México (Tejupilco, Luvianos, Amatepec, Tlatlaya, Sultepec, Texcaltitlán), northern and Tierra Caliente Guerrero (Arcelia, Teloloapan, Taxco, Coyuca de Catalán, San Miguel Totolapan), eastern Michoacán (Tuzantla, Zitácuaro, Huetamo) and parts of Morelos, with US distribution in Atlanta, Houston, New Mexico, North Carolina and Washington, DC. In 2019 a cell leader, 'El Zarco', publicly threatened CJNG boss 'El Mencho' over control of the Balsas River line, and the group fought Los Tequileros, Guerreros Unidos and Los Tlacos on its Guerrero flanks.\n\nViolence escalated after 2020. On March 18, 2021, 13 State of México police officers were ambushed and killed at Llano Grande, Coatepec Harinas, an attack attributed to the group. On August 24, 2022, fighting between the Hurtados and an associate known as 'El Chaparro' began in La Yerbabuena and spread to the main square of Tuzantla; eight people died, three of them minors, and the bodies were found at a butterfly sanctuary nearby. On October 5, 2022, gunmen of Los Tequileros, a Totolapan group aligned with the CJNG since 2020, attacked a private house and the town hall of San Miguel Totolapan and killed 20 people, including Mayor Conrado Mendoza Almeda and his father Juan Mendoza Acosta, himself a former mayor. On November 17, 2022, the US Treasury sanctioned LNFM and both brothers under Executive Order 14059, describing operations in about 35 municipalities of Michoacán, Guerrero, Morelos and the State of México and the trafficking of 'rainbow' fentanyl aimed at children and young users. A March 2023 Army operation in Petatlán failed to catch them. In May 2023 drone bombings drove about 600 people from Nuevo Poblado El Caracol in the Atoyac sierra, with InSight Crime reporting that the CJNG was now supplying LNFM with drones. On December 8, 2023, farmers in Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán, tired of paying one peso per square meter of crops, killed 10 extortionists, including plaza boss Rigoberto Sancha Santillán, 'El Payaso', losing four of their own.\n\nUS pressure intensified in 2024-2025. A Northern District of Georgia grand jury returned a three-count indictment against Johnny and José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga on September 24, 2024, charging conspiracy to manufacture, import and distribute heroin, cocaine, methamphetamine and fentanyl; the State Department designated LNFM a Foreign Terrorist Organization and Specially Designated Global Terrorist on February 20, 2025, one of eight cartels so listed; on March 29, 2025, Mexico's 'Operación Bastión' seized ranches and vacation homes in the State of México; and on April 15, 2025 the indictment was unsealed alongside charges against Guerrero-based associate Franco Tabares Martínez for methamphetamine trafficking and against Atlanta-area money launderer Guadalupe Tabares Martínez of Mableton, Georgia, while Treasury sanctioned all four siblings — Johnny, José Alfredo, Ubaldo ('El Flaco') and Adita ('La Venadita') — citing drone bombs, migrant smuggling, arms trafficking and illegal mercury and uranium mining, and the State Department offered rewards of US$5 million and US$3 million. The DEA estimates the group moves more than 36 tons of methamphetamine, 12 tons of heroin and 12 tons of cocaine a year, and the Navy counts its presence in 20 Michoacán, 18 Guerrero, 14 State of México and 4 Morelos municipalities. On May 15, 2025 the National Guard and Army arrested Miguel Ángel Reyna Román, 'El Orejón', described as the group's second in command in the Teloloapan region of Guerrero. In September 2025 the dismembered bodies of Colombian musicians Bayron Sánchez Salazar 'B-King' and Jorge Luis Herrera Lemos 'Regio Clown', abducted in Mexico City on September 16, were found in Cocotitlán, State of México beside a narcomessage signed in the group's name, though investigators also pointed to Unión Tepito operators and treated the message as a possible decoy.\n\nIn 2026 the group remains intact at the top. Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch linked it to the abduction of Taxco's mayor Juan Andrés Vega Carranza and his father Juan Vega Arredondo, seized on April 11 and 12, 2026 and freed on April 13 in a State of México community, an operation reportedly meant to force the release of local plaza boss 'El Gokú', detained on April 8. On July 8, 2026, a day-long gun and drone assault on the ejido of Guajes de Ayala, Coyuca de Catalán, dropped an estimated 100 to 150 bombs and drove families into a clinic in the neighboring community of El Pescado, and on August 4 villagers found a camp with about 120 improvised bomb cylinders, 140 kilograms of gunpowder, 500 tubes of adhesive and gallons of sulfuric acid. Arrests have hit the periphery: Mario Iván Mendoza Martínez, 'El Queso de Puerco', of the 'H1-A1' cell, caught with drugs and a gun in Iztapalapa's Central de Abasto area on July 13, 2026; extortion and retail-drug operators in Toluca and Metepec in August; and on August 20-21, 2026, 70-year sentences for five men who invoked the group's name during an express kidnapping in San Lucas, Aculco, in August 2025. As of September 8, 2026, Johnny, José Alfredo, Ubaldo and Adita Hurtado Olascoaga are all fugitives; no arrest of any of them has been reported.","leaders":[{"name":"José María Chávez Magaña","alias":"El Pony","role":"Leader of the Familia remnant in Guerrero and Estado de México","from":2011,"to":2014,"fate":"Native of Tepalcatepec, Michoacán; captured by the Navy on July 2, 2014 (reported in Pénjamo, Guanajuato, with some accounts placing the operation in Tejupilco); command passed to the Hurtado Olascoaga brothers"},{"name":"Johnny Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Pez / El Mojarro / El Pescado","role":"Top leader","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Born Mar 1, 1973 in Arcelia, Guerrero; fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned Nov 17, 2022 and Apr 15, 2025; indicted in the Northern District of Georgia Sept 24, 2024 (unsealed Apr 15, 2025); US$5 million State Department reward; at large as of Sept 2026"},{"name":"José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Fresa / La Fruta / El Feyo","role":"Second in command; operations in Guerrero and Estado de México; reported liaison with the CJNG","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned Nov 17, 2022 and Apr 15, 2025; indicted in the Northern District of Georgia Sept 24, 2024; US$3 million reward; at large as of Sept 2026"},{"name":"Ubaldo Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Flaco / H1","role":"Chief of extortion, sicario cells and illegal mining, including mercury and uranium","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; Estado de México arrest warrant since June 2024; OFAC-sanctioned Apr 15, 2025; at large"},{"name":"Adita Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"La Venadita","role":"Financial operator, money launderer and, per Treasury, arms procurement from the United States","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned Apr 15, 2025; at large"},{"name":"Rigoberto Sancha Santillán","alias":"El Payaso","role":"Plaza boss for Texcaltitlán, Estado de México","from":2020,"to":2023,"fate":"Killed by villagers in Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán, Dec 8, 2023"},{"name":"Miguel Ángel Reyna Román","alias":"El Orejón","role":"Second in command in the Teloloapan region of Guerrero","from":2020,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested by the National Guard and Army in Teloloapan, Guerrero, May 15, 2025, with three others; linked to homicide, forced disappearance and extortion"},{"name":"Franco Tabares Martínez","alias":"","role":"Guerrero-based associate who supplied multi-kilogram methamphetamine loads to the Atlanta area","from":2021,"to":0,"fate":"Indicted in the Northern District of Georgia for methamphetamine conspiracy; charges announced Apr 15, 2025 alongside those against Atlanta-area money launderer Guadalupe Tabares Martínez, indicted Apr 8, 2025"}],"factions":[{"id":"hurtado_core","name":"Hurtado Olascoaga command (Tuzantla–Arcelia)","from":2014,"to":0,"area":"Tuzantla and eastern Michoacán, Tierra Caliente of Guerrero, southern Estado de México","leader":"Johnny Hurtado Olascoaga","note":"Family-run core; Ubaldo runs enforcement and mining, Adita finances and arms procurement"},{"id":"chaparro_faction","name":"El Chaparro breakaway","from":2022,"to":2022,"area":"Tuzantla, Michoacán","leader":"'El Chaparro'","note":"Associate whose split from the Hurtado brothers triggered the Aug 24, 2022 Tuzantla shootout, in which eight people including three minors died"},{"id":"texcaltitlan_cell","name":"Texcaltitlán cell","from":2020,"to":2023,"area":"Texcaltitlán and Sultepec, Estado de México","leader":"Rigoberto Sancha Santillán 'El Payaso'","note":"Extorted farmers at one peso per square meter; wiped out by villagers on Dec 8, 2023 (10 members killed)"},{"id":"h1a1_cell","name":"H1-A1 cell","from":2024,"to":0,"area":"Iztapalapa, Iztacalco and Nezahualcóyotl (Mexico City metro)","leader":"","note":"Retail drug, kidnapping and extortion cell created to extend the group into eastern Mexico City; member Mario Iván Mendoza Martínez 'El Queso de Puerco' arrested July 13, 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2023,"to":0,"note":"Reported working arrangement from 2023: CJNG supplying drones for LNFM bombing campaigns in Guerrero; Mexican reporting names El Fresa as the liaison"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":2022,"note":"Border war along the Balsas River; 'El Zarco' threatened El Mencho in 2019; CJNG-aligned Los Tequileros attacked LNFM-held Totolapan in Oct 2022"},{"cartel":"other","from":2017,"to":2022,"note":"Los Tequileros, a San Miguel Totolapan group aligned with the CJNG since 2020, carried out the Oct 5, 2022 massacre of 20 people including Mayor Conrado Mendoza Almeda and his father"},{"cartel":"guerreros_unidos","from":2013,"to":0,"note":"Recurring disputes in the Taxco–Iguala corridor of northern Guerrero"},{"cartel":"tlacos","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Clashes in the Sierra of Guerrero and the Atoyac highlands, where LNFM drone attacks displaced villagers in 2023"},{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2023,"to":0,"note":"Villagers of Texcaltitlán (Dec 2023) and communities in Guerrero's Tierra Caliente (2026) have armed themselves against the group's extortion"},{"cartel":"government","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"Failed Petatlán operation (Mar 2023), Operación Bastión (Mar 29, 2025), continuous lieutenant arrests; FTO designation (Feb 20, 2025)"}],"activities":["Fentanyl (including 'rainbow' pills), methamphetamine, heroin and cocaine trafficking to the US, notably Atlanta and Houston","Opium poppy and marijuana cultivation in the Sierra of Guerrero","Extortion of avocado, lime, cattle, timber, agriculture, transport and tortilla sectors","Illegal mining including mercury and uranium","Migrant smuggling across the US southwest border","Fuel theft from the Tula–Toluca pipeline","Kidnapping","Drone-dropped explosives and improvised bombs against rivals and communities","Arms trafficking from the United States","Money laundering through used-clothing trade and US money-service businesses"],"peak":{"from":2021,"to":2026,"states":["MIC","GRO","MEX","MOR"],"note":"Dominant in the tri-state Tierra Caliente and southern Estado de México; Navy counted 56 municipalities across four states in 2025, with satellite cells in Mexico City"},"facts":["Treasury's Nov 17, 2022 designation described operations in about 35 municipalities of Michoacán, Guerrero, Morelos and the State of México and named the group as behind the spread of multicolored 'rainbow fentanyl'","The DEA estimates the group moves more than 36 tons of methamphetamine, 12 tons of heroin and 12 tons of cocaine into the US each year","Designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization on Feb 20, 2025, one of eight groups listed alongside CJNG, Sinaloa, CDN, Gulf, Cárteles Unidos, Tren de Aragua and MS-13","Indicted in the Northern District of Georgia on Sept 24, 2024 (unsealed Apr 15, 2025); on Apr 15, 2025 the US sanctioned all four Hurtado Olascoaga siblings and offered rewards of US$5 million (Johnny) and US$3 million (José Alfredo), US$8 million combined","Oct 5, 2022: 20 people including Mayor Conrado Mendoza Almeda and his father, former mayor Juan Mendoza Acosta, were killed in San Miguel Totolapan by CJNG-aligned Los Tequileros","Dec 8, 2023: villagers of Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán killed 10 extortionists including plaza boss 'El Payaso'; four villagers died and prosecutors ruled it self-defense","Mar 18, 2021: 13 State of México police officers were ambushed and killed at Llano Grande, Coatepec Harinas","Aug 24, 2022: an internal split with 'El Chaparro' produced the Tuzantla shootout; eight people died, three of them minors, and the bodies were found at a butterfly sanctuary near the town","Aug 4, 2026: villagers near El Pescado, Coyuca de Catalán found a camp with about 120 homemade bomb cylinders, 140 kg of gunpowder, 500 tubes of adhesive and sulfuric acid, after the July 8 assault on Guajes de Ayala dropped an estimated 100-150 drone bombs","Mexican Navy tallies place the group in 20 Michoacán, 18 Guerrero, 14 Estado de México and 4 Morelos municipalities, 56 in all"],"status_2026":"Active and structurally intact as of September 8, 2026: all four Hurtado Olascoaga siblings remain fugitives with no reported arrest, and the group continues drone and IED attacks in Guerrero's Tierra Caliente (July-August 2026), extortion across southern Estado de México and cell activity in Mexico City. Authorities linked it to the April 11-13, 2026 abduction and release of Taxco's mayor and his father and have made only mid- and low-level arrests, while US FTO sanctions and the US$8 million in combined rewards remain in force.","sources":["https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1116","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0087","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/leaders-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-and-atlanta-based-money-launderer-indicted","https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndga/pr/leaders-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-and-atlanta-based-money-launderer-indicted","https://ofac.treasury.gov/system/files/2024-06/20240620_nueva_familia_michoacana.pdf","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/02/20/2025-02873/foreign-terrorist-organization-designations-of-tren-de-aragua-mara-salvatrucha-cartel-de-sinaloa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Miguel_Totolapan_massacre","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuzantla_shootout","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/05/20/cae-el-orejon-segundo-al-mando-de-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-en-guerrero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/04/14/familia-michoacana-detras-del-secuestro-del-presidente-municipal-de-taxco-y-de-su-padre-anuncia-harfuch/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2026/07/09/estados/026n1est","https://www.cronica.com.mx/metropoli/2026/07/13/cae-el-queso-de-puerco-de-los-h1-a1-de-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-en-iztapalapa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/21/condenan-a-70-anos-de-prision-a-cinco-presuntos-miembros-de-la-familia-michoacana-por-secuestro-en-el-edomex/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/el-pez-y-el-fresa-20-anos-de-operar-en-impunidad-familia-michoacana-domina-en-4-estados/"],"verified":true},{"id":"santa_rosa_lima","name":"Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima","aka":["CSRL","Cártel Santa Rosa de Lima","Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel","Los Marros (armed wing)","La Marriza (armed wing, 2026 usage)"],"founded":2014,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"GUA","city":"Santa Rosa de Lima, Villagrán","lat":20.56631,"lng":-100.92561},"summary":"A Guanajuato fuel-theft (huachicol) organization built by José Antonio Yépez Ortiz 'El Marro' around the village of Santa Rosa de Lima and the Pemex pipelines feeding off the Salamanca refinery. Its war with the CJNG from 2017 made Guanajuato the state with Mexico's highest absolute homicide count; El Marro was captured on 2 August 2020 and sentenced to 60 years for kidnapping, yet the U.S. Treasury said on 17 December 2025 that he still directs a regenerated CSRL from prison. Since 2020 the cartel has operated as autonomous municipal cells under a collegial leadership of plaza chiefs, with the armed wing 'Los Marros' blamed for the January 2026 Salamanca massacre.","history":"Santa Rosa de Lima is a farming village in Villagrán, Guanajuato, sitting on the web of Pemex pipelines that run from the Salamanca refinery through the Celaya–Salamanca–Villagrán industrial corridor. José Antonio Yépez Ortiz, 'El Marro', born in 1980 in the Villagrán area, moved from cargo theft and small-scale drug dealing around 2010 into fuel theft, and by about 2014 his crew had consolidated as the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima (CSRL). It tapped pipelines, paid villagers as lookouts and laborers, and sold stolen gasoline and diesel to gas stations and factories; Pemex counted 1,696 illegal taps in Guanajuato in 2017, when the state became the national epicenter of huachicol. Some analysts describe the CSRL as a local cell that broke with the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación (CJNG) around 2017, though its roots lay in the village's own pipeline economy.\n\nFrom 2017 the CJNG pushed into the 'Bermuda Triangle' of Celaya, Villagrán, Salamanca and Apaseo to seize the fuel trade, and Guanajuato's homicide rate, 37 per 100,000 in 2017, exploded; INEGI has ranked the state first in absolute homicides every year from 2018 to 2025. On 3 March 2019 President López Obrador's 'Golpe de Timón' put soldiers, marines and federal police into Santa Rosa de Lima to hunt El Marro; his father was detained in a March 2019 navy-and-police operation, and he answered with narco-blockades, torched vehicles and a filmed threat to the president, while villagers on his payroll marched against the troops. On 9 March 2019 gunmen killed 15 people and wounded five at La Playa Men's Club in Salamanca; several of the dead were CFE electricity workers.\n\n2020 brought collapse. Mass arrests in January, when roughly 35 members were swept up, raised the tempo. On Saturday 20 June 2020 state police arrested his mother María Eva Ortiz, a sister and a cousin among 26 people detained at San Isidro Elguera, Celaya, seizing more than 2 million pesos of payroll cash; within hours vehicles were burning at dozens of points across a dozen municipalities and El Marro, weeping in a video, vowed to fight on alone and floated an alliance with the Sinaloa Cartel. A judge freed the women two days later. On 2 August 2020 soldiers, National Guard and state police captured him at a safe house in Franco Tavera, Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas, with five gunmen, rescuing a kidnapped Celaya businesswoman. Guanajuato closed 2020 with 5,370 homicides (INEGI), its worst year.\n\nSuccession was brief. Adán Ochoa, 'El Azul', a financial operator who knew which ducts to tap, was captured on 14 October 2020 in Celaya and sentenced on 19 March 2024 to 29 years. Fernando Emmanuel 'N', 'El Panther', a former municipal police officer who had worked as a bodyguard to Celaya mayors and had undergone cosmetic facial surgery to change his appearance, was captured in early October 2021, announced on 8 October. On 14 January 2022 a Valle de Santiago oral tribunal sentenced El Marro to 60 years for the kidnapping plus a 347,520-peso fine; five years after his capture he had still not been sentenced for fuel theft. The cartel splintered into autonomous municipal cells run by a collegial leadership of plaza chiefs around Celaya, Villagrán, Cortazar, Juventino Rosas and Salamanca; Brandon Alejandro 'El Trucha' was re-arrested in January 2023 and Fernando 'N', 'El Kin', in 2023. Massacres continued, among them the 23 May 2022 attack on the Hotel Gala and two nearby bars in Celaya's Lindavista area that killed 11 people in little more than a minute. INEGI counted 4,333, 4,329, 3,862 and 4,035 homicides in Guanajuato from 2021 to 2024.\n\nBy 2025 U.S. and Mexican officials concluded the CSRL had regenerated. On 17 December 2025 the U.S. Treasury sanctioned the cartel under Executive Order 13851 and El Marro under Executive Order 13581, saying he remained active from prison through intermediaries, that the CSRL had partnered with the Gulf and Sinaloa cartels against the CJNG, had recruited ex-Colombian military and paramilitary personnel to operate in Guanajuato, and trafficked heroin into the United States; Mexican forces had seized 164,000 liters of its stolen hydrocarbons in August 2025.\n\nOn Sunday 25 January 2026 gunmen from 'Los Marros', also called 'La Marriza', the armed wing answering to Mario Eleazar Lara Belman, 'El Negro' or 'Camorro', fired on an amateur soccer match at Loma de Flores, Salamanca, killing 11 and wounding six by the state prosecutor's count; handwritten threats to the CJNG had appeared beforehand. Three suspects were detained by 28 January, and on 19 February the state announced the capture of Moisés Soto Bermúdez, the cell chief who ordered the attack, at Cerrito de las Yerbas, Villagrán, with two accomplices, bringing arrests in the case to six. Security forces seized a cistern with about 35,000 liters of stolen fuel in Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas on 21 February and, on 25 February, more than 55,000 liters, five rifles, tactical gear and 310 boxes of stolen shock absorbers at properties in San Antonio de los Morales and Santa Rosa de Lima; officers were shot at in both operations and two state police were wounded. In May 2026 the FGR told a Guanajuato court that the CSRL was working with the Gulf Cartel while the CJNG had partnered with Los Chapitos for control of huachicol, and by July prosecutors were investigating Pemex personnel for helping drill clandestine taps.\n\nAs of September 2026 the CSRL remains an active if diminished fuel-theft and extortion network anchored in Villagrán, Juventino Rosas, Celaya, Salamanca, Irapuato and Valle de Santiago, still at war with the CJNG. El Marro is in a federal maximum-security prison; El Negro is a fugitive and the state's priority target. Guanajuato's homicides fell to 3,249 deaths in 2025 (INEGI), a 19.5 percent drop from 4,035 in 2024 and a rate of 51 per 100,000, the lowest in seven years, yet the state led Mexico in absolute killings for an eighth straight year.","leaders":[{"name":"José Antonio Yépez Ortiz","alias":"El Marro","role":"Founder and leader","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Captured 2 Aug 2020 at Franco Tavera, Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas; sentenced 14 Jan 2022 to 60 years for kidnapping by a Valle de Santiago tribunal plus a 347,520-peso fine; still not sentenced for fuel theft as of mid-2025; sanctioned by OFAC under E.O. 13581 on 17 Dec 2025 for allegedly directing the CSRL from prison"},{"name":"Adán Ochoa","alias":"El Azul / Comandante Azul","role":"Financial operator; successor leader after El Marro's arrest","from":2020,"to":2020,"fate":"Arrested 14 Oct 2020 in Celaya, Guanajuato; sentenced 19 Mar 2024 to 29 years. Several Mexican outlets give his full name as Adán González Ochoa"},{"name":"Fernando Emmanuel N","alias":"El Panther / El Pantera","role":"Operational leader designated after El Azul's capture","from":2020,"to":2021,"fate":"Captured in Guanajuato, announced 8 Oct 2021; a former municipal police officer and bodyguard to Celaya mayors who had undergone cosmetic facial surgery to evade capture"},{"name":"Brandon Alejandro N","alias":"El Trucha","role":"Cell leader and hit-squad chief in the Celaya–Villagrán corridor","from":2020,"to":2023,"fate":"Arrested twice; second detention reported 22-26 January 2023"},{"name":"Fernando N","alias":"El Kin / El Ranchero","role":"Regional operational chief (reported)","from":2021,"to":2023,"fate":"Reported arrested in Guanajuato in 2023; the specific date of 20 May 2023 could not be independently confirmed"},{"name":"Mario Eleazar Lara Belman","alias":"El Negro / Camorro","role":"Head of 'Los Marros' / 'La Marriza', the CSRL armed wing","from":2024,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; named Guanajuato's priority target after the 25 Jan 2026 Salamanca massacre; his family clan is reported to be tied to the CSRL's founding"},{"name":"Moisés Soto Bermúdez","alias":"","role":"Chief of the Los Marros cell that carried out the Salamanca massacre, answering to 'El Negro'","from":2025,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested at Cerrito de las Yerbas, Villagrán, announced 19 Feb 2026, with two accomplices, Jorge Gabriel 'N' and Margarita 'N'"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_marros","name":"Los Marros / La Marriza","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Salamanca, Irapuato, Valle de Santiago, Villagrán, Celaya, Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas (Guanajuato)","leader":"Mario Eleazar Lara Belman 'El Negro'; operations run by Moisés Soto Bermúdez until Feb 2026","note":"Armed wing that emerged after El Marro's 2020 capture, loyal to the Yépez family; blamed for the 25 Jan 2026 Loma de Flores soccer-field massacre in Salamanca (11 dead, six wounded per the state prosecutor) preceded by narcomantas threatening the CJNG, and for some 20 deaths in a single week that January"},{"id":"huachicol_core","name":"Santa Rosa de Lima / San Antonio de los Morales fuel-theft network","from":2014,"to":0,"area":"Villagrán and Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas pipeline corridor","leader":"Yépez family circle","note":"The original pipeline-tapping economy; about 35,000 L of fuel seized in Juventino Rosas on 21 Feb 2026 and more than 55,000 L in San Antonio de los Morales and Santa Rosa de Lima on 25 Feb 2026, plus 164,000 L in Aug 2025 per the U.S. Treasury"},{"id":"celulas_municipales","name":"Municipal cells under a collegial plaza-chief leadership","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Celaya, Villagrán, Cortazar, Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas, Salamanca, Irapuato","leader":"Rotating plaza chiefs (dirigencia colegiada)","note":"After El Marro's capture the CSRL fragmented into autonomous municipal cells coordinated by a council of plaza chiefs rather than a single boss, which is why successors fell in rapid sequence between 2020 and 2023"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"Alliance cited by the U.S. Treasury (17 Dec 2025) and by the FGR in a Guanajuato court (24-25 May 2026) for control of huachicol against a CJNG–Los Chapitos bloc"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"El Marro floated a Sinaloa alliance in his June 2020 video; the U.S. Treasury (17 Dec 2025) lists the Sinaloa Cartel among the CSRL's partners against the CJNG"},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2019,"to":2020,"note":"InSight Crime reports Los Viagras and Cárteles Unidos supplied personnel and matériel to the CSRL against the CJNG in Guanajuato, though the cooperation never produced consolidated gains"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"War for the Celaya–Salamanca–Villagrán 'Bermuda Triangle' fuel corridor; drove Guanajuato to Mexico's highest absolute homicide counts 2018-2025"}],"activities":["Fuel theft from Pemex pipelines (huachicol) and resale to gas stations and industry","Extortion of small and medium businesses, bars and transport","Kidnapping for ransom","Local methamphetamine and drug retail; heroin trafficking to the U.S. per the U.S. Treasury","Cargo and auto-parts theft","Contract killings and massacres against CJNG-linked targets","Narco-blockades and vehicle burnings as a coercive tactic","Money laundering through local businesses","Corruption of Pemex personnel to drill clandestine taps (under FGR investigation, 2026)"],"peak":{"from":2017,"to":2019,"states":["GUA"],"note":"Controlled much of the pipeline-tapping economy around the Salamanca refinery and resisted both the CJNG and the federal 'Golpe de Timón'; Guanajuato became the state with Mexico's highest absolute homicide count"},"facts":["Pemex recorded 1,696 illegal pipeline taps in Guanajuato in 2017, the CSRL's home turf and the peak of the national huachicol crisis","Guanajuato has ranked first in Mexico for absolute homicides every year from 2018 to 2025 (INEGI), peaking at 5,370 in 2020","On 20 June 2020, 26 people including El Marro's mother María Eva Ortiz were detained at San Isidro Elguera, Celaya, with more than 2 million pesos in payroll cash, touching off vehicle burnings and blockades across a dozen Guanajuato municipalities","El Marro was captured on 2 August 2020 in Franco Tavera, Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas; a kidnapped businesswoman was rescued from the safe house","On 14 January 2022 a Valle de Santiago tribunal sentenced him to 60 years for kidnapping plus a 347,520-peso fine, but five years after his capture he had still not been sentenced for fuel theft","Gunmen killed 15 people and wounded five at La Playa Men's Club in Salamanca on 9 March 2019, and 11 more at Celaya's Hotel Gala and two nearby bars on 23 May 2022","The U.S. Treasury sanctioned the CSRL under E.O. 13851 and El Marro under E.O. 13581 on 17 December 2025, citing 164,000 liters of stolen hydrocarbons seized in August 2025, the recruitment of ex-Colombian military personnel, and alliances with the Gulf and Sinaloa cartels","The 25 January 2026 Salamanca soccer-field massacre killed 11 and wounded six; six people including cell chief Moisés Soto Bermúdez had been arrested by 19 February 2026","Guanajuato recorded 3,249 homicide deaths in 2025 (INEGI), a 19.5 percent drop from 4,035 in 2024 and a rate of 51 per 100,000, its lowest in seven years, while still leading the country in absolute killings for an eighth year"],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the CSRL is active but diminished: El Marro remains in federal maximum-security custody while the U.S. Treasury and Mexican prosecutors say he still issues orders through intermediaries, and the 'Los Marros'/'La Marriza' wing under fugitive Mario Eleazar Lara Belman is fighting the CJNG around Salamanca, Villagrán, Irapuato and Celaya, reportedly with Gulf Cartel backing against a CJNG–Los Chapitos bloc. State and federal forces seized more than 90,000 liters of stolen fuel in two February 2026 raids that drew gunfire, and arrested the Salamanca massacre cell chief; Guanajuato's homicide rate is at a seven-year low, though the state still leads Mexico in absolute killings.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/mexico-santa-rosa-de-lima-cartel/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0339","https://www.milenio.com/policia/el-marro-sentencian-60-anos-lider-cartel-santa-rosa-lima","https://smallwarsjournal.com/2020/08/18/mexican-cartel-strategic-note-no-30-el-marro-jose-antonio-yepez-ortiz-leader-cartel-santa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/01/29/que-se-sabe-de-los-marros-celula-del-cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima-a-la-que-se-le-atribuye-masacre-en-salamanca/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/02/19/detienen-a-lider-de-cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima-que-ordeno-masacre-en-salamanca/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/02/25/dan-segundo-golpe-al-cartel-de-santa-rosa-en-guanajuato-decomisan-mas-de-55-mil-libros-de-huachicol/","https://www.am.com.mx/guanajuato/2026/08/03/guanajuato-tuvo-menor-tasa-de-homicidios-en-7-anos-pero-siguio-como-puntero-en-mexico-1827258.html","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/05/24/estados/cjng-se-alio-con-los-chapitos-y-cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima-con-el-del-golfo-para-controlar-huachicol-fgr-en-guanajuato","https://www.bbc.com/mundo/articles/salamanca-cancha-futbol-ataque-2026"],"verified":true},{"id":"carteles_unidos","name":"Cárteles Unidos","aka":["CU","United Cartels","Cárteles Unidos de Michoacán","Cártel del Abuelo / Cártel de Tepalcatepec (core faction)"],"founded":2019,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"MIC","city":"Tepalcatepec","lat":19.19206,"lng":-102.8454},"summary":"A coalition of Michoacán Tierra Caliente crime groups and ex-self-defense militias, led by Juan José Farías Álvarez 'El Abuelo' of Tepalcatepec, formed from 2019 and consolidated in May 2020 to stop the CJNG's advance from Jalisco. It fights a trench, drone and landmine war around Aguililla and Tepalcatepec, extorts lime and avocado producers, and was designated a U.S. Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025; the United States has offered $26 million in rewards for five of its leaders, $18 million of it still outstanding.","history":"Cárteles Unidos (CU) grew out of two decades of upheaval in Michoacán's Tierra Caliente, and revives a label first used in 2010, when Sinaloa Cartel leader Ignacio Coronel Villarreal knitted together Sinaloa, Gulf and Templar gunmen against Los Zetas. Juan José Farías Álvarez, 'El Abuelo', a rancher and cheese merchant from Tepalcatepec tied to the Milenio (Valencia) Cartel in the 1990s and 2000s, was jailed in 2009 on weapons and drug charges and freed when the case collapsed. When farmers took up arms against the Knights Templar in February 2013, he financed and led Tepalcatepec's self-defense group. In Buenavista, the Sierra Santana brothers, a clan of cockfighting breeders whose best-known figures are Carlos and Nicolás 'El Gordo', turned dissident Templars and Familia members into Los Viagras in 2013-2014; on 6 January 2015 a confrontation with federal police in Apatzingán left 10 civilians dead and 21 wounded, and in February 2015 the group helped corner Templar boss Servando Gómez 'La Tuta'. Both networks then absorbed the rackets they had displaced: methamphetamine, and extortion of avocado, lime and cattle producers. The navy arrested El Abuelo on 27 May 2018 over alleged CJNG ties; supporters blockaded roads and burned vehicles, and a judge freed him on 30 May for irregularities in the detention.\n\nThe catalyst for the coalition was the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación, whose leader Nemesio Oseguera comes from Aguililla, next door to Tepalcatepec, and with whom El Abuelo had once been allied. On 30 August 2019 a CJNG column attacked Tepalcatepec and was repulsed in an hours-long battle; local counts put the toll at nine dead and more than ten wounded, though federal and municipal authorities disagreed on the figures. El Abuelo's Cártel de Tepalcatepec federated with Los Viagras, Los Blancos de Troya of the Apatzingán valley, the Cártel de Los Reyes and remnants of La Familia and the Templars, and in May 2020 he publicly called on vigilante groups in four municipalities to unite against the CJNG, the moment InSight Crime treats as the alliance's consolidation. CU fought like a militia coalition: in September 2020 it paraded a home-built armored 'tank' through Bonifacio Moreno, known as El Aguaje, in Aguililla; in November 2020 it dug trenches around Tepalcatepec and Coalcomán; and on 3 December 2020 CJNG raids on Los Reyes, Cotija, Tocumbo and Tingüindín left seven dead.\n\nIn 2021 the war concentrated on Aguililla. On 30 March 2021 former Aguililla mayor Adalberto Fructuoso Comparán, 'El Fruto', a CU chief, was captured in Guatemala for brokering a half-ton methamphetamine shipment to Florida; he was sent to the United States and sentenced to 10 years in February 2023. Through April the CJNG cut the roads and fought for the town, and both sides began dropping homemade bombs from drones, an attack around 20 April wounding two police officers and another, in January 2022, hitting civilians. The CJNG held Aguililla for months until the army and National Guard pushed in and reopened the highway on 9-10 February 2022. Landmines followed, killing farmers, soldiers and police. Extortion of the lime belt of Apatzingán, Buenavista and Tepalcatepec provoked work stoppages on 16 August 2023 and again from 12 August 2024, when growers idled more than 23,000 hectares and 34 packing houses for eight days after quotas of one peso per kilo from grower and packer were doubled, sending lime to 60 pesos a kilo. On 29 June 2023 Viagras gunmen killed self-defense founder Hipólito Mora in La Ruana; suspects were still being detained in January and July 2026.\n\nPressure mounted from 2024. Milenio described a 'volatile alliance' between Los Viagras and the CJNG in October 2025, and by March 2026 reported that Los Viagras had turned on the CJNG to keep the plazas, straining the coalition further. On the night of 27 May 2025 a landmine in Los Reyes killed eight National Guard troops of the FERI reaction force returning from a CJNG camp in Jalisco, the deadliest IED strike on Mexican security forces on record, initially reported as six dead; most reporting blamed the CJNG, in an area contested with CU's Cártel de Los Reyes. The United States designated CU a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025 and on 14 August 2025 announced $26 million in rewards for five leaders ($10 million for El Abuelo, $5 million each for Nicolás 'El Gordo' Sierra Santana and Alfonso Fernández Magallón 'Poncho la Quiringua', $3 million each for Luis Enrique Barragán 'R5' and Edgar Orozco 'El Kamoni'), while OFAC sanctioned El Gordo, 'La Sirena' and 'El Bótox'. Tepalcatepec mayor Martha Mendoza Mendoza was shot dead with her husband outside her home on 17 June 2025, and Apatzingán citrus leader Bernardo Bravo was found tortured and killed on 20 October 2025. After a CJNG cell assassinated Uruapan's independent mayor Carlos Manzo on 1 November 2025, President Sheinbaum launched the 57-billion-peso, twelve-axis Plan Michoacán por la Paz y la Justicia on 9 November 2025; between 10 November and 20 January 2026 it reported 430 arrests, 240 firearms, 200 explosive devices and 773 kilos of methamphetamine.\n\n2026 has been CU's hardest year. El Bótox, named as the intellectual author of Bernardo Bravo's murder and carrying seven arrest warrants, was captured without a shot in Apatzingán on 22 January, and 24 properties linked to him were searched; reporting that month said the Blancos de Troya's alliance with El Abuelo had ended. On 18 April hundreds of soldiers, marines and police raided Tepalcatepec to seize El Abuelo; they missed him but detained a relative, Víctor Noé 'N', described variously as his son or brother, with rifles and a false passport, triggering blockades. His operator 'El Repollo' fell on 29 May and was charged on 15 June. On 2 July a federal grand jury in Washington charged his son Juan José Farías Mendoza 'Juanjo' and nephew Israel Vega Farías 'Papo' with methamphetamine conspiracy, material support to a terrorist organization and firearms offenses; on 3 July Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch confirmed Mexican warrants for father and son. On 16 July the State Department designated Los Viagras, and the Juárez Cartel, as separate FTOs. Poncho la Quiringua was captured on 1 August and sent to the Altiplano prison amid blockades, and R5, who had taken over the Cártel de Los Reyes and its 'Fuerzas Especiales Wicho' hit squad, was killed by the army at Rodeo del Pinal, Tocumbo, on Monday 31 August, confirmed by Sedena and García Harfuch on 1 September; a 15-year-old bodyguard died with him.\n\nAs of September 2026 CU survives as a weakened coalition holding Tepalcatepec, parts of Buenavista and Apatzingán and the Los Reyes–Tocumbo hills, while the CJNG dominates Aguililla, Uruapan and the coast and Jesús Ochoa Chávez 'La Venada' is reported as the Cártel de Los Reyes's new chief. El Abuelo, El Gordo and El Kamoni remain fugitives with $18 million in U.S. rewards on their heads, and mines and IEDs have killed at least 18 military personnel in Michoacán since 2024.","leaders":[{"name":"Juan José Farías Álvarez","alias":"El Abuelo","role":"Overall leader; head of the Cártel de Tepalcatepec","from":2019,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; arrested 2009 (case collapsed) and 27 May 2018 over alleged CJNG ties, freed 30 May 2018 for irregularities after blockades; charged in the District of Columbia with a $10 million U.S. reward announced 14 Aug 2025; evaded the 18 Apr 2026 Tepalcatepec raid; Mexican arrest warrant confirmed by García Harfuch on 3 Jul 2026"},{"name":"Nicolás Sierra Santana","alias":"El Gordo / El Coruco","role":"Leader of Los Viagras, with his brother Carlos Sierra Santana, at the head of the Sierra Santana clan","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; born 10 Sep 1977; OFAC-sanctioned and given a $5 million U.S. reward on 14 Aug 2025; suspected of ordering the 29 Jun 2023 murder of Hipólito Mora; Los Viagras designated a separate FTO on 16 Jul 2026"},{"name":"Alfonso Fernández Magallón","alias":"Poncho la Quiringua","role":"Leader of the Cártel de Los Reyes","from":2019,"to":2026,"fate":"Captured by federal forces in Michoacán on 1 Aug 2026 and moved to the Altiplano prison amid blockades; accused of drone attacks on troops, extortion and recruiting ex-Colombian military; a $5 million U.S. reward had been offered"},{"name":"Luis Enrique Barragán Chávez","alias":"R5 / El Güicho / El Wicho","role":"Head of the 'Fuerzas Especiales Wicho' hit squad; led the Cártel de Los Reyes after Poncho's capture","from":2026,"to":2026,"fate":"Killed in an army operation at Rodeo del Pinal, Tocumbo, on Monday 31 Aug 2026, confirmed by Sedena and García Harfuch on 1 Sept; a 15-year-old bodyguard died with him; $3 million U.S. reward"},{"name":"Jesús Ochoa Chávez","alias":"La Venada","role":"Reported successor at the head of the Cártel de Los Reyes","from":2026,"to":0,"fate":"At large; named in Mexican reporting from 2 September 2026 as the presumed new leader after R5's death"},{"name":"Edgar Orozco Cabadas","alias":"El Kamoni","role":"Chief of sicarios under El Abuelo","from":2019,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; $3 million U.S. reward announced 14 Aug 2025"},{"name":"César Alejandro Sepúlveda Arellano","alias":"El Bótox","role":"Leader of Los Blancos de Troya (Apatzingán extortion cell)","from":2019,"to":2026,"fate":"Born 26 Nov 1982; OFAC-sanctioned 14 Aug 2025; arrested without a shot in Apatzingán on 22 Jan 2026 under Plan Michoacán carrying seven arrest warrants, and named as the intellectual author of Bernardo Bravo's murder; 24 linked properties were searched"},{"name":"Heladio Cisneros Flores","alias":"La Sirena","role":"Los Viagras plaza chief in La Ruana, Buenavista","from":2018,"to":0,"fate":"Born 21 Feb 1986; OFAC-sanctioned 14 Aug 2025; at large; suspected in Hipólito Mora's murder and wanted for aggravated homicide and robbery"},{"name":"Adalberto Fructuoso Comparán Rodríguez","alias":"El Fruto","role":"Former Aguililla mayor; CU chief and meth broker","from":2019,"to":2021,"fate":"Captured in Guatemala, reported 30 Mar 2021, over a half-ton meth shipment bound for Florida; sent to the United States and sentenced to 10 years in February 2023"},{"name":"Juan José Farías Mendoza","alias":"Juanjo","role":"Son of El Abuelo; senior member","from":2022,"to":0,"fate":"Charged by a District of Columbia federal grand jury on 2 Jul 2026 with methamphetamine conspiracy, material support to a designated foreign terrorist organization and firearms offenses, alongside his cousin Israel Vega Farías 'Papo'; fugitive with a Mexican warrant confirmed 3 Jul 2026"}],"factions":[{"id":"cartel_de_tepalcatepec","name":"Cártel de Tepalcatepec (Cártel del Abuelo)","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Tepalcatepec, Coalcomán and the Jalisco border","leader":"Juan José Farías Álvarez 'El Abuelo'","note":"Core of the coalition; ex-autodefensa network that dug trenches around Tepalcatepec in Nov 2020; target of the 18 Apr 2026 federal raid that instead netted a relative, Víctor Noé 'N'"},{"id":"los_viagras","name":"Los Viagras","from":2014,"to":0,"area":"Buenavista (La Ruana), Apatzingán, Parácuaro","leader":"Nicolás Sierra Santana 'El Gordo' and the Sierra Santana clan","note":"Emerged 2013-2014 from dissident Templars and Familia members; clashed with federal police in Apatzingán on 6 Jan 2015 (10 civilians dead, 21 wounded); killed Hipólito Mora on 29 Jun 2023; Milenio described a 'volatile alliance' with the CJNG in Oct 2025 and reported a rupture with it in Mar 2026; designated a separate FTO on 16 Jul 2026"},{"id":"blancos_de_troya","name":"Los Blancos de Troya","from":2019,"to":2026,"area":"Apatzingán lime valley","leader":"César Alejandro Sepúlveda Arellano 'El Bótox' (arrested 22 Jan 2026)","note":"Extortion cell over lime growers and packers, blamed for the 20 Oct 2025 murder of citrus leader Bernardo Bravo; reported in Oct 2025 as part of a triple extortion arrangement with the CJNG and Los Viagras, and in Jan 2026 as having broken with El Abuelo"},{"id":"cartel_de_los_reyes","name":"Cártel de Los Reyes","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Los Reyes, Tocumbo, Cotija, Tingüindín","leader":"Alfonso Fernández Magallón 'Poncho la Quiringua' (captured 1 Aug 2026), then 'R5' (killed 31 Aug 2026), then reportedly Jesús Ochoa Chávez 'La Venada'","note":"Runs the 'Fuerzas Especiales Wicho' sicario unit and is accused of recruiting former Colombian soldiers as explosives experts; operates in the area where the 27 May 2025 landmine killed eight National Guard troops, an attack most reporting attributed to the CJNG"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2013,"to":2019,"note":"El Abuelo's Tepalcatepec militia and Los Viagras emerged from the 2013-2014 self-defense movement against the Knights Templar before absorbing the rackets they displaced"},{"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima","from":2019,"to":2020,"note":"Los Viagras and CU reportedly supplied personnel and matériel to the CSRL against the CJNG in Guanajuato (InSight Crime)"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2019,"to":2021,"note":"Remnant cells of La Familia and the Knights Templar joined the coalition against the CJNG (InSight Crime)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Existential war since the 30 Aug 2019 attack on Tepalcatepec; sieges of Aguililla 2021-22, drones, landmines. Complicated from 2024-2025 by a 'volatile alliance' between Los Viagras and the CJNG that ruptured in early 2026"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2013,"to":2015,"note":"Founding members fought the Knights Templar as autodefensas and helped corner La Tuta in Feb 2015"}],"activities":["Methamphetamine production and trafficking (a half-ton shipment to Florida brokered in 2021; U.S. indictments allege distribution in the U.S., Europe and Australia)","Cocaine trafficking from Colombia (per U.S. indictments)","Marijuana trafficking","Extortion of lime, avocado and cattle producers and packing houses","Kidnapping and contract killings","Weaponized drones and improvised explosive devices/landmines","Recruitment of former Colombian soldiers as explosives specialists (per Mexican authorities)","Taxing trafficking routes from Lázaro Cárdenas and the Pacific coast toward Guadalajara"],"peak":{"from":2020,"to":2022,"states":["MIC"],"note":"Held Tepalcatepec, Buenavista, Apatzingán, Coalcomán, Los Reyes and the Cotija–Tingüindín belt behind trenches and checkpoints, contesting Aguililla with the CJNG"},"facts":["The 30 August 2019 CJNG assault on Tepalcatepec that triggered the coalition left nine dead and more than ten wounded by local counts, with federal and municipal authorities disputing the toll","The CJNG fought for Aguililla through April 2021 and cut its roads for months until the army and National Guard reopened the highway on 9-10 February 2022","A landmine in Los Reyes killed eight National Guard troops of the FERI reaction force on the night of 27 May 2025, the deadliest IED strike on Mexican security forces on record; mines and explosives have killed at least 18 military personnel in Michoacán since 2024","The August 2024 lime strike, begun on 12 August, idled more than 23,000 hectares and 34 packing plants for eight days after extortion quotas of one peso per kilo from grower and packer were doubled; lime reached 60 pesos per kilo","On 14 August 2025 the U.S. announced $26 million in rewards for five CU leaders, including $10 million for El Abuelo and $5 million each for Nicolás 'El Gordo' Sierra Santana and 'Poncho la Quiringua'; $18 million remains outstanding","Cárteles Unidos was designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025; Los Viagras received a separate designation, alongside the Juárez Cartel, published in the Federal Register on 16 July 2026","Federal police killed 10 civilians and wounded 21 in Apatzingán on 6 January 2015 while confronting Los Viagras, who had seized the town hall","Plan Michoacán, launched 9 November 2025 with more than 57 billion pesos and 12 axes after mayor Carlos Manzo's murder, reported 430 arrests, 240 firearms, 200 explosive devices and 773 kilos of methamphetamine between 10 November 2025 and 20 January 2026","In 2026 the coalition lost El Bótox (arrested 22 January), Poncho la Quiringua (captured 1 August) and R5 (killed 31 August in Tocumbo), while El Abuelo's son and nephew were indicted in Washington on 2 July"],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 Cárteles Unidos is under its heaviest pressure yet: El Bótox, Poncho la Quiringua and R5 have been removed in 2026, El Abuelo's son and nephew face U.S. terrorism-support charges, and Plan Michoacán forces raided Tepalcatepec in April. El Abuelo, El Gordo and El Kamoni remain fugitives with $18 million in U.S. rewards, Jesús Ochoa Chávez 'La Venada' is reported as the Cártel de Los Reyes's new chief, and the coalition still holds Tepalcatepec and parts of Buenavista, Apatzingán and the Los Reyes–Tocumbo hills. Its war with the CJNG continues with drones and landmines, complicated by Los Viagras' on-again, off-again dealings with the Jalisco cartel.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/carteles-unidos/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/02/recompensas-de-eeuu-por-carteles-unidos-quienes-siguen-profugos-tras-la-captura-de-poncho-la-quiringua-y-la-muerte-del-r5/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/07/02/eu-acusa-al-hijo-y-sobrino-de-el-abuelo-lider-de-carteles-unidos-por-narcotrafico-y-otros-dos-cargos/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/viagras-quienes-son-gordo-sirena-botox-narcos-sancionados-por-eu","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/u-s-designates-2-more-mexican-cartels-as-foreign-terrorist-organizations","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/lime-producers-strike-michoacan/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/landmine-explosion-kills-8/","https://www.unotv.com/estados/michoacan/tres-meses-asesinato-carlos-manzo-ultimos-resultados-plan-michoacan/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/09/01/estados/confirman-muerte-de-el-r5-identificado-como-lider-del-cartel-los-reyes","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Viagras"],"verified":true},{"id":"sonora","name":"Sonora Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Sonora","Caro Quintero Organization","Los Caro Quintero"],"founded":1987,"dissolved":2007,"parent":"guadalajara","origin":{"state":"SON","city":"Agua Prieta","lat":31.3258,"lng":-109.5487},"summary":"The Sonora Cartel was the Caro Quintero family's share of the Guadalajara Cartel, assigned to Miguel Caro Quintero at the 1987 Acapulco meeting and run independently after Félix Gallardo's 1989 arrest. From Agua Prieta, Hermosillo and the family stronghold of Caborca it moved multi-ton monthly loads of marijuana, plus cocaine, across the Arizona desert. Miguel's December 2001 arrest ended it as an independent force; its routes were absorbed by the Sinaloa Federation, and the family later resurfaced as the Caborca Cartel.","history":"The Caro Quintero clan comes from La Noria, in the mountains of Badiraguato, Sinaloa. Rafael Caro Quintero built the marijuana arm of the Guadalajara Cartel in the early 1980s and, according to US court records, trafficked with his younger brother Miguel (born 1963 in Caborca, Sonora) between 1982 and 1986. After Rafael's arrest in Costa Rica on 4 April 1985, Miguel took over the family's Sonoran plantations and border crossings. At the 1987 Acapulco meeting Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo formally assigned the Sonora corridor to him, and when Félix Gallardo was arrested on 8 April 1989 the Sonora branch became an autonomous organization that the DEA labelled the Caro Quintero Organization or Sonora Cartel, run by Miguel with his brothers Jorge and Genaro.\n\nThe DEA described it in 1998 as one of Mexico's oldest and best-established trafficking groups, specializing in the cultivation, production and distribution of marijuana while also moving cocaine and some methamphetamine. Marijuana grown in Sinaloa, Durango and southern Sonora was flown in small aircraft to ranches in northern Sonora and then carried across the desolate border between San Luis Río Colorado-Yuma in the west and Agua Prieta-Douglas in the east by horses and human backpackers, in multi-ton quantities every month. Loads were staged in Agua Prieta and Hermosillo and delivered to distribution cells in Arizona, Colorado, California and Nevada. Miguel was indicted in federal courts in Colorado and Arizona; in his 2009 guilty plea he admitted moving more than 100 tons of marijuana into the United States and repatriating more than 100 million dollars.\n\nThe organization thrived on impunity. Miguel was arrested in 1992 on tax-evasion charges, but a federal judge in Hermosillo dismissed the case; the DEA attributed the outcome to threats and bribes. Through the 1990s he was among Mexico's most wanted men, with a US reward on his head, while the family's wealth in ranches, cattle and real estate around Caborca and Hermosillo remained largely untouched. The Sonora Cartel coexisted with, and increasingly depended on, the Sinaloa traffickers Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada and Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, who controlled the growing areas and the cocaine supply.\n\nThe end came in December 2001, when agents of Mexico's new Federal Investigation Agency arrested Miguel Caro Quintero in Los Mochis, Sinaloa, on a provisional warrant for extradition, carrying a .38 Super pistol. He was held in the Altiplano prison and later in Cefereso 3 in Matamoros, fought extradition for seven years, and was handed to US marshals on 25 February 2009. He pleaded guilty in Denver on 23 October 2009 to racketeering conspiracy (Colorado) and marijuana-distribution conspiracy (Arizona) and on 4 February 2010 was sentenced to 17 years. He served most of the term at ADX Florence, Colorado, earning reductions for good conduct, poetry and English classes, and was deported on 20 July 2019 through the El Chaparral crossing into Tijuana at age 56. Mexican authorities issued no charges, and he has kept a low profile since. Without him the Sonora Cartel lost its independence; by the mid-2000s the corridor was run as a branch of the Sinaloa Federation, and after the 2008 Beltrán Leyva split Sonora became one of the country's most contested border regions.\n\nThe family name returned to the map after Rafael Caro Quintero walked free on 9 August 2013. From Caborca he rebuilt a network known as the Caborca Cartel, with an armed wing called La Barredora 24/7 and alliances with La Línea, which from 2020 fought Los Chapitos' local allies, Los Salazar and Los Deltas, for Sonoyta, Caborca and Pitiquito. Rafael's recapture on 15 July 2022 and his transfer to the United States on 27 February 2025 weakened it; a US indictment unsealed in the Southern District of New York in May 2026 against ten defendants including former Sinaloa governor Rubén Rocha Moya described Los Chapitos as controlling the Sonora and Baja California trafficking corridors, which Mexican press read as Los Chapitos gaining ground at Caborca's expense. Even so, on 11 June 2026 police in Jilotzingo, State of Mexico, arrested three young Sinaloans who identified themselves as Caborca Cartel members, one claiming to be a nephew (some outlets said grandson) of Caro Quintero; the group has also been reported operating in Quintana Roo and Yucatán, where a Caborca plaza chief for Chetumal was arrested in Mérida in December 2024 and a January 2026 triple homicide in Dzilam González was attributed to it. On 29 August 2026 banners signed 'Cártel de Caborca' appeared in the Campeche municipalities of Hecelchakán, Tenabo and Hopelchén accusing state security officers of extortion; Campeche prosecutors said the attribution was unconfirmed.\n\nAs of September 2026 the original Sonora Cartel is defunct. Its founder Miguel Caro Quintero is free in Mexico; his brother Rafael awaits a March 2027 trial in Brooklyn; and the Sonora corridor is disputed between the Caborca remnants, Los Chapitos and La Mayiza, with the Caborca brand appearing opportunistically in the Yucatán peninsula.","leaders":[{"name":"Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero","alias":"El Miguelón","role":"Boss of the Sonora Cartel","from":1987,"to":2001,"fate":"Arrested Dec 2001 in Los Mochis by AFI agents; extradited 25 Feb 2009; pleaded guilty 23 Oct 2009; sentenced 4 Feb 2010 in Denver to 17 years; held at ADX Florence; deported to Mexico 20 Jul 2019 via El Chaparral, Tijuana; free and low-profile as of 2026"},{"name":"Rafael Caro Quintero","alias":"El Narco de Narcos","role":"Family patriarch; founder of the marijuana network","from":1987,"to":1989,"fate":"Imprisoned 1985-2013; after release founded the Caborca Cartel; recaptured 15 Jul 2022; in US custody since 27 Feb 2025 awaiting trial set for 8 Mar 2027"},{"name":"Jorge Caro Quintero","alias":"","role":"Brother; lieutenant for Sonora operations","from":1989,"to":2001,"fate":"Named by DEA as co-leader in 1998; no public record of arrest"},{"name":"Genaro Caro Quintero","alias":"","role":"Brother; lieutenant","from":1989,"to":2001,"fate":"Named by DEA as co-leader in 1998; no public record of arrest"}],"factions":[{"id":"agua_prieta_corridor","name":"Agua Prieta-Douglas corridor","from":1987,"to":2001,"area":"Northeastern Sonora / Cochise County, Arizona","leader":"Miguel Caro Quintero","note":"Backpacker and horse routes across the desert; staging in Agua Prieta and Hermosillo"},{"id":"caborca_base","name":"Caborca family base","from":1987,"to":2007,"area":"Caborca, Pitiquito, Sonoyta","leader":"Caro Quintero family","note":"Ranch and cattle holdings; later the seat of Rafael's Caborca Cartel after 2013"},{"id":"sinaloa_cultivation","name":"Sinaloa-Durango cultivation network","from":1987,"to":2001,"area":"Badiraguato, Los Mochis, Durango highlands","leader":"Miguel Caro Quintero","note":"Marijuana fields and airstrips feeding the Sonora corridor"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"guadalajara","from":1987,"to":1989,"note":"Sonora plaza assigned by Félix Gallardo at the 1987 Acapulco meeting"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":1989,"to":2007,"note":"Shared Sinaloan roots and supply; the corridor was progressively absorbed into the Sinaloa Federation after Miguel's 2001 arrest"},{"cartel":"other","from":1989,"to":2001,"note":"Colombian suppliers (Cali and Medellín remnants) for cocaine loads moved through Sonora"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"us_government","from":1988,"to":2010,"note":"Indictments in Colorado and Arizona; DEA reward; extradition 2009 and Denver conviction 2010"},{"cartel":"government","from":1992,"to":2001,"note":"1992 tax case dismissed in Hermosillo; AFI arrest in Los Mochis in December 2001"},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":1989,"to":1993,"note":"Tension with the Arellano Félix organization over Baja California and Sonora crossings during the post-Guadalajara wars"}],"activities":["Marijuana cultivation and smuggling (multi-ton monthly loads via backpackers, horses and light aircraft)","Cocaine transshipment into Arizona","Limited methamphetamine trafficking (1990s)","Money laundering through ranches, cattle and real estate in Sonora","Bribery of local police and judiciary"],"peak":{"from":1989,"to":1997,"states":["SON","SIN","DUR","JAL","NAY"],"note":"Controlled the Sonora-Arizona desert corridor from San Luis Río Colorado to Agua Prieta, moving multi-ton monthly marijuana loads"},"facts":["Miguel Caro Quintero admitted in his October 2009 US plea to trafficking more than 100 tons of marijuana and sending more than 100 million dollars back to Mexico","The DEA reported in 1998 that the organization smuggled multi-ton quantities per month across the desert between Yuma and Douglas using horses and human backpackers","Miguel's 1992 tax-evasion case in Hermosillo was dismissed; the DEA blamed threats and bribes","He fought extradition for more than seven years after his December 2001 arrest before being sent to Denver on 25 February 2009","Sentenced to 17 years on 4 February 2010, he served about 10 years in US custody, much of it at ADX Florence, and was deported on 20 July 2019","His brother Rafael's rebuilt Caborca Cartel fought Los Chapitos' allies in Sonora from 2020 until Rafael's recapture on 15 July 2022","A US indictment unsealed in May 2026 described Los Chapitos as controlling the Sonora and Baja California corridors after Caro Quintero's removal","Three self-described Caborca Cartel members, one claiming kinship with Caro Quintero, were arrested in Jilotzingo, State of Mexico, on 11 June 2026","Banners signed by the Caborca Cartel appeared in three Campeche municipalities (Hecelchakán, Tenabo and Hopelchén) on 29 August 2026"],"status_2026":"The Sonora Cartel no longer exists as an organization; Miguel Caro Quintero has lived quietly in Mexico since his July 2019 deportation. The family's successor network, the Caborca Cartel, survives in northwestern Sonora but has lost ground to Los Chapitos since Rafael Caro Quintero's July 2022 capture and February 2025 transfer to the United States, and in mid-2026 it was mainly visible through cells and arrests far from Sonora: three members detained in the State of Mexico in June, a documented presence in Quintana Roo and Yucatán, and narcomantas signed in its name in Campeche on 29 August 2026.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonora_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Caro_Quintero","https://irp.fas.org/congress/1998_hr/ct980226.htm","https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/former-cartel-leader-extradited-mexico","https://www.justice.gov/archive/usao/co/news/2010/February10/2_5_10.html","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2019/07/22/miguel-caro-quintero-sobrevivio-a-la-prision-supermax-lo-extraditaron-y-ya-es-hombre-libre-en-mexico/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Caro_Quintero","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/05/07/los-chapitos-aprovechan-caida-de-caro-quintero-ganan-terreno-en-sonora-segun-eu/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/06/12/capturan-a-tres-presuntos-integrantes-del-cartel-de-caborca-en-el-edomex-uno-seria-sobrino-de-caro-quintero/","https://www.elimparcial.com/mexico/2026/08/29/colocan-en-campeche-una-manta-atribuida-al-cartel-de-caborca-que-menciona-a-cuatro-elementos-de-seguridad-los-acusa-de-presunta-extorsion-y-ya-es-investigada-por-la-fiscalia/","https://tribunacampeche.com/31/aparecen-presuntas-narcomantas-en-tres-municipios-de-campeche/754131/","https://www.yucatan.com.mx/merida/2026/01/15/ataque-armado-triple-homicidio-dzilam-gonzalez-cartel-de-caborca-yucatan.html"],"verified":true},{"id":"colima","name":"Colima Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Colima","Los Amezcua","Amezcua Contreras Organization","Reyes de las Metanfetaminas (Kings of Methamphetamine)"],"founded":1988,"dissolved":2008,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"COL","city":"Colima","lat":19.2433,"lng":-103.725},"summary":"The Colima Cartel was the organization of brothers José de Jesús, Luis Ignacio and Adán Amezcua Contreras, natives of Colima who operated from Guadalajara. In the early 1990s they pioneered the bulk import of ephedrine from Asia and Europe and became, in the DEA's words, probably the largest ephedrine smugglers and clandestine methamphetamine producers in the world. Arrests in 1997-98 broke the leadership, but a precursor-chemical network tied to the family survived until a 2008 US Treasury designation.","history":"The Amezcua Contreras brothers grew up in Colima. José de Jesús, the eldest and the strategist, crossed into Los Angeles around 1978 as a teenager and by the mid-1980s was smuggling migrants and then cocaine for Colombian suppliers with his brothers Luis Ignacio and Adán. By 1988 they had formed their own organization, headquartered in Guadalajara with a home base in Colima and Tecomán, close to the port of Manzanillo. Initially a small cocaine transport group, it found its niche when the United States tightened controls on domestic ephedrine in 1989 and again in 1993, choking the Californian biker gangs that had dominated methamphetamine.\n\nThe brothers filled the gap. Because ephedrine could still be bought legally in bulk for pharmaceutical use, they set up front companies and buying agents in India, Thailand, the Czech Republic, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands. Jesús travelled to India in 1993 and to the Netherlands and Thailand in 1994 to secure suppliers. Air shipments routed through Mexico City and Guadalajara fed 'superlabs' in Jalisco, Michoacán, Colima and Baja California, and trusted associates in California, Texas, Arkansas, Georgia and North Carolina sold ephedrine and finished methamphetamine to Mexican distribution cells. The DEA estimated that between March and October 1994 alone the organization imported about five tons of ephedrine into the United States, and it worked alongside the Arellano Félix organization in Tijuana. By 1997 the agency told Congress the Amezcuas were 'probably the largest smuggler of ephedrine and clandestine producer of methamphetamine in the world'.\n\nPressure mounted from both sides of the border. In December 1997 a DEA operation against the network's US arm produced 101 arrests, 133 pounds of methamphetamine, 90 gallons of meth solution and 2.25 million dollars in assets; a key buyer, José Osorio Cabrera, was arrested in Bangkok. On 10 November 1997 Mexican soldiers arrested Adán on the Las Guásimas-Colima highway with a .38 pistol. On 1 June 1998 agents of the FEADS anti-drug prosecutor arrested Jesús and Luis in Guadalajara. Within nine days a judge dropped two of the three charges against them, and Mexican courts ultimately convicted them on domestic counts: in September 2005 a Jalisco federal court sentenced Luis to 49 years, and Jesús received a sentence reported as 53 years, with Mexican press reporting a reduction on appeal in December 2005 (to as little as 28 years by some accounts). On 1 June 2000 the US Treasury named Jesús and Luis among the first Kingpin Act designees, and in May 2002 a federal court blocked Jesús's extradition to the United States because he faced a possible life sentence. Adán, freed in 1999, was re-arrested in Zapopan on 4 May 2001 for money laundering and received a 22-year term that was cut in December 2005 to nine and a half years. Lieutenant Jaime Arturo Ladino Ávila, 'El Ojón', ran the remnants until his capture in 2000.\n\nThe organization never regained its size, but its chemical supply chain outlived its leaders. The White House identified the Amezcua Contreras Organization itself as a Kingpin on 1 June 2006, and on 2 October 2008 the US Treasury designated its corporate network: Adán, sister Patricia Amezcua Contreras, Ladino Ávila and pharmaceutical entrepreneur Telesforo Baltazar Tirado Escamilla, whose Productos Farmacéuticos Collins, together with Laboratorios Willmar and Lomedic, was accused of diverting multiple tons of pseudoephedrine to methamphetamine producers; ten individuals and six companies were listed while Jesús and Luis remained imprisoned in Mexico. By then Colima's coast and the port of Manzanillo were controlled by the Sinaloa Federation, and after 2010 by the Jalisco New Generation Cartel, whose meth economy was built on the routes and chemists the Amezcuas had trained. Los Mezcales, a former CJNG armed wing that broke away to contest Colima, is sometimes described in the press as the state's new home-grown cartel.\n\nThe brothers remain a live political issue. On 30 April 2025 judicial candidate César Gutiérrez Priego published audio recordings he said showed former president Ernesto Zedillo's wife, Nilda Patricia Velasco, talking with Jesús Amezcua in 1996, and on 1 May President Sheinbaum asked prosecutors to investigate; the claims remain unproven. On 27 February 2025 and again on 12 August 2025 Mexico transferred 29 and 26 prisoners to the United States; Jesús and Luis were not among them, and Mexican press listed them as candidates for a possible third wave, noting they had 'saved themselves' from extradition so far. As of September 2026 Jesús (Cefereso 2, Puente Grande) and Luis (Altiplano) Amezcua Contreras remain in Mexican federal custody with no reported transfer, Adán's whereabouts are not publicly confirmed, and Colima, contested between the CJNG and Los Mezcales, continues to register one of Mexico's highest per-capita homicide rates.","leaders":[{"name":"José de Jesús Amezcua Contreras","alias":"El Rey de las Metanfetaminas / Chuy","role":"Founder and chief; ephedrine procurement","from":1988,"to":1998,"fate":"Arrested Guadalajara 1 Jun 1998; Kingpin Act designee 1 Jun 2000; extradition to US blocked May 2002; sentenced to 53 years (press reports a Dec 2005 reduction on appeal); held at Cefereso 2 Puente Grande as of 2025, still in Mexican custody Sept 2026 and listed by press as a candidate for a future transfer to the US"},{"name":"Luis Ignacio Amezcua Contreras","alias":"","role":"Co-leader; logistics and money","from":1988,"to":1998,"fate":"Arrested Guadalajara 1 Jun 1998; Kingpin Act designee 1 Jun 2000; sentenced Sept 2005 to 49 years by a Jalisco federal court; held at Altiplano; still imprisoned in Mexico as of Sept 2026"},{"name":"Adán Amezcua Contreras","alias":"","role":"Co-leader; Colima operations and labs","from":1988,"to":2001,"fate":"Arrested 10 Nov 1997 on weapons charge; freed 1999; re-arrested Zapopan 4 May 2001 for money laundering; 22-year sentence reduced to 9.5 years in Dec 2005; designated by US Treasury 2 Oct 2008; current whereabouts unconfirmed"},{"name":"Jaime Arturo Ladino Ávila","alias":"El Ojón","role":"Successor operator after 1998","from":1998,"to":2000,"fate":"Captured 2000; designated by US Treasury Oct 2008"},{"name":"Patricia Amezcua Contreras","alias":"","role":"Sister; key associate running the family's precursor network","from":2001,"to":2008,"fate":"Designated under the Kingpin Act 2 Oct 2008; no public record of arrest"},{"name":"Telesforo Baltazar Tirado Escamilla","alias":"","role":"Owner of Productos Farmacéuticos Collins; pseudoephedrine diversion","from":2001,"to":2008,"fate":"Designated under the Kingpin Act 2 Oct 2008"}],"factions":[{"id":"guadalajara_hub","name":"Guadalajara command and import hub","from":1988,"to":1998,"area":"Guadalajara, Zapopan","leader":"Jesús and Luis Amezcua Contreras","note":"Front companies importing ephedrine from India, Thailand, Czech Republic, Germany and Switzerland"},{"id":"colima_cell","name":"Colima-Tecomán production cell","from":1988,"to":2001,"area":"Colima, Tecomán, Manzanillo","leader":"Adán Amezcua Contreras","note":"Labs and coastal logistics near the port of Manzanillo"},{"id":"us_distribution_cells","name":"US ephedrine and meth distribution cells","from":1992,"to":1998,"area":"California, Texas, Arkansas, Georgia, North Carolina","leader":"José Osorio Cabrera and trusted associates","note":"Sold ephedrine to Mexican meth cooks in the US; 101 arrests in Dec 1997"},{"id":"precursor_network","name":"Pharmaceutical precursor network","from":2001,"to":2008,"area":"Jalisco, Colima, Mexico City","leader":"Patricia Amezcua Contreras / Telesforo Tirado Escamilla","note":"Collins, Willmar and Lomedic laboratories diverting multiple tons of pseudoephedrine; OFAC designation 2 Oct 2008"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"tijuana","from":1992,"to":1998,"note":"Cooperation with the Arellano Félix organization on methamphetamine and ephedrine supply to Baja California labs"},{"cartel":"other","from":1988,"to":1992,"note":"Colombian cartels: early cocaine transport contracts"},{"cartel":"other","from":1992,"to":1998,"note":"Ephedrine suppliers and brokers in India, Thailand, Czech Republic, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"us_government","from":1994,"to":2008,"note":"DEA operation against US cells (Dec 1997), Kingpin Act designations (2000, 2006, 2008), extradition requests"},{"cartel":"government","from":1997,"to":2002,"note":"Arrests of Adán (1997, 2001) and of Jesús and Luis (1998); FEADS prosecutions"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2000,"to":2008,"note":"Sinaloa Federation took over Colima's coast, Manzanillo and the methamphetamine economy after the brothers' fall"}],"activities":["Bulk import and diversion of ephedrine and pseudoephedrine","Methamphetamine production in superlabs","Cocaine transport for Colombian suppliers (late 1980s)","Migrant smuggling (early years)","Money laundering through pharmaceutical, auto-parts and natural-products companies"],"peak":{"from":1993,"to":1998,"states":["COL","JAL","MIC","BCN","NAY"],"note":"World's leading ephedrine smuggler per DEA; supplied superlabs on both sides of the border from Guadalajara and Colima"},"facts":["The DEA estimated the Amezcuas imported about five tons of ephedrine into the United States between March and October 1994","In 1997 the DEA told Congress the group was probably the largest ephedrine smuggler and clandestine methamphetamine producer in the world","A December 1997 DEA operation against its US network yielded 101 arrests, 133 pounds of methamphetamine, 90 gallons of meth solution and 2.25 million dollars in assets","Jesús and Luis were arrested in Guadalajara on 1 June 1998; within nine days a judge dropped two of the three charges against them","Jesús and Luis were among the first twelve traffickers designated under the US Kingpin Act on 1 June 2000; a May 2002 ruling blocked Jesús's extradition because he faced life in prison","Mexican courts sentenced Luis to 49 years (September 2005) and Jesús to a reported 53 years, later reduced on appeal according to Mexican press","The US Treasury's 2 October 2008 designation covered 10 individuals and 6 companies, including pharmaceutical laboratories accused of diverting multiple tons of pseudoephedrine","Jesús and Luis were left out of both the 29-prisoner (27 Feb 2025) and 26-prisoner (12 Aug 2025) transfers to the United States and were listed as candidates for a third wave","On 1 May 2025 President Sheinbaum asked prosecutors to investigate audio recordings alleging 1996 contacts between former president Zedillo's wife and Jesús Amezcua, allegations that remain unproven"],"status_2026":"The Colima Cartel is defunct. Jesús and Luis Amezcua Contreras remain in Mexican federal prison (Puente Grande and Altiplano respectively) as of September 2026, having been left out of the February and August 2025 mass transfers to the United States but publicly listed as candidates for a future one; Adán's whereabouts are unconfirmed. Their home state is now fought over by the CJNG and its breakaway rival Los Mezcales, whose alleged second-in-command Carlos Miguel 'N', 'El Abulón', was arrested by marines and Oaxaca state agents in Oaxaca city on 21 August 2026 amid near-daily arrests of Mezcales members around Colima and Manzanillo.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colima_Cartel","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_Colima","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/mexico/etc/amezcua.html","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/hp1171","https://irp.fas.org/congress/1998_hr/ct980226.htm","https://www.milenio.com/policia/cartel-de-colima-como-se-fundo-quienes-son-hermanos-amezcua-contrera","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/como-detuvieron-a-los-amezcua-contreras-lideres-cartel-de-colima","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/hermanos-amezcua-contreras-lideres-cartel-colima-extradicion-eu","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/12/28/los-reyes-de-las-metanfetaminas-cual-fue-el-destino-que-tuvieron-los-hermanos-amezcua/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/05/24/cartel-de-colima-como-cayeron-los-hermanos-amezcua-los-principales-traficantes-de-metanfetaminas-en-mexico/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/08/14/ellos-son-los-narcos-mexicanos-que-podrian-ser-entregados-a-eeuu-en-una-tercera-fase/","https://zetatijuana.com/2025/05/sheinbaum-pide-investigar-supuestos-nexos-de-zedillo-y-su-esposa-con-los-reyes-de-las-metanfetaminas/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/22/el-abulon-segundo-al-mando-de-los-mezcales-es-detenido-en-oaxaca-lo-senalan-como-generador-de-violencia-en-colima/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/25/quien-es-el-abulon-y-como-llego-a-ser-el-segundo-al-mando-de-los-mezcales-exsocios-del-cjng/"],"verified":true},{"id":"oaxaca","name":"Oaxaca Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Oaxaca","Cártel del Istmo","Cártel de los Díaz Parada","Díaz Parada Organization"],"founded":1980,"dissolved":2011,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"OAX","city":"San Pedro Totolapa","lat":16.6667,"lng":-96.3},"summary":"The Oaxaca Cartel was the family organization of Pedro Díaz Parada, 'El Cacique de la Sierra', who turned marijuana planted around San Pedro Totolapa in the 1970s into a monopoly over cultivation in the Sierra Sur and the Isthmus of Tehuantepec, later adding cocaine transit from Chiapas. Famous for two prison escapes and the 1987 murder of the judge who sentenced him, Díaz Parada was finally captured in January 2007, after which his brothers ran a shrinking network that faded by about 2011.","history":"Pedro Díaz Parada Cruz was born around 1950 in San Pedro Totolapa, a Zapotec village on the road from the Central Valleys of Oaxaca to the Isthmus of Tehuantepec. In the 1970s he began sowing marijuana in the surrounding hills and, by paying growers and buying off local police and municipal bosses, became the region's cacique. By the early 1980s he controlled cultivation across the Sierra Sur (Yautepec, Miahuatlán, Sola de Vega) and the Isthmus, shipping bales to Mexico City and northern buyers, with his brothers Eugenio Jesús ('Don Chuy') and Domingo Aniceto ('Don Cheto') handling transport and finances. Operating far from the border and from the Sinaloan federations, the family built a regional cartel that, according to federal prosecutors at the time of his capture, eventually operated in seven states.\n\nThe Mexican army arrested him in 1985 and a federal judge, Pedro Villafuerte Gallegos, sentenced him to 33 years. Díaz Parada reportedly answered from the dock: 'I am going to leave, and you are going to die.' In 1987 he escaped from the Santa María Ixcotel prison in Oaxaca, and on 20 September 1987 the judge, who had been moved to Cuernavaca, Morelos, for his safety, was shot dead near his home; investigators counted 33 cartridge cases and a note reading 'one bullet per year'. Recaptured in May 1990 and sent to Mexico City's Reclusorio Oriente, he escaped again on 17-18 January 1992 and spent the next fifteen years as a fugitive in his own territory, protected by kin networks and, prosecutors later alleged, by Oaxacan officials. A suspected hitman in the judge's murder was not arrested until November 2001.\n\nDuring those years the organization moved beyond marijuana. Colombian and Guatemalan cocaine entering through Chiapas was moved by road and rail through Juchitán, Matías Romero and Salina Cruz toward Veracruz and Mexico City, and the family added arms trafficking, extortion and money laundering through ranches and transport companies. Federal authorities reported that the Díaz Parada group worked first with the Juárez Cartel and, from 2003, allied with the Arellano Félix organization of Tijuana, which needed a southern supply line; Sinaloan traffickers also bought its marijuana. The Isthmus became a strategic funnel for the whole industry, which by the mid-2000s drew the Zetas into southern Oaxaca and put the ageing regional boss under pressure from both the state and larger rivals.\n\nOn Tuesday 16 January 2007 federal agents captured Díaz Parada in Oaxaca city, his centre of operations, in a vehicle carrying drugs and weapons; the arrest was announced by the PGR the following day and he was flown to the Altiplano maximum-security prison. Proceso described the 'inheritance' left to his brothers, who kept the network running through 2011, while a December 2010 SIEDO investigation sought former Oaxaca officials over protection payments. The organization did not survive the loss of its founder and the arrival of the Zetas, and by 2011 analysts considered it dissolved. Relatives of the next generation, the Díaz Pantoja family, were prosecuted in October 2013, including a deputy-elect to the Oaxaca congress, and in 2017 a daughter who led a civic association and a nascent political party publicly denied that the family were traffickers. On 9 March 2018 a federal judge in Toluca sentenced Pedro Díaz Parada to 15 years for possession of drugs with intent to sell, possession of cartridges and carrying a military-use firearm. On 20-21 September 2019 Oaxacan outlets reported, without official confirmation, that he had been granted house arrest because of his health; no later official update has been published.\n\nAs of September 2026 the Díaz Parada organization exists only as a memory and a family name in Isthmus politics and transport unions. The territory it once ruled is contested by the Jalisco New Generation Cartel, which according to El Universal has operated in the Isthmus since 2023 seeking control of the Interoceanic Corridor, and by Sinaloa Cartel cells: on 28-29 July 2026 federal and state forces arrested in Ejutla de Crespo 'El Miky' (alias '086'), described by the Oaxaca prosecutor as second-in-command and plaza coordinator of a Sinaloa Cartel-Beltrán Leyva cell running the drug corridor that links the Coast and the Isthmus to the Central Valleys, and in August 2026 the state prosecutor reported that arrests of adolescents for drug dealing had risen 155 percent in a year as criminal groups recruit minors through social media. Pedro Díaz Parada, if alive, would be about 76; his brothers Eugenio and Domingo were never publicly reported arrested.","leaders":[{"name":"Pedro Díaz Parada Cruz","alias":"El Cacique de la Sierra / El Capo del Istmo","role":"Founder and boss","from":1980,"to":2007,"fate":"Arrested 1985, sentenced to 33 years; escaped 1987 (Santa María Ixcotel, Oaxaca) and 17-18 Jan 1992 (Reclusorio Oriente, Mexico City); recaptured Oaxaca city 16 Jan 2007; held in Altiplano; sentenced 9 Mar 2018 to 15 years; unconfirmed Sept 2019 reports of house arrest for ill health; no official update since"},{"name":"Eugenio Jesús Díaz Parada","alias":"Don Chuy","role":"Brother; co-leader after 2007","from":1985,"to":2011,"fate":"No public record of arrest; daughter publicly denied family narco ties in 2017 and 2023"},{"name":"Domingo Aniceto Díaz Parada","alias":"Don Cheto","role":"Brother; finances and transport, co-leader after 2007","from":1985,"to":2011,"fate":"No public record of arrest"}],"factions":[{"id":"sierra_sur_wing","name":"Sierra Sur cultivation wing","from":1980,"to":2007,"area":"San Pedro Totolapa, Yautepec, Miahuatlán, Sola de Vega","leader":"Pedro Díaz Parada","note":"Marijuana plantations and grower networks; family stronghold"},{"id":"istmo_wing","name":"Isthmus transit wing","from":1990,"to":2011,"area":"Juchitán, Tehuantepec, Matías Romero, Salina Cruz","leader":"Eugenio Jesús and Domingo Aniceto Díaz Parada","note":"Cocaine and marijuana transport toward Veracruz and Mexico City; transport companies and unions"},{"id":"chiapas_corridor","name":"Chiapas border supply line","from":1995,"to":2007,"area":"Chiapas-Guatemala border, Tapachula, Arriaga","leader":"Díaz Parada associates","note":"Cocaine received from Guatemalan and Colombian suppliers"},{"id":"diaz_pantoja_generation","name":"Díaz Pantoja family network","from":2007,"to":2013,"area":"Isthmus of Tehuantepec","leader":"Díaz Pantoja relatives","note":"Next-generation relatives prosecuted in Oct 2013, including a deputy-elect to the Oaxaca congress"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"juarez","from":1995,"to":2003,"note":"Reported cooperation with the Carrillo Fuentes organization on cocaine moving north from the Isthmus"},{"cartel":"tijuana","from":2003,"to":2007,"note":"Alliance with the Arellano Félix organization reported by federal authorities from 2003"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":1990,"to":2007,"note":"Marijuana supply to Sinaloan trafficking networks (reported links, weaker documentation)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"government","from":1985,"to":2007,"note":"Army arrest 1985; federal manhunts after the 1987 and 1992 escapes; capture by federal agents in Jan 2007"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2005,"to":2011,"note":"Zetas expansion into southern Oaxaca and the Isthmus displaced the weakened family network"}],"activities":["Marijuana cultivation in the Sierra Sur and Isthmus","Cocaine transit from Chiapas and Guatemala toward Veracruz and Mexico City","Arms trafficking","Extortion and cacique-style control of municipalities","Money laundering through ranches and transport companies","Murder of officials (judge Villafuerte Gallegos, 1987)"],"peak":{"from":1985,"to":2006,"states":["OAX","CHP","VER","GRO"],"note":"Monopolized Oaxacan marijuana and controlled the Isthmus transit corridor; federal prosecutors said the network operated in seven states"},"facts":["Díaz Parada was sentenced to 33 years in 1985; the judge who sentenced him was killed on 20 September 1987 with 33 cartridge cases left at the scene and a note reading 'one bullet per year'","He escaped prison twice, in 1987 from Santa María Ixcotel in Oaxaca and in January 1992 from Mexico City's Reclusorio Oriente","He spent 15 years as a fugitive before federal agents captured him in Oaxaca city on 16 January 2007, an arrest announced the next day","Federal authorities reported the cartel allied with the Tijuana Cartel in 2003 and operated in Oaxaca, Chiapas and five other states","A federal judge sentenced him on 9 March 2018 to 15 years for drug possession with intent to sell and weapons possession, 11 years after his capture","Reports of his release to house arrest for health reasons circulated on 20-21 September 2019 and were never officially confirmed","Relatives from the Díaz Pantoja family, including a deputy-elect to the Oaxaca congress, were prosecuted in October 2013","In late July 2026 federal and state forces arrested 'El Miky', alleged second-in-command of a Sinaloa Cartel-Beltrán Leyva cell controlling the drug corridor linking Oaxaca's Coast and Isthmus to the Central Valleys"],"status_2026":"The Díaz Parada organization has been defunct since roughly 2011 and its founder's current status is unknown: after a 15-year sentence in 2018, unconfirmed reports in September 2019 said he had been moved to house arrest for ill health, and nothing official has been published since. The Isthmus of Tehuantepec it once dominated is now disputed by the CJNG, present since 2023 and seeking control of the Interoceanic Corridor, and by Sinaloa Cartel cells, with a Sinaloa-Beltrán Leyva plaza coordinator ('El Miky') arrested in Ejutla de Crespo in late July 2026 and the state prosecutor reporting in August 2026 a 155 percent rise in arrests of adolescents recruited into drug dealing.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oaxaca_Cartel","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_Oaxaca","https://archivo.eluniversal.com.mx/notas/400960.html","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2007/01/18/index.php?section=politica&article=020n1pol","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2007/2/5/diaz-parada-la-herencia-del-capo-31459.html","https://heraldodemexico.com.mx/nacional/2021/10/15/pedro-diaz-parada-el-narco-que-amenazo-al-juez-que-lo-encarcelo-yo-me-voy-ir-tu-te-vas-morir-344558.html","https://www.proceso.com.mx/525505/dan-15-anos-de-prision-a-pedro-diaz-parada-lider-del-narco-en-oaxaca","https://imparcialoaxaca.mx/oaxaca/pedro-diaz-parada-sale-del-penal-del-altiplano/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2013/10/10/ligan-diputado-electo-de-oaxaca-dos-de-sus-hermanos-con-el-cartel-del-itsmo-124459.html","https://narcodata.animalpolitico.com/cartel-de-oaxaca-de-los-diaz-parada/","https://portal.fgeo.gob.mx/index.php/archivos/noticias/boletin-2670-detienen-a-el-miky-presunto-segundo-al-mando-de-celula-delictiva-vinculada-al-cartel-de-sinaloa-en-oaxaca-fgeo","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2026/8/14/detenciones-de-adolescentes-por-narcomenudeo-en-oaxaca-se-disparan-en-un-155-378008.html"],"verified":true},{"id":"pacifico_sur","name":"Cártel del Pacífico Sur","aka":["South Pacific Cartel","CPS","Facción de Héctor Beltrán Leyva","Cártel de los Beltrán Leyva (Morelos)"],"founded":2010,"dissolved":2014,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"MOR","city":"Cuernavaca","lat":18.9186,"lng":-99.2342},"summary":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva's rebranding of the Beltrán Leyva loyalists in Morelos, Guerrero and the State of Mexico after Arturo's death, created in April 2010 to fight Édgar 'La Barbie' Valdez for the family's southern territories. It won Morelos with Zetas support but was gutted by arrests in 2011, and its cells hardened into Los Rojos and Guerreros Unidos; the brand died with Héctor's arrest in October 2014.","history":"The killing of Arturo Beltrán Leyva by Navy marines in Cuernavaca on 16 December 2009 left the family's southern empire, Morelos, Guerrero, the State of Mexico and the routes into Mexico City, without an owner. Héctor, 'El H', claimed command from hiding, but the organization's chief of gunmen, Édgar Valdez Villarreal 'La Barbie', who ran Los Negros and the retail drug trade in Cuernavaca and Acapulco, refused to obey him. Through early 2010 the two sides killed each other's people across Morelos, and official reports in the first months of the year described an open war between Héctor, backed by his enforcer Sergio Villarreal 'El Grande', and La Barbie's faction.\n\nThe new name surfaced in Morelos in April 2010. After a wave of arson attacks on homes and businesses that began on 30 March, the initials 'CPS' appeared painted on the burned walls, and narcomantas and messages left on bodies in Cuernavaca and Acapulco announced the Cártel del Pacífico Sur, a coalition of Héctor's loyalists with gunmen supplied by Los Zetas, the BLO's allies since 2008. Its stated aim was to expel La Barbie and the 'traitors' from Morelos and Guerrero. The dispute produced hanging bodies from Cuernavaca overpasses and decapitations signed by both sides, and more than 150 deaths in Morelos alone within months. The balance shifted when federal police arrested La Barbie at a house in Lerma on 30 August 2010; two weeks later, on 12 September, marines captured El Grande in Puebla, removing Héctor's own top operator.\n\nWith Barbie gone, the CPS became the dominant force in Morelos in late 2010, running retail drug sales, kidnapping and extortion under local chief Julio de Jesús Radilla Hernández, 'El Negro Radilla'. In early December 2010 soldiers detained a 14-year-old US-born sicario, Édgar Jiménez Lugo 'El Ponchis', with a sister at Cuernavaca's airport as they tried to fly to Tijuana to reach their mother in San Diego; he confessed to beheading four victims on Radilla's orders and became the emblem of the group's use of children. On 28 March 2011 the bodies of Juan Francisco Sicilia, son of the poet Javier Sicilia, and six other people were found bound and asphyxiated in a car in Temixco, a crime that launched the Movimiento por la Paz con Justicia y Dignidad and turned national attention on Morelos. Federal police arrested Radilla on 25 May 2011 as the alleged author of that massacre, and Víctor Valdez, another leader, fell in mid-2011, after which the CPS was far less active.\n\nHéctor himself avoided the fighting, living as a businessman and art dealer in Querétaro and San Miguel de Allende. The CPS label was also raised in northern Sinaloa and Sonora, where Fausto Isidro Meza Flores' Los Mazatlecos, loyal to Héctor, signed banners as the Pacífico Sur during their war with the Sinaloa Cartel, and in Nayarit under 'H2' Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez. In the south, however, the organization's Morelos and Guerrero cells stopped answering to anyone. The Nava Romero family's gunmen became Los Rojos, and the Casarrubias network in Iguala became Guerreros Unidos (2011); by 2012-2013 those two ex-CPS factions were fighting each other for the heroin routes of northern Guerrero and Morelos, a conflict that culminated in the Ayotzinapa disappearances of 26 September 2014. Soldiers arrested Héctor, together with his financier Germán Goyenechea, at a seafood restaurant in San Miguel de Allende on 1 October 2014, formally ending the Pacífico Sur project; he died of a heart attack in Toluca on 18 November 2018 while held at the Altiplano prison.\n\nThe CPS left no active successor using its name. Los Rojos were dismantled by 2022 after federal forces captured Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete' in Leonardo Bravo, Guerrero, on 1 August 2019; a court gave him 20 years for organized crime in July 2023 and 105 years in July 2026. Guerreros Unidos remains active in northern Guerrero; Morelos passed to CJNG and La Familia Michoacana cells; and the northern loyalists of Chapo Isidro are now simply called the Beltrán Leyva Organization or Cártel de Guasave. As of September 2026 the Cártel del Pacífico Sur is a historical label, remembered chiefly for El Ponchis and the Sicilia murder.","leaders":[{"name":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El H / El Ingeniero","role":"Founder and leader","from":2010,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested by the Army with his financier Germán Goyenechea in San Miguel de Allende, Guanajuato, 1 Oct 2014; died of a heart attack in Toluca 18 Nov 2018 while imprisoned at Altiplano"},{"name":"Sergio Villarreal Barragán","alias":"El Grande","role":"Chief enforcer and second-in-command","from":2010,"to":2010,"fate":"Arrested by marines in Puebla 12 Sep 2010; extradited to the US in 2012; cooperated; released as a protected witness c. Dec 2019; testified against García Luna Jan 2023"},{"name":"Julio de Jesús Radilla Hernández","alias":"El Negro Radilla","role":"Morelos plaza chief; boss of El Ponchis","from":2010,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested by the Federal Police 25 May 2011, accused of ordering and organizing the Temixco killing of Juan Francisco Sicilia and six others (bodies found 28 Mar 2011)"},{"name":"Víctor Valdez","alias":"","role":"Morelos operational leader","from":2010,"to":2011,"fate":"Captured mid-2011"},{"name":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores","alias":"El Chapo Isidro","role":"Leader of the northern (Sinaloa/Sonora) loyalist wing signing as CPS","from":2010,"to":2014,"fate":"Fugitive; continued as head of the BLO remnant; added to the FBI Ten Most Wanted list Feb 2025"},{"name":"Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez","alias":"H2","role":"Nayarit chief loyal to Héctor","from":2010,"to":2014,"fate":"Killed with seven other gunmen by Navy marines in Tepic, Nayarit, 9 Feb 2017"}],"factions":[{"id":"cps_morelos","name":"Morelos cell","from":2010,"to":2011,"area":"Cuernavaca, Jiutepec, Temixco, Morelos","leader":"Julio de Jesús Radilla Hernández 'El Negro Radilla'","note":"Core of the organization; employed child sicarios including El Ponchis; collapsed after Radilla's arrest on 25 May 2011"},{"id":"los_rojos","name":"Los Rojos","from":2010,"to":2014,"area":"Morelos, central Guerrero, Puebla","leader":"Nava Romero family; Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete'","note":"Ex-CPS gunmen who went independent; fought Guerreros Unidos; dismantled by 2022 and Mazari sentenced to 105 years in July 2026"},{"id":"guerreros_unidos","name":"Guerreros Unidos","from":2011,"to":2014,"area":"Iguala, Cocula, northern Guerrero, Morelos","leader":"Mario Casarrubias Salgado 'El Sapo Guapo'","note":"Ex-BLO/CPS heroin network; responsible for the Ayotzinapa disappearances of 26 Sep 2014; still active"},{"id":"mazatlecos_cps","name":"Los Mazatlecos (northern wing)","from":2010,"to":2014,"area":"Guasave, Los Mochis, Mazatlán, southern Sonora","leader":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores","note":"Signed narcomantas as Cártel del Pacífico Sur during the war with the Sinaloa Cartel"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Zetas supplied gunmen and backing for Héctor's campaign against La Barbie"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2010,"to":2014,"note":"The CPS was the loyalist core of the BLO under Héctor"},{"cartel":"juarez","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Inherited the BLO-Juárez anti-Sinaloa alignment"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cida","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"La Barbie's Acapulco lieutenants who rejected Héctor and declared independence"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2010,"to":2014,"note":"Inherited the 2008 war; fought Sinaloa in northern Sinaloa and Sonora through Los Mazatlecos"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Competed in Morelos, Guerrero and the State of Mexico"},{"cartel":"gulf","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Enemy by virtue of the Zetas alliance"},{"cartel":"government","from":2010,"to":2014,"note":"Arrests of El Grande (Sept 2010), El Ponchis (Dec 2010), Radilla (May 2011) and Héctor (Oct 2014)"}],"activities":["Cocaine transit and wholesale","Retail drug sales (narcomenudeo) in Cuernavaca and Acapulco","Heroin from Guerrero poppy","Kidnapping for ransom","Extortion of businesses and arson intimidation","Contract killings and decapitations using child recruits"],"peak":{"from":2010,"to":2011,"states":["MOR","GRO","MEX","PUE","SIN","SON","NAY"],"note":"Controlled Morelos and contested Acapulco and the State of Mexico after La Barbie's arrest in Aug 2010; northern loyalists in Sinaloa, Sonora and Nayarit used the same name."},"facts":["The initials 'CPS' first appeared painted on homes and businesses burned in Morelos after 30 Mar 2010, announcing Héctor Beltrán Leyva's faction in April 2010.","The 2010 war between the CPS and La Barbie's faction killed more than 150 people in Morelos and neighbouring states within months.","El Ponchis, arrested at Cuernavaca airport in early December 2010 with a sister as they tried to fly to Tijuana, was 14 years old and confessed to four beheadings on orders of CPS chief 'El Negro Radilla'; a juvenile court gave him the three-year maximum and he was released on 26 Nov 2013 and sent to the United States.","On 28 March 2011 the bound and asphyxiated bodies of Juan Francisco Sicilia and six others were found in a car in Temixco, sparking poet Javier Sicilia's Movimiento por la Paz; the Federal Police arrested Radilla for the crime on 25 May 2011, though a judge freed six other accused in August 2023.","Héctor's top operator Sergio 'El Grande' Villarreal was captured in Puebla on 12 Sep 2010, thirteen days after La Barbie's arrest.","Héctor evaded capture for nearly five years under a US$5 million State Department reward and a 30 million peso Mexican reward, posing as an art and real-estate businessman in San Miguel de Allende until 1 Oct 2014.","Two ex-CPS splinters, Los Rojos and Guerreros Unidos, fought the war that produced the Ayotzinapa disappearances of 43 students on 26 Sep 2014.","Los Rojos leader Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete', captured in Guerrero on 1 Aug 2019, was sentenced to 20 years in July 2023 and to 105 years in July 2026."],"status_2026":"The Cártel del Pacífico Sur is defunct; its brand ended with Héctor Beltrán Leyva's arrest on 1 October 2014 and his death in custody in November 2018. Its Morelos and Guerrero cells evolved into Los Rojos, dismantled by 2022 and whose leader Santiago Mazari received a 105-year sentence in July 2026, and Guerreros Unidos, still active around Iguala, while Morelos itself is now disputed by CJNG and La Familia Michoacana cells. As of September 2026 no active group uses the CPS name; the northern loyalists led by Chapo Isidro operate as the Beltrán Leyva Organization or Cártel de Guasave.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/beltran-leyva-organization-profile/","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2010/04/new-cartel-cartel-del-pacifico-sur.html","https://archivo.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/2014/cronologia-sicilia-979830.html","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2011/03/29/politica/018n1pol","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-born-teen-el-ponchis-sentenced-to-three-years-for-four-mexico-beheadings/","https://www.kpbs.org/news/border-immigration/2013/11/26/mexico-frees-san-diego-born-teen-cartel-killer-pon","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2026/7/25/sentencian-a-105-anos-de-prision-a-santiago-mazari-el-carrete-lider-de-los-rojos-376817.html","https://www.nbcnews.com/news/world/mexican-drug-lord-beltran-leyva-dead-56-cardiac-arrest-n937851","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9ctor_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva"],"verified":true},{"id":"cida","name":"Cártel Independiente de Acapulco","aka":["CIDA","Independent Cartel of Acapulco","Cártel de Acapulco","Los Independientes"],"founded":2010,"dissolved":0,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Acapulco","lat":16.8531,"lng":-99.8237},"summary":"Acapulco street organization formed in 2010 by Édgar 'La Barbie' Valdez's local lieutenants, led by ex-policeman Moisés Montero 'El Koreano', who refused to submit to Héctor Beltrán Leyva after Barbie's arrest. Responsible for the 2010 massacre of 20 men from Michoacán and implicated in the January 2011 Plaza Sendero beheadings, it degenerated into a network of extortion and retail-drug cells that, despite near-annual leadership arrests, still fights Los Rusos and other gangs for Acapulco's neighbourhoods, markets and transport routes in 2026.","history":"Acapulco had been Beltrán Leyva territory since the mid-2000s, when Arturo's gunmen fought the Sinaloa Cartel and Los Zetas for the port and left the first beheaded policemen on its streets in 2006. After Arturo was killed in December 2009, the port's plaza was run by Édgar Valdez Villarreal, 'La Barbie', through Los Negros and local lieutenants. When federal police arrested Barbie on 30 August 2010, those lieutenants, led by Moisés Montero Álvarez 'El Koreano', a former Guerrero state ministerial police officer jailed for extortion in 1999, rejected Héctor Beltrán Leyva's Cártel del Pacífico Sur and proclaimed the Cártel Independiente de Acapulco. Within weeks a rival faction, La Barredora, led by Eder Jair Sosa Carvajal 'El Cremas' and Christian Hernández Tarín 'El Chris', broke away, reportedly with Sinaloa Cartel support, and Acapulco became a three-way battlefield among CIDA, La Barredora and the CPS.\n\nThe new group announced itself with atrocities. On 30 September 2010 its gunmen kidnapped 20 men from Morelia, Michoacán, mechanics and relatives on holiday, mistaking them for La Familia Michoacana operatives; after a video posted online named the burial site, investigators dug 18 bodies from a mass grave at Tuncingo, on the edge of Tres Palos, from 3 November. On 8 January 2011 the decapitated bodies of 15 young men were dumped outside the Plaza Sendero shopping centre, part of 28 killings in the city that weekend; the three messages left with them were signed in the name of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, a warning to groups extorting local businesses, and it was only later that a 21-year-old CIDA gunman, José Lozano, was arrested with six others and admitted taking part in the killings. On 7 April 2011 gunmen burned the Acatianguis market in the Emiliano Zapata neighbourhood, destroying about 110 stalls and leaving four dead; El Koreano was later accused both of that arson and of the October 2010 murder of Antonio Valdés Andrade, the former councilman who had created the market. Acapulco became Mexico's most violent large city, with more than 1,000 murders in 2011 and a homicide rate above 100 per 100,000. The Federal Police arrested El Koreano on 1 August 2011; La Barredora lost El Chris in October and Víctor Manuel Rivera 'El Gordo' in November 2011, and CIDA's own 'El Gordo', Víctor Hugo Aguirre Garzón, was also captured, leaving both groups headless by early 2012.\n\nCIDA reorganized in 2012 under José Alberto Quiroz Pérez 'Juan Diego', José Leopoldo Buendía 'El Chilango' and Javier Muñoz 'El Cholo', with early financing from Los Zetas, and shifted from trafficking to controlling the city's illicit street economy: retail drug sales in the popular neighbourhoods of the north and east, and systematic extortion of taxi and bus operators, market vendors and shopkeepers, enforced by the murder of drivers and merchants. Quiroz and Buendía were captured in 2012 and Armando Romero Pastrana in Nayarit in 2016, but the cells regenerated. By 2018 José Ángel Palacios Galeana 'El Capuchino' and the brothers Ulises and Gustavo Laredo Guerrero, 'El Virus' and 'El Virus 2', led the group; on 5 February 2018 its sicarios killed the YouTuber Pamela Montenegro del Real, 'Nana Pelucas', in a restaurant, and on 25 September 2018 federal forces disarmed the entire Acapulco municipal police over collusion with organized crime.\n\nFrom 2016 CIDA's main enemy became Los Rusos, a separate Beltrán Leyva splinter led by Carlos Alberto Navarrete Soriano 'El Ruso' and Jesús Orlando Rodríguez Soriano, and from 2019 the Jalisco New Generation Cartel, which on 3-4 April 2019 left narcomantas beside a severed head threatening 'El Virus' and the CIDA leadership. The group recruited minors to collect extortion payments because they could not be prosecuted as adults. Its leader in 2023, Omar Francisco 'Franky', triggered protests and vehicle burnings when his mother was arrested in August 2023, and after Hurricane Otis devastated Acapulco on 25 October 2023 CIDA distributed relief supplies to reinforce social control in its neighbourhoods while extortion of reconstruction businesses surged.\n\nSince 2024 the federal government's Acapulco security plan has produced a steady stream of arrests: cell leader Marco Antonio 'El Panadero', 59, in La Venta on 28 December 2024 on homicide, kidnapping, extortion, drug and weapons charges; Abner Noé Cervantes 'El Traumado', 38, who had escaped house arrest, in the Venustiano Carranza borough of Mexico City on 15 February 2025; Eduardo López Torres 'El Chambitas', who in March 2025 demanded 5,000 pesos from a vendor at the Central de Abasto under threat of arson, on 26 April 2025; and Gonzalo 'El Oso', charged with an August 2025 double murder in the Sinaí neighbourhood, on 13 September 2025. In late October 2025 former sicario chief 'El Negro Pipas' received 27 years and six months for the Nana Pelucas killing. On 9 July 2026 authorities captured José 'El Tucán' in Toluca, an ex-CIDA operator whose own cell terrorized transport workers, amid reports that six groups, CIDA, Los Rusos, Los Capuchinos, Los Granados, Los Maldonado and Los Viagras, were extorting some 20,000 transport units in the port. As of September 2026 CIDA survives as a decentralized set of cells in La Venta, Zapata, Renacimiento, Tres Palos, Moctezuma and the markets, weakened but unbroken, while Los Rusos hold the wealthier Zona Diamante.","leaders":[{"name":"Moisés Montero Álvarez","alias":"El Koreano","role":"Founder and first leader; ex-Guerrero state ministerial police officer","from":2010,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested by the Federal Police in Acapulco 1 Aug 2011 (announced 2 Aug), aged 42; accused of the kidnapping and murder of the 20 men from Michoacán, the Acatianguis arson and the kidnapping of two federal agents"},{"name":"Víctor Hugo Aguirre Garzón","alias":"El Gordo","role":"Successor leader after El Koreano","from":2011,"to":2012,"fate":"Captured by federal forces (2011-2012); imprisoned"},{"name":"José Alberto Quiroz Pérez","alias":"Juan Diego","role":"Co-leader of the 2012 reorganization","from":2012,"to":2012,"fate":"Captured 2012"},{"name":"José Leopoldo Buendía Domínguez","alias":"El Chilango","role":"Co-leader of the 2012 reorganization","from":2012,"to":2012,"fate":"Captured 2012"},{"name":"Javier Muñoz","alias":"El Cholo","role":"Co-leader of the 2012 reorganization","from":2012,"to":2014,"fate":"Fate not publicly documented"},{"name":"Armando Romero Pastrana","alias":"","role":"Leader","from":2014,"to":2016,"fate":"Arrested in Nayarit 2016"},{"name":"José Ángel Palacios Galeana","alias":"El Capuchino / El Culón","role":"Top leader; later associated with the splinter Los Capuchinos","from":2017,"to":2019,"fate":"Status unclear; Los Capuchinos listed among Acapulco's six warring groups in 2026"},{"name":"Ulises Laredo Guerrero","alias":"El Virus","role":"Operational chief, target of CJNG threats April 2019","from":2018,"to":2019,"fate":"Fate not publicly confirmed"},{"name":"Omar Francisco 'N'","alias":"Franky","role":"Leader","from":2022,"to":2024,"fate":"At large; his mother's arrest in Aug 2023 sparked protests and vehicle burnings"},{"name":"Marco Antonio 'N'","alias":"El Panadero","role":"Cell leader, La Venta and Zapata","from":2022,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested aged 59 in La Venta, Acapulco, 28 Dec 2024 on drug, homicide, kidnapping, vehicle-theft, extortion and weapons charges"},{"name":"Abner Noé Cervantes","alias":"El Traumado / El Pedo Sucio","role":"Leader; described in Mexican press as a founding figure of CIDA","from":2010,"to":2025,"fate":"Broke the house arrest imposed by a Guerrero judge in 2024; rearrested aged 38 in the Venustiano Carranza borough of Mexico City 15 Feb 2025 on drug-possession and qualified-homicide warrants"},{"name":"Eduardo López Torres","alias":"El Chambitas","role":"Cell leader, Central de Abasto extortion","from":2024,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested in Acapulco 26 Apr 2025 for aggravated extortion over a 5,000-peso demand made on 25 Mar 2025"},{"name":"Gonzalo 'N' (also reported as Roque 'N')","alias":"El Oso","role":"Leader","from":2025,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested in Acapulco de Juárez 13 Sep 2025 and bound over for qualified homicide in the August 2025 double murder in Colonia Sinaí"}],"factions":[{"id":"la_barredora_acapulco","name":"La Barredora (Acapulco)","from":2010,"to":2012,"area":"Acapulco","leader":"Eder Jair Sosa Carvajal 'El Cremas'; Christian Hernández Tarín 'El Chris'","note":"Split from CIDA weeks after its founding; fought it for the port with reported Sinaloa backing; distinct from the Tabasco La Barredora"},{"id":"los_capuchinos","name":"Los Capuchinos","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Acapulco","leader":"José Ángel Palacios Galeana 'El Capuchino'","note":"Splinter named among six groups disputing Acapulco in 2026"},{"id":"cida_norte","name":"Northern neighbourhood cells","from":2012,"to":0,"area":"La Venta, Zapata, Tres Palos, Renacimiento, Vacacional, Moctezuma, Sinaí","leader":"Rotating cell chiefs (El Panadero, El Oso)","note":"Retail drugs and extortion; hit by arrests Dec 2024-Sep 2025"},{"id":"cida_abasto","name":"Central de Abasto cell","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Acapulco central market","leader":"Eduardo López Torres 'El Chambitas'","note":"Extorted vendors 5,000 pesos under threat of arson; leader arrested Apr 2025"},{"id":"tucan_cell","name":"El Tucán transport-extortion cell","from":2024,"to":2026,"area":"Acapulco transport routes","leader":"José 'N' 'El Tucán'","note":"Ex-CIDA operator who formed his own cell; linked to driver murders and the burning of more than six transport units; arrested in Toluca de Lerdo 9 Jul 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Provided early financing and gunmen against La Barredora and the CPS"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"pacifico_sur","from":2010,"to":2012,"note":"Rejected Héctor Beltrán Leyva's authority after La Barbie's arrest"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2010,"to":2014,"note":"Sinaloa backed La Barredora against CIDA for control of the port"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2010,"to":2011,"note":"Killed 20 men from Michoacán in Sept 2010 believing them La Familia operatives"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"CJNG narcomantas with a severed head threatened CIDA leaders 3-4 Apr 2019; contest for Acapulco continues"},{"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Competes for Guerrero extortion and drug markets"},{"cartel":"caborca","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Los Rusos, CIDA's main local enemy, are reported to operate in a strategic alliance with the Cártel de Caborca in the Zona Diamante"},{"cartel":"government","from":2010,"to":0,"note":"El Koreano arrested 2011; municipal police disarmed Sept 2018; wave of arrests 2024-2026"}],"activities":["Retail drug sales in Acapulco's popular neighbourhoods","Extortion of taxi, bus and transport operators","Extortion of market vendors and shopkeepers, enforced by arson","Kidnapping","Contract killings and dismemberment as messaging","Recruitment of minors as collectors and lookouts","Local cocaine and marijuana distribution"],"peak":{"from":2010,"to":2011,"states":["GRO"],"note":"Dominated Acapulco's drug retail and extortion during the port's most violent years (over 1,000 murders in 2011), with reach into the Costa Chica and Costa Grande of Guerrero."},"facts":["On 30 Sep 2010 CIDA kidnapped 20 men visiting Acapulco from Morelia, Michoacán; 18 bodies were recovered from a mass grave at Tuncingo, next to Tres Palos, from 3 Nov 2010 after a video posted online revealed the site.","On 8 Jan 2011 the decapitated bodies of 15 young men were dumped outside Plaza Sendero, among 28 people killed in Acapulco that weekend; the messages left with them were signed in the name of 'El Chapo' Guzmán, but a 21-year-old CIDA gunman, José Lozano, was later arrested and admitted participating in the killings.","Founder Moisés Montero 'El Koreano', a former Guerrero ministerial police officer jailed for extortion in 1999, was arrested by the Federal Police on 1 Aug 2011 at age 42.","The 7 Apr 2011 arson of the Acatianguis market in Acapulco destroyed about 110 stalls and left four people dead; the market's founder, ex-councilman Antonio Valdés Andrade, had been murdered in October 2010.","Acapulco recorded more than 1,000 homicides in 2011 and a rate above 100 per 100,000, the highest of any large Mexican city, during CIDA's war with La Barredora.","On 25 Sep 2018 federal forces disarmed the entire Acapulco municipal police for collusion with organized crime.","On 3-4 Apr 2019 CJNG left narcomantas and a severed head threatening CIDA chief 'El Virus'.","Four CIDA leaders were arrested in nine months: El Panadero (28 Dec 2024), El Traumado (15 Feb 2025), El Chambitas (26 Apr 2025) and El Oso (13 Sep 2025).","In July 2026 authorities counted six criminal groups extorting roughly 20,000 public-transport units in Acapulco, CIDA among them.","In late Oct 2025 ex-sicario chief 'El Negro Pipas' was sentenced to 27 years and 6 months for the 5 Feb 2018 murder of YouTuber Pamela Montenegro, 'Nana Pelucas'."],"status_2026":"CIDA remains active in September 2026 as a decentralized cluster of extortion and retail-drug cells in northern Acapulco neighbourhoods (La Venta, Zapata, Renacimiento, Tres Palos, Moctezuma, Sinaí) and the Central de Abasto, despite the arrests of El Panadero (Dec 2024), El Traumado (Feb 2025), El Chambitas (Apr 2025) and El Oso (Sep 2025) and the July 2026 capture of ex-member El Tucán in Toluca. It is one of six groups, alongside Los Rusos, Los Capuchinos, Los Granados, Los Maldonado and Los Viagras, fighting over the port's transport routes and markets, with CJNG and La Familia Michoacana pressing from outside. Its main rival Los Rusos, led by Jesús Orlando Rodríguez Soriano and reported to be allied with the Cártel de Caborca, now hold the Zona Diamante and the coastal tourism corridor and are the port's dominant group, though their enforcement wing Los Amarillos lost its chief 'El Gobén', captured in Puebla in early September 2026. CIDA's own leadership is anonymous and replaceable, and its violence is now measured in murdered drivers and burned buses rather than mass beheadings.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/beheadings-in-acapulco-point-to-sinaloa-cartel/","https://animalpolitico.com/2011/08/detienen-a-el-koreano-presunto-lider-del-cartel-de-acapulco","https://www.informador.mx/Mexico/Capturan-a-el-Koreano-presunto-lider-criminal-en-Acapulco-20110802-0185.html","http://www.cnn.com/2010/WORLD/americas/11/03/mexico.mass.grave/index.html","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2019/04/04/quienes-estan-detras-del-cartel-independiente-de-acapulco-que-le-declaro-la-guerra-al-mencho-y-al-cjng-en-guerrero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/12/28/cae-el-panadero-lider-de-una-celula-del-cartel-independiente-de-acapulco-durante-operativo-en-guerrero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/02/15/detienen-en-cdmx-a-el-traumado-presunto-lider-del-cartel-independiente-de-acapulco/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/27/cae-el-chambitas-lider-del-cartel-independiente-de-acapulco-es-acusado-de-extorsion/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/09/20/detienen-y-procesan-a-el-oso-presunto-lider-del-cartel-independiente-de-acapulco/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/10/30/dan-27-anos-de-prision-a-el-negro-pipas-exjefe-de-sicarios-del-cartel-de-acapulco-por-homicidio-de-youtuber/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/09/cartel-independiente-de-acapulco-quien-era-el-tucan-y-como-afecto-al-transporte-publico-de-la-ciudad-turistica/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/09/03/quien-es-el-goben-vinculado-a-la-faccion-de-los-rusos-con-operaciones-en-zona-diamante-acapulco/","https://www.elsoldechilpancingo.mx/2022/08/06/los-rusos-el-grupo-delictivo-que-controla-el-sobrino-de-caro-quintero/"],"verified":true},{"id":"zetas_vieja_escuela","name":"Zetas Vieja Escuela","aka":["ZVE","Old School Zetas","Vieja Guardia Zeta","Grupo Operativo Zetas"],"founded":2016,"dissolved":0,"parent":"zetas","origin":{"state":"TAM","city":"Ciudad Victoria","lat":23.7369,"lng":-99.1411},"summary":"The Zetas Vieja Escuela are the old-guard Zeta commanders who rejected the Treviño family's Cártel del Noreste after 2014-15, coalescing by 2016 around Ciudad Victoria and northern Veracruz. Allied with the Gulf Cartel and Los Talibanes from March 2016, they lost their national leaders by 2019 and survive in 2026 as scattered cells in Veracruz, Tamaulipas and neighboring states.","history":"The Zetas Vieja Escuela (\"Old School Zetas\", ZVE) crystallized in 2014-2016 among veteran Zeta commanders who rejected the Treviño nephews' takeover of the organization after Z-40's capture in July 2013 and Z-42's in March 2015; English Wikipedia dates the group's formation to 2016. Calling themselves the \"old school\" or \"old guard\", they claimed to return to the founders' business of drug trafficking and to end the mass kidnapping and extortion associated with the Treviño era. Their first stronghold was Ciudad Victoria, Tamaulipas, with a second base in northern Veracruz. English Wikipedia names José María Guízar Valencia (Z-43) and Antonio Salas Perea as founders, alongside Luis Reyes Enríquez \"El Rex\" (Z-12) and Tamaulipas commander Martiniano de Jesús Jaramillo Silva \"El Pata de Queso\" (Z-74), a suspect in the 2010 San Fernando migrant massacre and in the 2017 murder of the activist Miriam Rodríguez Martínez.\n\nIn Ciudad Victoria the ZVE fused with Grupo Bravo, a cell led by \"Comandante Bravo 1\" that had split from the Gulf Cartel after 2014 and allied with a Zeta unit known as the Grupo Operativo Zetas. From March 2016 Grupo Bravo, the ZVE and Los Talibanes formed a pact with the Gulf Cartel against the Cártel del Noreste (CDN), turning the state capital and the Victoria-San Fernando corridor into a battleground through 2017. Federal Police arrested El Pata de Queso at the general hospital in Ciudad Victoria on 14 November 2017; he died three days later, on 17 November, in a Mexico City hospital, aged 56, of kidney complications and peritonitis.\n\nDecapitation followed quickly. Z-43, for whom Washington offered US$5 million, was arrested in Mexico City's Roma district on 9 February 2018; as a US-born dual citizen he waived extradition, pleaded guilty in the Eastern District of Texas on 20 December 2021 to cocaine trafficking and money laundering, and was sentenced to 480 months (40 years) on 15 November 2022 by Judge Richard A. Schell. El Rex was stabbed to death inside the Centro de Ejecución de Sanciones of Nuevo Laredo on the morning of Saturday 17 November 2018, hours after entering the prison; his body was handed to his family, stolen from the funeral home, dismembered and scattered near prisons in the Monterrey metropolitan area, with his head left outside the Ciudad Victoria prison with a message. José Roberto Stolberg Becerra \"La Barbie\", described as the group's leader and carrying a 1.5-million-peso Tamaulipas reward, was captured by the FGR in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga, Jalisco, on 9 April 2019 on a 2018 warrant for kidnapping and criminal association. Spanish-language reporting treats the national \"Vieja Escuela\" as dismantled with Z-43's fall, but cells kept the name in Veracruz, where they fought the CJNG, killing three alleged Jalisco gunmen in José Azueta on 2 April 2019, and in Tamaulipas, Nuevo León, Coahuila, San Luis Potosí, Zacatecas and Quintana Roo, with Wikipedia's estimate of some 3,000 members.\n\nA separate Zeta-remnant brand, Sangre Nueva Zeta, surfaced on 19 October 2019 when fuel-theft boss Roberto de los Santos de Jesús \"El Bukanas\" threatened Puebla's governor; rooted in Puebla's \"Red Triangle\", Tlaxcala and Veracruz and allied with the CJNG, it moved from huachicol into cocaine and extortion, and its operator Néstor Carvajal Colotla \"El Viejón\" was arrested on 17 December 2021. In Tamaulipas, InSight Crime reported in 2021 that the ZVE appeared to have crossed the aisle to support the Gulf Cartel's Ciclones and the July 2021 Gulf truce against the CDN, operating in Río Bravo, Valle Hermoso and San Fernando; a July 2023 interrogation video showed ZVE gunmen near Reynosa taunting the Escorpiones over San Fernando.\n\nBy 2024-2026 the ZVE was a residual, cell-based network rather than a cartel. It does not appear in the February 2026 criminal map of Zacatecas or in InSight Crime's 2026 Gulf Cartel profile, and no leader of national standing has emerged since 2019; Z-43, its founder, has been in US federal prison since 2022 and was not among the capos Mexico transferred to the United States in February or August 2025. As of September 2026 the name survives among gunmen in northern Veracruz's Huasteca, the San Fernando corridor of Tamaulipas and parts of San Luis Potosí, squeezed between the CDN, Gulf factions and the CJNG.","leaders":[{"name":"José María Guízar Valencia","alias":"Z-43","role":"Founder and top leader","from":2015,"to":2018,"fate":"Arrested 9 Feb 2018 in Mexico City's Roma district (US$5M reward); waived extradition as a US-born dual citizen; pleaded guilty in the Eastern District of Texas 20 Dec 2021; sentenced to 40 years on 15 Nov 2022"},{"name":"Luis Reyes Enríquez","alias":"El Rex / Z-12","role":"Co-leader; veteran Zeta founder-generation commander","from":2015,"to":2018,"fate":"Stabbed to death 17 Nov 2018 inside the Nuevo Laredo prison; body stolen from the funeral home, dismembered and scattered outside prisons in the Monterrey area, head left at Ciudad Victoria"},{"name":"Martiniano de Jesús Jaramillo Silva","alias":"El Pata de Queso / Z-74","role":"Tamaulipas commander; linked to the 2010 San Fernando massacre","from":2015,"to":2017,"fate":"Arrested by Federal Police 14 Nov 2017 in Ciudad Victoria; died 17 Nov 2017 in a Mexico City hospital of kidney failure and peritonitis, aged 56"},{"name":"José Roberto Stolberg Becerra","alias":"La Barbie","role":"Reported leader after Z-43's arrest","from":2018,"to":2019,"fate":"Arrested by the FGR 9 Apr 2019 in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga, Jalisco, on a 2018 kidnapping and criminal-association warrant"},{"name":"Antonio Salas Perea","alias":"","role":"Co-founder","from":2016,"to":2018,"fate":"Fate not publicly reported"},{"name":"Comandante Bravo 1 (identity not confirmed)","alias":"Bravo 1","role":"Leader of Grupo Bravo in Ciudad Victoria","from":2014,"to":2017,"fate":"Not publicly reported"}],"factions":[{"id":"grupo_bravo","name":"Grupo Bravo","from":2014,"to":2017,"area":"Ciudad Victoria, Tamaulipas","leader":"Comandante Bravo 1","note":"Ex-Gulf Cartel cell that allied with the Grupo Operativo Zetas and, from March 2016, with the Gulf Cartel and Talibanes against the CDN"},{"id":"grupo_operativo_zetas","name":"Grupo Operativo Zetas","from":2014,"to":2016,"area":"Ciudad Victoria and central Tamaulipas","leader":"Not publicly identified","note":"Zeta cell that pacted with Grupo Bravo; an early label for the Vieja Escuela"},{"id":"zve_norte_veracruz","name":"ZVE northern Veracruz","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Northern Veracruz (Huasteca, Pánuco-Tuxpan corridor)","leader":"Not publicly identified","note":"Northern Veracruz is described as a group headquarters; fuel theft, extortion and clashes with CJNG (José Azueta, Apr 2019)"},{"id":"zve_tamaulipas_norte","name":"ZVE northern Tamaulipas","from":2016,"to":0,"area":"Río Bravo, Valle Hermoso, San Fernando","leader":"Not publicly identified","note":"Cooperated with the Gulf Cartel's Ciclones against the CDN after the July 2021 Gulf truce; fought the Escorpiones near Reynosa in 2023"},{"id":"sangre_nueva_zeta","name":"Sangre Nueva Zeta","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Puebla 'Red Triangle', Tlaxcala, Veracruz","leader":"Roberto de los Santos de Jesús 'El Bukanas'","note":"Autonomous Zeta-remnant brand allied with the CJNG; huachicol, cocaine and extortion; operator 'El Viejón' arrested 17 Dec 2021"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"March 2016 pact against the CDN; later cooperation with the Ciclones faction and the July 2021 Gulf truce"},{"cartel":"talibanes","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Fellow Zeta breakaway in the March 2016 anti-CDN alliance"},{"cartel":"other","from":2014,"to":2017,"note":"Grupo Bravo (Ciudad Victoria) merged its fight with the ZVE"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cdn","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Core enmity: the Treviño family's cartel versus the old guard; fighting in Ciudad Victoria, San Fernando and Veracruz"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Killings in Veracruz in March-April 2019; CJNG expansion along the Gulf coast"},{"cartel":"government","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Federal Police, Army, Navy and National Guard operations; Z-43 arrest 2018"}],"activities":["Cocaine and synthetic-drug trafficking along the Gulf coast","Fuel theft (huachicol) in Veracruz and Tamaulipas","Extortion","Kidnapping, including of migrants","Retail drug sales","Contract killing"],"peak":{"from":2016,"to":2018,"states":["TAM","VER","NLE","COA","SLP","ZAC","ROO"],"note":"Under Z-43 and El Rex the ZVE contested Ciudad Victoria and the San Fernando corridor with the CDN and held cells across seven states; Wikipedia's peak estimate is about 3,000 members"},"facts":["The ZVE's founding leader Z-43 carried a US$5 million US reward and was arrested in Mexico City on 9 February 2018.","Z-43 waived extradition as a US-born dual citizen, pleaded guilty on 20 December 2021 and was sentenced to 40 years in the Eastern District of Texas on 15 November 2022.","El Rex (Z-12) was stabbed to death inside the Nuevo Laredo prison on 17 November 2018, hours after arriving there; his remains were later dismembered and scattered outside prisons.","El Pata de Queso (Z-74), tied to the 2010 murder of 72 migrants, was arrested on 14 November 2017 and died three days later of kidney failure and peritonitis.","From March 2016 the ZVE, Grupo Bravo and Los Talibanes formed a four-way alliance with the Gulf Cartel against the Cártel del Noreste.","ZVE gunmen killed three alleged CJNG members in José Azueta, Veracruz, on 2 April 2019.","Wikipedia estimates the ZVE at roughly 3,000 members across seven Mexican states.","The affiliated Sangre Nueva Zeta announced itself on 19 October 2019 with threats against Puebla's governor; its operator 'El Viejón' was arrested on 17 December 2021."],"status_2026":"Active only as a residual, cell-based network: no national leader has emerged since La Barbie's April 2019 arrest, and founder Z-43 has been serving a 40-year US sentence since November 2022. The group is absent from the February 2026 Zacatecas criminal map and from InSight Crime's 2026 Gulf Cartel profile. As of September 2026 ZVE cells persist in northern Veracruz, the San Fernando corridor of Tamaulipas and parts of San Luis Potosí, cooperating opportunistically with Gulf factions against the CDN.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zetas_Vieja_Escuela","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edtx/pr/two-high-ranking-los-zetas-cartel-members-latest-sentenced-federal-prison-drug","https://insightcrime.org/news/cyclones-scorpions-old-school-killers-war-tamaulipas/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/zetas-profile/","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2018/11/20/asesinaron-al-z-12-uno-de-los-fundadores-del-cartel-de-los-zetas/","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/2017/11/17/1201900","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2019/4/9/detienen-en-jalisco-lider-de-los-zetas-vieja-escuela-223046.html","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2023/07/the-zeta-vieja-escuela-interrogates.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sangre_Nueva_Zeta"],"verified":true},{"id":"talibanes","name":"Los Talibanes","aka":["Cártel de Los Talibanes","Los Talibanes de San Luis Potosí","Grupo del Z-50"],"founded":2012,"dissolved":0,"parent":"zetas","origin":{"state":"SLP","city":"San Luis Potosí","lat":22.1565,"lng":-100.9855},"summary":"Los Talibanes are the Zeta cell of Iván Velázquez Caballero 'El Talibán' (Z-50), formed around 2007 and independent after his 2012 break with Z-40. Following his capture that September the group survived under relatives as a regional armed force in the San Luis Potosí Altiplano and southeastern Zacatecas. Allied with the Gulf Cartel and later with the Sinaloa Cartel against the CJNG, it remains in 2026 a small kidnapping- and extortion-driven remnant controlling a corridor between the two states.","history":"Los Talibanes take their name from Iván Velázquez Caballero, \"El Talibán\" or Z-50, born in Nuevo Laredo on 10 February 1970. A Gulf Cartel plaza boss in Nuevo Laredo in 2004, he rose within Los Zetas to run, from about 2007, their operations in San Luis Potosí, Zacatecas, Aguascalientes, Guanajuato and Nuevo León, including Monterrey, commanding some 400 men and acting as a major financial operator and money launderer. His cell in San Luis Potosí came to be called \"Los Talibanes\"; some accounts date the group's formation to 2007, when Z-40 left him in charge of trafficking and kidnapping in the Zacatecas and San Luis Potosí plazas, and others to its 2012 break with the Zetas.\n\nIn 2012 Velázquez broke with Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales (Z-40) over the imposition of plaza chiefs and, through banners and YouTube videos, accused him of betraying his own men. He allied with the Gulf Cartel and the Knights Templar, and Mexico offered 30 million pesos for his capture. Marines acting on DEA intelligence arrested him without a shot in the Tangamanga area of San Luis Potosí city on 26 September 2012, seizing weapons, grenades and US$20,000. Extradited to Laredo in November 2013, he pleaded guilty in McAllen on 7 April 2014, and on 21 July 2017 Judge Micaela Alvarez sentenced him to 30 years with a US$10 million forfeiture.\n\nThe group survived as a regional Zeta remnant under relatives, chiefly Raúl Velázquez Caballero \"El Talibancito\", reported by Infobae and Wikipedia as his son, entrenched in the Altiplano of San Luis Potosí (Salinas, Santo Domingo, Villa de Ramos) and southeastern Zacatecas. From March 2016 it joined the Zetas Vieja Escuela, Grupo Bravo and the Gulf Cartel against the Cártel del Noreste (CDN). In San Luis Potosí it infiltrated an elite state police unit, dissolved in November 2017, and on 4 February 2019 gunmen shot up the vehicle of federal deputy Pedro Carrizales \"El Mijis\"; on 6 March 2019 President López Obrador named Los Talibanes among the country's active criminal threats.\n\nThe Zacatecas war transformed the group. From late 2019 it aligned with the Sinaloa Cartel's \"Operativa MZ\" against the CJNG's push into Zacatecas, serving as local muscle on the Pinos-Ojocaliente belt and the federal highways toward the border. Homicides in Zacatecas rose 255% in 2020, and in early October 2020 46 executions were recorded on the San Luis Potosí-Zacatecas line, including six tortured bodies found at Vanegas on 3 October. The Navy described Los Talibanes in 2020 as the only local cartel in San Luis Potosí, though one that did not dominate the state, where the Gulf Cartel held 19 municipalities, Zeta remnants 11 and the CJNG 7. Cell leaders fell in succession: Martín \"R\" detained on 5 November 2020, Víctor Manuel \"El Destripador\" arrested on 24 August 2021, and Gary \"N\" \"El Gary\" killed on 12 May 2022. Zacatecas recorded 981 homicides in 2022 while Infobae still described the Talibanes in October 2023 as Sinaloa's allies against the CJNG.\n\nViolence in Zacatecas then fell sharply, to 114 intentional homicides in 2025 according to Expansión, as the Sinaloa-CJNG front cooled and the CDN pressed into Pinos and the San Luis Potosí border. A criminal map published by Politico.mx on 17 February 2026 listed Los Talibanes as a remnant Zeta cell operating locally in southeastern Zacatecas municipalities and along the San Luis Potosí border, beside the CJNG in the south and west, the Sinaloa Cartel in Fresnillo, the north and the capital, and the CDN in the east and north, including Pinos. On 2 September 2026, ahead of President Sheinbaum's visit to the state, Víctor Manuel Sánchez, a security analyst at the Universidad Autónoma de Coahuila, told Expansión that Los Talibanes still control a corridor connecting Zacatecas to San Luis Potosí, while a car bomb in Ojocaliente on Sunday 30 August 2026 that wounded ten people, two of them municipal police, and five bodies hung from a bridge at Pozo de Gamboa, in the municipality of Pánuco, in July signalled renewed CJNG-Sinaloa fighting. Authorities blamed the car bomb on the CJNG in reprisal for the arrest of an 18-year-old gunman. As of September 2026 Los Talibanes remain a small armed cell tied to Sinaloa interests, not an independent cartel.","leaders":[{"name":"Iván Velázquez Caballero","alias":"El Talibán / Z-50 / L-50","role":"Founder; former Zetas regional boss","from":2007,"to":2012,"fate":"Captured by the Navy 26 Sep 2012 in San Luis Potosí; extradited Nov 2013; pleaded guilty 7 Apr 2014; 30 years and US$10M forfeiture, 21 Jul 2017"},{"name":"Raúl Velázquez Caballero","alias":"El Talibancito","role":"Leader after El Talibán's capture (reported son)","from":2013,"to":0,"fate":"At large; identified by San Luis Potosí and federal authorities as the group's current chief"},{"name":"Martín 'R' (surname withheld)","alias":"","role":"Cell leader","from":2018,"to":2020,"fate":"Detained 5 Nov 2020"},{"name":"Víctor Manuel 'N'","alias":"El Destripador","role":"Enforcer / cell leader","from":2019,"to":2021,"fate":"Arrested 24 Aug 2021"},{"name":"Gary 'N'","alias":"El Gary","role":"Cell leader","from":2020,"to":2022,"fate":"Killed 12 May 2022"}],"factions":[{"id":"talibanes_altiplano_slp","name":"Talibanes of the San Luis Potosí Altiplano","from":2012,"to":0,"area":"Salinas, Santo Domingo, Villa de Ramos, Vanegas and the SLP-Zacatecas border","leader":"Raúl Velázquez Caballero 'El Talibancito'","note":"Core territory; described by the Navy in 2020 as the state's only home-grown cartel; kidnapping and extortion"},{"id":"talibanes_zacatecas","name":"Talibanes of southeastern Zacatecas","from":2012,"to":0,"area":"Southeastern Zacatecas and the Pinos-Ojocaliente belt toward San Luis Potosí","leader":"Local cells under El Talibancito","note":"Fought alongside the Sinaloa Cartel's Operativa MZ against the CJNG from 2019; contested by the CDN in Pinos in 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2012,"to":0,"note":"El Talibán allied with the Gulf Cartel against Z-40 in 2012; the pact carried into the March 2016 anti-CDN alliance"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Local muscle for the Sinaloa Cartel (Operativa MZ) in the Zacatecas war against the CJNG"},{"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Fellow Zeta breakaway in the March 2016 alliance against the CDN"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2012,"to":2013,"note":"El Talibán sought Knights Templar support when he broke with Z-40"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":2020,"note":"A short-lived arrangement in Zacatecas reported in late 2019; by 2021 the two were enemies"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"zetas","from":2012,"to":2015,"note":"Broke with Z-40 in 2012 over plaza appointments"},{"cartel":"cdn","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"CDN pushes into Pinos and the SLP border corridor"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"Zacatecas war; Talibanes fight on the Sinaloa side"},{"cartel":"government","from":2012,"to":0,"note":"Navy capture of El Talibán 2012; cell leaders arrested 2020-21"}],"activities":["Kidnapping for ransom","Extortion of businesses and ranchers","Methamphetamine and cocaine transit along the SLP-Zacatecas corridor to the border","Retail drug sales","Fuel theft","Contract violence for larger cartels"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2022,"states":["ZAC","SLP"],"note":"As Sinaloa's local ally in the Zacatecas war the group reached its greatest visibility; Zacatecas homicides rose 255% in 2020 and hit 981 in 2022"},"facts":["El Talibán commanded roughly 400 gunmen and Zeta operations in five states before his 2012 split with Z-40.","Mexico offered 30 million pesos for El Talibán; marines captured him on 26 September 2012 with US$20,000, weapons and grenades.","He received a 30-year US sentence and a US$10 million forfeiture order on 21 July 2017 after pleading guilty on 7 April 2014.","On 4 February 2019 Talibanes gunmen attacked federal deputy Pedro Carrizales 'El Mijis' in San Luis Potosí.","Zacatecas homicides rose 255% in 2020 as the Talibanes fought the CJNG alongside the Sinaloa Cartel.","In early October 2020, 46 executions were recorded on the SLP-Zacatecas border, including six tortured bodies at Vanegas on 3 October.","In 2020 the Navy counted the Gulf Cartel in 19 SLP municipalities, Zeta remnants in 11 and the CJNG in 7, with the Talibanes the only local cartel.","Zacatecas fell from 981 homicides in 2022 to 114 in 2025 (Expansión), yet the Talibanes still held a corridor to San Luis Potosí in September 2026.","San Luis Potosí dissolved an elite state police unit in November 2017 over infiltration by the Talibanes.","A car bomb in Ojocaliente on 30 August 2026 wounded ten people, including two municipal police officers, in a CJNG-Sinaloa dispute over the corridor the Talibanes help hold."],"status_2026":"Active but small. Politico.mx's 17 Feb 2026 criminal map listed Los Talibanes as a remnant Zeta cell operating locally in southeastern Zacatecas and along the San Luis Potosí border, and on 2 Sept 2026 Expansión quoted Víctor Manuel Sánchez of the Universidad Autónoma de Coahuila saying they still control a corridor linking Zacatecas to San Luis Potosí amid a renewed CJNG-Sinaloa dispute marked by the 30 Aug 2026 Ojocaliente car bomb. Led by Raúl Velázquez 'El Talibancito', the group operates as a Sinaloa-aligned local armed cell focused on kidnapping, extortion and drug transit.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Talibanes","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/10/13/quienes-son-los-talibanes-los-aliados-del-cartel-de-sinaloa-que-enfrentan-al-cjng-en-zacatecas/","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2020/10/08/quienes-son-los-talibanes-el-cartel-local-que-siembra-el-terror-en-san-luis-potosi/","https://sanluis.eluniversal.com.mx/seguridad/el-taliban-el-narco-fantasma-que-traiciono-los-zetas-desde-san-luis-potosi/","https://politico.mx/2026/02/17/zacatecas-bajo-fuego-este-es-el-mapa-delictivo-de-la-entidad/","https://politica.expansion.mx/estados/2026/09/02/zacatecas-disputa-cjng-cartel-sinaloa","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases/2017/07/21/30-years-los-zetas-cartel-leader","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iv%C3%A1n_Vel%C3%A1zquez_Caballero"],"verified":true},{"id":"guerreros_unidos","name":"Guerreros Unidos","aka":["GU","Los Guerreros Unidos","Los Acapulcos (eastern Morelos cell)","La Bandera (Iguala cell)"],"founded":2010,"dissolved":0,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Iguala de la Independencia","lat":18.3444,"lng":-99.5389},"summary":"Guerreros Unidos is a Beltrán Leyva splinter built after Arturo Beltrán Leyva's death in December 2009 by the Casarrubias Salgado brothers around Iguala and Cocula in northern Guerrero, with a second base in Morelos. It ran a heroin pipeline to Chicago hidden in passenger buses, captured Iguala's municipal government and became globally notorious for the 26 September 2014 disappearance of 43 Ayotzinapa students. Decapitated by arrests since 2014, it survives as fragmented cells in northern Guerrero and eastern Morelos.","history":"When marines killed Arturo Beltrán Leyva in Cuernavaca on 16 December 2009, the Beltrán Leyva Organization's structure in northern Guerrero and Morelos broke apart. Mario Casarrubias Salgado, \"El Sapo Guapo\", who had worked as a security operator for the Beltrán Leyva brothers and came from the Iguala–Cocula area, and Cleotilde Toribio Rentería, \"El Tilde\", assembled their own organization around 2010–2011, keeping the Iguala–Cocula–Taxco corridor and the poppy zones of the northern sierra. The group first appeared publicly in December 2011, claiming a triple homicide in Morelos, and from 2011 fought the other Beltrán Leyva offshoot, Los Rojos, for Iguala, the Chilpancingo–Cuernavaca corridors and heroin routes. El Tilde was arrested in Mexico City in 2012; Mario's brothers Sidronio, José Ángel (\"El Mochomo\") and Adán (\"El Tomate\") formed the ruling clan.\n\nGuerreros Unidos' distinctive asset was a heroin and cocaine pipeline to the US Midwest: drugs from Guerrero's sierra travelled in hidden compartments of commercial passenger buses to Chicago, where Pablo Vega Cuevas rented warehouses in Aurora, Batavia and Chicago between 2012 and 2014 and ran wholesale distribution. The DEA intercepted roughly 23,000 BlackBerry messages during that investigation and a December 2014 indictment in the Northern District of Illinois charged Vega and associates. Locally the group lived from extortion and kidnapping and captured municipal power: Iguala mayor José Luis Abarca and his wife María de los Ángeles Pineda Villa, sister of slain Beltrán Leyva lieutenants, ran the city, and her brother Salomón Pineda Villa, \"El Molón\", was a Guerreros Unidos boss. Iguala's municipal police answered to the cartel.\n\nMario was captured in April 2014 in Toluca, State of Mexico, and was later sentenced in 2017 to ten years for weapons and drug offences; his brother Sidronio, \"El Chino\", took over. On 30 June 2014 the army killed 22 alleged members in a warehouse in Tlatlaya, State of Mexico, most of them after surrendering. On the night of 26 September 2014, Iguala municipal police, with officers from Cocula and Huitzuco and under army observation, attacked buses commandeered by students of the Ayotzinapa rural teachers' college, killed six people and handed 43 students to Guerreros Unidos gunmen, who disappeared them. Morelos boss Benjamín Mondragón shot himself in Jiutepec on 14 October 2014 when surrounded by federal police; Sidronio was arrested on 16 October 2014 near Toluca; Abarca and Pineda were caught in Mexico City on 4 November 2014. Attorney General Jesús Murillo Karam's \"historical truth\" of November 2014 held that the cartel burned the bodies at the Cocula dump, a version the Inter-American experts (GIEI) declared scientifically impossible in September 2015.\n\nThe crackdown fragmented the group into cells in Iguala, Cocula, Huitzuco, Taxco and Teloloapan and in eastern Morelos (Cuautla–Yautepec, the so-called Los Acapulcos), fighting La Familia Michoacana in northern Guerrero and, from 2020, Los Tlacos. Iguala plaza chief Gildardo López Astudillo, \"El Gil\", was arrested in Taxco on 16 September 2015; Adán Casarrubias was arrested in October 2015 together with Cocula's mayor. In September 2019 a judge in Tamaulipas freed El Gil after excluding more than forty items of torture-tainted evidence, and in 2020 the FGR turned him into a protected witness; José Ángel Casarrubias was arrested in Metepec on 29 June 2020, released by a judge and re-arrested within hours. Mario died of COVID-19 in Altiplano prison on 25 July 2021. On 30 September 2021 Los Tlacos circulated a video of 20 captured members of the Iguala cell La Bandera, a sign of how far the group had weakened.\n\nIn August 2022 the presidential Truth Commission called Ayotzinapa a \"state crime\" and Murillo Karam was arrested on 19 August 2022; Adán Casarrubias had been extradited to Chicago on 26 May 2022. On 17 May 2023 Abarca was sentenced to 92 years and six months for kidnapping six Unidad Popular activists on 30 May 2013, and on 2 September 2023 the New York Times published the 23,000 intercepted texts showing the cartel's control over police and soldiers. El Gil went into flight in May 2024 and was re-arrested on 6 September 2024 at an address in Santa Martha Acatitla, Mexico City, on organized-crime charges unrelated to the disappearances.\n\nIn 2025–2026 the judicial record became contradictory. Sidronio was acquitted of organized crime on 3 April 2025 on double-jeopardy grounds but stays in Altiplano on forced-disappearance charges, as does El Mochomo after a March 2025 acquittal. On 16 May 2025 a judge in Iguala confirmed sentences of 25 to 119 years and three months against six members for kidnappings in Taxco; \"El Tomate\" got 11 years in Chicago on 5 March 2025 and Pablo Vega Cuevas, who testified against the cartel, was sentenced to ten years on 19 August 2025 and walked free on time served. Abarca received 20 more years in April 2025 for the murder of síndico Justino Carvajal, saw his acquittal over the 43 confirmed on 3 June 2025 and obtained an amparo suspension of the 92-year sentence in September 2025. \"El Pajarraco\", Juan Miguel Pantoja Miranda, was arrested in Almoloya, Hidalgo, on 4 June 2025 and \"La Vara\", head of the eastern Morelos cell, in Cuautla on 27 October 2025. Rolando Salgado \"El Gama\" got 54 years and 10 months on 2 July 2026. On 6 August 2026 former governor Ángel Aguirre was detained in the State of Mexico for allegedly ordering the destruction of Iguala courthouse footage of bus 1531 and entered Altiplano the next day; on 31 August 2026 La Jornada reported more than 20 bags of calcined remains and an unregistered crematorium at the El Ángel funeral home in Iguala, a version the parents publicly rejected days later. As of September 2026 Guerreros Unidos persists as extortion and trafficking cells around Iguala–Taxco–Teloloapan and Cuautla, at war with La Familia Michoacana and Los Tlacos, while the Sheinbaum government prepares a public case report for the families around the 12th anniversary on 26 September 2026.","leaders":[{"name":"Mario Casarrubias Salgado","alias":"El Sapo Guapo","role":"Co-founder and top leader","from":2010,"to":2014,"fate":"Captured April 2014 in Toluca, State of Mexico; sentenced to 10 years in 2017; died of COVID-19 in Altiplano prison on 25 July 2021"},{"name":"Cleotilde Toribio Rentería","alias":"El Tilde","role":"Co-founder","from":2010,"to":2012,"fate":"Arrested in 2012 in Mexico City; imprisoned"},{"name":"Sidronio Casarrubias Salgado","alias":"El Chino","role":"Top leader after brother's capture","from":2014,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested 16 Oct 2014 near Toluca; acquitted of organized crime in May 2023 and again on 3 Apr 2025 (double jeopardy) but held in Altiplano awaiting trial for the forced disappearance of the 43 students"},{"name":"José Ángel Casarrubias Salgado","alias":"El Mochomo","role":"Leader of the Casarrubias clan after 2014","from":2014,"to":2020,"fate":"Arrested 29 June 2020 in Metepec; freed and re-arrested within hours; acquitted of organized crime March 2025 but still jailed on forced-disappearance charges"},{"name":"Adán Casarrubias Salgado","alias":"El Tomate","role":"Head of the Chicago heroin pipeline","from":2012,"to":2015,"fate":"Arrested October 2015 with the mayor of Cocula; extradited to the US 26 May 2022; pleaded guilty to drug conspiracy and money laundering and was sentenced in Chicago to 11 years on 5 March 2025"},{"name":"Gildardo López Astudillo","alias":"El Gil / El Cabo Gil","role":"Iguala plaza chief","from":2013,"to":2015,"fate":"Arrested 16 Sept 2015 in Taxco; freed Sept 2019 after torture-tainted evidence was excluded; became an FGR protected witness in 2020; fugitive from May 2024 and re-arrested 6 Sept 2024 in Mexico City on organized-crime and drug charges; in federal prison, alleging torture (Dec 2025)"},{"name":"Benjamín Mondragón Pereda","alias":"El Benjamón","role":"Morelos boss","from":2012,"to":2014,"fate":"Shot himself on 14 Oct 2014 in Jiutepec, Morelos, when surrounded by federal police"},{"name":"Pablo Vega Cuevas","alias":"El Transformer","role":"Chicago/Aurora distribution boss","from":2012,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested in the Chicago area in 2014; cooperated with the DEA and testified against the cartel; sentenced to 10 years on 19 Aug 2025 and released the same day on time served, with $1.75 million forfeited"},{"name":"Salomón Pineda Villa","alias":"El Molón","role":"Iguala boss; brother-in-law of mayor Abarca","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"At large; still named by InSight Crime as a remaining leader"},{"name":"José Antonio N.","alias":"La Vara / El Flaco","role":"Leader of the eastern Morelos cell (Los Acapulcos)","from":2021,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 27 Oct 2025 in the Santa Cruz neighbourhood of Cuautla, Morelos, with four other people"}],"factions":[{"id":"iguala_cocula_cell","name":"Iguala–Cocula cell (El Gil / La Bandera)","from":2011,"to":0,"area":"Iguala, Cocula, Huitzuco, Taxco (GRO)","leader":"Gildardo López Astudillo 'El Gil' (2013–15); later 'El Molón' and 'El Chucky'","note":"Executed the 26 Sept 2014 attack on the Ayotzinapa students; its La Bandera cell lost 20 men to Los Tlacos in Sept 2021"},{"id":"morelos_cell","name":"Morelos cell","from":2011,"to":2014,"area":"Cuernavaca, Jiutepec, Temixco (MOR)","leader":"Benjamín Mondragón 'El Benjamón'","note":"Group's first public act was a triple homicide in Morelos in Dec 2011; Mondragón killed himself Oct 2014"},{"id":"los_acapulcos","name":"Los Acapulcos (eastern Morelos)","from":2018,"to":2025,"area":"Cuautla, Yautepec, Yecapixtla (MOR)","leader":"José Antonio N. 'La Vara'","note":"Named by Morelos authorities as the main generator of violence in eastern Morelos; leader arrested 27 Oct 2025"},{"id":"chicago_pipeline","name":"Chicago distribution network","from":2012,"to":2014,"area":"Aurora, Batavia and Chicago, Illinois","leader":"Pablo Vega Cuevas","note":"Heroin and cocaine moved in hidden compartments of passenger buses and unloaded in rented warehouses; dismantled by the DEA in 2014"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2009,"to":2014,"note":"Origin structure; kept Beltrán Leyva contacts for heroin supply"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2016,"to":2022,"note":"Reported tactical alignment against Los Rojos and La Familia Michoacana (InSight Crime)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"rojos","from":2011,"to":2019,"note":"War for Iguala, Chilpancingo and heroin routes; Rojos leader El Bocinas killed by GU gunmen Dec 2012"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2012,"to":0,"note":"Fighting in Tierra Caliente and northern Guerrero (Teloloapan, Taxco, Iguala)"},{"cartel":"tlacos","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Los Tlacos displaced GU from parts of Iguala; 20 La Bandera members captured Sept 2021"},{"cartel":"templarios","from":2012,"to":2014,"note":"Clashes on the Michoacán–Guerrero–Estado de México border"},{"cartel":"government","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"Tlatlaya killings (June 2014) and the post-Ayotzinapa crackdown"}],"activities":["Heroin production and trafficking (Guerrero poppy to Chicago via passenger buses)","Cocaine trafficking to the US Midwest","Extortion of merchants, transport operators and gas stations","Kidnapping for ransom","Control and corruption of municipal police and mayors","Forced disappearance and clandestine graves","Small-scale gold and poppy control in the Tixtla area"],"peak":{"from":2012,"to":2014,"states":["GRO","MOR","MEX"],"note":"Controlled Iguala, Cocula, Taxco and eastern Morelos, ran a bus-borne heroin pipeline to Chicago and owned Iguala's city government until the Sept 2014 Ayotzinapa attack triggered its dismantling"},"facts":["43 Ayotzinapa students were disappeared on 26 Sept 2014; only three have been forensically identified (Dec 2014, Sept 2015, July 2020) and six other people were killed that night.","The army killed 22 alleged members in a Tlatlaya warehouse on 30 June 2014, most after they had surrendered.","The DEA intercepted about 23,000 BlackBerry messages between the cartel, police and soldiers, published by the New York Times on 2 Sept 2023.","Iguala mayor José Luis Abarca was sentenced to 92 years and six months on 17 May 2023 for kidnapping six activists, and to 20 more years in April 2025 for the murder of the city's síndico Justino Carvajal.","At a 4 September 2025 meeting with the families, the government cited 158 people detained and 120 still imprisoned in the Ayotzinapa case.","A judge in Iguala confirmed on 16 May 2025 sentences of between 25 and 119 years and three months against six members for kidnappings in Taxco, reinstating a 2022 conviction.","Adán Casarrubias 'El Tomate' received 11 years in a Chicago federal court on 5 March 2025 after admitting he shipped 52 kilos of heroin to Chicago and had $600,000 smuggled back by bus.","Former Guerrero governor Ángel Aguirre was detained on 6 Aug 2026 and jailed in Altiplano the next day for allegedly ordering the destruction of courthouse video of bus 1531.","Founder Mario Casarrubias died of COVID-19 in prison on 25 July 2021; his three brothers have all been prosecuted."],"status_2026":"Guerreros Unidos survives in September 2026 as fragmented cells around Iguala, Cocula, Huitzuco, Taxco and Teloloapan and in eastern Morelos, living from extortion and local drug sales and fighting La Familia Michoacana and Los Tlacos; 'La Vara', head of its Morelos cell, was arrested in Cuautla on 27 October 2025. The historic leadership is dead, imprisoned or convicted in the United States, but Sidronio Casarrubias and El Mochomo remain untried for the disappearances after repeated acquittals on other counts, and both Adán Casarrubias and Pablo Vega Cuevas have now finished their US sentences. The Ayotzinapa case is again in flux after ex-governor Aguirre's August 2026 arrest and after La Jornada's 31 August 2026 report of bagged remains and an unregistered crematorium in Iguala, which the parents rejected as a confusing mixture of old evidence; the government is preparing a public report for the families around the 12th anniversary on 26 September 2026.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/guerreros-unidos-mexico/","https://chicago.suntimes.com/the-watchdogs/2025/03/05/former-guerreros-unidos-cartel-leader-el-tomate-gets-11-years-in-prison-for-smuggling-drugs-to-chicago","https://chicago.suntimes.com/the-watchdogs/2025/08/19/mexican-cartel-guerreros-unidos-pablo-vega-cuevas-heroin-buses-aurora-dea-mexico-college-students","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/03/absuelven-a-lider-del-grupo-criminal-guerreros-unidos-involucrado-en-el-caso-de-los-estudiantes-de-ayotzinapa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/05/17/jose-luis-abarca-fue-sentenciado-a-92-anos-de-prision-por-el-delito-de-secuestro-agravado/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/06/04/detienen-a-pajarraco-miembro-de-guerreros-unidos-vinculado-al-caso-ayotzinapa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/07/dictan-prision-preventiva-contra-angel-aguirre-exgobernador-de-guerrero-ligado-al-caso-ayotzinapa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/02/padres-de-ayotzinapa-niegan-hallazgo-de-20-bolsas-con-restos-y-exigen-pruebas-cientificas/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/10/29/quien-es-la-vara-presunto-lider-de-guerreros-unidos-detenido-en-morelos/","https://lasillarota.com/estados/2026/7/2/dan-54-anos-rolando-salgado-lea-el-gama-de-guerreros-unidos-por-secuestro-narcotrafico-606677.html","https://adondevanlosdesaparecidos.org/2025/09/25/ayotzinapa-de-vuelta-a-la-verdad-historica/"],"verified":true},{"id":"rojos","name":"Los Rojos","aka":["Cártel de los Rojos","Cártel de la Sierra (early Nava faction)","Los Rojos de Morelos"],"founded":2009,"dissolved":0,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Chilpancingo de los Bravo","lat":17.5514,"lng":-99.5008},"summary":"Los Rojos grew out of the Beltrán Leyva cell of Jesús Nava Romero, 'El Rojo', which moved Guerrero opium into Morelos, and became an independent organization after his death in December 2009. Under Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete' in Morelos and the Nava family around Chilpancingo and Chilapa it dominated central Guerrero's heroin economy and fought Guerreros Unidos and Los Ardillos, until the arrests of 2019 left it a fragmented brand operating in southern Morelos and Puebla's Mixteca.","history":"Jesús Nava Romero, \"El Rojo\", was an escort and trusted operator of Arturo Beltrán Leyva who moved opium gum and heroin from the Guerrero sierra to Morelos, and whose nickname gave the group its name. He died on 16 December 2009 in Cuernavaca, in the naval operation that killed Beltrán Leyva, and his cell became an autonomous organization run by his relatives. His nephew Jesús Nava Marino, \"R2\", briefly led what was then called the Cártel de la Sierra before being found dead in Iguala's dump in 2010. Crisóforo Rogelio Maldonado Jiménez, \"El Bocinas\", consolidated Los Rojos across Morelos and central Guerrero in 2010–2012; wounded and arrested in Cuernavaca on 10 December 2012, he was shot dead days later in a Mexico City hospital by gunmen attributed to Guerreros Unidos, sealing the feud between the two Beltrán Leyva offshoots. Command passed to Santiago Mazari Hernández, \"El Carrete\", born in San Gabriel las Palmas, Amacuzac, in southern Morelos, while the Nava clan ran Guerrero: Leonor Nava Romero, \"El Tigre\", \"La Garra\" or \"El Tío\", a brother of El Rojo, until his arrest at a checkpoint in Nuxco, Tecpan de Galeana, on 17 May 2014, and Zenén Nava Sánchez, \"El Chaparro\", from Apango and Chilapa.\n\nBetween 2013 and 2016 Los Rojos reached their peak. They controlled poppy zones in Heliodoro Castillo, Eduardo Neri and the Montaña Baja, the heroin routes through Chilpancingo, Zitlala and Chilapa toward Cuernavaca, and extortion in Chilpancingo, Iguala's outskirts and southern Morelos. The 2014 \"historical truth\" on Ayotzinapa claimed Guerreros Unidos attacked the students believing Los Rojos had infiltrated them, dragging the group into the case. War with Los Ardillos for Chilapa escalated: on 9–14 May 2015 some 300 armed men linked to Los Ardillos occupied the town hunting El Chaparro and at least 16 people disappeared. On 23 February 2015 fifteen Rojos gunmen were killed in a clash with La Familia Michoacana near Tetela del Río. In Morelos the group financed eleven municipal candidacies in 2015, and on 2 January 2016 Temixco's new mayor Gisela Mota was murdered the day after taking office, a killing state authorities blamed on Los Rojos. The DEA's 2020 National Drug Threat Assessment listed Los Rojos among the Mexican organizations operating in the United States, moving heroin to Oklahoma City, Jackson (Mississippi) and Dallas.\n\nThe decline came fast. Los Ardillos expelled Los Rojos from Chilapa by 2018; El Carrete's son Alexis Oswaldo was arrested in August 2018; Amacuzac's mayor, El Carrete's uncle Alfonso Miranda Gallegos, was jailed for kidnapping. El Carrete himself was captured on 1 August 2019 in Leonardo Bravo, Guerrero, wounded and lifted out of the sierra by an army helicopter, and El Chaparro was arrested on 21 August 2019 in Chilapa by state police along with three other men. On 24 October 2019 nine Rojos gunmen died in a clash at Pocha-Huizco, Zitlala. On 12 July 2023 a federal court in Uruapan sentenced El Carrete to 20 years for organized crime.\n\nFrom 2020 the organization fragmented into semi-autonomous cells: southern Morelos (Amacuzac, Puente de Ixtla, Jojutla), Puebla's Mixteca (Izúcar de Matamoros, Acatlán, Chietla, Tehuitzingo) and the southern State of Mexico, pressed by the CJNG, La Familia Michoacana and local rivals. In Chilpancingo the remnant under former ministerial policeman Orlando Pérez Nava, \"R13\", folded into Los Tlacos, which is why authorities still cite Rojos–Tlacos links; R13 was shot dead in the El Tomatal neighbourhood of Chilpancingo on 9 October 2024. Juan Miguel N., \"El Johnny\", the group's cocaine broker, was arrested on 16 April 2022 in Tepalcatepec, Michoacán, where he was buying from Cárteles Unidos; another Mazari son, Eric, \"El Erick\" or \"El Carretito\", fell on 3 April 2022 in Morelos.\n\nThe last two years have brought convictions rather than revival. Omar Cuenca Marino, \"El Niño Popis\", indicted by a federal grand jury in Georgia in December 2016 and described in Mexican reporting as a founder, was extradited to the United States in late February 2025 to face methamphetamine, cocaine, heroin and money-laundering charges in Atlanta. In November 2025 a federal judge sentenced Leonor Nava Romero to 15 years and three days for weapons, heroin, bribery and false-document offences. On 24 July 2026 a court sentenced El Carrete to 105 years for organized crime and kidnapping, on top of his 20-year term; he is held in Cefereso 13 in Oaxaca. On 14 August 2026 the navy and Puebla state prosecutors captured Calep N., 54, described as a Rojos boss for Acatlán, Izúcar, Chietla and Tehuitzingo, in the town of Tilapa, and Puebla investigators separately detained \"El Piojo\", named as the cell's second in command in Acatlán. The massacre of ten members of the Torres family at Texcalapa, Tehuitzingo, on 17 May 2026 was at first read as cartel violence, but ballistic work pointed to handguns rather than assault rifles and prosecutors ruled out organized crime, arresting a nephew of one of the victims, Juan Manuel N., \"El Pony\", on 19 May 2026 on a presumed family motive. In July 2026 the army also linked detained Cipog-EZ leader Jesús Plácido Galindo to Los Rojos and Los Tlacos, an accusation his supporters reject. As of September 2026 Los Rojos have no unified command: their Guerrero heartland belongs to Los Ardillos and Los Tlacos, and what remains are rural cells in the Mixteca and southern Morelos that extort ranchers, sell drugs and recruit deported youths.","leaders":[{"name":"Jesús Nava Romero","alias":"El Rojo","role":"Founder; Beltrán Leyva escort and lieutenant","from":2009,"to":2009,"fate":"Killed 16 Dec 2009 in Cuernavaca in the naval operation against Arturo Beltrán Leyva"},{"name":"Jesús Nava Marino","alias":"R2","role":"Interim leader (Cártel de la Sierra)","from":2009,"to":2010,"fate":"Found dead with signs of torture in Iguala's garbage dump in 2010"},{"name":"Crisóforo Rogelio Maldonado Jiménez","alias":"El Bocinas","role":"Top leader","from":2010,"to":2012,"fate":"Wounded and arrested 10 Dec 2012 in Cuernavaca; shot dead days later in a Mexico City hospital by gunmen attributed to Guerreros Unidos"},{"name":"Santiago Mazari Hernández","alias":"El Carrete","role":"Top leader (Morelos)","from":2012,"to":2019,"fate":"Arrested 1 Aug 2019 in Leonardo Bravo, Guerrero; sentenced to 20 years for organized crime on 12 July 2023 and to 105 years for organized crime and kidnapping on 24 July 2026"},{"name":"Leonor Nava Romero","alias":"El Tigre / La Garra / El Tío","role":"Guerrero leader; brother of El Rojo","from":2012,"to":2014,"fate":"Arrested 17 May 2014 at a checkpoint in Nuxco, Tecpan de Galeana, Guerrero; sentenced to 15 years and three days in November 2025"},{"name":"Zenén Nava Sánchez","alias":"El Chaparro","role":"Chilapa–Apango boss; nephew of El Rojo","from":2013,"to":2019,"fate":"Arrested 21 Aug 2019 in Chilapa by Guerrero state police; imprisoned"},{"name":"Omar Cuenca Marino","alias":"El Niño Popis","role":"Co-founder and trafficking operator","from":2010,"to":2019,"fate":"Indicted in Georgia in Dec 2016; extradited to the United States in late February 2025 on drug-trafficking and money-laundering charges"},{"name":"Juan Miguel N.","alias":"El Johnny","role":"International cocaine broker","from":2019,"to":2022,"fate":"Arrested 16 Apr 2022 in Tepalcatepec, Michoacán"},{"name":"Calep N.","alias":"Caleb","role":"Mixteca poblana boss","from":2020,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 14 Aug 2026 in Tilapa, Puebla, by the navy and the state prosecutor's office"}],"factions":[{"id":"morelos_mazari","name":"Morelos faction (El Carrete)","from":2012,"to":0,"area":"Amacuzac, Puente de Ixtla, Jojutla, Cuernavaca (MOR)","leader":"Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete'; later his sons","note":"Financed 2015 municipal campaigns; blamed for the Jan 2016 murder of Temixco mayor Gisela Mota; sons Alexis Oswaldo (2018) and Eric 'El Carretito' (2022) arrested"},{"id":"guerrero_nava","name":"Guerrero faction (Nava clan)","from":2009,"to":2019,"area":"Chilpancingo, Apango, Zitlala, Chilapa, Heliodoro Castillo (GRO)","leader":"Leonor Nava 'El Tigre', then Zenén Nava 'El Chaparro'","note":"Lost Chilapa to Los Ardillos by 2018; collapsed with El Chaparro's Aug 2019 arrest"},{"id":"mixteca_poblana","name":"Mixteca poblana cell","from":2013,"to":0,"area":"Izúcar de Matamoros, Acatlán, Chietla, Tehuitzingo (PUE)","leader":"Calep N. (arrested Aug 2026)","note":"Extorts ranchers and recruits deported youths; its second in command 'El Piojo' was detained in Acatlán in 2026"},{"id":"chilpancingo_remnant","name":"Chilpancingo remnant (R13)","from":2019,"to":2024,"area":"Chilpancingo (GRO)","leader":"Orlando Pérez Nava 'R13'","note":"Absorbed by Los Tlacos; R13 was killed in the El Tomatal neighbourhood of Chilpancingo on 9 Oct 2024"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2009,"to":2012,"note":"Origin cell; inherited Beltrán Leyva heroin routes to Morelos"},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2021,"to":2022,"note":"El Johnny sourced cocaine in Tepalcatepec from Cárteles Unidos territory"},{"cartel":"tlacos","from":2019,"to":2024,"note":"Chilpancingo remnants merged into Los Tlacos after 2019 under R13"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"guerreros_unidos","from":2011,"to":2019,"note":"Founding feud between the two Beltrán Leyva offshoots; El Bocinas killed Dec 2012"},{"cartel":"ardillos","from":2013,"to":2019,"note":"War for Chilapa; May 2015 incursion and hundreds of disappearances"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"15 Rojos killed near Tetela del Río Feb 2015; pressure in Morelos and Estado de México"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"CJNG expansion into Morelos and Puebla displaced Rojos cells"},{"cartel":"government","from":2012,"to":0,"note":"Leadership arrests in 2014, 2019, 2022, 2025 and 2026"}],"activities":["Poppy cultivation and heroin trafficking to the US (Oklahoma City, Jackson, Dallas)","Cocaine and methamphetamine brokering","Extortion of ranchers, merchants and transport","Kidnapping for ransom","Political financing and control of municipal governments in Morelos","Homicide and forced disappearance"],"peak":{"from":2013,"to":2018,"states":["GRO","MOR","PUE","MEX"],"note":"Dominated central Guerrero's heroin economy (Chilpancingo, Chilapa, Zitlala) and southern Morelos under El Carrete, with cells in Puebla's Mixteca and southern Estado de México"},"facts":["Founder Jesús Nava Romero died on 16 Dec 2009 in the same Cuernavaca naval operation that killed Arturo Beltrán Leyva.","Some 300 armed men entered Chilapa on 9–14 May 2015 hunting Rojos boss El Chaparro; at least 16 people disappeared.","The DEA's 2020 threat assessment listed Los Rojos among the Mexican organizations trafficking in the US, dominating heroin sales in Oklahoma City.","Los Rojos financed 11 municipal candidacies in Morelos in 2015; Temixco mayor Gisela Mota was murdered on 2 Jan 2016, one day into office.","El Carrete was captured on 1 Aug 2019 in Leonardo Bravo and El Chaparro on 21 Aug 2019 in Chilapa, three weeks apart.","Nine Rojos gunmen were killed in a single clash at Pocha-Huizco, Zitlala, on 24 Oct 2019.","El Carrete was sentenced to 105 years on 24 July 2026, on top of the 20-year term imposed on 12 July 2023.","Omar Cuenca Marino was linked by US investigators to seizures of 75 kg of methamphetamine, 23 kg of heroin and 47 kg of cocaine in Georgia in October 2013 and to $425,900 in cash seized in Missouri in February 2016."],"status_2026":"By September 2026 Los Rojos are a fragmented label rather than a cartel: their historic leaders are imprisoned (El Carrete sentenced to 105 years in July 2026, El Chaparro jailed since 2019, Leonor Nava sentenced in November 2025) or extradited (El Niño Popis, February 2025), and their Guerrero heartland has been taken by Los Ardillos and Los Tlacos. Active cells persist in southern Morelos and above all in Puebla's Mixteca, where alleged boss Calep N. was arrested in Tilapa on 14 August 2026; the ten-victim Tehuitzingo massacre of May 2026, initially read as cartel work, was attributed by Puebla prosecutors to a family dispute rather than to the group. Mexican security agencies still cite Rojos links in Chilpancingo, but those networks now operate under Los Tlacos.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/leader-mexico-los-rojos-arrested-michoacan-group-evolves/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_los_Rojos","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2026/7/25/sentencian-a-105-anos-de-prision-a-santiago-mazari-el-carrete-lider-de-los-rojos-376817.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/07/12/condenaron-a-el-carrete-lider-de-los-rojos-a-20-anos-de-carcel/","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/2014/05/18/959867","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/02/25/quien-es-el-nino-popis-lider-del-grupo-criminal-los-rojos-extraditado-a-eeuu-por-narcotrafico/","https://lasillarota.com/estados/2026/8/14/detienen-a-calep-n-presunto-lider-de-los-rojos-en-la-mixteca-poblana-524527.html","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/05/19/detienen-a-el-pony-por-masacre-de-familia-en-tehuitzingo-es-sobrino-de-uno-de-los-asesinados/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/10/10/quien-era-orlando-perez-alias-r13-cabecilla-de-los-tlacos-asesinado-en-chilpancingo/"],"verified":true},{"id":"ardillos","name":"Los Ardillos","aka":["Cártel de Los Ardillos","Los Ortega","Familia Ortega Jiménez"],"founded":2000,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Quechultenango","lat":17.4167,"lng":-99.2333},"summary":"Los Ardillos are a family organization from Quechultenango in Guerrero's Montaña Baja, founded around 2000 by former rural policeman Celso Ortega Rosas and run since his 2011 murder by his sons Celso, Jorge Iván and Antonio Ortega Jiménez, while a fourth brother, Bernardo, built a career in the PRD. Never a splinter but a local poppy and kidnapping clan that operated under the Beltrán Leyva umbrella, they drove Los Rojos out of Chilapa, terrorized Nahua communities and now fight Los Tlacos for Chilpancingo and the Montaña in a war that displaced whole villages in 2026.","history":"Celso Ortega Rosas, \"La Ardilla\", a former rural policeman from Tlanicuilulco in the municipality of Quechultenango, 38 km from Chilpancingo, grew poppy in the 1980s and 1990s and by around 2000 headed a family gang, built with three of his seven sons, that combined opium gum sales with kidnapping and extortion; in the mid-2000s reporting places it under the Beltrán Leyva umbrella and, in some accounts, in contact with Zetas cells then disputing Guerrero. The family's political arm appeared early: his son Bernardo Ortega Jiménez was PRD mayor of Quechultenango in 2002–2005 and then state deputy (2005–2008 and 2012–2015, presiding over the state Congress in 2013–2014), and is reported to have served as a federal deputy in 2018–2021. The patriarch was arrested in 2008 for the kidnapping and killing of two undercover SEIDO agents, was released at the start of 2011 and was shot dead in Tlanicuilulco on 26 January 2011. His sons Celso (\"La Vela\"), Jorge Iván (\"El Barco\") and Antonio (\"La Parota\") Ortega Jiménez took command.\n\nAfter the Beltrán Leyva collapse the brothers expanded from Quechultenango toward Chilapa de Álvarez, the commercial hub of the Montaña Baja and a poppy corridor then held by Los Rojos under Zenén Nava, \"El Chaparro\". Violence spread through some two dozen communities of Chilapa and José Joaquín de Herrera from mid-2014. The war for Chilapa (2013–2018) was one of Guerrero's darkest chapters: on 9–14 May 2015 some 300 armed men linked to Los Ardillos, posing as community police, occupied the town and at least 16 people vanished; hundreds more disappeared in the municipality over the following years. On 26 November 2015 the group was linked to an ambush of CRAC community police in Tixtla, and on 28 April 2018 it executed Chilapa's police commander Isidro Casarrubias Tlatempa. By 2018 Los Rojos had been expelled, and El Chaparro was captured in Chilapa in August 2019.\n\nControl of Chilapa put Los Ardillos against the Nahua community police CRAC-PF and the indigenous organization CIPOG-EZ around Rincón de Chautla, Ayahualtempa and Alcozacán. Attacks on 11 and 27 January 2019 left a dozen dead; in October 2019 some 150 gunmen assaulted Rincón de Chautla and were repelled by about 30 community police; on 17 January 2020 all ten members of the band Sensación from Alcozacán were murdered on their way home. Days later CRAC-PF paraded armed children in Ayahualtempa to protest state abandonment, repeating the protest in April 2021. As heroin prices collapsed with the rise of fentanyl, the group diversified into extortion of transport, markets, mining companies and municipal budgets, cattle and vehicle theft and the imposition of candidates.\n\nThe 2021 elections opened a new front. Los Tlacos, accused of backing Morena's Evelyn Salgado for governor and Norma Otilia Hernández for mayor, seized Chilpancingo's rackets, and Los Ardillos counterattacked from Petaquillas and the south of the capital. Los Tlacos declared war in June 2022. On 5 July 2023 two men described as transport leaders — Jesús Echeverría Peñafiel, \"El Topo\", named as head of the Ardillos' armed wing, and Bernardo Chávez Cruz — were detained; on 10 July protesters blockaded the Autopista del Sol, seized a police armoured vehicle, forced their way into the state government palace and took thirteen public servants hostage (five state police, four National Guard, three state and one federal official), moving them to Petaquillas. The captives were freed on 11 July after negotiations, and President López Obrador publicly blamed Los Ardillos and asked residents to abandon them. On 24 June 2023 severed heads were left in Chilpancingo with a message for the mayor, and on 5 July 2023 a video surfaced of Norma Otilia Hernández breakfasting with Celso Ortega Jiménez. A truce brokered by priest Filiberto Velázquez on 19 February 2024 barely lasted the year. On 3 October 2024 the incoming security chief, army captain Ulises Hernández Martínez, was killed, and on 6 October 2024 mayor Alejandro Arcos Catalán (PRI-PAN-PRD), six days in office, was beheaded after driving to Petaquillas without his escort, his head left on a pickup in the Villas del Roble neighbourhood; his security director Germán Reyes Reyes was arrested on 12 November 2024 for qualified homicide, accused of acting for Los Ardillos after the mayor refused their demands.\n\nBy 2025 analysts described a group of perhaps 2,000 members present in up to 29 of Guerrero's 85 municipalities, with relatives in office in Chilapa and interests in mining concessions in the Montaña and Costa Chica. In early May 2026 Los Ardillos launched an offensive with drones and high-calibre weapons on Tula, Xicotlán, Acahuehuetlán and Alcozacán in Chilapa, while the Me'phaa community of San Pedro Huitzapula, in neighbouring Atlixtac, was besieged for 17 days. The federal government counted 96 displaced people, four bodies on the Chilapa–Tlapa highway and six wounded, and the state put displacement at about 120, but CIPOG-EZ and the Tlachinollan human rights centre said more than 800 families and over a thousand people had fled. Security secretary Omar García Harfuch confirmed on 12 May 2026 that the Ardillos–Tlacos war was the cause and the federal government sent 690 soldiers, 400 National Guard and 200 state police with 164 vehicles and five helicopters; 118 people returned to Xicotlán by 14 May, though many communities did not go home. On 5 September 2026 a National Guard agent, Eduardo Bustos Pérez, was found dismembered with three others on the Tierra Colorada–Plan de Lima road in Juan R. Escudero after a coerced video in which he named an army superior and a state prosecution official as working for Los Tlacos against Los Ardillos; the same day a joint operation in rural Acapulco arrested 16 alleged Ardillos, including a financial operator, and on 7 September two more fell with improvised explosives. As of September 2026 the Ortega Jiménez brothers remain free and sought by the FGR.","leaders":[{"name":"Celso Ortega Rosas","alias":"La Ardilla / El Ardillo","role":"Founder","from":2000,"to":2011,"fate":"Arrested 2008 for the kidnapping and killing of two SEIDO agents; released early 2011; shot dead 26 Jan 2011 in Tlanicuilulco, Quechultenango"},{"name":"Celso Ortega Jiménez","alias":"La Vela / El Ardillo Mayor","role":"Top leader","from":2011,"to":0,"fate":"At large; sought by the FGR; filmed meeting Chilpancingo mayor Norma Otilia Hernández (video surfaced July 2023)"},{"name":"Jorge Iván Ortega Jiménez","alias":"El Barco","role":"Co-leader","from":2011,"to":0,"fate":"At large"},{"name":"Antonio Ortega Jiménez","alias":"La Parota","role":"Co-leader","from":2011,"to":0,"fate":"At large"},{"name":"Bernardo Ortega Jiménez","alias":"","role":"Political operator (PRD mayor of Quechultenango 2002–05, state deputy 2005–08 and 2012–15, president of the state Congress 2013–14, reported federal deputy 2018–21)","from":2002,"to":0,"fate":"Never charged; denies links, calling himself 'a public person'"},{"name":"Jesús Echeverría Peñafiel","alias":"El Topo","role":"Alleged head of the armed wing","from":2020,"to":2023,"fate":"Detained 5 July 2023 with Bernardo Chávez Cruz; his release was the demand behind the 10 July 2023 hostage-taking in Chilpancingo"},{"name":"Germán Reyes Reyes","alias":"","role":"Chilpancingo interim public security director accused of acting for the group","from":2024,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested 12 Nov 2024 for the qualified homicide of mayor Alejandro Arcos; bound over 19 Nov 2024; trial pending"}],"factions":[{"id":"chilapa_montana","name":"Chilapa / Montaña Baja wing","from":2013,"to":0,"area":"Chilapa de Álvarez, Zitlala, José Joaquín de Herrera, Atlixtac (GRO)","leader":"Celso Ortega Jiménez","note":"Core of the 2015–2018 war with Los Rojos and the 2019–2026 attacks on CRAC-PF and CIPOG-EZ communities"},{"id":"chilpancingo_petaquillas","name":"Chilpancingo–Petaquillas wing","from":2021,"to":0,"area":"Chilpancingo south, Petaquillas, Mochitlán (GRO)","leader":"Ortega Jiménez brothers","note":"Front line against Los Tlacos; the 13 hostages of July 2023 were taken to Petaquillas and mayor Alejandro Arcos was killed after visiting it on 6 Oct 2024"},{"id":"costa_chica_acapulco","name":"Costa Chica / rural Acapulco cell","from":2022,"to":0,"area":"Juan R. Escudero, Tecoanapa, rural Acapulco (GRO)","leader":"Unnamed financial operator (arrested Sept 2026)","note":"16 members including a financial operator arrested 5 Sept 2026 in an interinstitutional operation in Acapulco-area towns"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2005,"to":2010,"note":"Operated under the Beltrán Leyva umbrella until the organization's collapse"},{"cartel":"zetas","from":2005,"to":2008,"note":"Reported early contacts of the Ortega family with Zetas cells disputing Guerrero (Infobae, 2024)"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"rojos","from":2013,"to":2019,"note":"War for Chilapa; May 2015 incursion; Rojos expelled by 2018"},{"cartel":"tlacos","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"War for Chilpancingo and the Montaña; truce Feb 2024 collapsed; May 2026 Chilapa offensive"},{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2015,"to":0,"note":"Clashes with CRAC-PF and CIPOG-EZ community police in Rincón de Chautla, Ayahualtempa and Alcozacán"},{"cartel":"government","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"July 2023 hostage-taking blamed on the group by López Obrador; federal operations after the Arcos murder and the 2026 displacement crisis"}],"activities":["Opium poppy cultivation and heroin trafficking (declining)","Extortion of transport, merchants, markets and religious festivals","Extortion and control of gold and silver mining concessions","Kidnapping for ransom","Cattle and vehicle theft","Imposition of candidates and capture of municipal budgets","Forced displacement of indigenous communities, drone attacks"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2026,"states":["GRO"],"note":"Estimated at around 2,000 members present in up to 29 of Guerrero's 85 municipalities, controlling Chilapa and Quechultenango, contesting Chilpancingo and expanding to Costa Chica and rural Acapulco"},"facts":["Founder Celso Ortega Rosas was murdered on 26 Jan 2011 in Tlanicuilulco, weeks after leaving prison for the killing of two federal agents.","Some 300 gunmen linked to the group occupied Chilapa on 9–14 May 2015 and at least 16 people disappeared.","All ten members of the band Sensación from Alcozacán were killed on 17 Jan 2020; CRAC-PF answered by arming children in Ayahualtempa.","On 10 July 2023 protesters blamed on the group held 13 public servants hostage in Chilpancingo — five state police, four National Guard, three state and one federal official — releasing them the next day.","Chilpancingo mayor Alejandro Arcos was beheaded on 6 Oct 2024, six days after taking office; his security chief was arrested on 12 Nov 2024 for the homicide.","Analysts estimate no fewer than 2,000 members and a presence in 29 of Guerrero's 85 municipalities.","The May 2026 Chilapa offensive left an official count of 96 displaced, four dead and six wounded, while CIPOG-EZ said more than 800 families were affected; San Pedro Huitzapula, in Atlixtac, was besieged for 17 days.","The federal government deployed 690 soldiers, 400 National Guard and 200 state police, plus 164 vehicles and five helicopters, to Chilapa in May 2026.","A 5 Sept 2026 operation in rural Acapulco netted 16 alleged members, including the group's financial operator."],"status_2026":"In September 2026 Los Ardillos remain one of Guerrero's strongest groups, led from Quechultenango by the fugitive Ortega Jiménez brothers and locked in a war with Los Tlacos that in May 2026 emptied Nahua and Me'phaa villages around Chilapa and Atlixtac and drew a federal deployment of nearly 1,300 troops and police. State and federal forces have hit the group's periphery, arresting 16 members including its financial operator on 5 September 2026 after a National Guard agent was found dismembered in Juan R. Escudero, and two more with explosives on 7 September, but the leadership is untouched and the family's political protection in Chilapa and the Montaña remains under public scrutiny.","sources":["https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/12/quienes-son-los-ardillos-historia-lideres-y-crimenes-del-grupo-que-desplaza-comunidades-indigenas-en-guerrero/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Ardillos","https://www.milenio.com/policia/quienes-son-los-ardillos-radiografia-de-sus-operaciones-en-guerrero","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/el-horror-de-los-ardillos-en-chilpancingo-la-decapitacion-del-alcalde-alejandro-arcos-catalan-como-firma-de-la-violencia/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2024/11/12/asesinato-del-alcalde-de-chilpancingo-detienen-a-german-n-por-el-homicidio-de-alejandro-arcos/","https://zetatijuana.com/2023/07/mas-bloqueos-y-protestas-en-chilpancingo-se-llevan-a-13-rehenes-gobierno-de-amlo-culpa-a-ardillos/","https://politica.expansion.mx/mexico/2026/05/17/quienes-son-los-ardillos-los-tlacos-desplazamiento-chilapa","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2026/5/16/hallan-restos-de-la-refriega-contra-habitantes-de-san-pedro-huitzapula-guerrero-371981.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/06/caen-16-presuntos-integrantes-de-los-ardillos-luego-del-asesinato-de-un-agente-de-la-guardia-nacional-en-guerrero/"],"verified":true},{"id":"tlacos","name":"Los Tlacos","aka":["Cártel de la Sierra","Federación Guerrerense","Policía Comunitaria de General Heliodoro Castillo","FUPCEG (Frente Unido de Policías Comunitarias del Estado de Guerrero)"],"founded":2017,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"GRO","city":"Tlacotepec (General Heliodoro Castillo)","lat":17.7833,"lng":-99.9833},"summary":"Los Tlacos, named after Tlacotepec in the poppy-growing sierra of General Heliodoro Castillo, were organized in May 2017 as a 'community police' by Onésimo Marquina Chapa, 'El Necho', a local strongman with Rojos and Guerreros Unidos ties, after the Beltrán Leyva collapse left the sierra in dispute. They grew out of self-defence structures rather than as a splinter of any cartel, became central Guerrero's dominant armed group, seized Chilpancingo's rackets after the 2021 elections and have fought Los Ardillos since 2021 and La Familia Michoacana since 2022, amid persistent accusations of official protection.","history":"Tlacotepec, seat of General Heliodoro Castillo, sits in the heart of Guerrero's opium sierra. After Arturo Beltrán Leyva's death in December 2009 the mountains were contested by Los Rojos, Guerreros Unidos, Los Granados and the Cártel del Sur of Isaac Navarrete Celis, \"El Señor de la I\". Onésimo Marquina Chapa, \"El Necho\", a local figure who had worked with Rojos bosses Zenén Nava and Santiago Mazari and with Guerreros Unidos' Gildardo López Astudillo, organized a \"Policía Comunitaria\" in May 2017 that replaced the municipal police of Heliodoro Castillo; the group therefore grew out of community-police and self-defence structures rather than splitting off from a cartel. From 2018 he headed the Frente Unido de Policías Comunitarias del Estado de Guerrero (FUPCEG), which pushed into Leonardo Bravo and Eduardo Neri to control the Mezcala mining zone around the Los Filos–El Bermejal gold mine and fight the Cártel del Sur; the November 2018 offensive around Filo de Caballos displaced some 1,200 people. His brother Javier Marquina Chapa, \"El Barbas\", was second in command and Humberto Moreno Catalán, \"El Comunitario\", the public face.\n\nThe capture of the Rojos leadership in August 2019 let Los Tlacos absorb Rojos remnants and expand north and east. On 2 February 2020 an armed attack on a rodeo in Zacacoyuca, near Iguala, killed one person and emptied the village; on 22 August 2020 a long firefight in Tianquizolco, Cuetzala del Progreso, left ten dead and marked their advance against Guerreros Unidos. El Barbas died in June 2021 in a Chilpancingo hospital after an internal clash in Tetela del Río. Bishop Salvador Rangel alleged that the group backed Morena's Evelyn Salgado for governor and Norma Otilia Hernández for mayor of Chilpancingo in the June 2021 elections. In Iguala, on 30 September 2021, Los Tlacos circulated a video of 20 kneeling captives from the Guerreros Unidos cell La Bandera — some accounts say the men were then executed and displayed outside the mayor-elect's campaign office — threatened the mayor for dealing with rivals and announced that tortilla shops and butchers would stop paying quotas to Guerreros Unidos. On 22 December 2021 about a hundred armed men freed two detained members from state police in Chichihualco.\n\nFrom 2022 Los Tlacos took over Chilpancingo's extortion of transport, the slaughterhouse and markets, declaring war on Los Ardillos in June 2022. La Familia Michoacana pressed from Tierra Caliente, where its 5 October 2022 massacre of 20 people including mayor Conrado Mendoza in San Miguel Totolapan showed the scale of the contest. In July 2023 the capital was paralysed for days by blockades, arson and a hostage-taking that the federal government attributed to Los Ardillos, not to Los Tlacos, in a fight the two groups were waging over the city's rackets; President López Obrador publicly acknowledged the power of the armed groups in Chilpancingo. In late 2023 Los Tlacos rebranded in videos and corridos as the Federación Guerrerense. On 4 January 2024 a Familia Michoacana drone strike killed at least five in Buenavista de los Hurtado, Heliodoro Castillo; on 4–5 February 2024 the Michoacanos threatened El Necho by video, and a clash in San Miguel Totolapan left about 12 dead. Priest Filiberto Velázquez then brokered truces with Los Ardillos (19 February 2024) and La Familia Michoacana (21–22 February), and on 5 March 2024 Los Tlacos declared the \"narco-war\" over.\n\nThe truce collapsed within months. After Los Ardillos beheaded Chilpancingo mayor Alejandro Arcos on 6 October 2024, Tlacos operator Orlando Pérez Nava, \"R13\", a former ministerial policeman and ex-Rojos, was shot dead in the El Tomatal neighbourhood of the capital on 9 October 2024; the Chilpancingo plaza answered to Pedro Geovanny Gutiérrez, \"El Marras\". In November 2024 state police dismantled a Tlacos vehicle-theft ring working the Autopista del Sol. Through 2025 the group entrenched itself in the capital's rackets — two men linked to it were caught with drugs, weapons and tactical gear in Chilpancingo on 28 October 2025 — staged an armed caravan in Tlacotepec in December 2025 and pushed into Tierra Caliente under Leonel Barragán Tolentino, \"La Changa\" or \"El 80\", adding illegal logging and mining to extortion and opium.\n\nIn 2026 the war with Los Ardillos spread into the Montaña: the May offensive around Chilapa and Atlixtac displaced at least a hundred people by official count and, according to CIPOG-EZ and Tlachinollan, more than 800 families, and security secretary Omar García Harfuch named both groups on 12 May 2026. In July 2026 the army linked detained Cipog-EZ leader Jesús Plácido Galindo to Los Tlacos and Los Rojos, and on 28 July a drone attack on El Fresno, in the Chilpancingo sierra, injured four people including three minors. In August six members were arrested for demanding 300,000 pesos from a merchant and cell leader \"El Traka\" was captured; on 27 August 2026 gunmen under \"La Changa\" attacked seven houses at Cerro de las Lumbreras, San Miguel Totolapan, at about four in the morning, killing six members of one family, among them a disabled 18-year-old woman, after they refused to sell their land for logging. On 5 September 2026 National Guard agent Eduardo Bustos Pérez was found dismembered with three others on the Tierra Colorada–Plan de Lima road in Juan R. Escudero, after an eight-minute video recorded under visible coercion in which he named his superior and a Guerrero prosecution official as taking money from Los Tlacos to act against Los Ardillos and Los Rusos; the Defense Ministry promised to investigate. As of September 2026 El Necho remains free and Los Tlacos are the strongest force in central Guerrero and Chilpancingo, contested only by Los Ardillos.","leaders":[{"name":"Onésimo Marquina Chapa","alias":"El Necho / El Señor del Mal","role":"Founder and top leader","from":2017,"to":0,"fate":"At large; publicly threatened by La Familia Michoacana in Feb 2024; no capture reported"},{"name":"Javier Marquina Chapa","alias":"El Barbas","role":"Second in command; brother of El Necho","from":2017,"to":2021,"fate":"Died June 2021 in a Chilpancingo hospital of wounds from an internal clash in Tetela del Río"},{"name":"Humberto Moreno Catalán","alias":"El Comunitario","role":"Spokesman and senior commander","from":2017,"to":0,"fate":"At large; reported cousin of former state official Mario Moreno Arcos"},{"name":"Leonel Barragán Tolentino","alias":"La Changa / El 80","role":"Chief of gunmen; Tierra Caliente operator","from":2021,"to":0,"fate":"At large; named by residents as leading the 27 Aug 2026 San Miguel Totolapan killings"},{"name":"Pedro Geovanny Gutiérrez Hernández","alias":"El Marras","role":"Chilpancingo plaza boss","from":2022,"to":0,"fate":"At large"},{"name":"Orlando Pérez Nava","alias":"R13 / AK-47","role":"Chilpancingo operator; ex-ministerial police and ex-Rojos","from":2019,"to":2024,"fate":"Shot dead on 9 Oct 2024 in the El Tomatal neighbourhood of Chilpancingo, days after the murder of mayor Alejandro Arcos"}],"factions":[{"id":"policia_comunitaria_heliodoro_castillo","name":"Policía Comunitaria / FUPCEG","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"General Heliodoro Castillo, Leonardo Bravo, Eduardo Neri (GRO)","leader":"Onésimo Marquina Chapa 'El Necho'","note":"Armed core of the group; replaced municipal police in May 2017 and fought the Cártel del Sur for the Mezcala mining zone and the Los Filos–El Bermejal gold mine"},{"id":"chilpancingo_plaza","name":"Chilpancingo plaza","from":2021,"to":0,"area":"Chilpancingo de los Bravo (GRO)","leader":"Pedro Geovanny Gutiérrez 'El Marras'; Orlando Pérez 'R13' (killed Oct 2024)","note":"Controls transport, market and slaughterhouse extortion in the state capital"},{"id":"iguala_cell","name":"Iguala cell","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Iguala, Buenavista de Cuéllar, Huitzuco, Cuetzala del Progreso (GRO)","leader":"Unnamed","note":"Displaced Guerreros Unidos' La Bandera; video of 20 captives on 30 Sept 2021"},{"id":"tierra_caliente_cell","name":"Tierra Caliente / San Miguel Totolapan cell","from":2024,"to":0,"area":"San Miguel Totolapan, Apaxtla, Teloloapan (GRO)","leader":"Leonel Barragán Tolentino 'La Changa'","note":"Illegal logging and land seizures; six of one family killed at Cerro de las Lumbreras on 27 Aug 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"rojos","from":2017,"to":2024,"note":"El Necho rose alongside Rojos bosses; absorbed Rojos remnants in Chilpancingo after 2019 under R13"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Reported supply and tactical alignment against La Familia Michoacana"},{"cartel":"other","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"Los Peques, a local sierra group, cited as allies"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"ardillos","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"War for Chilpancingo and the Montaña; truce 19 Feb 2024 collapsed; Chilapa offensive May 2026"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2022,"to":0,"note":"Drone attacks in Heliodoro Castillo Jan 2024; Feb 2024 threats against El Necho; truce Feb 2024"},{"cartel":"guerreros_unidos","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Displaced GU in Iguala and Cuetzala; 20 La Bandera captives Sept 2021"},{"cartel":"other","from":2018,"to":2022,"note":"Cártel del Sur of Isaac Navarrete 'El Señor de la I' in Leonardo Bravo and Eduardo Neri"},{"cartel":"government","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Clashes with state police (Chichihualco, Dec 2021), dismantling of its Autopista del Sol car-theft ring (Nov 2024) and 2025–26 arrests of Chilpancingo extortion cells"}],"activities":["Opium gum production and heroin trafficking","Extortion of transport, markets, slaughterhouse and merchants in Chilpancingo","Illegal mining and timber extraction, land seizures","Kidnapping and murder","Retail drug sales and vehicle theft on the Autopista del Sol","Drone attacks on rival communities","Political pressure and alleged campaign support"],"peak":{"from":2021,"to":2026,"states":["GRO"],"note":"Dominant in the central sierra and Chilpancingo after the 2021 elections, present from Heliodoro Castillo and Leonardo Bravo to Iguala, Chilapa and San Miguel Totolapan"},"facts":["Los Tlacos began in May 2017 as a 'community police' that replaced the municipal police of General Heliodoro Castillo.","Their November 2018 push against the Cártel del Sur displaced about 1,200 people from the Filo de Caballos area.","A firefight in Tianquizolco, Cuetzala del Progreso, on 22 Aug 2020 killed ten people.","On 30 Sept 2021 they filmed 20 captured Guerreros Unidos gunmen kneeling in Iguala, and on 22 Dec 2021 about 100 gunmen freed two detained members from state police in Chichihualco.","A Familia Michoacana drone strike on Buenavista de los Hurtado killed at least five people on 4 Jan 2024.","Priest Filiberto Velázquez brokered truces with Los Ardillos (19 Feb 2024) and La Familia Michoacana (21–22 Feb 2024), and the group declared the war over on 5 March 2024.","Six members of one family were killed at Cerro de las Lumbreras, San Miguel Totolapan, on 27 Aug 2026 for refusing to sell land for logging.","National Guard agent Eduardo Bustos Pérez was found dismembered with three others on 5 Sept 2026 after a coerced video naming an army commander and a state prosecutor as being paid by Los Tlacos.","They dispute the Mezcala mining zone around the Los Filos–El Bermejal gold mine, which produced roughly 45,000 ounces of gold in 2020."],"status_2026":"In September 2026 Los Tlacos, still led by the fugitive Onésimo Marquina Chapa 'El Necho', are the dominant armed group in central Guerrero and Chilpancingo, fighting Los Ardillos across the Montaña since the May 2026 Chilapa displacement crisis and expanding into Tierra Caliente through logging and land seizures that produced the 27 August 2026 killing of six people in San Miguel Totolapan. State forces have arrested Chilpancingo extortion cells (six members and 'El Traka' in August 2026), but the coerced video in which a National Guard agent alleged that an army commander and a state prosecutor were on the group's payroll, followed by his dismemberment on 5 September 2026 in Juan R. Escudero, has put official protection of Los Tlacos at the centre of the state's security debate.","sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Tlacos","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/12/quienes-son-los-tlacos-historia-lideres-y-actividades-criminales-del-grupo-que-asedia-y-violenta-comunidades-en-la-sierra-de-guerrero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/12/asi-inicio-la-guerra-entre-los-tlacos-y-los-ardillos-el-conflicto-que-desangra-guerrero-y-golpea-a-comunidades-indigenas/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/02/28/cual-es-el-pasado-criminal-de-el-necho-estos-eran-los-nexos-del-lider-de-los-tlacos-en-guerrero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/10/10/quien-era-orlando-perez-alias-r13-cabecilla-de-los-tlacos-asesinado-en-chilpancingo/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/08/27/incursion-de-los-tlacos-en-guerrero-deja-seis-muertos-y-varios-heridos/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/07/de-mandos-del-ejercito-a-funcionarios-de-la-fiscalia-de-guerrero-los-senalados-por-agente-de-la-gn-asesinado-de-presuntos-nexos-con-los-tlacos/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2024/11/27/desarticulan-banda-de-los-tlacos-dedicada-al-robo-de-vehiculos-en-la-autopista-del-sol-339594.html","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masacre_de_San_Miguel_Totolapan"],"verified":true},{"id":"union_tepito","name":"La Unión Tepito","aka":["La Unión","La Unión de Tepito","Unión Insurgentes (2013 cell, a.k.a. Unión Condesa)"],"founded":2009,"dissolved":0,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"CMX","city":"Tepito, Cuauhtémoc, Ciudad de México","lat":19.4436,"lng":-99.1283},"summary":"La Unión Tepito is Mexico City's largest home-grown criminal organization, built between 2009 and 2010 from Tepito drug retailers and defectors of the Beltrán Leyva/La Barbie network and of La Familia Michoacana. It dominates retail drug sales, extortion of street vendors and nightlife venues, and predatory 'gota a gota' lending across central Mexico City, and since 2017 has been locked in a war with its splinter, Fuerza Anti-Unión. Repeated leadership arrests since 2018 have fragmented it into semi-autonomous cells that remain the capital's main extortion machine in 2026.","history":"Tepito, the sprawling contraband market north of Mexico City's Zócalo, had been run for decades by smuggling families with Beltrán Leyva connections when the 2009-2010 rupture between Édgar Valdez Villarreal, 'La Barbie', and the Beltrán Leyva brothers reached the capital. Press and prosecutors date the birth of La Unión Tepito to a meeting in Tepito in May 2010 at which the neighbourhood's dealers were pulled into a single structure at Valdez Villarreal's initiative; InSight Crime places its emergence between 2009 and 2012 among defectors from the Beltrán Leyva Organization and La Familia Michoacana. Its first operational chief was Francisco Javier Hernández Gómez, 'Pancho Cayagua'. In October 2012 six alleged Sinaloa Cartel distributors were executed as the new group cleared the field.\n\nThe group announced itself with the Heaven case. On 26 May 2013 thirteen young people from Tepito, among them sons of old-guard capos Jorge Ortiz 'El Tanque' and Alfonso Sánchez 'El Duque', were abducted in broad daylight from the Heaven after-hours bar in the Zona Rosa by the 'Unión Insurgentes' cell. The trigger was the killing two days earlier, on 24 May 2013, of dealer Horacio Vite Ángel, 'El Chaparro', outside the Black bar in Condesa; case files say Vite had been selling there without the permission of Jerzy Ortiz, son of 'El Tanque'. The thirteen bodies were found on 23 August 2013, three months after the abduction, in a clandestine grave in Tlalmanalco, Estado de México; three perpetrators were reported sentenced to 520 years each in September 2015. The massacre eliminated the old families and left La Unión dominant. Between 2013 and 2017 it took over bars and clubs in Roma, Condesa, Zona Rosa and Polanco as drug outlets, squeezed street vendors, and spread predatory lending, extending into Gustavo A. Madero, Venustiano Carranza, Benito Juárez, Miguel Hidalgo and Iztapalapa.\n\nSuccession tore the group apart in 2017. Roberto Moyado Esparza, 'El Betito', imposed himself; Pancho Cayagua was shot dead in October 2017 in a shopping-centre car park at Indios Verdes and his loyalists were purged by December. Jorge Flores Concha, 'El Tortas', broke away and founded Fuerza Anti-Unión, and from late 2017 dismembered bodies with narco-messages appeared on the capital's avenues. El Betito was arrested on 8 August 2018 in Santo Tomás Ajusco, Tlalpan, and on 14 September 2018 gunmen dressed as mariachis killed six people in Plaza Garibaldi, an attack for which Unión chief Víctor Hugo Ávila Fuentes, 'El Huguito', was formally charged in August 2024. David García Ramírez, 'El Pistache', who ran Polanco bar extortion, was captured on 30 October 2018 in Santa Fe, Álvaro Obregón, charged with drug offences and money laundering on 5 November and moved to the Cefereso in Ciudad Juárez on 8 November, leaving Óscar Andrés Flores Ramírez, 'El Lunares', in command.\n\nThe state responded. On 18 September 2019 six people were killed at a food stand in Colonia Doctores; on 22 October 2019 police stormed Peralvillo 33 in Tepito, finding tunnels and an altar with human skulls and arresting 31 people, 27 of whom a judge freed within days. Under security chief Omar García Harfuch the pressure became systematic: El Lunares was captured on 31 January 2020 in Pachuca, Hidalgo, in a joint Navy and SSC operation in which he offered officers five million pesos to let him go; a federal judge freed him in February and he was immediately re-arrested at the prison gate for the 2019 murder of a woman shot in the face at a quesadilla stand in Colonia Morelos. His brother 'El Junior' fell in May 2020, 'El Irving' in August 2020, and Raúl Rojas Molina, 'Mi Jefe', who took over in November 2020, was arrested in May 2022. The UIF froze about 1,500 accounts holding roughly US$5.2 million, and roughly 550 members were arrested between January 2020 and April 2022, when Harfuch declared the group 'irreparably fragmented'.\n\nFragmentation did not mean disappearance. Eduardo Ramírez Tiburcio, 'El Chori', ran the Centro Histórico extortion network with a 5-million-peso bounty on his head until his arrest on 18 March 2024; he was placed in formal preventive detention on 27 March 2024 over a 2015 homicide, his amparo against transfer to the Altiplano was nullified on 30 July 2024, and two further warrants for criminal association and illicit use of resources were served on 16 October 2024. El Huguito was caught in Cancún on 27 July 2024, flown to Mexico City the next day and linked to trial for Garibaldi on 6 August 2024; a hearing in his case was postponed on 3 December 2025 after a shooting at Plaza Garibaldi. Courts handed El Lunares 27 years 6 months (convicted October 2021, ratified 28 June 2022) and El Betito 43 years 9 months (December 2023). At least five factions kept the brand: El Chori's Centro cells, the Herrera Segura family around Irving Herrera 'El Irving', the youth cell UJ40 in Colonia Morelos, 'Los Gregory' in Miguel Hidalgo and Coyoacán, and all-female sales cells, with offshoots in Estado de México, Morelos, Hidalgo, Querétaro, Puebla, Tlaxcala and Veracruz.\n\nLa Unión was not among the six Mexican cartels the United States designated as foreign terrorist organizations in February 2025, though by mid-2026 Mexico City officials feared it could be next. In January 2026 authorities documented links with Tren de Aragua over sexual exploitation, and in June 2026 a CJNG video declared war on La Unión and the Cártel de Tláhuac in order to 'bring order' to Mexico City and Estado de México. Roughly a month later motorcycle gunmen tied to the CJNG-backed Anti-Unión hunted Unión members across Cuauhtémoc: Jesús Ismael Mendoza 'El Maelo' on 9 July, Lizeth Yuridia Juárez 'La Chofis' on 14 July, Edgar 'N' 'El Valentín' and Esteban Gerardo 'El Diablo' on 27 July, and the 16-year-old 'El Dilan' on 29 July, at least a dozen killings in all. On 5 August 2026 Milenio reported that the war had left 24 dead and produced 54 arrests from both groups in a month. 'El Chícharo', described as operating under El Irving's orders in the Centro, was captured on 6 August; five inmates including two Unión members were moved to a federal prison in Chiapas on 18 August; El Pistache was reported released on 24 August; UJ-40 members were linked to a double homicide in Iztapalapa on 1 September; a member sought since March, identified in Unión circles as 'El Chilaquil', was shot dead by motorcyclists in Exhipódromo de Peralvillo on 4 September; on 5 September relatives of El Irving, still named as leader of the extortion structure, were detained; and 'El Tripa' was murdered leaving a wake on 7 September. As of September 2026 La Unión Tepito survives as a federation of cells, weakened but still the dominant extortion force in central Mexico City.","leaders":[{"name":"Francisco Javier Hernández Gómez","alias":"Pancho Cayagua","role":"Founding operational leader","from":2009,"to":2017,"fate":"Shot dead in October 2017 in a shopping-centre car park at Indios Verdes, Gustavo A. Madero; his loyalists were purged by December 2017"},{"name":"Roberto Moyado Esparza","alias":"El Betito","role":"Top leader","from":2017,"to":2018,"fate":"Arrested 8 Aug 2018 in Santo Tomás Ajusco, Tlalpan; sentenced Dec 2023 to 43 years 9 months for a 2015 murder in Colonia Morelos"},{"name":"David García Ramírez","alias":"El Pistache","role":"Leader after El Betito's arrest; ran Polanco bar extortion","from":2018,"to":2018,"fate":"Captured 30 Oct 2018 in Santa Fe, Álvaro Obregón; charged 5 Nov 2018 with drug offences and money laundering and transferred to the Cefereso in Ciudad Juárez on 8 Nov 2018; reported released 24 Aug 2026"},{"name":"Óscar Andrés Flores Ramírez","alias":"El Lunares","role":"Top leader","from":2018,"to":2020,"fate":"Arrested 31 Jan 2020 in Pachuca, Hidalgo, by Navy and SSC after offering a 5-million-peso bribe; freed by a federal judge in Feb 2020 and re-arrested at the prison gate; convicted Oct 2021 and sentence of 27 years 6 months ratified 28 June 2022 for the 2019 murder of a woman in Colonia Morelos"},{"name":"Brother of Óscar Andrés Flores Ramírez","alias":"El Junior","role":"Interim leader","from":2020,"to":2020,"fate":"Arrested May 2020"},{"name":"Víctor Hugo Ávila Fuentes","alias":"El Huguito","role":"Leader of the Morelos/Tepito structure; described by SSC in 2020 as top leader","from":2020,"to":0,"fate":"Captured 27 July 2024 in Cancún and moved to Mexico City on 28 July; linked to trial 6 Aug 2024 for the Sept 2018 Plaza Garibaldi attack; hearing postponed 3 Dec 2025; per SSC (2026) still directs the UJ40 cell from prison"},{"name":"Irving Herrera","alias":"El Irving","role":"Second-in-command in 2020; by 2026 named head of the extortion network in Cuauhtémoc, Venustiano Carranza and Iztapalapa (Herrera Segura family)","from":2020,"to":0,"fate":"Arrested 11 Aug 2020 aged 23, investigated for at least eight killings; operators such as 'El Chícharo' (6 Aug 2026) still described as under his orders, and in Sept 2026 SSC arrested two of his relatives"},{"name":"Raúl Rojas Molina","alias":"Mi Jefe","role":"Top leader","from":2020,"to":2022,"fate":"Took over in November 2020; arrested May 2022"},{"name":"Eduardo Ramírez Tiburcio","alias":"El Chori","role":"Leader of the Centro Histórico extortion network","from":2022,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested 18 March 2024 with a 5-million-peso reward outstanding; formal preventive detention 27 March 2024 for a 2015 homicide, amparo against Altiplano transfer nullified 30 July 2024, two further warrants 16 Oct 2024; held in Reclusorio Norte"}],"factions":[{"id":"union_insurgentes","name":"La Unión Insurgentes / Unión Condesa","from":2012,"to":2015,"area":"Zona Rosa, Condesa, Roma (Cuauhtémoc)","leader":"Javier Joel Rodríguez Fuentes 'El Javi'","note":"Cell that carried out the 26 May 2013 Heaven bar kidnapping of 13 Tepito youths; three convicted members reported sentenced to 520 years each in 2015"},{"id":"fuerza_anti_union_split","name":"Fuerza Anti-Unión (breakaway)","from":2017,"to":2017,"area":"Guerrero, Morelos, Peralvillo, Doctores","leader":"Jorge Flores Concha 'El Tortas'","note":"Split led by El Tortas after his dispute with El Betito; became a separate rival organization (see anti_union)"},{"id":"chori_centro","name":"El Chori network (Centro Histórico)","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Centro Histórico, Tepito, Lagunilla","leader":"Eduardo Ramírez Tiburcio 'El Chori' (jailed 2024); principal operator Fernando Sánchez 'Fernandito' arrested Aug 2024 and again 11 Oct 2025","note":"Street-vendor and merchant extortion in the historic centre; relatives and associates arrested through 2025-2026"},{"id":"herrera_segura","name":"Herrera Segura family / El Irving","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Cuauhtémoc, Venustiano Carranza, Iztapalapa","leader":"Irving Herrera 'El Irving'","note":"Family-based extortion and homicide network; several members killed or arrested July-Sept 2026, including two relatives on 5 Sept 2026"},{"id":"uj40","name":"UJ40 youth cell","from":2022,"to":0,"area":"Colonia Morelos (housing unit 40)","leader":"Alexis Alan 'El Alexis' (reported arrested June 2026)","note":"Youth-dominated cell that SSC says takes orders from El Huguito in prison; linked to a double homicide in Iztapalapa on 1 Sept 2026"},{"id":"gregory","name":"Los Gregory","from":2023,"to":0,"area":"Miguel Hidalgo and Coyoacán","leader":"'El Gregory' (arrested 15 June 2026 in Coyoacán as a priority target)","note":"Cell linked to La Unión and to homicides and extortion; members arrested with drugs and ledgers in 2025"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2009,"to":2012,"note":"Formed by defectors of the Beltrán Leyva network under Édgar Valdez 'La Barbie', who convened the May 2010 Tepito meeting"},{"cartel":"familia_michoacana","from":2009,"to":2012,"note":"InSight Crime places former La Familia Michoacana operatives among the founding defectors"},{"cartel":"other","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"Tren de Aragua: January 2026 reports of agreements over sexual exploitation and human trafficking in Mexico City"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"other","from":2010,"to":2013,"note":"Old 'Cártel de Tepito' families ('El Tanque', 'El Duque'); their sons were among the 13 Heaven victims"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2012,"to":0,"note":"Six alleged Sinaloa distributors executed in October 2012 during La Unión's takeover; InSight Crime still lists the Sinaloa Cartel among its rivals"},{"cartel":"anti_union","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"War with El Tortas's breakaway since late 2017: Garibaldi 2018, dismemberments, and the July 2026 motorcycle killings"},{"cartel":"tlahuac","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Competition over retail drug points in Iztapalapa and the eastern boroughs"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"CJNG backs Anti-Unión; a June 2026 video declared war on La Unión and the Cártel de Tláhuac"},{"cartel":"other","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Los Rodolfos (Xochimilco/Tlalpan) and Los Tanzanios compete for southern and eastern territory"}],"activities":["Retail drug sales (cocaine, methamphetamine, marijuana, fentanyl) in bars, clubs and street points","Extortion of street vendors, merchants and nightlife venues (cobro de piso)","'Gota a gota' predatory lending","Contract killings and kidnapping","Human trafficking and sexual exploitation","Contraband and counterfeit goods distribution","Property seizures (despojo)","Money laundering through bars and entertainment businesses"],"peak":{"from":2016,"to":2019,"states":["CMX","MEX","MOR","HID","PUE","QUE","TLA","VER"],"note":"Dominant in Cuauhtémoc, Venustiano Carranza, Gustavo A. Madero, Benito Juárez, Miguel Hidalgo and Iztapalapa, with cells in Estado de México and satellite operations in neighbouring states"},"facts":["On 26 May 2013 the Unión Insurgentes cell abducted 13 young people from the Heaven bar in the Zona Rosa in revenge for the 24 May killing of Horacio Vite Ángel 'El Chaparro'; the bodies were found on 23 August 2013 in Tlalmanalco and three perpetrators were reported sentenced to 520 years each.","On 14 September 2018 gunmen dressed as mariachis killed six people in Plaza Garibaldi; Unión boss 'El Huguito' was linked to trial for the attack on 6 August 2024.","The 22 October 2019 raid on Peralvillo 33 in Tepito yielded 31 arrests, tunnels and an altar with human skulls; a judge released 27 of the detainees within days.","Roughly 550 members were arrested between January 2020 and April 2022, and the UIF froze about 1,500 bank accounts holding roughly US$5.2 million.","Óscar Flores 'El Lunares' was arrested in Pachuca on 31 January 2020 after offering police a 5-million-peso bribe, freed by a federal judge, re-arrested at the prison gate, convicted in October 2021 and confirmed on 28 June 2022 to 27 years 6 months.","'El Betito' received 43 years 9 months in December 2023 for a 2015 murder outside a bar in Colonia Morelos.","Mexico City offered 5 million pesos for 'El Chori' before his arrest on 18 March 2024; he was placed in preventive detention on 27 March 2024 and given two further warrants on 16 October 2024.","On 18 September 2019 six people were shot dead at a food stand in Colonia Doctores in a Unión-linked attack.","Milenio reported on 5 August 2026 that the war between La Unión and the Anti-Unión had left 24 dead and produced 54 arrests in a single month.","La Unión was not included in the February 2025 US foreign-terrorist-organization designations of six Mexican cartels, but Mexico City officials feared a designation in mid-2026."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 La Unión Tepito is a fragmented federation of cells rather than a single command: El Chori and El Huguito are jailed, El Pistache was reported released in August 2026, and SSC names Irving Herrera 'El Irving' and the Herrera Segura family as the core of its extortion network, arresting two of his relatives on 5 September 2026. It is under a CJNG-declared war (June 2026 video) and a killing campaign by the CJNG-backed Anti-Unión that Milenio counted at 24 dead and 54 arrests in the month to 5 August 2026; killings continued into September with 'El Chilaquil' in Peralvillo on 4 September and 'El Tripa' on 7 September. It nonetheless remains the dominant extortion and retail-drug actor in Tepito, Colonia Morelos and the Centro Histórico, and has not been designated by the United States.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/la-union-tepito-profile/","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2026/07/union-tepito-members-hunted-down-across.html","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/tag/el-chori/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/tag/la-union-tepito/","https://www.milenio.com/temas/el-huguito","https://www.milenio.com/temas/la-union-tepito","https://www.milenio.com/temas/el-pistache","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2022/06/28/el-lunares-de-la-union-tepito-se-quedara-con-sentencia-de-27-anos-tras-haber-impugnado/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/cdmx/2023/12/22/el-betito-de-la-union-tepito-es-sentenciado-43-anos-de-prision-por-homicidio-en-la-morelos-320844.html","https://www.cronica.com.mx/notas-bar_heaven_el_plagio_que_dio_pie_a_la_rivalidad_sangrienta_en_tepito-1120465-2019.html","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uni%C3%B3n_Tepito"],"verified":true},{"id":"anti_union","name":"Fuerza Anti-Unión Tepito","aka":["La Anti-Unión","Fuerza Anti-Unión","Anti Unión Tepito","FAU"],"founded":2017,"dissolved":0,"parent":"union_tepito","origin":{"state":"CMX","city":"Colonia Guerrero, Cuauhtémoc, Ciudad de México","lat":19.4447,"lng":-99.1455},"summary":"Fuerza Anti-Unión is the breakaway of La Unión Tepito founded in 2017 by Jorge Flores Concha, 'El Tortas', after his falling-out with 'El Betito'. Based in the colonias Guerrero, Morelos, Peralvillo and Doctores, it fought a gruesome war with La Unión for the capital's extortion and retail-drug markets and, from 2019, became the CJNG's proxy in Mexico City. Its founder has been jailed since May 2019 and the group operates in 2026 as loosely led cells.","history":"Jorge Flores Concha began as a waiter in Zona Rosa bars while committing armed robberies. Jailed in 1995 (four years in Reclusorio Oriente for armed robbery) and again in 2005 in Reclusorio Sur, from where he obtained a conditional release, he built a network with Ricardo Andueza Velázquez 'El Miraviones' and Paulo Sergio Contreras Hernández 'El Avispa' that controlled drug sales inside and outside prison. Around 2010 Édgar Valdez 'La Barbie' recruited him into the new La Unión Tepito confederation. In April 2015 he dragged two men out of the La Soldadera restaurant near the Monumento a la Revolución in broad daylight and tried to buy off police with 100,000 pesos; he was imprisoned in Reclusorio Norte until January 2017. Infobae dates his push to reassert control from behind bars to about 2015, with the open war coming after his release.\n\nBy 2016 he was at war with Roberto Moyado 'El Betito', who was seizing control of La Unión; press accounts say Betito had one of Tortas's brothers killed and another kidnapped. On his release in 2017 Flores Concha founded 'Fuerza Anti-Unión Tepito' from Unión cells that followed him, with strongholds in the colonias Guerrero, Morelos, Peralvillo, Doctores, Buenos Aires and the Zona Rosa. InSight Crime records a rival theory that the group began as a vigilante reaction to Unión extortion, but its methods were identical: cobro de piso, retail drug sales and public killings. From late 2017 the two groups traded dismembered bodies and narco-banners across the Cuauhtémoc borough; the 14 September 2018 Plaza Garibaldi massacre, in which mariachi-dressed Unión gunmen killed six, was the war's bloodiest single episode.\n\nThe Anti-Unión peaked in 2018-2019. It pushed into Álvaro Obregón, Tlalpan, Coyoacán, Iztacalco, Miguel Hidalgo and Magdalena Contreras, contested Plaza Garibaldi and the Central de Abasto, and reached Chalco, Valle de Chalco, Ixtapaluca and Nezahualcóyotl in Estado de México. Its decisive move was an alliance with the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación: federal officials said in May 2019 that CJNG supplied money, weapons, drugs, vehicles and training, with El Tortas reportedly sealing a pact with 'El Mencho' through CJNG envoy Luis Eusebio Duque Reyes, 'El Duque', and planning expansion into six Edomex municipalities. In late May 2019 federal prosecutors, the Army and the Navy captured El Tortas in a raid in Tlalpan with marijuana, methamphetamine, cocaine, ammunition and two firearms of different calibres; most accounts give 28 May 2019, though Milenio's own report on the sentencing dates the raid to 18 May. By 18 June 2020 a federal judge had sentenced him to eight years for drug possession for sale and possession of military-grade firearms and ammunition, sending him and co-defendant Lucino Jesús Celis to the Altiplano maximum-security prison and Mireya Hernández to Santa Martha Acatitla. In October 2019 Mexico City moved 140 high-risk inmates to federal prisons in Chiapas and Oaxaca, a transfer that Unión and Anti-Unión leaders initially fought in court.\n\nLeadership then rotated rapidly. 'El Lucas', tied to sales in Cuauhtémoc and Venustiano Carranza, was arrested in October 2020; Jesús Olguín Rojas, 'El Churpias', described as El Tortas's right hand, fell in February 2022 with fentanyl and marijuana; and on 27 March 2024 the SSC captured six members including 'La Patrona', Flores Concha's wife and second-in-command. By August 2024 InSight Crime noted that authorities considered the group effectively dismantled, while warning that the CJNG connection kept it alive. Cells in Colonia Morelos kept shooting: a member then aged 20, Jesús Daniel 'N' 'El Donas', was linked to four attacks between June 2024 and April 2025 - in June, October and December 2024 and April 2025 - that left one dead and several wounded.\n\nThe Anti-Unión re-emerged in 2025-2026 as the CJNG's spearhead against La Unión. On 11 November 2025 police seized 92 one-kilo packages of marijuana, nearly 100 kg, plus a bag of crystal methamphetamine from Jesús Alberto Sánchez Velázquez and Luis Miguel Sáenz Arias in Colonia Morelos, Venustiano Carranza; in January 2026 two members were charged with a Venustiano Carranza murder; on 4 February 2026 El Tortas's 71-year-old father, Jorge Flores Galicia, was arrested in Colonia Guerrero with 100 doses of cocaine, 50 bags of marijuana and 100 of methamphetamine; and El Donas was caught on 7 March 2026 in Colonia Morelos, Cuauhtémoc. After the CJNG's June 2026 video declaring war on La Unión and the Cártel de Tláhuac, motorcycle gunmen attributed to the Anti-Unión killed at least a dozen Unión members across Tepito, Morelos and the Centro during July. The SSC answered with mass arrests: four members (three of them minors) on 4 July, four more on 11 July, 54 members of both groups in a month reported on 5 August alongside a toll of 24 dead, and 12 people at Mineros and Carpintería streets in Colonia Morelos, Venustiano Carranza, on 31 August 2026, where officers seized a firearm, two bags of marijuana and 146 envelopes of cocaine. As of September 2026 El Tortas remains in the Altiplano with his eight-year term running to about 2027, no successor is publicly identified, and the Anti-Unión functions as a set of youthful, CJNG-supplied cells in Morelos, Guerrero and Doctores.","leaders":[{"name":"Jorge Flores Concha","alias":"El Tortas","role":"Founder and leader","from":2017,"to":2019,"fate":"Arrested in Tlalpan in late May 2019 (28 May per most accounts, 18 May per Milenio) by FGR, Army and Navy; sentenced by 18 June 2020 to 8 years for drug and military-grade weapons offences; held in the Altiplano federal prison (Almoloya de Juárez) as of February 2026"},{"name":"Unknown","alias":"El Lucas","role":"Leader after El Tortas's capture","from":2019,"to":2020,"fate":"Arrested October 2020; linked to drug distribution in Cuauhtémoc and Venustiano Carranza"},{"name":"Jesús Olguín Rojas","alias":"El Churpias","role":"Right-hand man of El Tortas; cell leader","from":2019,"to":2022,"fate":"Arrested February 2022 with fentanyl and marijuana"},{"name":"Wife of Jorge Flores Concha","alias":"La Patrona","role":"Second-in-command; ran operations during El Tortas's imprisonment","from":2019,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested 27 March 2024 by the SSC with five other members"},{"name":"Jesús Daniel 'N'","alias":"El Donas","role":"Cell gunman in Colonia Morelos; SSC priority target","from":2024,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 7 March 2026 aged 20 in Colonia Morelos, Cuauhtémoc, carrying a firearm and marijuana; linked to four shootings (June 2024, October 2024, December 2024 and April 2025) with one death"}],"factions":[{"id":"tortas_core","name":"El Tortas core (Guerrero/Morelos)","from":2017,"to":2019,"area":"Colonias Guerrero, Morelos, Peralvillo, Doctores, Buenos Aires, Zona Rosa","leader":"Jorge Flores Concha 'El Tortas'","note":"Original breakaway structure; CJNG-supplied from 2019"},{"id":"lucas_cell","name":"El Lucas cell","from":2019,"to":2020,"area":"Cuauhtémoc and Venustiano Carranza","leader":"'El Lucas'","note":"Drug distribution; dismantled with his October 2020 arrest"},{"id":"churpias_cell","name":"El Churpias cell","from":2019,"to":2022,"area":"Cuauhtémoc","leader":"Jesús Olguín Rojas 'El Churpias'","note":"Fentanyl and marijuana retail; leader arrested Feb 2022"},{"id":"patrona_network","name":"La Patrona network","from":2019,"to":2024,"area":"Cuauhtémoc","leader":"'La Patrona' (wife of El Tortas)","note":"Kept the organization running while El Tortas was jailed; six arrested 27 March 2024"},{"id":"morelos_cells_2026","name":"Morelos/Guerrero cells","from":2024,"to":0,"area":"Colonias Morelos, Guerrero, Doctores (Cuauhtémoc and Venustiano Carranza)","leader":"Unknown (El Donas cell dismantled March 2026)","note":"Youth-heavy cells behind the July 2026 motorcycle killings of Unión members; 12 arrested at Mineros and Carpintería on 31 Aug 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"CJNG supplies weapons, drugs, money and gunmen; pact reportedly brokered by 'El Duque' in 2019; the Anti-Unión was seen as CJNG's proxy in the July 2026 offensive"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"union_tepito","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Founding rivalry over Cuauhtémoc extortion and drug points; dismemberments 2017-2019, motorcycle killings July 2026"}],"activities":["Retail drug sales (marijuana, cocaine, methamphetamine, fentanyl)","Extortion and cobro de piso of merchants and bars","Kidnapping","Contract killings and public display of bodies","Weapons trafficking","Robbery"],"peak":{"from":2018,"to":2019,"states":["CMX","MEX"],"note":"Contested Cuauhtémoc, Venustiano Carranza and southern boroughs plus Chalco, Valle de Chalco, Ixtapaluca and Nezahualcóyotl with CJNG backing before El Tortas's May 2019 arrest"},"facts":["El Tortas was released from Reclusorio Norte in January 2017 and founded the Anti-Unión within months of leaving prison.","In April 2015 El Tortas dragged two men out of the La Soldadera restaurant near the Monumento a la Revolución and tried to bribe police with 100,000 pesos.","He was captured in a Tlalpan raid in late May 2019 with marijuana, methamphetamine, cocaine, ammunition and two firearms, and sentenced by 18 June 2020 to 8 years, serving them in the Altiplano.","Federal officials said in May 2019 that CJNG provided the group with money, weapons, drugs, vehicles and training for expansion into six Estado de México municipalities.","Six members including 'La Patrona', El Tortas's wife and second-in-command, were arrested on 27 March 2024.","On 11 November 2025 police seized 92 one-kilo packages of marijuana (nearly 100 kg) and a bag of crystal methamphetamine from two members in Colonia Morelos, Venustiano Carranza.","El Tortas's 71-year-old father, Jorge Flores Galicia, was arrested on 4 February 2026 in Colonia Guerrero with 100 doses of cocaine, 50 bags of marijuana and 100 of methamphetamine.","Milenio reported on 5 August 2026 that 54 members of La Unión and the Anti-Unión had been arrested in a month in a war that had left 24 dead; 12 more Anti-Unión suspects were detained in Colonia Morelos on 31 August 2026 with a firearm and 146 envelopes of cocaine.","Three of the four members arrested on 4 July 2026 were minors."],"status_2026":"In September 2026 the Anti-Unión has no publicly identified top leader - El Tortas remains in the Altiplano with an eight-year term running to about 2027 - but its Morelos, Guerrero and Doctores cells are active and, with CJNG backing, were blamed for the July 2026 wave of motorcycle killings of La Unión members after the CJNG's June 2026 declaration of war. The SSC has responded with continuous raids, including 12 arrests at Mineros and Carpintería in Colonia Morelos on 31 August 2026, and describes the violence as a reorganization following the capture of both groups' main leaders.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/fuerza-anti-union/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/fuerza-anti-union-tepito-tortas-sentenciado-8-anos-carcel","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/03/28/esta-es-la-historia-de-el-tortas-de-mesero-de-zona-rosa-a-lider-de-la-anti-union-tepito/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/cdmx/2026/02/04/detienen-a-jorge-flores-papa-de-el-tortas-ex-lider-de-la-anti-union-tepito-durante-operativo-en-cdmx/","https://www.cronica.com.mx/metropoli/2026/08/31/golpe-a-la-anti-union-tepito-detienen-a-12-en-la-morelos/","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/ciudad-de-mexico/cae-donas-fuerza-antiunion-objetivo-prioritario-cdmx","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/11/11/caen-dos-presuntos-narcomenudistas-de-la-anti-union-tepito-con-casi-100-kilos-de-marihuana/","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2026/07/union-tepito-members-hunted-down-across.html","https://www.milenio.com/temas/la-union-tepito"],"verified":true},{"id":"tlahuac","name":"Cártel de Tláhuac","aka":["Cártel de Tláhuac-Chalco","Organización de 'El Ojos'","Los Ojos"],"founded":2012,"dissolved":0,"parent":"beltran_leyva","origin":{"state":"CMX","city":"La Conchita Zapotitlán / Del Mar, Tláhuac, Ciudad de México","lat":19.293,"lng":-99.035},"summary":"The Cártel de Tláhuac was the family-run retail-drug and extortion organization of Felipe de Jesús Pérez Luna, 'El Ojos', which grew out of Beltrán Leyva-linked 'La Mano con Ojos' remnants around 2012 and controlled Tláhuac, parts of Iztapalapa, Xochimilco, Milpa Alta and the Chalco corridor through a network of roughly a thousand mototaxis used as lookouts. Marines killed El Ojos on 20 July 2017, triggering the first narco-blockades in Mexico City's history; his ex-partner, children and nephews ran the remains until a 2024-2025 arrest campaign dismantled the family line.","history":"Felipe de Jesús Pérez Luna, 'El Ojos', was a Tláhuac native who moved from kidnapping crews into retail drug sales under the umbrella of the Beltrán Leyva-aligned 'La Mano con Ojos'. When that group collapsed after 2011, he built his own structure, dated by prosecutors and press to 2012, from the colonias Del Mar, Miguel Hidalgo, La Conchita Zapotitlán, La Nopalera and La Estación in the west of Tláhuac. The organization, sometimes called the Cártel de Tláhuac-Chalco, supplied drug points across Tláhuac, Iztapalapa, Xochimilco, Milpa Alta, parts of Tlalpan and Coyoacán (including Ciudad Universitaria) and the Estado de México municipalities of Chalco, Valle de Chalco and Ixtapaluca.\n\nIts signature was the mototaxi. El Ojos financed vehicles at 20,000 and later 16,000 pesos, charged operators 300 pesos a week recorded in notebooks by name and unit number, took 200 pesos from independents and paid 'halcones' up to 2,000 pesos weekly to report police and rival movements; about 1,000 units were organized into twelve coordinations of roughly 90 vehicles run by his son Miguel and his brother Víctor Manuel, generating an estimated half a million pesos a week, with collections every Friday at his house and at cantinas in Del Mar. The Navy later claimed as many as 10,000 of the roughly 30,000 mototaxis registered in the city worked for the group in some fashion. The money financed sicario cells, extortion, property seizures and predatory lending; authorities later attributed more than 20 executions and the disappearance of some 30 young people to the group. Employment for hundreds of families gave El Ojos a social base that made Tláhuac a no-go zone for police.\n\nAt dawn on 20 July 2017 about 80 Navy (SEMAR) intelligence operatives, working with federal police and other units, hit a house on Calle Simón Álvarez in La Conchita Zapotitlán. The firefight lasted roughly five minutes and left eight dead, including El Ojos, his lawyer Delfino López Álvarez and two gunmen from Chiapas and Tamaulipas. Mototaxi drivers reacted by torching three minibuses and a dump truck and blocking Avenida Tláhuac and nearby roads for some 36 hours, the first documented narcobloqueos in the capital; 23 people were detained (some accounts say 16) and 47 mototaxis impounded. The Mexico City government, which had denied cartels existed in the capital, was forced to acknowledge organized crime in the city.\n\nSuccession stayed in the family. Nephew Ricardo Ferro Pérez, 'El Peque', took the lead until marines killed him on 27 January 2018. Son Miguel Ángel Pérez Ramírez, 'El Micky', detained in Acapulco in June 2017 and sentenced to 27 years for homicide in October 2021, co-directed the group from prison with his ex-partner, María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu, 'La Gela', who was named as the likely successor. Another son, Felipe Pérez Flores, 'El Felipillo' (given by Milenio as Luis Felipe Pérez Flores), ran La Estación, Del Mar and Miguel Hidalgo from a federal prison in Michoacán through his partner Verónica Morales Soto, 'La Jefa'; daughter Diana Karen, 'La Princesa de Tláhuac', and her husband Carlos Mendoza Sandoval, 'El Cindy', operated the 'Jenufa City Mafia 132' cell; daughter Estefanía, 'La China', ran retail sales; younger sons Kevin Pérez Cortés (arrested at 16 in April 2018) and Alexis (arrested at 14 in January 2020) led their own cells. More than 80 members were detained or killed in 2017-2018. In October 2020 the UIF's 'Operación Zócalo' froze 1,452 accounts with about 4.8 billion pesos in movements linked to the capital's cartels, and 'El Chato Venegas' and 13 others were arrested; Estefanía fell in 2021 and Bertha Alicia Flores, El Felipillo's mother, in June 2022. Between 2020 and February 2024 authorities arrested 83 suspected members and dismantled 12 cells.\n\nThe endgame came in 2024-2025. On 18 April 2024 Mexico City put María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu and daughters Liliana 'La Voz' and Samantha 'La Sam' on its most-wanted list with 500,000-peso rewards each; Kevin was recaptured on 17 May 2024. Nephew Luis Fernando Pérez 'El Fercho' was arrested in June 2025; the sicario 'El Miguelón' on 10 September 2025; on the evening of 7 September 2025 Ramírez Arvizu and Samantha were arrested in the San Javier neighbourhood of Pachuca, Hidalgo, for criminal association; brother-in-law Juan Carlos Vázquez Serrano 'El Ramiro' fell with his partner in Colonia Miguel Hidalgo on 18 September 2025; Liliana, who managed accounts for some 50 drug-sales points, was caught in Cuautla, Morelos, on 3 October 2025; and La Jefa was detained in October 2025, prompting El Financiero to declare the 'dynasty of El Ojos' extinguished. Yet cells persisted: 'El Escorpión', Agustín Octavio 'N', was arrested with four associates on 9 January 2026 with hundreds of drug doses and weapons; 'El Elmo', head of a faction called Los Elmos, was detained on 28 January 2026 after firing at police; 'El Perrillas' was arrested for the third time on 9 July 2026 after trying to bribe officers, with reported links to La Familia Michoacana; 'El Minimi' and 'El Pitufo' fell on 31 July 2026; two Los Perrillas members were charged with aggravated extortion on 3 August 2026; and in August 2026 prosecutors listed the Cártel de Tláhuac alongside La Unión Tepito and Los Rodolfos as the groups behind property seizures.\n\nBy 2026 the vacuum was being filled from outside. Milenio reported on 3 May 2026 that CJNG had moved into eastern Mexico City after the Tláhuac cartel's collapse, taking over street dealing and territory, and in June 2026 a CJNG video threatened both La Unión Tepito and the Cártel de Tláhuac; shootings at La Turba and Delfín in Tláhuac on 29 July 2026 were part of that wave. On 7 September 2026 Diana Karen Pérez Ramírez was released and immediately recaptured outside Cefereso 16, the federal women's prison in Morelos. As of September 2026 the Cártel de Tláhuac survives only as remnant cells and imprisoned relatives issuing orders, squeezed between Los Rodolfos, La Unión Tepito and the CJNG.","leaders":[{"name":"Felipe de Jesús Pérez Luna","alias":"El Ojos","role":"Founder and leader","from":2012,"to":2017,"fate":"Killed by Navy marines on 20 July 2017 on Calle Simón Álvarez, La Conchita Zapotitlán, Tláhuac, with seven others including his lawyer Delfino López Álvarez"},{"name":"Ricardo Ferro Pérez","alias":"El Peque / Richie","role":"Nephew; early successor overseeing drug retail, cobro de piso and hits","from":2017,"to":2018,"fate":"Killed in a clash with marines on 27 January 2018"},{"name":"María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu","alias":"La Gela","role":"Ex-partner of El Ojos (widely described as his widow); identified as successor and head of the family structure","from":2017,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested on the evening of 7 September 2025 in San Javier, Pachuca, Hidalgo, for criminal association, after a 500,000-peso reward was posted in April 2024"},{"name":"Miguel Ángel Pérez Ramírez","alias":"El Micky / El Mike / El MK","role":"Son; co-led with his mother from prison; controlled Del Mar, Miguel Hidalgo, Nopalera and Zapotitlán and ran the mototaxi network with his uncle Víctor Manuel","from":2017,"to":2021,"fate":"Detained June 2017 in Acapulco; sentenced October 2021 to 27 years for homicide (linked to seven killings)"},{"name":"Felipe Pérez Flores (given by Milenio as Luis Felipe Pérez Flores)","alias":"El Felipillo / Felipín","role":"Son; faction leader for La Estación, Del Mar and Miguel Hidalgo drug points, directing from prison","from":2017,"to":0,"fate":"Imprisoned in a federal prison in Michoacán for homicide, bribery, drug retail and weapons; his partner 'La Jefa', who relayed his orders, was arrested in October 2025 and he was still described in 2025 as directing Tláhuac drug points from prison"},{"name":"Samantha Pérez Ramírez","alias":"La Sam","role":"Daughter; financial operator and drug distribution","from":2017,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 7 September 2025 in Pachuca, Hidalgo, with her mother"},{"name":"Liliana Pérez Ramírez","alias":"La Voz / La Li","role":"Daughter; drug sales and distribution, managing accounts for some 50 sales points","from":2017,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 3 October 2025 in Cuautla, Morelos"},{"name":"Diana Karen Pérez Ramírez","alias":"La Princesa de Tláhuac / La Negra","role":"Daughter; ran the Miguel Hidalgo colonia and the 'Jenufa City Mafia 132' cell with husband 'El Cindy'","from":2017,"to":2020,"fate":"Detained 2020; released and immediately recaptured outside Cefereso 16, the federal women's prison in Morelos, on 7 September 2026"},{"name":"Verónica Morales Soto","alias":"La Jefa / Verito","role":"Partner of El Felipillo; collected payments and directed extortion, drug sales and property seizures in Tláhuac","from":2022,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested October 2025"}],"factions":[{"id":"felipillo_faction","name":"El Felipillo faction","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"La Estación, Del Mar, La Conchita, Miguel Hidalgo, Zapotitlán (Tláhuac)","leader":"Felipe Pérez Flores 'El Felipillo' (from prison), via 'La Jefa' until Oct 2025","note":"Merchant extortion around the Tláhuac metro terminal and drug points"},{"id":"micky_karen_faction","name":"El Micky / La Princesa faction (Jenufa City Mafia 132)","from":2017,"to":2021,"area":"Miguel Hidalgo, Del Mar, Nopalera, Zapotitlán","leader":"Miguel Ángel Pérez Ramírez and Diana Karen Pérez Ramírez with 'El Cindy'","note":"Homicides and drug distribution; El Micky sentenced 2021, Diana Karen recaptured at the prison gate in Sept 2026"},{"id":"widow_zapotitla","name":"La Gela's network (Zapotitla)","from":2017,"to":2025,"area":"Zapotitla and Zapotitlán, Tláhuac; refuge in Hidalgo and Morelos","leader":"María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu with daughters Samantha and Liliana","note":"Finances and distribution behind commercial fronts; dismantled Sept-Oct 2025"},{"id":"perrillas_cell","name":"Los Perrillas","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Tláhuac","leader":"Carlos Ramón 'N' 'El Perrillas'","note":"Sicario and extortion cell with reported La Familia Michoacana links; leader arrested for the third time on 9 July 2026, two members charged with aggravated extortion on 3 Aug 2026"},{"id":"elmos_cell","name":"Los Elmos","from":2024,"to":0,"area":"Tláhuac","leader":"'El Elmo'","note":"Splinter cell whose leader was detained on 28 January 2026 after firing at police and crashing his vehicle while fleeing"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2012,"to":2017,"note":"Emerged from the Beltrán Leyva-aligned 'La Mano con Ojos' remnants; supply and protection ties toward Morelos"},{"cartel":"other","from":2012,"to":2017,"note":"La Mano con Ojos (Óscar García Montoya 'El Compayito') remnants absorbed into El Ojos's structure"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"union_tepito","from":2016,"to":0,"note":"Competition over retail points in Iztapalapa and the eastern boroughs"},{"cartel":"other","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"Los Rodolfos (Xochimilco, Tlalpan, Milpa Alta) contest the southern drug and despojo market"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"CJNG moved into eastern Mexico City after the cartel's collapse (reported 3 May 2026) and threatened it in a June 2026 video"},{"cartel":"government","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Navy raid of 20 July 2017 and sustained SSC/FGJ campaign that arrested 83 members 2020-Feb 2024 and the family leadership in 2025"}],"activities":["Retail drug sales (marijuana, cocaine, methamphetamine) in Tláhuac, Iztapalapa, Xochimilco, Milpa Alta and Chalco","Control and taxation of mototaxi transport used as a lookout network","Extortion and cobro de piso of merchants","Kidnapping","Property seizures (despojo)","Predatory lending","Vehicle and fuel theft","Contract killings"],"peak":{"from":2014,"to":2017,"states":["CMX","MEX"],"note":"Controlled Tláhuac and parts of Iztapalapa, Xochimilco, Milpa Alta, Tlalpan and Coyoacán plus Chalco, Valle de Chalco and Ixtapaluca through roughly 1,000 mototaxis before El Ojos's death"},"facts":["El Ojos was killed on 20 July 2017 in a roughly five-minute firefight with about 80 Navy operatives on Calle Simón Álvarez, La Conchita Zapotitlán; eight people died, including his lawyer Delfino López Álvarez and two gunmen from Chiapas and Tamaulipas.","The reaction produced Mexico City's first documented narcobloqueos: mototaxi drivers burned three minibuses and a dump truck and blocked Avenida Tláhuac for about 36 hours; 23 people were detained and 47 mototaxis impounded.","The cartel ran about 1,000 mototaxis in twelve coordinations of roughly 90 units, charging 300 pesos a week per vehicle and paying lookouts up to 2,000 pesos a week, an estimated half a million pesos weekly.","Authorities attributed more than 20 executions and the disappearance of about 30 young people to El Ojos's organization.","More than 80 members were detained or killed in 2017-2018, and 83 more were arrested with 12 cells dismantled between 2020 and February 2024.","The UIF's 'Operación Zócalo' (October 2020) froze 1,452 bank accounts with about 4.8 billion pesos in movements linked to Mexico City's cartels.","Son 'El Micky' was sentenced to 27 years in October 2021; sons Kevin and Alexis were first arrested at 16 (April 2018) and 14 (January 2020).","Mexico City offered 500,000 pesos each in April 2024 for María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu and daughters Liliana and Samantha; Ramírez Arvizu and Samantha were arrested in Pachuca on 7 September 2025 and Liliana in Cuautla on 3 October 2025.","On 7 September 2026 daughter Diana Karen 'La Princesa de Tláhuac' was released and recaptured at the gate of Cefereso 16, the federal women's prison in Morelos."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Cártel de Tláhuac is a shell of remnant cells: María de los Ángeles Ramírez Arvizu, daughters Samantha and Liliana, 'La Jefa' and other relatives were arrested between September and October 2025, sons El Micky and El Felipillo remain imprisoned, and 2026 arrests (El Escorpión on 9 January, El Elmo on 28 January, El Perrillas on 9 July, El Minimi and El Pitufo on 31 July) targeted mid-level operators. Milenio reported on 3 May 2026 that the CJNG has taken over eastern Mexico City after the cartel's collapse and a June 2026 CJNG video threatened it directly, while prosecutors still list it with La Unión Tepito and Los Rodolfos as an actor in property seizures. Diana Karen Pérez Ramírez was recaptured on release on 7 September 2026.","sources":["https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2022/07/20/la-caida-de-el-ojos-el-dia-que-la-cdmx-conocio-los-narcobloqueos/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/ojos-armo-red-mil-mototaxis-tlahuac","https://www.milenio.com/policia/familia-el-ojos-perfiles-clave-detras-cartel-de-tlahuac","https://www.milenio.com/policia/la-caida-del-cartel-de-tlahuac-el-linaje-de-el-ojos","https://www.milenio.com/temas/cartel-de-tlahuac","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/cdmx/2025/09/08/se-extingue-la-dinastia-de-el-ojos-lider-del-cartel-de-tlahuac/","https://politica.expansion.mx/cdmx/2024/05/18/cartel-de-tlahuac-el-grupo-criminal-de-la-cdmx-que-inicio-su-declive-en-2017","https://grupoanimal.mx/estados/detienen-viuda-hija-el-ojos-cartel-tlahuac-pachuca-hidalgo","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enfrentamientos_y_narcobloqueos_en_Tl%C3%A1huac_2017"],"verified":true},{"id":"barredora","name":"La Barredora","aka":["La Barredora de Tabasco","La Hermandad (precursor network, 2019-2023)","Cártel Policíaco","The Sweeper"],"founded":2020,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"TAB","city":"Villahermosa","lat":17.9892,"lng":-92.9475},"summary":"La Barredora is a Tabasco criminal network built from inside the state police by security secretary Hernán Bermúdez Requena ('El Abuelo', 'Comandante H') and ex-Zeta commander Ulises Pinto Madera, operating as the CJNG's franchise for extortion, fuel theft, migrant smuggling and retail drugs in Villahermosa, the Chontalpa and northern Chiapas. A December 2023 split between its two founders unleashed record violence in Tabasco, and Bermúdez's flight, arrest in Paraguay in September 2025 and prosecution have become the biggest narco-political scandal of the Sheinbaum era, engulfing former governor and senator Adán Augusto López.","history":"Hernán Bermúdez Requena, born in Mérida on 30 September 1953 and a UNAM-trained lawyer, spent three decades in Tabasco's security apparatus (director of public security in 1992, prison director 1995-97, under-secretary of prevention 2001-05, investigation police director in 2019) and was briefly detained by the federal SIEDO in 2006 over the kidnapping and murder of rancher Ponciano Vázquez Lagunes. On 11 December 2019 Governor Adán Augusto López Hernández made him Secretary of Security and Citizen Protection, a post he held until 5 January 2024 under two governors.\n\nAccording to Tabasco prosecutors, FGR witnesses and press reconstructions, Bermúdez began assembling the network almost as soon as he took office, with the structure consolidating through 2020 alongside Ulises Pinto Madera ('El Pinto', 'El Mamado'), a former Federal Judicial Police officer identified as an ex-Zeta from about 2009 who had run drug logistics on the Chiapas-Guatemala border. The police-embedded group was first known as 'La Hermandad' or the 'cártel policíaco'. Its declared pretext was to wipe out fuel-theft boss Andrés Rodríguez 'El Kalimba'; in practice the state Special Forces under Pinto took over extortion, huachicol, kidnapping, migrant smuggling and retail drug sales in Villahermosa and the Chontalpa, 'sweeping' rivals away, hence the name. A feud with Gulf Cartel remnants pushed the network into alliance with the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación, and Sedena intelligence files from 2019-2021, exposed by the Guacamaya hack in October 2022, already described 'Comandante H' as the CJNG's protector in Tabasco.\n\nThe group consolidated between 2020 and 2022 under Bermúdez's protection. Carlos Tomás Díaz Rodríguez, 'Licenciado Tomasín', 'El Lic' or 'El 12', an adviser at the secretariat, handled strategic communications and finance; regional chiefs such as Daniel Hernández Montejo 'Prada' and Euler Ruvalcaba Colorado 'Comandante Rayo' ran plazas, with Leonardo Arturo Leyva Ávalos 'El Carnal' reported as the likely CJNG contact; cells operated openly in Reforma, Pichucalco and Juárez in northern Chiapas and in parts of Veracruz. Rayo escaped from the Las Palmas centre in Cárdenas, Tabasco, on 12 March 2023 with three other inmates in a stolen patrol vehicle. When President López Obrador and Adán Augusto, by then Interior Secretary, were confronted with the leaked files in October 2022, both dismissed the reports, and Bermúdez stayed in office under interim governor Carlos Manuel Merino.\n\nThe network broke apart on 22 December 2023, when Bermúdez and Pinto fell out over the division of proceeds. The result was the 'Villahermosazo': a shoot-out in the Club Campestre residential zone, burning vehicles and Oxxo stores across the capital and simultaneous prison riots. Bermúdez resigned on 5 January 2024; Pinto moved to the Guadalajara area under CJNG protection while Bermúdez's loyalists kept the La Barredora name, and the state government has since described the violence as a turf war between 'CJNG' and 'La Barredora'. Tabasco murders rose sharply through 2024; on 24 November 2024 gunmen killed six people and wounded ten at the DBar nightclub in Villahermosa, and INEGI's ENSU for the fourth quarter of 2024 made Villahermosa the city with the highest perception of insecurity in Mexico, above Culiacán and Uruapan.\n\nGovernor Javier May, in office from 1 October 2024, and the Sheinbaum government moved against the group in 2025. Tomasín was arrested on 19 January 2025 near the former San Lorenzo hacienda in San Martín Texmelucan, Puebla, bound over on 5 February for money laundering and tax fraud and moved to a maximum-security prison in Nayarit on 26 March. On 26 January Bermúdez flew from Mérida to Panama and went on to Spain, Brazil and, from March, Paraguay. Tabasco issued a warrant on 14 February and Interpol a red notice on 17 July. The Navy captured Pinto in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga, Jalisco, on 23 July 2025, and on 27 August the FGR made him a collaborating witness. Federal forces took third-in-command Arturo Gómez Marín 'El Vampiro' in Centro on 6 August. On 12 September 2025 Paraguayan prosecutors and SENAD arrested Bermúdez in Mariano Roque Alonso; he was expelled to Mexico on 17 September, flown to Toluca and lodged in the Altiplano prison, where a Villahermosa judge bound him over on 23 September for aggravated kidnapping, extortion and criminal association. Morena expelled him on 10 October 2025. Tabasco homicides fell sharply over the second half of 2025, though the year still averaged 2.08 killings a day, the state's highest on record.\n\nThe political fallout centred on Adán Augusto López, who insists he knew nothing. He resigned as Morena's Senate coordinator on 1 February 2026 and was replaced by Ignacio Mier. Bermúdez was processed for forced disappearance on 4 April 2026 and embezzlement on 13 April; he sought an amparo in March against extradition to the United States, and on 22 May a tribunal admitted his complaint against the ruling that threw it out. His intermediate hearing, set for 1 June, was postponed to 15 June, when Tabasco prosecutors asked for 154 years in prison. On 14 April 2026 the US Treasury sanctioned Casino Centenario (Nuevo Laredo), Diamante Casino (Tampico) and their operator CAMSA as Cártel del Noreste laundering fronts, with Mexican outlets reporting that a company run by Bermúdez's brother worked under the same gaming permit; on 17 August the FGR moved to seize his assets and accounts. In mid-August he circulated a letter from the Altiplano calling himself an innocent victim of political and media persecution.\n\nBy September 2026 La Barredora survives as a fragmented brand. Its main structure is attributed to Yeison Daniel Hernández Montejo 'Prada', while Pinto's old operators regrouped as 'Pueblo Unido' under Jorge Luis 'N', 'El Koki', arrested with two operatives on 26 August 2026 on the México-Pachuca highway in Hidalgo. Dismembered bodies appeared in Centro and neighbouring municipalities in July 2026 and seven people were killed in two days in early September. First-half 2026 homicides fell against 2025 but remain far above pre-2024 levels; Bermúdez awaits an oral trial, and Adán Augusto, sidelined from the Senate leadership, was named Morena's coordinator for the fourth electoral circumscription, based in Hidalgo, in August 2026.","leaders":[{"name":"Hernán Bermúdez Requena","alias":"El Abuelo / Comandante H","role":"Founder and top leader; Tabasco Secretary of Security and Citizen Protection (11 Dec 2019 - 5 Jan 2024)","from":2020,"to":2025,"fate":"Fled Mexico 26 Jan 2025 (Panama, Spain, Brazil, Paraguay); Tabasco warrant 14 Feb 2025; Interpol red notice 17 Jul 2025; arrested 12 Sept 2025 in Mariano Roque Alonso, Paraguay; expelled to Mexico 17 Sept 2025; held in Altiplano; bound over 23 Sept 2025 for aggravated kidnapping, extortion and criminal association, plus forced disappearance (4 Apr 2026) and embezzlement (13 Apr 2026); sought an amparo against US extradition (Mar 2026); intermediate hearing postponed from 1 Jun to 15 Jun 2026, when prosecutors sought 154 years; FGR asset-forfeiture action 17 Aug 2026; awaiting oral trial Sept 2026"},{"name":"Ulises Pinto Madera","alias":"El Pinto / El Mamado","role":"Co-founder and second-in-command; ex-Federal Judicial Police and ex-Zeta; commander of Tabasco police Special Forces; led the CJNG-backed wing after the Dec 2023 split","from":2020,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested by the Navy 23 Jul 2025 in Tlajomulco de Zúñiga, Jalisco; won an amparo against torture and incommunicado detention 25 Jul 2025; designated a collaborating witness (testigo colaborador) by the FGR on 27 Aug 2025; in federal custody"},{"name":"Carlos Tomás Díaz Rodríguez","alias":"Licenciado Tomasín / El Lic / El 12","role":"Adviser to the security secretariat handling strategic communications and finances","from":2020,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 19 Jan 2025 near the former San Lorenzo hacienda, San Juan Tuxco, San Martín Texmelucan, Puebla, after 15 days of surveillance; bound over 5 Feb 2025 for operations with illicit resources and tax fraud; transferred to a maximum-security prison in Nayarit on 26 Mar 2025"},{"name":"Arturo Gómez Marín","alias":"El Vampiro","role":"Third-in-command; coordinated extortion cells and drug routes in Tabasco","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 6 Aug 2025 in Centro, Tabasco, by a joint federal-state operation announced by security minister Omar García Harfuch; accused of extortion and homicide"},{"name":"Yeison Daniel Hernández Montejo","alias":"Prada / El H","role":"Regional boss (replaced 'El Pantera' after 2021); reported head of La Barredora's main structure after the 2025 arrests","from":2025,"to":0,"fate":"At large with an outstanding Tabasco arrest warrant (Sept 2026); also reported in earlier coverage as Daniel Hernández Montejo"},{"name":"Jorge Luis N","alias":"El Koki","role":"Operator under Pinto; leader of the Pueblo Unido splinter formed after Pinto's July 2025 capture","from":2025,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 26 Aug 2026 with two operatives on the México-Pachuca highway, Hidalgo, by a CNI-Sedena-Semar-National Guard operation on two Tabasco warrants for criminal association and burglary"},{"name":"Euler Ruvalcaba Colorado","alias":"Comandante Rayo","role":"Plaza chief and operational commander for CJNG-affiliated zones","from":2021,"to":0,"fate":"Escaped the Las Palmas centre in Cárdenas, Tabasco, on 12 Mar 2023 with three other inmates in a stolen patrol vehicle; fugitive, with a 250,000-peso reward posted by the Tabasco prosecutor's office in December 2025; surname also reported as Rubalcaba"},{"name":"Francisco Javier Custodio Luna","alias":"El Guasón","role":"Former state police commander; intelligence and protection for La Barredora, lieutenant to Prada","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"Arrested 27 Mar 2025 in Tabasco; accused of extortion of traders and drug trafficking, and linked to femicide cases"}],"factions":[{"id":"la_hermandad","name":"La Hermandad / Cártel Policíaco","from":2020,"to":2023,"area":"Villahermosa and the Chontalpa (Tabasco)","leader":"Hernán Bermúdez Requena and Ulises Pinto Madera","note":"Original police-embedded network run from the state security secretariat, assembled from late 2019; renamed La Barredora as it 'swept' rivals from the plaza"},{"id":"barredora_prada","name":"La Barredora (Bermúdez loyalists)","from":2023,"to":0,"area":"Centro (Villahermosa), Nacajuca, Cárdenas; northern Chiapas (Reforma, Pichucalco, Juárez)","leader":"Hernán Bermúdez Requena, then Yeison Daniel Hernández Montejo 'Prada'","note":"Kept the brand after the 22 Dec 2023 rupture; the Tabasco government frames the 2024-2026 violence as CJNG vs La Barredora"},{"id":"pinto_cjng_wing","name":"Pinto wing (CJNG franchise)","from":2023,"to":2025,"area":"Villahermosa; commanded from the Guadalajara area","leader":"Ulises Pinto Madera 'El Pinto'","note":"Operated under CJNG protection after the split until Pinto's capture on 23 Jul 2025 and his turn as a collaborating witness in August 2025"},{"id":"pueblo_unido","name":"Pueblo Unido","from":2025,"to":0,"area":"Centro and Nacajuca, Tabasco","leader":"Jorge Luis 'N', 'El Koki' (arrested 26 Aug 2026)","note":"Splinter formed by Pinto's operators after his July 2025 capture; disputes territory with Prada's structure, both reportedly courting CJNG backing"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2020,"to":2023,"note":"La Barredora served as the CJNG's operational arm in Tabasco; the alliance was sought to counter Gulf Cartel remnants. After the Dec 2023 split the CJNG label stayed with Pinto's wing"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"gulf","from":2020,"to":2022,"note":"Gulf Cartel remnants in Tabasco were the original adversary whose pressure drove La Hermandad into the CJNG orbit"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2023,"to":0,"note":"After the 22 Dec 2023 'Villahermosazo' the Tabasco government describes the violence as a CJNG vs La Barredora turf war; DBar massacre (24 Nov 2024), the July 2026 dismemberments and 2025-26 killings attributed to it. The CJNG publicly threatened 'El 12' in October 2024"}],"activities":["Extortion (cobro de piso) of businesses, bars and nightclubs","Fuel theft (huachicol) from Pemex pipelines and fuel smuggling","Retail drug sales (narcomenudeo)","Migrant smuggling and human trafficking","Kidnapping, including express kidnapping","Contract killings and vehicle arson","Money laundering through companies and casinos","Sale of police protection to criminal cells; state police reportedly paid 5,000 pesos a week to belong","Control of drug distribution inside Tabasco prisons"],"peak":{"from":2021,"to":2024,"states":["TAB","CHP","VER"],"note":"Controlled Tabasco's criminal economy from inside the state security secretariat, with cells operating freely in northern Chiapas and parts of Veracruz until the Dec 2023 rupture and 2025 arrests; from 2025 Mexican press also reported Barredora-linked cells and killings in Puebla and Tlaxcala"},"facts":["Bermúdez ran Tabasco's police for 1,486 days (11 Dec 2019 to 5 Jan 2024) under two governors while allegedly leading the group.","Tabasco homicides jumped from 0.64 a day in 2023, the decade's low, to 2.08 a day in 2025, the highest on record, with the first half of 2025 averaging 2.34 a day.","The DBar nightclub attack in Villahermosa on 24 Nov 2024 killed 6 and wounded 10; a third suspect, 'El Montana', was caught on 1 Dec 2025 and another was bound over in May 2026.","INEGI's ENSU for the fourth quarter of 2024 made Villahermosa the Mexican city with the highest perception of insecurity, ahead of Culiacán and Uruapan.","Bermúdez's flight lasted 229 days: he left Mérida on 26 Jan 2025 and was arrested in Mariano Roque Alonso, Paraguay, on 12 Sept 2025, under an Interpol red notice issued 17 Jul 2025.","Tabasco prosecutors asked on 15 Jun 2026 for 154 years in prison, at a hearing postponed from 1 June.","Adán Augusto López resigned as Morena's Senate coordinator on 1 Feb 2026, replaced by Ignacio Mier, five months after his former security chief was jailed.","On 14 Apr 2026 OFAC designated 6 targets as Cártel del Noreste laundering fronts: two casinos (Casino Centenario in Nuevo Laredo and Diamante Casino in Tampico), their operator CAMSA, and three individuals.","Ulises Pinto, the group's co-founder, became an FGR collaborating witness on 27 Aug 2025, one month after his capture.","Bermúdez was the only one of 17 members Morena expelled in the year to January 2026 to be removed over alleged criminal ties."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 La Barredora is degraded and split: founder Hernán Bermúdez Requena is in the Altiplano awaiting an oral trial on state charges (154 years sought) and federal organized-crime charges, while fighting extradition to the United States and an FGR asset seizure; Ulises Pinto is a collaborating witness, 'El Vampiro' is jailed, and 'El Koki', head of the Pueblo Unido splinter, was captured on 26 August 2026, leaving 'Prada' and 'Comandante Rayo' as the main fugitives. Violence in Tabasco fell in the first half of 2026 versus 2025 but a spate of dismemberments in July and seven killings in two days in early September show the CJNG-La Barredora dispute is unresolved, while the case continues to shadow Senator Adán Augusto López, now Morena's electoral coordinator for the fourth circumscription in Hidalgo.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hern%C3%A1n_Berm%C3%BAdez_Requena","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/07/24/quien-es-quien-en-la-barredora-lo-que-se-sabe-hasta-ahora-del-organigrama-del-grupo-criminal-que-se-apodero-de-tabasco/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/barredora-quienes-son-lideres-grupo-criminal-tabasco","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/01/21/quien-es-el-tomasin-presunto-lider-de-la-barredora-detenido-en-puebla/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2025/12/04/van-por-el-rayo-fiscalia-de-tabasco-ofrece-250-mil-pesos-por-lider-de-la-barredora-que-escapo-de-prision/","https://elpais.com/mexico/2025-10-10/morena-expulsa-a-hernan-bermudez-el-exjefe-policial-nombrado-por-adan-augusto-lopez-que-lideraba-un-grupo-criminal.html","https://serendipia.digital/seguridad/homicidios-en-tabasco/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/26/cae-el-koki-objetivo-prioritario-y-exoperador-de-la-barredora-en-tabasco/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0440","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2025/08/27/politica/acepta-el-mamado-segundo-al-mando-de-la-barredora-ser-testigo-colaborador-de-fgr"],"verified":true},{"id":"chiapas_guatemala","name":"Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala","aka":["CCyG","CCYG","Cártel Chiapas-Guatemala","Chiapas-Guatemala Cartel","Los Maíz / Mano Izquierda (armed wing)"],"founded":2022,"dissolved":0,"parent":"sinaloa","origin":{"state":"CHP","city":"Frontera Comalapa","lat":15.6625,"lng":-92.1414},"summary":"The Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala is a CJNG-aligned coalition formed around 2022 in the Sierra Madre and border zone of Chiapas by Sinaloa defector Juan Manuel Valdovinos Mendoza ('El Señor de los Caballos') and the Calderón Calderón cocaine clan of Vueltamina, Huehuetenango. Its war with the Sinaloa Cartel for the La Mesilla-Frontera Comalapa corridor has produced massacres, roadblocks and the forced displacement of more than 15,000 people in Frontera Comalapa, Chicomuselo, Motozintla and Amatenango de la Frontera, spilling into Guatemala.","history":"For most of the 2000s and 2010s the Chiapas-Guatemala border around Frontera Comalapa and La Mesilla was a Sinaloa Cartel corridor for Colombian cocaine, weapons and migrants. Los Zetas contested it in 2008-2010 (17 dead in a 30 December 2008 clash with Guatemala's Los Huistas at Agua Zarca) but Sinaloa prevailed, running Chiapas through Gilberto Rivera Amarillas, arrested at La Aurora airport in Guatemala City in 2016, and then his son Ramón Gilberto Rivera Estrada, 'El Junior'. The CJNG's presence in Guatemala was first documented in 2019 and grew after 2021. On 7 July 2021 CJNG-allied gunmen killed El Junior in Tuxtla Gutiérrez. When Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada installed Jesús Esteban Machado Meza 'El Güero Pulseras' as regional chief instead of him, the Sinaloa logistics man Juan Manuel Valdovinos Mendoza, 'El Señor de los Caballos', defected to the CJNG, opening the war.\n\nAround 2022 Valdovinos's betrayal, an existing CJNG cell and a small group from Vueltamina, a hamlet of La Mesilla in Huehuetenango, combined to form the Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala (CCyG). Its founder was the Vueltamina patriarch Baldemar Calderón Carrillo, 'Don Balde' or 'Tío Balde', who had moved from farm labour into cocaine transport and had been targeted by the DEA's Operation Guerrilla Unity since 2017. The CCyG became the Jalisco cartel's logistics arm on the southern border; it first appeared publicly in April 2024 in videos calling on border residents to join it against Sinaloa. Its armed wing, Los Maíz ('Mano Izquierda'), under Bladimir López Orantes and Antonio Martínez, held El Jocote, El Sabinalito, 20 de Mayo and Santa Teresa Llano Grande, from which the CJNG and CCyG extended control over the Frontera Comalapa seat and hundreds of Sierra communities from about 2020. At the clandestine Santa Teresa Llano Grande-Guailá crossing 1,000-1,500 vehicles a day paid tolls of 50-500 pesos on basic goods and used clothing and more than 20,000 pesos on trucks of new motorcycles or auto parts, while large businesses paid 20,000 pesos and small ones 1,000 pesos in derecho de piso. More than 20 narcocorridos since 2022 chronicle the clan, its sons Walfre Donaldo 'El Teniente Jr' and Amado Baldemar, and its CJNG ties.\n\nThe war became visible in May 2023, when at least 60 people were killed in two weeks around Frontera Comalapa and more than 3,000 fled before 1,500 soldiers and police deployed on 29 May. On 23-25 September 2023 videos showed villagers applauding a Sinaloa convoy that had cleared CJNG-side roadblocks. In January 2024 Los Maíz took control of several communities and on 16-17 January thousands from Chicomuselo, Frontera Comalapa and Socoltenango walked out to the La Angostura dam. On 12 May 2024 eleven members of one family of Catholic catechists, aged 15 to 73, who had refused to work for the gunmen were massacred in Nuevo Morelia, Chicomuselo, an attack attributed to the CJNG; on 1 July 19 bodies were found in a dump truck near La Concordia; on 23-24 July about 600 Mexicans fled to Cuilco, Guatemala, and on 10 August 248 more crossed to La Democracia. Frontera Comalapa's PVEM mayor-elect Aníbal Roblero Castillo was seized in Tuxtla Gutiérrez on 3 September 2024 and remains disappeared; the man who took the office, José Antonio Villatoro Herrera, was arrested on 5 January 2025 on homicide and forced-disappearance charges tied to the CJNG, and a municipal council under Marli Trejo Posada replaced him on 13 January.\n\nGovernor Eduardo Ramírez Aguilar took office on 8 December 2024 and launched an operation that day with a new elite unit, the Fuerza de Reacción Inmediata Pakal; the cartel's local chiefs fled. On 8 June 2025 Tío Balde and three others were killed in a Pakal operation that began in Frontera Comalapa and crossed into Guatemalan territory near La Mesilla; Mexico's foreign ministry apologised to Guatemala on 10 June and Chiapas opened an internal investigation, while the family claimed the body on 28 June. Those killed were also blamed for the 2 June 2025 ambush that killed five Chiapas state police near Las Champas. In August 2025 fifty-nine Cintalapa municipal police were detained over criminal ties; seven Frontera Comalapa municipal police were arrested on 2 July 2025; and Roger Irabier Roblero López 'El Peque', 39, a reported Los Maíz chief wanted for a January 2021 homicide and carrying an Interpol red notice, was detained in La Democracia, Huehuetenango, on 30 September. A federal-state mega-operation on 13-14 October 2025 to capture Valdovinos failed but arrested three men and triggered narco-blockades and vehicle burnings in Arriaga, Jiquipilas, Ocozocoautla and Villaflores. On 8 December 2025 Sinaloa gunmen using drones, rifles and explosives attacked CCyG positions in six Guatemalan municipalities, among them La Democracia, Nentón, Cuilco and Santa Ana Huista in Huehuetenango and Tacaná in San Marcos, leaving at least eight dead by 11 December and prompting President Bernardo Arévalo to reinforce the border.\n\nIn 2026 the state has kept up arrests: plaza chief Hugo 'N', 'El Espíritu', a national-priority target who controlled Jiquipilas, Cintalapa, Berriozábal and Villaflores, was captured in Ocozocoautla on 4 February; two foreign members were caught with drugs and guns on 31 March; four were arrested selling methamphetamine in Jiquipilas in March and two more there in April; six were seized in Jiquipilas on 5 July with 13 AK-47s, a Browning .308 belt-fed machine gun and 95 bags of crystal; and a clash with security forces in Cintalapa on 27 August left one gunman dead and one detained. The group circulated a video of the beheading of a La Mayiza member and still disputes Villaflores and the Fraylesca with Sinaloa. As of September 2026 Valdovinos and Walfre Calderón remain at large, extortion continues on the border, reporting on a CJNG payroll ledger has placed the Pakal force itself under suspicion, and Chiapas journalists describe the cartel as weakened but not extinct.","leaders":[{"name":"Juan Manuel Valdovinos Mendoza","alias":"El Señor de los Caballos","role":"Top leader; former Sinaloa operator in Chiapas who defected to the CJNG in 2021 after being passed over, then became its regional logistics chief; stronghold in Cintalapa, Jiquipilas and Ocozocoautla","from":2021,"to":0,"fate":"At large; a joint federal-state mega-operation on 13-14 Oct 2025 failed to capture him and triggered narco-blockades across central Chiapas"},{"name":"Baldemar Calderón Carrillo","alias":"Don Balde / Tío Balde","role":"Founder; patriarch of the Vueltamina (La Mesilla, Huehuetenango) cocaine clan; ran CCyG logistics and recruitment inside Guatemala","from":2022,"to":2025,"fate":"Killed on 8 Jun 2025, with three others, in a Fuerza Pakal operation that began in Frontera Comalapa and crossed into Guatemalan territory near La Mesilla; body claimed by family in Guatemala on 28 Jun 2025"},{"name":"Walfre Donaldo Calderón Calderón","alias":"El Teniente Jr","role":"Son of Don Balde; reported successor at the head of the Calderón wing and of the group","from":2025,"to":0,"fate":"At large (Sept 2026); subject of the best-known CCyG narcocorrido"},{"name":"Edgar Yovani Calderón Calderón","alias":"Panon","role":"Calderón clan cocaine trafficker","from":2022,"to":2024,"fate":"Arrested in France in Feb 2024, extradited to the US and sentenced in May 2025 to 87 months (Southern District of California, Operation Guerrilla Unity)"},{"name":"Bladimir López Orantes","alias":"","role":"Regional commander of Los Maíz in El Sabinalito and Santa Teresa Llano Grande","from":2023,"to":0,"fate":"Fled after the 8 Dec 2024 state operation; at large"},{"name":"Roger Irabier Roblero López","alias":"El Peque","role":"Border operator, reported leader of the Los Maíz faction","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"Detained 30 Sept 2025 in La Democracia, Huehuetenango, Guatemala; 39 years old, held on an Interpol red notice and a January 2021 homicide charge"},{"name":"Hugo N","alias":"El Espíritu","role":"Plaza chief and reported lieutenant controlling Jiquipilas, Cintalapa, Berriozábal and Villaflores","from":2024,"to":2026,"fate":"Captured 4 Feb 2026 in Ocozocoautla by state police, the FGE, Army and National Guard; had already been prosecuted in 2021"}],"factions":[{"id":"calderon_clan","name":"Calderón Calderón clan (Vueltamina)","from":2022,"to":0,"area":"La Mesilla-Frontera Comalapa corridor; Huehuetenango and San Marcos, Guatemala","leader":"Baldemar Calderón Carrillo (killed 8 Jun 2025), then Walfre Donaldo Calderón Calderón","note":"Guatemalan cocaine-transport family that founded the group and supplies the CJNG; source of the binational name"},{"id":"valdovinos_network","name":"Valdovinos / Valles Zoque network","from":2021,"to":0,"area":"Cintalapa, Jiquipilas, Ocozocoautla, Villaflores, Arriaga","leader":"Juan Manuel Valdovinos Mendoza","note":"CJNG logistics hub for drugs and weapons moving north from the border; hit by the Feb 2026 capture of 'El Espíritu' and repeated Jiquipilas raids"},{"id":"los_maiz","name":"Los Maíz (Mano Izquierda)","from":2023,"to":0,"area":"Frontera Comalapa communities (El Jocote, El Sabinalito, 20 de Mayo, Santa Teresa Llano Grande), Chicomuselo, Motozintla","leader":"Bladimir López Orantes and Antonio Martínez (reported); Roger Roblero López 'El Peque' also named as a chief","note":"Armed wing that consolidated control of border communities in January 2024 and runs extortion at the Santa Teresa-Guailá crossing"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"CCyG is described as the CJNG's logistical and operational arm on the Guatemala border; Valdovinos is a direct CJNG operator and the group is routinely labelled 'Cártel Chiapas y Guatemala-CJNG'"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"War began with the Jul 2021 killing of Sinaloa boss 'El Junior'; Sinaloa's regional chief 'El Güero Pulseras' contests Frontera Comalapa, Chicomuselo and Villaflores; the Sinaloa incursion into six Guatemalan border municipalities on 8 Dec 2025 left at least 8 dead"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2025,"to":0,"note":"CCyG released a video in 2026 of the beheading of a La Mayiza member; the Zambada faction leads Sinaloa's southern front"},{"cartel":"other","from":2022,"to":0,"note":"Los Huistas (Huehuetenango), historically Sinaloa-linked, are listed as CCyG rivals in Guatemalan reporting, although InSight Crime documented the CJNG forming an alliance with the Huistas around 2022-2023 and Guatemalan outlets have also reported possible CCyG-Huista ties"}],"activities":["Cocaine transshipment from Guatemala to Mexico","Methamphetamine distribution","Migrant smuggling and fees on coyotes","Arms trafficking","Extortion of cross-border trade at Santa Teresa Llano Grande-Guailá and of businesses (derecho de piso)","Forced recruitment and forced labour at roadblocks","Control or infiltration of municipal police and electoral coercion","Pressure on communities over illegal mining"],"peak":{"from":2023,"to":2025,"states":["CHP"],"note":"Held the Frontera Comalapa seat and hundreds of Sierra Mariscal, Soconusco and Valles Zoque communities, with satellite presence in Huehuetenango and San Marcos, before the Pakal offensive and the 2025-26 arrests"},"facts":["More than 15,000 people were displaced and over 600 disappeared in Chiapas by the Sinaloa-CJNG war, largely in Frontera Comalapa and Chicomuselo, according to human-rights reporting published in March 2025.","At least 60 people were killed in two weeks in Frontera Comalapa in May 2023 before 1,500 troops deployed on 29 May 2023.","On 12 May 2024 eleven members of one family of Catholic catechists, aged 15 to 73, were massacred in Nuevo Morelia, Chicomuselo.","On 1 July 2024 authorities found 19 bodies in a dump truck near La Concordia, blamed on the Sinaloa-CCyG war.","Some 600 Mexicans fled to Cuilco, Guatemala, on 23-24 July 2024 and 248 more to La Democracia on 10 August 2024.","The Santa Teresa Llano Grande-Guailá crossing moved 1,000-1,500 vehicles a day, taxed 50-500 pesos on basic loads and over 20,000 pesos on trucks of new motorcycles or auto parts, while large businesses paid 20,000 pesos in derecho de piso and small ones 1,000.","On 8 December 2025 a Sinaloa incursion into six Guatemalan border municipalities against CCyG positions left at least 8 dead, using drones, rifles and explosives.","The 5 July 2026 Jiquipilas raid seized 13 AK-47 rifles in 7.62x39, a Browning .308 belt-fed machine gun with a 100-round belt and 95 bags of methamphetamine from six suspects, five men and one woman.","Founder 'Tío Balde' was killed on 8 June 2025 in a cross-border raid that forced a Mexican diplomatic apology to Guatemala, and his relative 'Panon' was sentenced in the US in May 2025 to 87 months.","Fifty-nine Cintalapa municipal police were detained in August 2025 and seven Frontera Comalapa officers on 2 July 2025 over alleged ties to the group."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 the Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala is weakened but active: founder Tío Balde is dead, 'El Peque' and plaza chief 'El Espíritu' are jailed, and state-federal raids in Jiquipilas (5 July) and Cintalapa (27 August) keep dismantling cells, yet Juan Manuel Valdovinos and Walfre Calderón remain free. The group still fights the Sinaloa Cartel and La Mayiza for Frontera Comalapa, Villaflores and the Huehuetenango corridor, extortion at the border crossings continues, and reporting on a CJNG payroll ledger that appears to include the Fuerza Pakal has raised doubts about the Chiapas offensive itself.","sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_Chiapas_y_Guatemala","https://www.latintimes.com/mexican-forces-kill-alleged-chiapas-guatemala-cartel-leader-don-balde-cross-border-operation-584822","https://www.plazapublica.com.gt/justicia/informacion/los-narco-corridos-que-retratan-al-cartel-chiapas-y-guatemala","https://www.prensalibre.com/guatemala/comunitario/10-datos-para-entender-el-conflicto-entre-el-cartel-de-sinaloa-y-el-cartel-de-chiapas-en-guatemala/","https://lahora.gt/nacionales/rrsagastume/2025/12/16/drogas-armas-y-migrantes-el-origen-del-cartel-de-chiapas-guatemala-y-sus-vinculos-con-otras-estructuras/","https://www.informador.mx/mexico/el-mencho-asi-opera-el-cartel-en-la-frontera-chiapas---guatemala-20260318-0094.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/05/capturan-a-seis-presuntos-miembros-del-cartel-de-chiapas-y-guatemala-con-armamento-de-uso-del-ejercito-mexicano/","https://insightcrime.org/news/chiapas-bleeds-cjng-sinaloa-cartel-guatemala-trafficking-routes/","https://elpais.com/mexico/2025-01-05/detenido-el-alcalde-de-frontera-comalapa-en-chiapas-por-presuntos-vinculos-con-el-crimen-organizado.html"],"verified":true},{"id":"mezcales","name":"Los Mezcales","aka":["Cártel Independiente de Colima","CIC","Los Mezcales de Colima"],"founded":2022,"dissolved":0,"parent":"cjng","origin":{"state":"COL","city":"Colima","lat":19.2433,"lng":-103.725},"summary":"Los Mezcales, named for the El Mezcalito barrio of Colima city, were the CJNG's local armed wing until a deadly prison riot on 25 January 2022 sealed their break as the self-styled Cártel Independiente de Colima under José Bernabé Brizuela Meraz, 'La Vaca'. Their war with the Jalisco cartel for the capital and the port of Manzanillo made Colima the world's most violent city in 2022 and 2023 and Mexico's most lethal state for four straight years, and since January 2026 the group fights on with the backing of the Sinaloa Cartel's La Mayiza faction.","history":"Los Mezcales take their name from El Mezcalito, a working-class neighbourhood of Colima city long used as a retail drug hub. Colima matters far beyond its size because Manzanillo is Mexico's busiest container port and a main entry point for precursor chemicals, and it sits between CJNG-dominated Jalisco and Michoacán; the Jalisco cartel had dominated the state since the early 2010s. José Bernabé Brizuela Meraz, 'La Vaca', 'La Bestia' or 'El Animal', a gunman with a criminal record traceable to 2007 and homicide arrests in 2011 and 2013, ran the barrio gang as the Jalisco cartel's local armed wing under regional chiefs Aldrín Miguel Jarquín Jarquín 'El Chaparrito' and José Jarquín 'R-32'. Tension built after a prison stint that La Vaca blamed on CJNG betrayal, and centred on the split of extortion income, trafficking routes and the port; released shortly before the rupture, he moved against his former bosses.\n\nThe break came on 25 January 2022, when a riot between CJNG and Mezcales inmates in dormitories A and B of the Colima Cereso left nine prisoners dead, several bodies mutilated, and seven injured. In early 2022 Los Mezcales circulated narcomantas claiming 'El Mencho' had died of a heart attack; La Vaca, who had said he owed loyalty only to El Mencho, declared the 'Cártel Independiente de Colima' and, in later banners, said Jalisco commanders had ordered him to kill Governor Indira Vizcaíno and he had refused. In February 2022 government sources told Milenio the group had switched to the Sinaloa Cartel; rival CJNG and CIC banners appeared on 8 February; prosecutors logged 15 killings in five days across Colima, Villa de Álvarez, Coquimatlán and Manzanillo and 22 in a single mid-February week. Later banners claimed a pact with the Cártel del Noreste. La Vaca was arrested on 18 August 2022 in a hotel in Mexico City's Miguel Hidalgo borough and entered the Altiplano federal prison the next day, setting off vehicle burnings and shoot-outs in Colima; a judge declined to bind him over in that case on 25 August, but he stayed in custody on further warrants, including a Tamaulipas homicide case opened at the end of August. His compadre and logistics coordinator José de Jesús Herrera González, 'El Cejón' or 'El Fumador', who had been trying to extend the group into Aguascalientes, was caught there in September 2022 with about 3,000 fentanyl doses and sentenced on 1 June 2023 to nine years and nine months.\n\nThe war made Colima the most violent city on earth. The Consejo Ciudadano para la Seguridad Pública y la Justicia Penal ranked it first in the world in 2022 with 601 homicides, 181.94 per 100,000, and first again for 2023 at 140.32, before it slipped to second for 2024 and was displaced by Culiacán in the 2025 ranking; the Mexico Peace Index rated Colima the country's most violent state. The CJNG held Manzanillo, Tecomán, Armería, Villa de Álvarez and the capital's outskirts; Los Mezcales kept the capital's barrios and prison wings. Carlos Miguel 'N', 'El Abulón' or 'El León', the group's second-in-command, was arrested by the Navy in June 2023 but later released; El Chaparrito, freed in 2022, was recaptured in Mérida, Yucatán, in an operation announced on 19 August 2024.\n\nViolence stayed extreme through 2025. Colima was Mexico's most violent state for the fourth consecutive year: 89.2 homicides per 100,000 between September 2024 and August 2025 and 84.8 in the twelve months to October 2025, with 507 murders in the first ten months of the year among roughly 730,000 residents; Manzanillo's rate of 143.4 was the third-highest of any municipality in the country. On 18-19 November 2025 the Navy's Pez Vela 2025 operation announced 54 arrests in Manzanillo, Tecomán, Villa de Álvarez and Colima, attributed by Infobae's sources to Los Mezcales and by Reforma to the CJNG; the operation passed 300 arrests by late December and 650 by February 2026. On 27 November 2025 the Navy also captured 'El Placas', a La Mayiza chief, in Colima.\n\nOn 20 January 2026 banners in Villa de Álvarez, Coquimatlán, Tecomán and Manzanillo announced that the Sinaloa Cartel's La Mayiza faction of Ismael Zambada Sicairos, 'El Mayito Flaco', was backing Los Mezcales ('estos muchachos de mezcal ya no están solos... Pura gente del MZ MF Mayito Flaco ya está en Colima'), turning the state into a front of the national CJNG-Mayiza war over the prisons, the capital and the port. On 31 January gunmen murdered the aunt and cousin of federal education secretary Mario Delgado in Colima, three attackers were killed by police, and on 4 February the state replaced its security secretary with Lieutenant Fabián Gómez. The state then pressed arrests: sicario chief José Luis 'N', 'Billyboy', on 13 May; 'El Blanco' and logistics operator Héctor Miguel 'N' on 8 June; 'El Chuy Roñas' on 11 June; hitman 'El Pirul' on 20 August; El Abulón again, in Oaxaca, on 22 August; two men and a minor with more than 2,000 methamphetamine doses and two rifles on 31 August; and, on 3 September 2026, two 29-year-old dealers, one of them a US citizen, with 100 doses of methamphetamine and 114 of fentanyl. A state prosecutors' operation in Tecomán on 25 May 2026 left one gunman dead and two agents wounded and triggered blockades, burning trailers and suspended classes in four municipalities. As of September 2026 Los Mezcales are decapitated at the top but still active in the capital, Villa de Álvarez, Tecomán and Manzanillo, fighting the CJNG for the port with Mayiza support in a four-way dispute that also draws in other groups.","leaders":[{"name":"José Bernabé Brizuela Meraz","alias":"La Vaca / La Bestia / El Animal","role":"Founder and leader; former CJNG armed-wing chief in Colima","from":2019,"to":2022,"fate":"Arrested 18 Aug 2022 in a hotel in Miguel Hidalgo, Mexico City, by capital police and the Army; entered the Altiplano federal prison on 19 Aug 2022; a judge declined to bind him over in that case on 25 Aug 2022 but he remained held on further warrants, including a Tamaulipas homicide case"},{"name":"José de Jesús Herrera González","alias":"El Cejón / El Fumador","role":"Logistics coordinator and compadre of La Vaca; ran the attempted expansion into Aguascalientes and was blamed for the violence that followed La Vaca's capture","from":2022,"to":2022,"fate":"Detained Sept 2022 in Aguascalientes with about 3,000 fentanyl doses, a firearm, two vehicles and two phones; sentenced 1 Jun 2023 to 9 years 9 months"},{"name":"Carlos Miguel N","alias":"El Abulón / El León","role":"Second-in-command; controlled the Colima capital","from":2022,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested by the Navy in June 2023, later released; re-arrested 22 Aug 2026 in Oaxaca by the Oaxaca state prosecutor's investigative police (AEI/FGEO)"},{"name":"José Luis N","alias":"Billyboy","role":"Head of sicarios","from":2025,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 13 May 2026 in Colima by the Navy and the SSPC as a priority target"},{"name":"Francisco Javier N","alias":"El Blanco","role":"Sicario coordinator","from":2025,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 8 Jun 2026 alongside logistics operator Héctor Miguel N"},{"name":"José de Jesús N","alias":"El Chuy Roñas / El Venado","role":"Cell leader and priority target","from":2025,"to":2026,"fate":"Arrested 11 Jun 2026 in Colima"}],"factions":[{"id":"mezcalito_core","name":"El Mezcalito core cell","from":2022,"to":0,"area":"Colima city and Villa de Álvarez barrios; Colima Cereso","leader":"La Vaca, then El Abulón","note":"Original barrio gang and the group's retail-drug and extortion base; repeatedly decapitated by arrests 2022-2026"},{"id":"manzanillo_tecoman_cells","name":"Manzanillo-Tecomán cells","from":2022,"to":0,"area":"Manzanillo port, Tecomán, Armería, Coquimatlán","leader":"unknown","note":"Contest the CJNG for the port corridor where most of Colima's criminal economy is concentrated; targets of the Nov 2025 Pez Vela sweep and the May 2026 Tecomán operation"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2022,"to":2022,"note":"Government sources reported in Feb 2022 that the breakaway cell had switched to the Sinaloa Cartel"},{"cartel":"cdn","from":2022,"to":2023,"note":"Later 2022 banners claimed an alliance with the Cártel del Noreste"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2026,"to":0,"note":"Banners in the early hours of 20 Jan 2026 in Villa de Álvarez, Coquimatlán, Tecomán and Manzanillo announced La Mayiza ('Mayito Flaco') support for Los Mezcales against the CJNG for the prisons, the capital and Manzanillo"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"cjng","from":2022,"to":0,"note":"Break sealed by the 25 Jan 2022 Cereso riot; CJNG Colima chief 'El Chaparrito' (recaptured in Mérida, announced 19 Aug 2024) led the offensive; the dispute over the capital and Manzanillo continues in 2026"}],"activities":["Retail sales of methamphetamine and fentanyl","Extortion (cobro de piso)","Contract killings","Kidnapping","Control of prison wings and drug distribution inside the Colima Cereso","Dispute over Manzanillo port logistics for precursor chemicals"],"peak":{"from":2022,"to":2023,"states":["COL"],"note":"Held the capital's barrios and prison wings while contesting Manzanillo, driving Colima to the top of world homicide rankings in 2022 (181.94 per 100,000) and 2023 (140.32)"},"facts":["The 25 Jan 2022 Colima Cereso riot that sealed the split killed nine inmates and injured seven, with several bodies mutilated.","CCSPJP ranked Colima city the most violent in the world in 2022 with 601 homicides among 330,329 residents, 181.94 per 100,000, and again for 2023 at 140.32; it fell to second for 2024 and was displaced by Culiacán in the 2025 ranking.","Prosecutors logged 15 killings in five days in early February 2022 and 22 in a single mid-February week as the war began.","Colima recorded 507 murders in the first ten months of 2025 in a population of roughly 730,000.","Colima's rate of 84.8 homicides per 100,000 in the 12 months to October 2025 was the highest in Mexico, its fourth consecutive year in first place.","Manzanillo's rate of 143.4 per 100,000 (Sept 2024-Aug 2025) was the third-highest of any Mexican municipality, behind Huajicori, Nayarit (278) and Santiago Jamiltepec.","The Navy's Pez Vela 2025 operation announced 54 arrests in four municipalities on 18-19 Nov 2025 and had passed 650 detentions by February 2026.","Between May and August 2026 authorities captured five senior Mezcales figures, including second-in-command 'El Abulón' in Oaxaca on 22 Aug 2026.","La Vaca had been arrested for homicide in 2011 and 2013, with a record traceable to 2007, before founding the independent cartel.","The 3 September 2026 street arrest seized 100 doses of methamphetamine and 114 of fentanyl from two 29-year-olds, one of them a US citizen."],"status_2026":"As of September 2026 Los Mezcales remain active but heavily degraded: second-in-command 'El Abulón' was recaptured in Oaxaca on 22 August 2026, sicario chiefs 'Billyboy', 'El Blanco' and 'El Chuy Roñas' fell between May and June, and street-level arrests with methamphetamine and fentanyl continue weekly in Colima city. The group still disputes the capital, Tecomán and Manzanillo with the CJNG, openly backed by La Mayiza since the 20 January 2026 banners, and Colima entered 2026 as Mexico's highest-rate homicide state for the fourth year running, with the January murder of two relatives of education secretary Mario Delgado forcing a change of state security minister.","sources":["https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/los-mezcales-vs-cjng-la-ruptura-entre-carteles-que-genero-la-ola-de-violencia-en-colima/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/los-mezcales-en-colima-el-cartel-que-se-enfrenta-al-cjng","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/06/01/dieron-9-anos-de-prision-para-operador-de-los-mezcales-y-compadre-de-la-vaca/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/authorities-arrest-54-suspected-cjng-operatives-in-colima-sweep/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/01/22/con-mantas-la-mayiza-anuncia-alianza-con-los-mezcales-en-colima/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/08/22/estados/detienen-en-oaxaca-a-el-abulon-segundo-al-mando-de-los-mezcales","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/31/capturan-a-tres-integrantes-de-los-mezcales-con-mas-de-2-mil-dosis-de-droga-en-colima/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/04/caen-dos-integrantes-de-los-mezcales-con-mas-de-200-dosis-de-droga-en-colima/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/5-tourism-destinations-among-mexico-most-violent-municipalities/"],"verified":true},{"id":"caborca","name":"Caborca Cartel","aka":["Cártel de Caborca","Los Caro Quintero","Los Páez","Los Paleteros","Organización Caro Quintero (segunda etapa)"],"founded":2017,"dissolved":0,"parent":"sonora","origin":{"state":"SON","city":"Heroica Caborca","lat":30.7146,"lng":-112.1594},"summary":"The Caborca Cartel is the Caro Quintero family network that Rafael Caro Quintero rebuilt after his 2013 release to retake the Caborca–Altar–Sonoyta smuggling corridor into Arizona from the Sinaloa Cartel's local arm, Los Salazar. Run day to day by his nephews Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero and José Gil Caro Quintero, it fought a bloody war with the Chapitos-aligned Los Salazar and Los Deltas from 2018, then lost its founder (captured July 2022, sent to the US February 2025) and most of its command tier to arrests in 2023–2025. It survives as a diminished but active player in northwestern Sonora and the southern cocaine air bridge.","history":"The Caro Quintero clan of La Noria, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, had controlled Sonora's routes since the Guadalajara Cartel era: Rafael co-founded that cartel, was captured in Alajuela, Costa Rica on 4 April 1985 after the murder of DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena, and was sentenced to 40 years. His brother Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero ran the family's 'Sonora cartel' through the 1990s until his arrest in Los Mochis, Sinaloa, in December 2001; Miguel was extradited to the United States on 25 February 2009, sentenced in Denver on 4 February 2010 to 17 years for a marijuana distribution conspiracy, and deported back to Mexico through the El Chaparral crossing in Tijuana on 20 July 2019. The family retained influence over the Guaymas–Nogales and Caborca–Altar–Sonoyta corridors even while Rafael was in prison. On 9 August 2013 a Jalisco court freed him after 28 years on a jurisdictional technicality; he disappeared into the Sinaloa–Sonora sierra, the DEA raised its reward to 20 million dollars in 2018, and he denied any return to trafficking.\n\nMexican and US intelligence say the modern organization took shape around 2017, when Caro Quintero convened relatives and veteran operators to retake northwestern Sonora from Los Salazar, the Sinaloa Cartel's historic Sonora arm (the clan of Adán Salazar Zamorano), which had aligned with Los Chapitos. Day-to-day command fell to nephews Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero, 'El R', who ran the Los Páez (Los Paleteros) cell in Caborca, Altar, Pitiquito and Sonoyta, and José Gil Caro Quintero, 'El Pelo Chino', who ran the southern logistics wing receiving Colombian cocaine at clandestine airstrips in Bacalar and Othón P. Blanco (Quintana Roo) and moving it through Chiapas, Oaxaca and Tabasco. Cousin Sajid Emilio Quintero Navidad, 'El Cadete', controlled the La Plaza cell in Guaymas–Empalme, and La Barredora 24/7 under Jesús Darío Murrieta Navarro, 'La Cara de Cochi', served as the Caborca armed wing. The network allied with La Línea of the Juárez Cartel and with Fausto Isidro Meza Flores 'Chapo Isidro' of the Beltrán Leyva remnant in southern Sonora and Guasave. In this period InSight Crime classed Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada's faction, alongside Los Chapitos, among the Caborca Cartel's enemies; the working alignment between Caborca cells and Mayo-aligned groups such as Los Rusos and Los Gigio dates from the Sinaloa civil war that began in September 2024, not from the group's founding.\n\nThe war with Los Chapitos escalated after 2018, when the Guzmán brothers created Los Deltas as their Sonora enforcement arm across nine municipalities: San Luis Río Colorado, Caborca, Carbó, Cajeme, Altar, General Plutarco Elías Calles, Sáric, Sásabe and Puerto Peñasco. In May 2020 the cartel left three dismembered bodies in coolers with a signed message at kilometer 75 near Caborca. On 21 June 2020 a night of fighting on the Caborca–Sonoyta highway left 12 dead (two civilians and ten gunmen), four burned vehicles, a seized Barrett M82 rifle and severed hands tied along the road, part of a dispute that also covered the gold tailings of Ejido El Bajío, 65 kilometers east of Puerto Peñasco. On 30 September 2020 gunmen clashed with the National Guard, injuring ten; on 26 June 2021 three gunmen were killed and seven detained near Los Alamitos; on 8 September 2021 five bodies were dumped by the railway between Caborca and Pitiquito. In January 2021 federal intelligence formally named Caro Quintero as the group's head, and in March 2021 second-in-command Juan Pablo Quintero Martínez, 'El Pablito', was arrested in the State of Mexico. Sonora recorded 1,765 murders in 2021, a roughly 20 percent rise, with Cajeme and Empalme up more than 40 percent over 2020.\n\nOn 15 July 2022 the Navy captured Caro Quintero in San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa, and moved him to the Altiplano federal prison; a Black Hawk carrying marines crashed in Los Mochis the same day, killing 14. Decapitation followed. On 7 February 2023 Mexico City police arrested his brother Carlos Caro Quintero. On 20 March 2023 a battle with Chapitos gunmen in Caborca left seven dead and four wounded. On 30 April 2023 Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero was arrested in Zapopan, Jalisco, wounded by at least three gunshots from a firefight with Los Chapitos; he was extradited to the District of Arizona on 14 July 2024 on drug-trafficking and money-laundering charges. On 12 October 2023 Quintana Roo state agents detained José Gil Caro Quintero in Tulum and released him hours later; the federal security cabinet confirmed the release and three agents were detained. On 13 January 2025 his son Martín Caro Monge, 'El M' or 'El Uno', 32, whom OFAC had sanctioned in December 2023, was arrested with nine others in Pinotepa Nacional, Oaxaca. Meanwhile Los Deltas terrorized the corridor: on 18 October 2024 they killed a US citizen and former Marine, named in Mexican reporting as Nicholas Douglas, at a service station on the road toward Puerto Peñasco after he drove past one of their illegal checkpoints, and on 20 November 2024 they opened fire on state AMIC investigators and marines in Sáric, killing one officer and wounding two officers and a marine while losing three gunmen.\n\nOn 27 February 2025 Mexico flew Caro Quintero and 28 other prisoners to the United States; he pleaded not guilty in Brooklyn federal court on 28 February to running a continuing criminal enterprise, cocaine importation conspiracy and the Camarena murder. At a second hearing on 26 March 2025 Judge Frederic Block ordered him held at MDC Brooklyn and set a 90-day deadline for the death-penalty decision; on 5 August 2025 prosecutors withdrew the death penalty, leaving life imprisonment as the maximum. A 18 September 2025 hearing addressed his conditions of isolation and a further hearing on 16 October 2025 reviewed the Bureau of Prisons' special confinement measures. Separately, the Camarena family sued him under the Anti-Terrorism Act in the Southern District of California on 20 March 2025 (case 3:25-cv-00651, Judge Cathy Ann Bencivengo), and the clerk entered a default against him on 6 June 2025. The Sinaloa Cartel's internal war, raging since 9 September 2024, remade Sonora: the Deltas' chief Omar Félix Loaiza 'El Pelón' was arrested on 14 November 2024 in Sinaloa, the Caborca Deltas boss 'Keka' fell on 23–24 February 2025, and on 24 May 2025 the Sonora prosecutor declared Los Deltas dismantled, while Caborca cells and Mayo-aligned Los Rusos kept contesting Caborca, San Luis Río Colorado and Sonoyta against surviving Salazar cells. Banners in Caborca in 2025 accused local police of working for Los Salazar.\n\nIn 2026 the organization is weakened but not extinct. Sonora dismantled a Caborca cell on 21 January 2026, and on 12 June 2026 three alleged members, including a suspected Caro Quintero relative, were arrested in the State of Mexico. At a hearing on 19 March 2026 the parties disclosed that Caro Quintero and prosecutors were in plea negotiations through defense lawyer Elizabeth Macedonio; Judge Block extended the special confinement measures, entertained a government request for an anonymous jury, set a jury trial for 8 March 2027 and scheduled a further status conference for 1 October 2026. As of September 2026 Rafael Caro Quintero, 73, remains jailed in Brooklyn with no plea entered on the public record, José Gil Caro Quintero remains a fugitive leading what is left of the network, and Sonora authorities continued arresting Los Salazar cell leaders through June–July 2026.","leaders":[{"name":"Rafael Caro Quintero","alias":"El Narco de Narcos / Don Rafa / El Príncipe","role":"Founder and top boss","from":2017,"to":2022,"fate":"Born 24 Oct 1952; captured 4 Apr 1985 in Alajuela, Costa Rica, sentenced to 40 years; freed 9 Aug 2013 after 28 years; recaptured 15 Jul 2022 in San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa; transferred to US 27 Feb 2025; pleaded not guilty in EDNY 28 Feb 2025; death penalty withdrawn 5 Aug 2025; plea negotiations disclosed at the 19 Mar 2026 hearing; jury trial set 8 Mar 2027 before Judge Frederic Block; jailed at MDC Brooklyn"},{"name":"Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero","alias":"El R / El Lito / El Mario","role":"Operational chief; head of Los Páez cell (Caborca–Altar–Sonoyta)","from":2017,"to":2023,"fate":"Arrested wounded 30 Apr 2023 in Zapopan, Jalisco, after a firefight with Los Chapitos; extradited 14 Jul 2024 to the District of Arizona on drug-trafficking and money-laundering charges; awaiting trial per last public reports"},{"name":"José Gil Caro Quintero","alias":"El Pelo Chino","role":"Southern plaza boss (Quintana Roo, Chiapas, Oaxaca, Tabasco); de facto leader after 2023","from":2017,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive wanted by US; detained and released by Quintana Roo agents in Tulum 12 Oct 2023 (three agents later detained); still at large Sept 2026"},{"name":"Martín Caro Monge","alias":"El M / El Uno","role":"Senior leader; son of José Gil Caro Quintero and nephew of Rafael","from":2023,"to":2025,"fate":"OFAC-sanctioned Dec 2023; arrested 13 Jan 2025 aged 32 in Pinotepa Nacional, Oaxaca, with nine others; in Mexican custody"},{"name":"Juan Pablo Quintero Martínez","alias":"El Pablito","role":"Second-in-command","from":2017,"to":2021,"fate":"Arrested March 2021 in the State of Mexico over a November 2020 homicide"},{"name":"Sajid Emilio Quintero Navidad","alias":"El Cadete","role":"Commander of La Plaza cell (Guaymas–Empalme); cousin of Rafael","from":2017,"to":0,"fate":"Reported detained in 2017 and said to keep directing La Plaza from prison; detention date and current custody status not independently confirmed"},{"name":"Jesús Darío Murrieta Navarro","alias":"La Cara de Cochi","role":"Head of La Barredora 24/7 armed wing in Caborca","from":2020,"to":0,"fate":"At large as of last public reporting (2021); status unclear"},{"name":"Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero","alias":"El Señor de los Cielos del Desierto","role":"Predecessor boss of the family's Sonora organization (pre-Caborca era); brother of Rafael","from":1985,"to":2001,"fate":"Arrested Dec 2001 in Los Mochis; extradited to the US 25 Feb 2009; sentenced in Denver 4 Feb 2010 to 17 years for marijuana distribution conspiracy; deported to Mexico 20 Jul 2019; no confirmed public role in the Caborca Cartel"}],"factions":[{"id":"los_paez","name":"Los Páez / Los Paleteros","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Caborca, Altar, Pitiquito, Sonoyta, Puerto Peñasco (SON)","leader":"Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero 'El R'","note":"Core border-corridor cell; badly weakened after El R's April 2023 arrest and July 2024 extradition"},{"id":"la_barredora_247","name":"La Barredora 24/7","from":2019,"to":0,"area":"Caborca municipality (SON)","leader":"Jesús Darío Murrieta Navarro 'La Cara de Cochi'","note":"Armed wing in Caborca; not related to the Tabasco group of the same name; predecessor hitman chief 'El Barbas' reportedly killed 21 Jul 2020"},{"id":"la_plaza","name":"La Plaza","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Guaymas, Empalme, Yaqui Valley (SON)","leader":"Sajid Emilio Quintero Navidad 'El Cadete'; field chief Francisco Javier Espinoza Camacho 'El Picipi'","note":"Linked to a September 2019 arson that killed two children in Empalme and a clandestine crematorium with 27 sets of remains found December 2020"},{"id":"southern_air_bridge","name":"José Gil Caro Quintero southern network","from":2017,"to":0,"area":"Quintana Roo (Bacalar, Othón P. Blanco), Chiapas, Oaxaca, Tabasco, Campeche","leader":"José Gil Caro Quintero 'El Pelo Chino'; son Martín Caro Monge 'El M' until Jan 2025","note":"Receives Colombian cocaine and precursors by light aircraft and speedboat; contested with Los Chapitos in Tabasco, Campeche and Yucatán"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"juarez","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"La Línea, the Juárez Cartel's armed wing, shared the anti-Chapitos front along the Chihuahua–Sonora sierra"},{"cartel":"beltran_leyva","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores 'Chapo Isidro' network (Guasave, southern Sonora) allied against Los Chapitos"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2024,"to":0,"note":"Mayo-aligned Los Rusos and Los Gigio (Nogales–Magdalena) cooperated with Caborca cells against Los Deltas after the Sinaloa civil war began Sept 2024; before that InSight Crime listed El Mayo's faction among Caborca's rivals"},{"cartel":"sonora","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Revival of the Caro Quintero brothers' earlier Sonora organization; same family, routes and Caborca base"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"chapitos","from":2018,"to":0,"note":"Main enemy; fought Los Salazar, Los Deltas, Gente Nueva, Cazadores and Rumas cells for the Caborca–Sonoyta corridor (12 dead 21 Jun 2020; 7 dead 20 Mar 2023)"},{"cartel":"sinaloa","from":2017,"to":0,"note":"Los Salazar clan, Sinaloa's historic Sonora arm, is the direct adversary in the Caborca, Altar and Sonoyta plazas and the El Bajío gold zone"},{"cartel":"mayiza","from":2017,"to":2024,"note":"InSight Crime's Sonora analysis placed Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada's faction among Caborca's enemies, including three days of shootings in Magdalena de Kino in July 2021, before the post-2024 realignment"},{"cartel":"government","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Clashes with National Guard (30 Sep 2020) and Navy; leadership arrests 2021–2026; Caro Quintero transferred to US Feb 2025"}],"activities":["Cocaine transshipment from South America via clandestine airstrips in Quintana Roo","Fentanyl and methamphetamine smuggling into Arizona via Sonoyta–Lukeville and Sásabe–Altar","Precursor-chemical imports through the Port of Guaymas","Marijuana and heroin trafficking (legacy routes)","Migrant smuggling through Altar and Sásabe","Illegal gold extraction and control of mine tailings at Ejido El Bajío","Extortion, kidnapping and contract killings in northwestern Sonora","Weapons trafficking and armored-vehicle building"],"peak":{"from":2019,"to":2022,"states":["SON","ROO","CAM","OAX","CHP"],"note":"Before Caro Quintero's July 2022 capture the network held the Caborca–Altar–Sonoyta corridor, the Guaymas–Empalme plaza and a southern cocaine air bridge, and was strong enough to launch open assaults on Los Salazar positions"},"facts":["Rafael Caro Quintero served 28 years before a court freed him on 9 August 2013; the DEA later offered a 20-million-dollar reward, the largest ever posted for a Mexican trafficker at the time","His brother Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero, who ran the family's Sonora organization in the 1990s, was arrested in December 2001, extradited on 25 February 2009, sentenced to 17 years in Denver on 4 February 2010 and deported to Mexico on 20 July 2019","His 15 July 2022 capture in Choix, Sinaloa, was followed hours later by a Navy Black Hawk crash in Los Mochis that killed 14 marines","On 21 June 2020 fighting on the Caborca–Sonoyta highway left 12 dead (2 civilians, 10 gunmen), four burned vehicles and severed hands tied along the road","A 20 March 2023 gun battle with Los Chapitos in Caborca left 7 dead and 4 wounded; 11 weapons and 10 vehicles were seized","Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero was arrested on 30 April 2023 with at least three gunshot wounds and extradited to the District of Arizona on 14 July 2024","Mexico transferred Caro Quintero and 28 other prisoners to the United States on 27 February 2025; he pleaded not guilty in Brooklyn the next day","US prosecutors withdrew the death penalty on 5 August 2025; plea negotiations surfaced at the 19 March 2026 hearing and a jury trial is set for 8 March 2027, with a status conference on 1 October 2026","Martín Caro Monge 'El M', 32, sanctioned by OFAC in December 2023, was arrested with nine others in Pinotepa Nacional, Oaxaca, on 13 January 2025","The rival Los Deltas operated in nine Sonora municipalities and were declared dismantled by Sonora prosecutors on 24 May 2025 after leader Omar Félix Loaiza's 14 November 2024 arrest","Sonora recorded 1,765 murders in 2021, about 20 percent more than the previous year, with Cajeme and Empalme up more than 40 percent"],"status_2026":"Active but diminished: with Caro Quintero jailed at MDC Brooklyn (plea negotiations disclosed 19 March 2026, status conference 1 October 2026, trial set 8 March 2027) and El R, El Pablito and El M in custody, the fugitive José Gil Caro Quintero leads a network that still contests Caborca, Sonoyta and the southern cocaine air bridge, now leaning on Mayo-aligned Los Rusos against surviving Los Salazar cells. Mexican forces dismantled a Caborca cell in Sonora on 21 January 2026 and arrested three alleged members, including a suspected Caro Quintero relative, in the State of Mexico on 12 June 2026, while Sonora continued arresting Los Salazar cell leaders in June–July 2026.","sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/caborca-cartel/","https://insightcrime.org/noticias/three-criminal-fronts-behind-violence-sonora-mexico/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/rafael-caro-quinteros-nephew-detained-to-be-extradited-to-us/","https://proyectopuente.com.mx/2024/07/15/extraditan-a-eeuu-a-rodrigo-paez-quintero-sobrino-de-rafael-caro-quintero-y-presunto-lider-del-cartel-de-caborca/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/rafael-caro-quintero-cronologia-caso-pena-muerte-eu","https://www.usnews.com/news/world/articles/2026-03-19/alleged-mexican-drug-lord-rafael-caro-quintero-in-plea-talks-in-u-s","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2024/11/20/quienes-son-los-deltas-brazo-armado-de-los-chapitos-que-aterrorizan-el-norte-de-sonora/","https://zetatijuana.com/2025/01/detienen-en-oaxaca-a-el-m-lider-del-cartel-de-caborca-y-sobrino-de-caro-quintero/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Caro_Quintero","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Caro_Quintero","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_Caborca","https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/camarena-v-caro-quintero-3-25-cv-00651/"],"verified":true},{"id":"autodefensas","name":"Autodefensas (Self-Defense Militias)","aka":["Grupos de autodefensa","Policía comunitaria","Guardias comunitarias","Consejo General de Autodefensas de Michoacán","Fuerza Rural Estatal (2014)","CRAC-PC","UPOEG","Pueblos Unidos","El Machete"],"founded":2013,"dissolved":0,"parent":"","origin":{"state":"MIC","city":"Tepalcatepec","lat":19.1878,"lng":-102.8481},"summary":"Autodefensas are armed civilian militias that rose against cartel extortion where the Mexican state failed, most famously in Michoacán's Tierra Caliente from 24 February 2013, building on older indigenous community police in Guerrero (CRAC-PC, 1995) and Cherán (2011). The Michoacán movement helped destroy the Knights Templar but was legalized as the Fuerza Rural in May 2014, infiltrated by traffickers and, within a few years, several of its wings had become cartels in their own right (Los Viagras, Cárteles Unidos, Pueblos Unidos). New community militias keep appearing in Guerrero, Chiapas and the State of Mexico into 2026.","history":"Community self-defense in Mexico predates the cartel wars. On 15 October 1995 Mixtec, Tlapanec and Nahua communities of Guerrero's Costa Chica and Montaña founded the Coordinadora Regional de Autoridades Comunitarias–Policía Comunitaria (CRAC-PC) in San Luis Acatlán, a customary-law police and justice system later recognized by Guerrero's Law 701 (April 2011); it grew to about 2,500 police serving 210 communities in 29 municipalities. On 15 April 2011 the Purépecha town of Cherán expelled illegal loggers and their gunmen and won legal self-government. In October 2012 Nestora Salgado led a CRAC-PC unit in Olinalá; she was jailed from 21 August 2013 until 18 March 2016, after a UN working group found her detention arbitrary. On 5 January 2013 more than 1,000 armed men of Bruno Plácido's Unión de Pueblos y Organizaciones del Estado de Guerrero (UPOEG) took Ayutla de los Libres and Tecoanapa to fight Los Rojos.\n\nThe word 'autodefensas' entered global usage on 24 February 2013, when physician José Manuel Mireles Valverde led an uprising in Tepalcatepec and lime grower Hipólito Mora another in La Ruana (Buenavista) against the Knights Templar, who taxed every harvest and abducted and raped at will. Juan José Farías 'El Abuelo', a Tepalcatepec rancher already detained in 2009 in Buenavista Tomatlán with weapons and drugs, was among the founders. Militias spread through Buenavista, Coalcomán, Aguililla, Chinicuila, Tancítaro and Los Reyes, entering Apatzingán in February 2014; the movement's Consejo General claimed some 20,000 members in more than 30 of Michoacán's 113 municipalities. After Mireles was injured in a 4 January 2014 plane crash and soldiers killed civilians in Antúnez on 13 January, President Peña Nieto named Alfredo Castillo federal commissioner on 15 January; on 27 January the militias signed a pact to register weapons and legalize as rural defense corps. Federal forces then killed Templar chiefs Nazario Moreno (9 March) and Enrique Plancarte (31 March). Mora was jailed from 11 March to May 2014 over two killings, and in March the council replaced Mireles as spokesman with Estanislao Beltrán 'Papá Pitufo'.\n\nOn 10 May 2014 Castillo swore in the first 120 members of the Fuerza Rural in Tepalcatepec, with more than 3,000 applications pending and 6,442 firearms registered by the day before. Mireles refused to disarm and was arrested on 27 June 2014 in La Mira, Lázaro Cárdenas, under the federal firearms law together with a large group of followers (counts reported from 45 to 185); he was paroled on 11 May 2017 on 30,000 pesos bail and acquitted on 18 July 2018 for insufficient evidence. The legalized force absorbed men with cartel pasts, including Luis Antonio Torres 'El Americano' and the Sierra Santana brothers' Los Viagras, former Templar and Familia gunmen. On 16 December 2014 a shootout in La Ruana between Mora's men and El Americano's group killed 11, including Mora's son Manuel; both leaders were jailed on 27 December and freed in March 2015. Los Viagras, who had seized Apatzingán's municipal palace in December 2014, were violently evicted by federal police on 6 January 2015, leaving 10 civilians dead and 21 wounded. Servando Gómez 'La Tuta' was captured on 27 February 2015, Castillo left in January 2015, and the Fuerza Rural was wound down.\n\nWhat followed was co-optation. Los Viagras, founded by the Sierra Santana brothers in Buenavista Tomatlán and led by Nicolás Sierra Santana 'El Gordo' (also 'El Coruco'), turned to extortion, meth and kidnapping across Buenavista, Apatzingán and Huetamo. El Abuelo, arrested by the Navy on 27 May 2018 and released by a federal judge who cited procedural irregularities, formed the Cártel de Tepalcatepec and in 2019 united it with Los Viagras and other ex-militias as Cárteles Unidos to resist the Jalisco Cartel's sieges of Tepalcatepec and Aguililla. In August 2020 avocado growers in Ario de Rosales, Salvador Escalante, Nuevo Urecho and Taretan armed hundreds of men as Pueblos Unidos, raising 54 barricades; the army labeled it a Cárteles Unidos proxy, and on 21 May 2022 state forces turned back a convoy of more than 20 vehicles heading toward Morelia. Mireles died of COVID-19 on 25 November 2020, aged 62, while serving as an ISSSTE medical sub-delegate in Michoacán. Hipólito Mora, who won 54,794 votes (3.13 percent) for the Partido Encuentro Solidario in the 6 June 2021 gubernatorial election, was ambushed with Barrett rifles and burned in his armored Tahoe in La Ruana on 29 June 2023, aged 67, together with three bodyguards, two of them in state Guardia Civil uniform; prosecutors blame Los Viagras. Bruno Plácido was shot dead in Chilpancingo on 17 October 2023, two days after UPOEG co-founder Juan Carlos González. In Chiapas, Tsotsil and Tseltal villagers formed El Machete in Pantelhó in July 2021 after the 5 July killing of activist Simón Pedro Pérez López, then abducted 21 people on 26 July 2021, in a still-running feud with the Los Herrera clan.\n\nThe pattern continued into the mid-2020s. On 8 December 2023 farmers in Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán (State of Mexico), summoned to a soccer field by La Familia Michoacana to pay one peso per square meter of bean and oat crops, fought back with machetes and guns: 14 died (10 gunmen, including a leader known as 'El Payaso', and 4 villagers) and prosecutors ruled it legitimate defense. In Guerrero, about 50 armed men of Guajes de Ayala, formed in 2020, track La Nueva Familia Michoacana with drones on the road to Acapulco. Washington now treats the militia offspring as terrorists: Cárteles Unidos was designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization in February 2025 alongside five other Mexican groups; on 14 August 2025 the Treasury sanctioned Cárteles Unidos and Los Viagras together with seven leaders — Juan José Farías Álvarez 'El Abuelo', Luis Enrique Barragán Chávez 'Wicho', Alfonso Fernández Magallón 'Poncho' and Edgar Valeriano Orozco Cabadas 'El Kamoni' of Cárteles Unidos, and Nicolás Sierra Santana 'El Gordo', Heladio Cisneros Flores 'La Sirena' and César Alejandro Sepúlveda Arellano 'El Botox' of Los Viagras. A 10-million-dollar US reward was posted for El Abuelo in July 2025, and in July 2026 the State Department designated Los Viagras a Foreign Terrorist Organization.\n\nAs of September 2026 the original Michoacán movement exists only as legacy and as cartels. A joint army–navy raid on Tepalcatepec on 18 April 2026 seized a son of El Abuelo but missed him; on 2 July 2026 a federal grand jury in the District of Columbia indicted his son Juan José 'Juanjo' Farías Mendoza, 31, and nephew Israel 'Papo' Vega Farías, 37, for conspiring to manufacture and distribute methamphetamine for import into the United States, providing material support to a foreign terrorist organization and using machine guns and destructive devices in drug trafficking, each facing up to life in prison. The Mora murder case advanced with an arrest on 3 January 2026 and the 24 July 2026 capture in California of alleged gunman 'El Conejillo', whose extradition Michoacán requested on 11 August 2026; Mora's nephew had been killed in La Ruana on 1 November 2025. After Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo was shot dead at the Festival de las Velas on 1 November 2025, Michoacán's answer was mass protest — a Morelia march on 2 November, nationwide demonstrations in more than 50 cities on 15 November — the swearing-in of his widow Grecia Quiroz as mayor on 5 November, and President Sheinbaum's federal Plan Michoacán para la Paz y la Justicia, not new militias; by July 2026 authorities had arrested the alleged mastermind, CJNG-linked Ramón Álvarez Ayala 'R1'. El Machete re-armed in July 2026 after the killing of its commander Fernando Ruiz in Chenalhó, and the CRAC-PC continues in 29 Guerrero municipalities while fighting in court to restore the Law 701 recognition stripped in 2018.","leaders":[{"name":"José Manuel Mireles Valverde","alias":"El Doctor Mireles","role":"Founder of the Tepalcatepec militia; spokesman of the Consejo General de Autodefensas","from":2013,"to":2014,"fate":"Born 24 Oct 1958 in Tepalcatepec; ousted as spokesman March 2014; arrested 27 Jun 2014 in La Mira, Lázaro Cárdenas, on federal firearms charges; paroled 11 May 2017; acquitted 18 Jul 2018; ISSSTE medical sub-delegate in Michoacán from August 2019; died of COVID-19 on 25 Nov 2020, aged 62"},{"name":"Hipólito Mora Chávez","alias":"","role":"Founder and commander of the La Ruana (Buenavista) militia","from":2013,"to":2014,"fate":"Jailed Mar–May 2014 and Dec 2014–Mar 2015; son Manuel killed 16 Dec 2014; ran for governor for Partido Encuentro Solidario on 6 Jun 2021 (54,794 votes, 3.13%, fifth place); ambushed with Barrett rifles and killed with three bodyguards in La Ruana 29 Jun 2023, aged 67; Los Viagras suspects charged 2024–2026, alleged gunman arrested in California Jul 2026"},{"name":"Estanislao Beltrán Torres","alias":"Papá Pitufo","role":"Spokesman of the Consejo General (pro-legalization wing); Buenavista commander","from":2014,"to":2014,"fate":"Led the wing that joined the Fuerza Rural May 2014; sought a 2015 federal deputy candidacy; alive, out of public office, still making occasional statements (2025)"},{"name":"Juan José Farías Álvarez","alias":"El Abuelo","role":"Tepalcatepec militia founder turned boss of the Cártel de Tepalcatepec and Cárteles Unidos","from":2013,"to":0,"fate":"Born 10 Aug 1970 in Tepalcatepec; detained 2009; arrested by the Navy 27 May 2018 and freed by a federal judge; charged in the US; Cárteles Unidos designated FTO Feb 2025; US$10M reward Jul 2025; OFAC-sanctioned 14 Aug 2025; escaped the 18 Apr 2026 Tepalcatepec raid in which a son was seized; son and nephew indicted in Washington 2 Jul 2026; fugitive"},{"name":"Luis Antonio Torres González","alias":"El Americano","role":"Commander of the Buenavista rival militia (H3) inside the Fuerza Rural","from":2014,"to":2014,"fate":"Jailed 27 Dec 2014 after the La Ruana shootout (11 dead); released Mar 2015; later status unclear"},{"name":"Nicolás Sierra Santana","alias":"El Gordo / El Coruco","role":"Los Viagras founder who entered the movement and the Fuerza Rural","from":2014,"to":0,"fate":"Fugitive; OFAC-sanctioned 14 Aug 2025 as founder and leader of Los Viagras; Los Viagras designated FTO July 2026"},{"name":"Bruno Plácido Valerio","alias":"","role":"Co-founder of CRAC-PC (1995) and founder-leader of UPOEG (Guerrero)","from":1995,"to":2023,"fate":"Arrested Aug 2022 and released within 24 hours; shot dead outside a state office in Chilpancingo 17 Oct 2023"},{"name":"Nestora Salgado García","alias":"","role":"Commander of the Olinalá CRAC-PC community police","from":2012,"to":2013,"fate":"Arrested 21 Aug 2013 on kidnapping charges; freed 18 Mar 2016 after a UN working group called her detention arbitrary; Morena senator for Guerrero 1 Sep 2018 – 31 Aug 2024; out of the Senate since"},{"name":"Alfredo Castillo Cervantes","alias":"","role":"Federal Commissioner for Security in Michoacán who legalized militias as the Fuerza Rural","from":2014,"to":2015,"fate":"Appointed 15 Jan 2014; left post Jan 2015; later headed CONADE; Fuerza Rural wound down after his departure"}],"factions":[{"id":"consejo_general","name":"Consejo General de Autodefensas y Comunitarios de Michoacán","from":2013,"to":2014,"area":"Tierra Caliente: Tepalcatepec, Buenavista, Coalcomán, Aguililla, Apatzingán, Tancítaro (MIC)","leader":"José Manuel Mireles; Estanislao Beltrán from March 2014","note":"Unified command of the 2013–14 uprising; split in March 2014 between Mireles' independents and Beltrán's pro-government wing"},{"id":"fuerza_rural","name":"Fuerza Rural Estatal","from":2014,"to":2016,"area":"Michoacán Tierra Caliente","leader":"Alfredo Castillo (state); Estanislao Beltrán, Luis Antonio Torres 'El Americano'","note":"Created 10 May 2014 with 120 recruits and over 3,000 applicants; infiltrated by Los Viagras and ex-Templarios; wound down after Castillo left in Jan 2015"},{"id":"grupo_h3","name":"Grupo H3","from":2014,"to":2014,"area":"Buenavista, Los Reyes (MIC)","leader":"Luis Antonio Torres 'El Americano'","note":"Absorbed former Templar gunmen; fought Hipólito Mora's group in La Ruana on 16 Dec 2014 (11 dead)"},{"id":"los_viagras_militia","name":"Los Viagras (militia phase)","from":2014,"to":2019,"area":"Buenavista Tomatlán, Apatzingán, Huetamo (MIC)","leader":"Carlos and Nicolás Sierra Santana","note":"Templar/Familia sicarios who rebranded as autodefensas in 2014 and joined the Fuerza Rural; occupied Apatzingán's municipal palace in Dec 2014 and were evicted 6 Jan 2015 (10 civilians dead); became a cartel and Cárteles Unidos pillar; FTO July 2026"},{"id":"tepalcatepec_farias","name":"Autodefensas de Tepalcatepec / El Abuelo network","from":2013,"to":2019,"area":"Tepalcatepec (MIC)","leader":"Juan José Farías 'El Abuelo'","note":"Re-armed in 2019 against CJNG sieges and evolved into the Cártel de Tepalcatepec, core of Cárteles Unidos"},{"id":"cheran_ronda","name":"Ronda Comunitaria de Cherán","from":2011,"to":0,"area":"Cherán, Meseta Purépecha (MIC)","leader":"Consejo Mayor de Gobierno Comunal","note":"Purépecha uprising of 15 Apr 2011 against loggers and their gunmen; legally recognized self-government; not co-opted"},{"id":"ostula_guardia","name":"Guardia Comunal de Santa María Ostula","from":2009,"to":0,"area":"Aquila, Michoacán coast","leader":"Cemeí Verdía Zepeda (commander from 2014)","note":"Nahua community guard revived in 2014 against the Templarios; Verdía was arrested in July 2015 and later freed"},{"id":"crac_pc","name":"CRAC-PC","from":1995,"to":0,"area":"Costa Chica and Montaña of Guerrero (San Luis Acatlán, Malinaltepec, Marquelia, Olinalá, Chilapa)","leader":"Rotating regional coordinators","note":"Founded 15 Oct 1995; about 2,500 police in 210 communities of 29 municipalities; Law 701 recognition (2011) weakened in 2018"},{"id":"upoeg","name":"UPOEG (Sistema de Seguridad y Justicia Ciudadana)","from":2013,"to":0,"area":"Ayutla de los Libres, Tecoanapa, San Marcos, Florencio Villarreal (GRO)","leader":"Bruno Plácido Valerio (killed 2023)","note":"Armed uprising of 5 Jan 2013 with over 1,000 men against Los Rojos; later accused of links to crime; leader killed 17 Oct 2023"},{"id":"pueblos_unidos","name":"Pueblos Unidos","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Ario de Rosales, Salvador Escalante, Nuevo Urecho, Taretan (MIC)","leader":"José Artemio Maldonado 'El Michoacano' (identified by authorities)","note":"Avocado growers' militia with 54 barricades; army labeled it a Cárteles Unidos proxy against CJNG; convoy halted near Morelia 21 May 2022"},{"id":"el_machete","name":"El Machete","from":2021,"to":0,"area":"Pantelhó and Chenalhó, Los Altos de Chiapas (CHP)","leader":"Collective command; commander Fernando Ruiz killed July 2026","note":"Tsotsil-Tseltal militia formed July 2021 against the Los Herrera clan after the 5 Jul killing of Simón Pedro Pérez López; abducted 21 people 26 Jul 2021; federal forces entered Pantelhó Feb 2025"},{"id":"texcapilla","name":"Texcaltitlán villagers (Texcapilla)","from":2023,"to":0,"area":"Texcaltitlán, southern State of Mexico (MEX)","leader":"None (spontaneous)","note":"On 8 Dec 2023 killed 10 Familia Michoacana extortionists, including 'El Payaso', and lost 4 of their own; no charges filed; 8 residents still missing a year later"},{"id":"guajes_de_ayala","name":"Guajes de Ayala self-defense group","from":2020,"to":0,"area":"Sierra of Guerrero on the route to Acapulco (GRO)","leader":"Local commanders","note":"About 50 armed men using drones against La Nueva Familia Michoacana; profiled by France 24 in March 2026"}],"allies":[{"cartel":"government","from":2014,"to":2015,"note":"27 Jan 2014 pact and 10 May 2014 Fuerza Rural incorporated militias under federal commissioner Alfredo Castillo; joint operations against the Templarios"},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Ex-militia networks (El Abuelo's Tepalcatepec group, Los Viagras, Pueblos Unidos) became or backed Cárteles Unidos against CJNG"},{"cartel":"autodefensas","from":2013,"to":2014,"note":"Michoacán and Guerrero militias exchanged advisers and rhetoric; UPOEG and CRAC-PC models inspired Tierra Caliente"}],"rivals":[{"cartel":"templarios","from":2013,"to":2015,"note":"Founding enemy in Michoacán; militias helped federal forces dismantle the Templarios by 2015 (Nazario Moreno 9 Mar 2014, Enrique Plancarte 31 Mar 2014, La Tuta 27 Feb 2015)"},{"cartel":"cjng","from":2019,"to":0,"note":"Sieges of Tepalcatepec and Aguililla drove ex-militias into Cárteles Unidos; Pueblos Unidos formed to block CJNG in the avocado belt"},{"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana","from":2020,"to":0,"note":"Texcaltitlán villagers killed 10 of its gunmen on 8 Dec 2023; Guerrero militias such as Guajes de Ayala fight its expansion"},{"cartel":"rojos","from":2013,"to":2018,"note":"UPOEG rose in Ayutla in January 2013 against Los Rojos' kidnappings in the Costa Chica"},{"cartel":"ardillos","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"CRAC-PC units around Chilapa clashed repeatedly with Los Ardillos"},{"cartel":"carteles_unidos","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"Los Viagras wing fought and killed the Mora family (Manuel 2014, Hipólito 2023, nephew 2025)"},{"cartel":"government","from":2014,"to":0,"note":"Army killings in Antúnez 13 Jan 2014, the arrest of Mireles 27 Jun 2014, the 6 Jan 2015 federal police eviction in Apatzingán (10 civilians dead) and the 18 Apr 2026 Tepalcatepec raid"},{"cartel":"other","from":2021,"to":0,"note":"Los Herrera clan in Pantelhó, Chiapas, versus El Machete; commander Fernando Ruiz killed July 2026"}],"activities":["Armed checkpoints and barricades to expel cartel cells","Community policing and customary-law justice (CRAC-PC, Cherán)","Seizure of cartel property and expulsion of extortionists from lime and avocado zones","Co-opted wings: extortion of avocado, lime and citrus growers, cattle ranchers and whole towns","Co-opted wings: methamphetamine, cocaine and synthetic-opioid trafficking (Los Viagras, Cártel de Tepalcatepec)","Co-opted wings: kidnapping, contract killing and forced internet-service rackets","Co-opted wings: use of improvised explosive devices and recruitment of foreign mercenaries (Cárteles Unidos)"],"peak":{"from":2013,"to":2014,"states":["MIC","GRO"],"note":"By early 2014 the Michoacán movement claimed about 20,000 members in more than 30 municipalities and had registered 6,442 weapons by 9 May 2014, while UPOEG and CRAC-PC fielded thousands in Guerrero"},"facts":["The Michoacán uprising began on 24 February 2013 with simultaneous revolts in Tepalcatepec and La Ruana against the Knights Templar","By 9 May 2014 militias had registered 6,442 firearms; on 10 May the first 120 recruits were sworn into the Fuerza Rural with more than 3,000 applications pending","José Manuel Mireles was arrested on 27 June 2014 with a large group of followers, spent almost three years in prison before parole on 11 May 2017 and was acquitted on 18 July 2018","The 16 December 2014 La Ruana shootout between rival militias killed 11 people, including Hipólito Mora's son Manuel; three weeks later, on 6 January 2015, federal police evicting Los Viagras from Apatzingán's municipal palace left 10 civilians dead and 21 wounded","Hipólito Mora won 54,794 votes (3.13%) for governor on 6 June 2021 and was killed aged 67 with three bodyguards on 29 June 2023","Guerrero's CRAC-PC, founded 15 October 1995, fields about 2,500 community police in 210 communities across 29 municipalities","On 5 January 2013 more than 1,000 UPOEG men seized Ayutla de los Libres; founder Bruno Plácido was murdered on 17 October 2023","On 8 December 2023 villagers of Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán, killed 10 Familia Michoacana gunmen demanding one peso per square meter of crops; 4 villagers died","Pueblos Unidos raised 54 barricades across four avocado municipalities and sent a convoy of more than 20 vehicles toward Morelia on 21 May 2022","El Machete abducted 21 people in Pantelhó on 26 July 2021; the US posted a US$10 million reward for ex-militia leader El Abuelo in July 2025","On 14 August 2025 the US Treasury sanctioned Cárteles Unidos, Los Viagras and seven of their leaders, four from Cárteles Unidos and three from Los Viagras","On 2 July 2026 a grand jury in the District of Columbia indicted El Abuelo's son Juan José Farías Mendoza, 31, and nephew Israel Vega Farías, 37, for meth trafficking, material support to a foreign terrorist organization and machine-gun offenses, each facing up to life"],"status_2026":"The original Michoacán militias are gone as a movement: their leaders are dead (Mireles 2020, Mora 2023), retired, or, like El Abuelo and Nicolás Sierra Santana, fugitive cartel bosses whose groups Washington designated terrorist organizations (Cárteles Unidos February 2025, Los Viagras July 2026), sanctioned on 14 August 2025 and indicted on 2 July 2026, and whom Mexican forces targeted in the 18 April 2026 Tepalcatepec raid that seized a son of El Abuelo but missed him. Prosecutions for Mora's murder continue (arrest 3 January 2026; alleged gunman caught in California 24 July 2026, extradition requested 11 August 2026), and after Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo's assassination on 1 November 2025 the response was mass protest, his widow Grecia Quiroz's swearing-in on 5 November and a federal Plan Michoacán rather than new militias, with the alleged mastermind arrested by July 2026. Community self-defense nonetheless persists and regenerates: CRAC-PC still polices 29 Guerrero municipalities, Guajes de Ayala fights La Nueva Familia Michoacana, and El Machete re-armed in July 2026 after its commander was killed in Chenalhó.","sources":["https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/lo-que-sabemos-de-la-emboscada-y-asesinato-de-hipolito-mora-en-la-ruana-michoacan/","https://www.razon.com.mx/mexico/2020/11/25/muere-jose-manuel-mireles-fundador-de-las-autodefensas/","https://expansion.mx/nacional/2014/05/10/las-autodefensas-se-ponen-el-uniforme-y-empiezan-labor-como-fuerza-rural","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/12/09/sube-a-14-la-cifra-de-muertos-en-enfrentamiento-de-texcaltitlan-once-son-de-la-familia-michoacana/","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2022/05/21/comando-de-pueblos-unidos-fue-frenado-en-su-intento-de-acercarse-a-la-capital-de-michoacan/","https://grupoanimal.mx/estados/quien-es-bruno-placido-autodefensas-guerrero","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0224","https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/two-high-ranking-members-united-cartels-charged-drug-trafficking-providing-material-support","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Manuel_Mireles_Valverde","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip%C3%B3lito_Mora","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nestora_Salgado","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Viagras","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Far%C3%ADas_%C3%81lvarez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Manzo"],"verified":true}];
